local _, ns = ...
local E = ns.DB.entries
E["item:ambercorn"] = {t="item",n="Ambercorn",z="mulgore",s="Ambercorn is a sacred nut that falls from the tall, ancient trees scattered across Mulgore. Infused with natural magic, it serves as a vital reagent in tauren shamanic brews, most notably used to induce spiritual visions during youth trials.",h="Ambercorn is a sacred nut that falls from the tall, ancient trees scattered across Mulgore.",sec={{t="Gifts of the Canopy",b="Gathered from beneath the sturdy boughs of Mulgore's great trees, ambercorns are more than ordinary nuts to the tauren. Rich with the spiritual vitality of the land, they hold latent magical properties that tauren mystics have understood and utilized for generations. Young hunters and wanderers learn early to keep their eyes on the grass beneath the canopy, honoring the Earth Mother's gifts that fall freely from above.",sp=0},{t="The Seer's Brew",b="In Bloodhoof Village, the seer Zarlman Two-Moons relies on ambercorn when preparing young tauren for the Rite of Vision. Combined with stones gathered from the plains' pristine water wells, ambercorn is steeped into the potent Water of the Seers. Consuming this ceremonial elixir before the ancestral flames allows an initiate's senses to shift, revealing a guiding spirit animal meant to lead them to their next destination.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is an ambercorn?",a="Ambercorn is a seedpod or nut that drops from the large, ancient trees of Mulgore. It carries innate natural magic attuned to the spirits of the land.",al={"ambercorn","what are ambercorns"},it="identity"},{q="Where can ambercorn be found?",a="They are scattered directly on the ground underneath the canopies of the mighty trees spread across the grasslands of Mulgore, especially in the areas surrounding Bloodhoof Village.",al={"ambercorn location","where to find ambercorn","how to get ambercorn"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What is ambercorn used for?",a="Tauren seers combine ambercorn with well stones to brew the Water of the Seers, a ceremonial liquid consumed during the Rite of Vision to reveal spiritual guides.",al={"ambercorn purpose","what does ambercorn do"},it="purpose"},{q="Who asks for ambercorn in Bloodhoof Village?",a="Zarlman Two-Moons, a respected seer residing in Bloodhoof Village, tasks initiates with gathering ambercorn as part of the trials required to prove one's connection to the Earth Mother.",al={"who needs ambercorn","ambercorn quest giver"}},{q="Where does Ambercorn come from, and who made it?",a="Ambercorn is not crafted by mortal hands; it is a natural nut or cone that drops from the mighty trees standing across the plains of Mulgore. The tauren revere these seeds as gifts from the sacred earth, holding inherent magical elements woven directly into the land.",al={"where does ambercorn come from","ambercorn origin","who made ambercorn","where to find ambercorn lore"},it="origin",add=true},{q="Why does anyone want Ambercorn?",a="Tauren shamans like Zarlman Two-Moons seek Ambercorn for its latent magical properties. When combined with sacred well stones and brewed into the potent Water of the Seers, it induces a spiritual trance before the tribal fire, granting young tauren the visions required to complete the sacred Rite of Vision.",al={"why is ambercorn needed","why collect ambercorn","who wants ambercorn","ambercorn uses"},it="why_wanted",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Every young tauren undergoing the traditional Rites of the Earth Mother learns to recognize ambercorn as an essential ingredient for the vision quest that reveals their spiritual path.",["class:Shaman"]="Shamanic tradition honors natural catalysts like ambercorn, recognizing that the Earth Mother embeds spiritual magic in the physical harvest of her trees and soil."},kw={"ambercorn","bloodhoof village","mulgore","rite of vision","tauren trial","water of the seers","zarlman two-moons"},rel={"quest:767","item:water-of-the-seers","item:well-stone","npc:zarlman-two-moons","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore"},src={"Ambercorn","Rite of Vision (2)"}}
E["item:battleboar-flank"] = {t="item",n="Battleboar Flank",z="mulgore",s="Battleboar Flanks are hearty cuts of meat harvested from the vicious battleboars trained by Bristleback quilboar in Mulgore. Collected by young hunters around Camp Narache, they serve both as proof of culled threats and as nourishing sustenance for the tauren tribe.",h="Battleboar Flanks are hearty cuts of meat harvested from the vicious battleboars trained by Bristleback...",sec={{t="Sustenance from the Bristleback Threat",b="The Bristleback quilboar lurking in Brambleblade Ravine have trained vicious battleboars to encroach on tauren hunting grounds. While these aggressive beasts pose a direct hazard to the young tauren coming of age at Camp Narache, nothing taken from nature is wasted. Grull Hawkwind directs novice hunters to harvest the flanks of these felled beasts alongside their snouts. The cuts of flank provide rich, sustaining meat that is cooked down into hearty stew for the tribe's youth, turning a quilboar weapon of war into vital tribal nourishment.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is a Battleboar Flank used for?",a="Battleboar Flanks are collected by young hunters at Camp Narache on the orders of Grull Hawkwind. The meat is prepared into stew to feed the young tauren of the village, ensuring that defeated foes also help sustain the tribe.",al={"battleboar flank use","what is battleboar flank","why collect battleboar meat"},it="item",gp=true},{q="Where are Battleboar Flanks found?",a="They are carved from the aggressive Battleboars and Bristleback Battleboars prowling near Brambleblade Ravine east of Camp Narache in Red Cloud Mesa.",al={"battleboar flank source","where do battleboars drop flanks","who drops battleboar flanks"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why are the battleboars encroaching on tauren land?",a="The battleboars are bred and trained by the Bristleback quilboar to be hostile and malicious. As tensions rise between the Bristlebacks and the tauren, the quilboar drive these trained beasts into traditional tribal hunting grounds to harass the newcomers.",al={"why are battleboars attacking","quilboar boars mulgore","brambleblade battleboars"},it="conflict"},{q="What is a Battleboar Flank?",a="It is a hearty cut of meat harvested from the battleboars that roam the red plains and ravines of Mulgore. Though these fierce swine are bred and trained for war rather than livestock, their meat is wholesome and substantial enough to sustain the tribes when game runs lean.",al={"what is battleboar flank","battleboar flank info","battleboar meat"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Where does Battleboar Flank come from?",a="These flanks are carved from battleboars and Bristleback battleboars prowling near Brambleblade Ravine. The creatures are raised and trained to be aggressive by the hostile Bristleback quilboar, turning what would normally be wild game into ferocious territorial beasts.",al={"battleboar flank origin","where do battleboar flanks come from","who has battleboar meat"},it="origin",add=true},{q="Why does Grull Hawkwind want Battleboar Flanks?",a="Grull Hawkwind gathers the flanks to feed the tribe's youth at Camp Narache. Cooking the meat into nourishing stew ensures that nothing taken from the hunt is wasted, all while punishing the Bristleback quilboar for driving their aggressive beasts into ancestral tauren hunting grounds.",al={"why collect battleboar flank","why does grull want flanks","who wants battleboar flanks"},it="why_wanted",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Hunting the encroaching battleboars teaches young tauren that the defense of their homeland goes hand in hand with the sacred duty to respect and make use of the Earth Mother's bounty.",["profession:Cooking"]="Though rough and gamey from beasts raised for combat, the flank yields plenty of rich, filling meat when boiled slowly into communal camp stews."},kw={"battleboar","battleboar flank","brambleblade ravine","bristleback","camp narache","grull hawkwind","mulgore","red cloud mesa"},rel={"quest:780","item:battleboar-snout","npc:grull-hawkwind","subzone:camp-narache","subzone:brambleblade-ravine","topic:quilboar","zone:mulgore"},src={"Battleboar Flank","The Battleboars"}}
E["item:battleboar-snout"] = {t="item",n="Battleboar Snout",z="mulgore",s="Battleboar Snouts are harvested from the aggressive boars trained by the Bristleback quilboar in Mulgore. Collected by young tauren hunters, they serve both as proof of thinning an invasive threat and as hearty ingredients for tribal cooking in Camp Narache.",h="Battleboar Snouts are harvested from the aggressive boars trained by the Bristleback quilboar in Mulgore.",sec={{t="Trophies and Provisions",b="In the shadow of Red Cloud Mesa, the Bristleback quilboar train malicious battleboars within Brambleblade Ravine to probe tauren hunting grounds. When young braves are sent out by Grull Hawkwind to push back against this encroachment, the snout serves as undeniable proof of a beast brought down. Rather than wasting any part of the kill, the tauren use these snouts alongside cuts of flank to prepare rich, sustaining stews to feed their young.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What are Battleboar Snouts used for?",a="Grull Hawkwind asks young hunters in Camp Narache to collect them along with meat flanks. They serve as proof that the threatening boars have been slain, and they are thrown into cooking pots to make stew for the tribe's youth.",al={"battleboar snout use","why keep snouts","what does grull do with snouts"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Where do these snouts come from?",a="They are carved from Battleboars and Bristleback Battleboars found near the fringes of Red Cloud Mesa and throughout Brambleblade Ravine to the east.",al={"where to find battleboars","who drops snouts","battleboar location"},it="origin"},{q="Why are the battleboars encroaching on tauren land?",a="The battleboars have been trained by the hostile Bristleback quilboar to be exceptionally vicious, pushing outward from Brambleblade Ravine into traditional tribal hunting grounds.",al={"why are boars attacking","quilboar boars","brambleblade battleboars"},it="conflict"},{q="What is a Battleboar Snout?",a="It is the severed snout of a battleboar raised and trained by the Bristleback quilboars near Brambleblade Ravine. Grull Hawkwind and the tauren of Camp Narache value these cuts alongside boars' flanks as hearty ingredients to boil down into stew for the tribe's youth, while simultaneously serving as physical proof that their hunters are culling the encroaching beasts.",al={"battleboar snout","what is battleboar snout","what is this item"},it="identity",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Taking the snout alongside the meat honors the Earthmother's balance, ensuring an encroaching predator is curbed while providing sustenance for the young in Camp Narache.",["profession:Cooking"]="Tough cuts like snouts require slow boiling over a fire, making them staple ingredients in tribal campfire stews."},kw={"battleboar","battleboar snout","brambleblade ravine","bristleback","camp narache","grull hawkwind","red cloud mesa"},rel={"quest:780","item:battleboar-flank","npc:grull-hawkwind","subzone:camp-narache","subzone:brambleblade-ravine","topic:quilboar","zone:mulgore"},src={"Battleboar Snout","The Battleboars"}}
E["item:bristleback-attack-plans"] = {t="item",n="Bristleback Attack Plans",z="mulgore",s="The Bristleback Attack Plans are crude maps and tactical notes drafted by the quilboar of Brambleblade Ravine. They outline a coordinated siege aimed directly at overrunning the tauren settlement of Camp Narache.",h="The Bristleback Attack Plans are crude maps and tactical notes drafted by the quilboar of Brambleblade Ravine.",sec={{t="A Plot Against the Mesa",b="Hidden deep within a dirt cave in Brambleblade Ravine, these scribbled tactical sketches reveal that the Bristleback quilboar are no longer just skirmishing on the borders of the Red Cloud Mesa. Instead, their leadership planned an organized, sustained assault to push into Camp Narache and wipe out the young tauren training there. Delivering this map to Chief Hawkwind allows the tribe to send word to Bloodhoof Village for reinforcements, blunting the impending invasion before it can begin.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What do the Bristleback Attack Plans contain?",a="The plans consist of a tactical map and crude instructions detailing a full-scale siege against Camp Narache, indicating the quilboar are organizing for a prolonged assault rather than sporadic raids.",al={"what are the attack plans","what is on the map","bristleback battle plans"},it="identity"},{q="Where are these plans found?",a="They lie abandoned on the dirt floor of an elevated cave in Brambleblade Ravine, just up a side path from the hut of the Bristleback chieftain.",al={"where are the plans","where do i find the map","bristleback attack plans location"},it="origin",gp=true},{q="What does Chief Hawkwind do once he receives the plans?",a="Chief Hawkwind recognizes the imminent danger to the mesa and uses the intelligence to summon aid and warriors from Bloodhoof Village to reinforce Camp Narache's defenses.",al={"what does hawkwind do","camp narache response","chief hawkwind reaction"},sp=1,it="next_step"},{q="Why are the Bristleback attacking Camp Narache?",a="The Bristleback tribe claims territorial dominance over the thorny ravines and aggressively views the tauren presence on the mesa as an intrusion, driving them to attempt to drive the settlement out entirely.",al={"why are quilboar attacking","bristleback motive"},it="motive"},{q="Where did the Bristleback Attack Plans come from?",a="The plans originated deep within Brambleblade Ravine, drafted by the warchief of the Bristleback quilboar. Placed upon a crude map hidden within their cave network, they detail the tribe's coordinated scheme to mount an extended siege against the tauren settlement of Camp Narache.",al={"who made bristleback attack plans","bristleback attack plans origin","who wrote bristleback attack plans"},it="origin",add=true},{q="Why does Chief Hawkwind need the Bristleback Attack Plans?",a="Chief Hawkwind needs these plans because they expose the quilboar's scheme to lay siege to Camp Narache. With proof of the looming threat in hand, the chief can immediately call on reinforcements from nearby Bloodhoof Village to prepare defenses and thwart the attack before the settlement is overrun.",al={"why want bristleback attack plans","why does hawkwind want attack plans","bristleback attack plans purpose"},it="why_wanted",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Uncovering this plot defends the very training grounds where young tauren take their first rites, shielding your elders and novices from a brutal quilboar siege.",["faction:Horde"]="Thwarting the Bristleback offensive ensures the Horde retains a secure, unthreatened foothold across the sacred plains of Mulgore."},kw={"attack plans","brambleblade ravine","bristleback","camp narache","chief hawkwind","mulgore","quilboar"},rel={"quest:781","subzone:camp-narache","npc:chief-hawkwind","subzone:brambleblade-ravine","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore"},src={"Bristleback Attack Plans","Attack on Camp Narache"}}
E["item:bristleback-belt"] = {t="item",n="Bristleback Belt",z="mulgore",s="A Bristleback Belt is a piece of crudely crafted gear worn by the quilboar infesting Brambleblade Ravine. For young tauren coming of age in Camp Narache, collecting these belts serves as physical proof of valor during the Rite of Strength.",h="A Bristleback Belt is a piece of crudely crafted gear worn by the quilboar infesting Brambleblade Ravine.",sec={{t="Proof of the Rite",b="The Red Cloud tribe tests every youth who aspires to earn respect in Thunder Bluff through the ancient Rites of the Earthmother. The first trial, the Rite of Strength, requires the initiate to protect the tribe from active threats. Because the hostile Bristleback quilboar repeatedly raid tribal food supplies and ambush hunting parties outside Camp Narache, Chief Hawkwind demands the belts of these intruders. Returning twelve belts to the chieftain proves that an initiate can confront danger directly and strike a blow for the tribe's defense.",sp=0},{t="Quilboar Craft",b="Stripped from Bristleback quilboar warriors and shamans throughout Brambleblade Ravine, these belts are fashioned from rough hide, twine, and scrap. While worthless as apparel to a tauren warrior, they are distinctly quilboar make and serve as unmistakable evidence of a slain enemy. Bringing them back to the camp circle fulfills tradition without wasting the natural bounty of the land on meaningless trophies.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What are Bristleback Belts used for?",a="Chief Hawkwind in Camp Narache requires twelve Bristleback Belts as physical proof that an initiate has completed the Rite of Strength, the first of the traditional Rites of the Earthmother.",al={"why do i need bristleback belt","bristleback belt purpose","what is this belt for"},it="purpose",gp=true},{q="Where do Bristleback Belts come from?",a="They are taken from Bristleback quilboar and Bristleback shamans dwelling inside Brambleblade Ravine, just east of Camp Narache in the Red Cloud Mesa.",al={"where to get bristleback belt","who drops bristleback belts","bristleback belt location"},it="origin",gp=true},{q="Why does Chief Hawkwind want belts instead of heads or weapons?",a="Belts are unmistakable personal effects of the quilboar that can be easily collected and carried without dishonoring the Earthmother's principles against taking wasteful, gruesome trophies.",al={"why belts","hawkwind proof"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Why are the Bristlebacks being targeted?",a="The Bristlebacks have encroached onto tribal land from Brambleblade Ravine, raiding Camp Narache by cover of night and ambushing tauren hunting parties across the mesa.",al={"why kill bristlebacks","quilboar threat mulgore"},it="conflict"},{q="What is a Bristleback Belt?",a="A Bristleback Belt is a simple piece of raiment worn by the quilboar dwelling in Brambleblade Ravine. Stripped from fallen raiders, these rough leather or corded girdles serve as undeniable trophies of battle, proving to tribal elders that an encroaching threat has been confronted and slain.",al={"bristleback belt","what is bristleback belt","bristleback belt item"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Where does a Bristleback Belt come from, and who makes it?",a="These belts are fashioned by the Bristleback quilboar who have established a thorn-choked settlement within Brambleblade Ravine, just east of Camp Narache. Both their frontline warriors and shamans wear them into skirmishes against the tauren across the Red Cloud Mesa.",al={"where do bristleback belts come from","who makes bristleback belts","bristleback belt origin"},it="origin",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Taking the belt of a fallen Bristleback is a rite of passage for every youth on the Red Cloud Mesa, marking the first step toward recognition by the elders in Thunder Bluff.",["faction:Horde"]="Curbing the aggressive quilboar of Mulgore secures the southern borders of the Horde's heartland in central Kalimdor."},kw={"belt","brambleblade ravine","bristleback","camp narache","chief hawkwind","quilboar","red cloud mesa","rite of strength"},rel={"quest:757","npc:chief-hawkwind","subzone:camp-narache","subzone:brambleblade-ravine","quest:763","topic:tauren","topic:quilboar","zone:mulgore"},src={"Bristleback Belt","Rite of Strength (Classic)"}}
E["item:broken-tools"] = {t="item",n="Broken Tools",z="mulgore",s="Broken Tools are the shattered remnants of dwarven prospector picks, intentionally destroyed at an anvil. They serve as physical proof to Baine Bloodhoof that the invasive dwarven excavation threatening Mulgore's land and wildlife has been disrupted.",h="Broken Tools are the shattered remnants of dwarven prospector picks, intentionally destroyed at an anvil.",sec={{t="Sabotaging the Dig",b="To the northwest of Bloodhoof Village, dwarven excavators have set up operations to delve into the hills of Mulgore. While the dwarves seek buried ancient history, the tauren view their aggressive excavation as a direct defilement of the Earth Mother. The noise and destruction agitate the local wildlife, particularly the roaming kodo herds. Rather than merely driving the excavators away, young Baine Bloodhoof instructs defenders to confiscate the prospectors' iron picks and shatter them upon a forge, rendering the expedition unable to gouge further into the earth.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What are these Broken Tools?",a="They are the ruined heads and splintered shafts of Prospector's Picks, taken from the dwarven excavators in Mulgore and deliberately crushed at a forge.",al={"broken tools","what are broken tools","shattered picks"},it="identity"},{q="Why does Baine Bloodhoof want them destroyed?",a="Baine believes that tearing up the soil defiles the land and enrages Mulgore's wildlife, especially the kodos. Smashing their tools halts the digging and sends a clear message that the land will not yield its wisdom to reckless excavation.",al={"why destroy picks","why baine want broken tools","baine dwarven digging"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Where can these picks be broken?",a="Any functional blacksmithing forge will do. You can use the forge located directly at the dwarven dig site, or visit one in Bloodhoof Village or up in Thunder Bluff.",al={"how to break picks","where is forge","break prospector pick"},gp=true},{q="Why are dwarves digging in Mulgore?",a="The dwarves believe deep secrets and relics of antiquity lie buried under Mulgore's soil. In their obsessive search for their origins, they frequently hollow out foreign lands without regard for the native inhabitants.",al={"why dwarves in mulgore","dwarf digsite mulgore"},it="motive"},{q="Where do these broken tools come from?",a="These mangled implements were originally sturdy mining picks forged and carried by dwarven prospectors. Seeking titan relics and ancient secrets beneath Mulgore, the expedition brought their iron tools to tear into the earth to the northwest. By hauling those intact picks to a forge and smashing them to pieces, you reduce their mining gear to unusable scrap before returning it to the tauren as proof the excavation has been halted.",al={"broken tools origin","who made broken tools","where did broken tools come from"},it="origin",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For the tauren, bringing ruined tools to Baine represents the defense of the Earth Mother against invasive gouging that torments the native beasts.",["profession:Blacksmithing"]="Using an anvil to systematically destroy fine dwarven ironwork turns the crafter's forge into an instrument of territorial defense."},kw={"baine bloodhoof","broken tools","dig site","dwarven digging","forge","mulgore","prospector's pick"},rel={"quest:746","npc:baine-bloodhoof","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff"},src={"Broken Tools","Dwarven Digging"}}
E["item:chief-sharptusk-thornmantle-s-head"] = {t="item",n="Chief Sharptusk Thornmantle's Head",z="mulgore",s="Chief Sharptusk Thornmantle's Head is taken as grim proof of victory over the leader of the Bristleback quilboar threatening Red Cloud Mesa. Recovered from the depths of Brambleblade Ravine, it is presented to Brave Windfeather to mark the end of a persistent menace against young tauren braves.",h="Chief Sharptusk Thornmantle's Head is taken as grim proof of victory over the leader of the Bristleback...",sec={{t="Trophy of the Hunt",b="The tauren carve their peaceful homeland out of Mulgore through vigilance and strength. The Bristleback quilboar entrenched within Brambleblade Ravine have waged an aggressive campaign against the tribes of Camp Narache, urged on by their ferocious chieftain, Sharptusk Thornmantle. Severing his head serves not out of senseless cruelty, but as definitive proof of his demise and a stark warning to the surviving quilboar clans encroaching upon mesa borders.",sp=0},{t="A Lesson in the Hunt",b="Brave Windfeather demands the chieftain's head as a rite of passage for fledgling hunters and defenders of the tribe. To the tauren, facing an adversary cunning enough to retaliate and organize an entire war band provides an essential test of martial prowess. Presenting Sharptusk's head fulfills that trial, earning the young combatant an established reputation among the braves of Red Cloud Mesa.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why does Brave Windfeather want the head of Sharptusk Thornmantle?",a="Brave Windfeather requires undisputed proof that the Bristleback quilboar chieftain has fallen. Bringing back his head demonstrates that Sharptusk will no longer direct raids against Camp Narache and signals to the remaining quilboar that their aggression will be met with decisive force.",al={"why take his head","reason for sharptusk head","windfeather head request"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Who was Chief Sharptusk Thornmantle?",a="Chief Sharptusk Thornmantle was the leader of the Bristleback quilboar inhabiting Brambleblade Ravine on the eastern edge of Red Cloud Mesa. Under his command, the Bristlebacks continuously harassed and attacked young tauren braves.",al={"who is sharptusk","sharptusk thornmantle lore","quilboar chief mulgore"},it="identity"},{q="Where is this trophy obtained?",a="The head is obtained by slaying Chief Sharptusk Thornmantle in his makeshift tribal village, located deep inside Brambleblade Ravine to the east of Camp Narache.",al={"where do i find the head","sharptusk head location"},it="origin",gp=true},{q="Where does Chief Sharptusk Thornmantle's Head come from?",a="This grim trophy is taken directly from the chieftain of the Bristleback quilboar who nest within Brambleblade Ravine, just east of Camp Narache. Sharptusk Thornmantle rallied his makeshift settlement against the tauren of Red Cloud Mesa, prompting the Braves to demand his severed head as undeniable proof that the Bristleback war effort has been broken.",al={"sharptusk head origin","where did sharptusk head come from","who has sharptusk head"},it="origin",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Claiming the head of a formidable adversary marks an important milestone for young tauren, proving they possess the resolve and skill to protect the newly established home of their people.",["class:Hunter"]="For hunters, Sharptusk represents the ultimate prey: an intelligent enemy capable of hunting back, turning a survival skirmish into a true test of tracking and marksmanship."},kw={"brambleblade","bristleback","head","mulgore","sharptusk","thornmantle","windfeather"},rel={"quest:3376","subzone:brambleblade-ravine","topic:quilboar","topic:tauren","zone:mulgore"},src={"Chief Sharptusk Thornmantle's Head","Break Sharptusk!"}}
E["item:cougar-claws"] = {t="item",n="Cougar Claws",z="mulgore",s="Cougar Claws are harvested from flatland cougars that roam the central plains of Mulgore. They serve as a vital material in tauren ritual craft, embodying the untamed balance and natural ferocity of the wild predators.",h="Cougar Claws are harvested from flatland cougars that roam the central plains of Mulgore.",sec={{t="A Vessel of Natural Balance",b="The flatland cougar is one of the apex hunters of Mulgore's sprawling grasslands, stalking prey with instinctual grace. In tauren tradition, taking the claws of such a predator is not an act of casual slaughter or trophy hunting, but a way to harness the natural spirit of the beast. When goblin operations begin defiling the ancestral waters of the plains, Mull Thunderhorn calls upon young hunters to gather these claws alongside those of prairie stalkers. The tauren believe that the pure, uncorrupted predatory instincts of native beasts can be woven into a ritual totem capable of scouring unnatural toxins and foul sorcery from the poisoned Thunderhorn Water Well.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What are Cougar Claws used for?",a="Mull Thunderhorn uses them along with stalker claws to craft the Thunderhorn Cleansing Totem. The tauren weave the pure spirit of native predators into ritual objects to purify water wells poisoned by goblin industrial ventures.",al={"cougar claws use","why get cougar claws","cougar claws purpose"},it="purpose"},{q="Where do Cougar Claws come from?",a="They are taken from flatland cougars found prowling the rolling plains of Mulgore, primarily east and west of Bloodhoof Village.",al={"where to get cougar claws","cougar claw source","who drops cougar claws"},it="origin"},{q="Why does Mull Thunderhorn need predator claws to cleanse a well?",a="Tauren shamanic and druidic traditions revere the purity with which wild beasts hunt. Unlike goblins who exploit and defile the earth, native predators live in complete balance with the land, making their essence ideal for counteracting artificial corruption.",al={"why predator claws","mull claws reason","totem predator claws"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Are Cougar Claws useful for anything else?",a="Outside of crafting the ritual totem for the Thunderhorn Water Well, they have no specialized use and are typically sold to local traders.",al={"other uses cougar claws","sell cougar claws"},gp=true},{q="What are Cougar Claws?",a="These are the natural talons taken from the paws of flatland cougars stalking the plains of Mulgore. Rather than mere trophies, these claws are viewed by tauren shamans as emblems of the natural hunt, embodying the pure and untainted balance of the beasts that prowl the wilderness.",al={"what is cougar claws","cougar claws item lore","what are these claws"},it="identity",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Gathering these claws teaches young tauren that hunting is rooted in balance and spiritual necessity, standing in stark contrast to the reckless pollution brought by goblin enterprises.",["class:Shaman"]="Shaman recognize that the essence of local predators holds an unbroken tie to the spirits of the plains, making them essential conduits for purifying wards and cleansing totems."},kw={"cleansing totem","cougar claws","flatland cougar","mulgore","mull thunderhorn","thunderhorn totem"},rel={"quest:756","item:thunderhorn-cleansing-totem","npc:mull-thunderhorn","item:stalker-claws","subzone:thunderhorn-water-well","quest:758","zone:mulgore","topic:goblin"},src={"Cougar Claws","Thunderhorn Totem (Classic)"}}
E["item:etched-note"] = {t="item",n="Etched Note",z="mulgore",s="The Etched Note is a formal summons delivered to promising young tauren hunters resting at Camp Narache. Penned by the camp's hunter trainer, Lanka Farshot, the message welcomes the novice into the traditions of the hunt and calls them forward for their initial instruction.",h="The Etched Note is a formal summons delivered to promising young tauren hunters resting at Camp Narache.",sec={{t="A Call to the Hunt",b="Within the safe confines of Camp Narache, young shu'halo are recognized early for their natural inclinations and family lineages. The Etched Note is delivered by a runner on behalf of Lanka Farshot, the resident hunter mentor. It names the hunter as the heart of the tribe while serving as a stern reminder that raw instinct must be tempered through rigorous instruction and a deep reverence for the wilderness of Mulgore.",sp=0},{t="Traditions of the Shu'halo",b="For the tauren, hunting is far more than a practical method of survival—it is an honored, sacred discipline woven into tribal society. The note bids the reader to study every stretch of the Red Cloud Mesa and surrounding plains intimately. By presenting the letter to Lanka, the student takes their first official steps into learning weaponcraft, understanding beasts, and fulfilling their duty to defend and sustain the tribes.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of the Etched Note?",a="It serves as an introductory summons for novice tauren hunters. Reading the note directs the bearer to present themselves to Lanka Farshot at Camp Narache to begin their formal training in marksmanship and beast lore.",al={"what does the note do","etched note use","why did i get etched note"},it="purpose"},{q="Who wrote this note?",a="The message was written by Lanka Farshot, the hunter trainer situated in Camp Narache. He dispatches couriers across the mesa to locate young hunters who are ready to learn.",al={"who sent etched note","who wrote it","etched note author"},it="origin"},{q="Why is hunting so important to the tauren?",a="Hunting is a cornerstone of tauren survival, spirituality, and tribal tradition. Skilled hunters provide food and materials for their people while living in direct balance with the land and honoring the spirits of the beasts they fell.",al={"tauren hunter lore","why do tauren hunt","hunting in tauren culture"}},{q="Where should this note be brought?",a="Take the note directly to Lanka Farshot, who stands near the pavilion and training grounds within Camp Narache on the Red Cloud Mesa.",al={"where do i take the note","who do i turn this in to","etched note turn in"},it="next_step",gp=true},{q="What is the Etched Note?",a="The Etched Note is a written summons directed specifically to novice tauren hunters arriving at Camp Narache. Carved with a brief message from the camp's hunter trainer, Lanka Farshot, it reminds you that hunters are the heart of the tribe and bids you to seek him out in Camp Narache to begin mastering the art of the hunt.",al={"what is etched note","etched note item","etched note mulgore"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Why does Lanka Farshot want you to bring him the Etched Note?",a="Lanka Farshot has the note sent to bring promising young hunters directly under his wing. He intends to test your readiness and ground you in the vital traditions of the hunt—from handling ranged weaponry to taming beasts—ensuring you can serve and protect the tribe across the plains of Mulgore.",al={"why deliver etched note","why does lanka want etched note","etched note purpose"},it="why_wanted",add=true}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="The note marks your formal induction into the hunter tradition, promising lessons in handling ranged weapons and communing with the beasts of the plains.",["race:Tauren"]="Carrying the expectations of your elders and bloodline, receiving this note calls you to master the rolling grasslands of Mulgore before traveling to distant lands."},kw={"camp narache","etched note","lanka farshot","mulgore","red cloud mesa","tauren hunter","trainer summons"},rel={"quest:3092","npc:lanka-farshot","subzone:camp-narache","topic:hunter","zone:mulgore"},src={"Etched Note","Etched Note (quest)"}}
E["item:flatland-prowler-claw"] = {t="item",n="Flatland Prowler Claw",z="mulgore",s="A Flatland Prowler Claw is taken from the dangerous feline predators that stalk the grassy plains of Mulgore. For aspiring hunters, gathering these claws serves as a traditional rite of passage, demonstrating cunning and martial resolve to the elders of Thunder Bluff.",h="A Flatland Prowler Claw is taken from the dangerous feline predators that stalk the grassy plains of Mulgore.",sec={{t="A Hunter's Proof",b="Flatland prowlers are among the most cunning and territorial predators roaming the wind-swept plains of Mulgore. Quick on their feet and adept at ambushing prey in the tall grass, they present a genuine threat to travelers and a worthy test for young hunters. Collecting their sharp claws is not merely a task of survival, but a recognized tradition. Skorn Whitecloud advises promising wanderers to harvest these trophies to present to Melor Stonehoof atop Thunder Bluff's Hunter Rise, proving they possess the resolve and fieldcraft necessary to pursue the hunter's way.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What are Flatland Prowler Claws used for?",a="They serve as a physical demonstration of skill and courage. Young hunters bring four of these claws to Melor Stonehoof on the Hunter Rise in Thunder Bluff to prove their readiness to walk the hunter's path.",al={"prowler claw use","why get prowler claws","hunter's way claws"},it="item",gp=true},{q="Who asks you to collect them?",a="The elder hunter Skorn Whitecloud directs promising trackers to gather claws from the prowlers of Mulgore as an offering of proof before presenting themselves to Melor Stonehoof.",al={"who wants prowler claws","quest giver prowler claws"},gp=true},{q="Why are flatland prowlers considered a fitting hunt?",a="Flatland prowlers are tough, stealthy, and cunning carnivores native to the Mulgore plains. Successfully tracking and besting such alert predators proves that an aspiring hunter has the patience and combat instincts required of tauren tradition.",al={"why flatland prowlers","are prowlers dangerous"}},{q="What is a Flatland Prowler Claw?",a="It is a claw harvested from a flatland prowler, one of the predatory wild cats roaming the grassy plains of Mulgore. Among tauren hunters, presenting these claws serves as tangible proof of a young hunter's cunning, patience, and emerging tracking prowess.",al={"flatland prowler claw","what is prowler claw"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Where do Flatland Prowler Claws come from?",a="These claws come directly from the predatory flatland prowlers that stalk the windswept grasslands of Mulgore. Rather than being crafted or manufactured, they must be cleanly harvested by a hunter after tracking and defeating the agile beasts in the wild.",al={"where to get prowler claws","how are prowler claws obtained"},it="origin",add=true},{q="Why does anyone want Flatland Prowler Claws?",a="Skorn Whitecloud directs aspiring wanderers to gather these claws to prove their mettle to Melor Stonehoof atop the Hunter's Rise in Thunder Bluff. Hunting is one of the highest honors in tauren culture, and offering the claws of such tough, cunning predators demonstrates that you possess the burgeoning skill and spirit required to walk the hunter's path.",al={"why collect prowler claws","who needs prowler claws","why does melor stonehoof want claws"},it="why_wanted",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For the tauren, the hunt is an honorable spiritual tradition, and presenting the claws of a cunning plains predator marks an initiate's first steps toward serving the tribe as a tracker.",["class:Hunter"]="Taking down stealthy prowlers tests a hunter's field awareness, making these claws a symbol of earned respect among the marksmen gathered on Thunder Bluff's Hunter Rise."},kw={"flatland prowler","flatland prowler claw","hunter rise","melor stonehoof","mulgore","skorn whitecloud","the hunter's way","thunder bluff"},rel={"quest:861","npc:melor-stonehoof","npc:skorn-whitecloud","zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff","topic:tauren","topic:hunter"},src={"Flatland Prowler Claw","The Hunter's Way (Classic)"}}
E["item:plainstrider-feather"] = {t="item",n="Plainstrider Feather",z="mulgore",s="Plucked from the swift birds roaming the plains of Mulgore, Plainstrider Feathers are essential materials gathered by young hunters on Red Cloud Mesa. The tauren of Camp Narache use them primarily for crafting clothing and tribal adornments, while honoring the creatures that provide for the village.",h="Plucked from the swift birds roaming the plains of Mulgore, Plainstrider Feathers are essential materials...",sec={{t="A Hunter's First Offering",b="On Red Cloud Mesa, young tauren learn early that survival relies on keen tracking and hunting. Plainstriders run wild across the grassy plateau, offering both sustenance and practical materials. Plucking their sturdy feathers allows the camp to craft traditional garments and gear. Presenting these feathers alongside fresh meat to Grull Hawkwind demonstrates respect for the tribe's needs and serves as the very first test toward being recognized by Chief Hawkwind and welcomed in Thunder Bluff.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What are Plainstrider Feathers used for?",a="The tauren of Camp Narache use them primarily for making clothing and ceremonial items. Grull Hawkwind collects them from novice hunters to restock tribal supplies.",al={"feather purpose","plainstrider feather use","why collect feathers"},it="item"},{q="Who gathers Plainstrider Feathers on Red Cloud Mesa?",a="Grull Hawkwind requests them on behalf of Camp Narache to replenish village stores and to test whether young hunters possess the discipline needed to provide for the tribe.",al={"who wants feathers","who needs feathers","grull hawkwind feathers"},it="people"},{q="Where can plainstriders be found?",a="They roam the open grasslands across Red Cloud Mesa and throughout the rolling plains of Mulgore.",al={"where to find plainstriders","plainstrider location","feather farm"},it="origin",gp=true},{q="Why does Grull Hawkwind require these feathers for tribal rites?",a="Hunting is a cornerstone of tauren culture, undertaken both for survival and sport. Successfully returning with meat and plumage proves to Chief Hawkwind that an initiate can sustain the tribe before venturing onward to Thunder Bluff.",al={"the hunt begins lore","proving hunt tauren","why hunt plainstriders"},it="why_wanted"},{q="What is a Plainstrider Feather?",a="A Plainstrider Feather is plumage gathered from the large, flightless birds that wander the grasslands of Mulgore. The tauren of Red Cloud Mesa utilize every harvest from the land with deep respect, and these sturdy, colorful plumes serve as practical materials for crafting everyday garments as well as traditional adornments.",al={"what is plainstrider feather","plainstrider feather info","plainstrider feathers"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Where do Plainstrider Feathers come from?",a="These feathers are naturally grown plumage harvested directly from the wild plainstriders roaming Red Cloud Mesa and the broader plains of Mulgore. They are not crafted by mortal hands, but instead gathered by hunters after a successful hunt upon the open prairie.",al={"plainstrider feather source","where to get plainstrider feathers","who made plainstrider feather"},it="origin",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For a young tauren, harvesting feathers from a plainstrider is an essential first lesson in taking nothing from the Earth Mother's creatures for granted, utilizing every part of the kill for food and clothing.",["class:Hunter"]="Stalking swift plainstriders across Red Cloud Mesa provides an initiate's foundational trial in patience, tracking, and honoring the quarry."},kw={"camp narache","feather","grull hawkwind","mulgore","plainstrider","red cloud mesa","the hunt begins"},rel={"quest:747","item:plainstrider-meat","npc:grull-hawkwind","subzone:camp-narache","zone:mulgore","npc:chief-hawkwind","topic:tauren","zone:thunderbluff"},src={"Plainstrider Feather","The Hunt Begins"}}
E["item:plainstrider-meat"] = {t="item",n="Plainstrider Meat",z="mulgore",s="Plainstrider Meat is freshly harvested game taken from the flightless birds that wander the plains of Mulgore. For the young tauren coming of age on Red Cloud Mesa, gathering this sustenance serves as both vital winter provisions and a core rite of passage.",h="Plainstrider Meat is freshly harvested game taken from the flightless birds that wander the plains of Mulgore.",sec={{t="Sustenance for the Mesa",b="Tauren life is inextricably bound to the land, balancing respect for nature with the practical necessity of survival. On Red Cloud Mesa, the settlement of Camp Narache depends heavily on game hunted from the surrounding grasslands. Plainstrider meat provides essential food reserves for the village when stocks run lean, while the birds' plumage offers material for clothing and ceremonial adornments.",sp=0},{t="A Young Hunter's Trial",b="Hunting plainstriders is one of the initial tests given to youth seeking to earn the tribe's respect. Grull Hawkwind directs hopeful hunters to stalk the swift birds across the mesa, gathering meat and feathers to prove their tracking skills and self-reliance before appearing before his father, Chief Hawkwind.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Plainstrider Meat used for?",a="Plainstrider Meat is gathered by young hunters on Red Cloud Mesa to resupply Camp Narache's depleted food stores, fulfilling an essential task for Grull Hawkwind.",al={"why plainstrider meat","plainstrider meat quest","what do i do with plainstrider meat"},it="item",gp=true},{q="Who asks for this meat?",a="Grull Hawkwind, son of Chief Hawkwind, asks initiates to hunt the plainstriders of the mesa to replenish the tribe's food supplies and prove their worth as providers.",al={"who wants plainstrider meat","grull hawkwind meat"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Where can Plainstrider Meat be found?",a="It is harvested from the flightless plainstriders wandering the grasslands of Mulgore, notably across the rolling hills of Red Cloud Mesa.",al={"where to get plainstrider meat","plainstrider locations","farm plainstrider meat"},it="origin",gp=true},{q="Why is hunting plainstriders considered important to the tauren?",a="The tauren hunt both for practical necessity and as a spiritual discipline. Bringing game back to the camp demonstrates respect for the Earth Mother, self-reliance, and the ability to sustain the tribe.",al={"tauren hunting culture","why hunt plainstriders"},it="motive"},{q="What is Plainstrider Meat?",a="Plainstrider Meat is fresh game harvested from the flightless birds wandering the plains of Mulgore. For the tauren of Red Cloud Mesa, it serves as a vital staple food, gathered through traditional hunts that sustain the settlement and honor the bond between hunter and prey.",al={"plainstrider meat info","what is plainstrider meat","plainstrider meat lore"},it="identity",add=true,nx={6}},{q="Where does Plainstrider Meat come from?",a="Plainstrider Meat comes from the wild plainstriders native to Mulgore. Young hunters harvest it directly from the beasts roaming Red Cloud Mesa during their first hunting rites for the tribe.",al={"where does plainstrider meat come from","plainstrider meat origin","how to get plainstrider meat"},it="origin",add=true},{q="Why is Plainstrider Meat wanted in Camp Narache?",a="Camp Narache's stores of food have run low, and the tribe relies on keen hunting to replenish its supplies. Grull Hawkwind asks young hunters to collect Plainstrider Meat to feed the village and prove their skill to Chief Hawkwind.",al={"why need plainstrider meat","plainstrider meat purpose","why collect plainstrider meat"},it="why_wanted",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For tauren youths stepping out onto Red Cloud Mesa, hunting plainstriders is the first practical step toward winning the respect of Chief Hawkwind and earning the right to walk into Thunder Bluff.",["class:Hunter"]="The swift plainstrider offers fledgling hunters an early test of their tracking and marksmanship across the open plains of Mulgore."},kw={"camp narache","chief hawkwind","grull hawkwind","mulgore","plainstrider","plainstrider meat","red cloud mesa","the hunt begins"},rel={"quest:747","item:plainstrider-feather","npc:grull-hawkwind","subzone:camp-narache","npc:chief-hawkwind","topic:tauren","zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff"},src={"Plainstrider Meat","The Hunt Begins"}}
E["item:plainstrider-talon"] = {t="item",n="Plainstrider Talon",z="mulgore",s="A Plainstrider Talon is a sharp claw harvested from the large, flightless plainstriders native to Mulgore. Tauren shamans use these talons alongside other natural reagents to craft sacred cleansing totems when the land's water sources are corrupted.",h="A Plainstrider Talon is a sharp claw harvested from the large, flightless plainstriders native to Mulgore.",sec={{t="Bonds of the Plains",b="The large, flightless plainstriders roam the open grasses of Mulgore, relying on powerful legs and sharp claws to defend against predators and forage through the tough prairie earth. To the tauren of Bloodhoof Village, every part of a hunted beast carries spiritual resonance. When goblin ventures tainted sacred sources like the Winterhoof Water Well, shaman Mull Thunderhorn sought plainstrider talons alongside prairie wolf paws. In tauren ritual crafting, gathering reagents from native predators and foragers allows the spiritual essence of the land to be channeled into totems capable of purging unnatural poisons.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What creatures drop a Plainstrider Talon?",a="Plainstrider talons are harvested from adult plainstriders, elder plainstriders, and the notorious stride Mazzranache across the grasslands of Mulgore.",al={"who drops plainstrider talon","where to get plainstrider talons","plainstrider talon source"},it="origin",gp=true},{q="What are Plainstrider Talons used for?",a="Mull Thunderhorn in Bloodhoof Village requires plainstrider talons and prairie wolf paws to craft a cleansing totem, which is used to purify the corrupted Winterhoof Water Well.",al={"plainstrider talon use","why do i need talons","plainstrider talon quest"},it="why_wanted",gp=true},{q="Why does Mull Thunderhorn need animal parts to clean the water?",a="Tauren shamanism relies on harmony with the natural spirits of Mulgore. By binding elements of local wildlife into the ritual totem, the shaman calls upon the raw power of the land itself to fight off foreign pollutants introduced by Venture Company goblins.",al={"why animal paws and claws","shaman cleansing ritual talons"},it="motive"},{q="Who poisoned the water wells in Mulgore?",a="Goblins—specifically agents of the Venture Company operating in Mulgore—and their lackeys have tainted the sacred tribal water wells, disrupting the local environment and threatening the tauren tribes.",al={"who tainted the wells","who poisoned mulgore water","poison water culprit"},it="threat"},{q="What is a Plainstrider Talon?",a="A Plainstrider Talon is the sharp claw harvested from the large, flightless plainstriders roaming Mulgore. While naturally used by these swift birds for defense and foraging across the plains, the talons also carry an innate connection to the natural world. To the tauren, such materials represent the land's strength and can be channeled through shamanic ritual.",al={"what is plainstrider talon","plainstrider talons lore","plainstrider claws"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Where do Plainstrider Talons come from?",a="These talons are taken from the mature plainstriders that roam the open plains of Mulgore, southwest of Bloodhoof Village. Hunters gather them from adult and elder plainstriders, or even rare beasts like Mazzranache, as offerings of the land's bounty.",al={"plainstrider talon source","where to get plainstrider talons","plainstrider talon origin"},it="origin",add=true},{q="Why does Mull Thunderhorn want Plainstrider Talons?",a="Mull Thunderhorn in Bloodhoof Village requires these talons to craft a cleansing totem for the Winterhoof Water Well. Goblins and their Venture Company lackeys have tainted the sacred water, and Mull must call upon the primal essence of Mulgore's native beasts to counteract the poison and restore purity to the well.",al={"why does mull want talons","why need plainstrider talon","plainstrider talon purpose"},it="why_wanted",add=true}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="Using beasts' physical parts like talons to fashion purifying totems exemplifies traditional tauren shamanic practices, drawing power directly from the native spirits of the land.",["race:Tauren"]="Honoring the sacrifice of Mulgore's wildlife is central to the tauren hunt, ensuring that every beast taken helps heal the Earth Mother when goblin exploitation strikes."},kw={"bloodhoof village","cleansing totem","mulgore","mull thunderhorn","plainstrider","plainstrider talon","poison water","winterhoof water well"},rel={"quest:748","npc:mull-thunderhorn","item:prairie-wolf-paw","subzone:winterhoof-water-well","quest:766","zone:mulgore","topic:goblin"},src={"Plainstrider Talon","Poison Water (Classic)"}}
E["item:prairie-alpha-tooth"] = {t="item",n="Prairie Alpha Tooth",z="mulgore",s="A Prairie Alpha Tooth is taken from the fierce pack leaders roaming the plains around Thunder Bluff. In tauren tradition, these teeth serve as a potent offering to cleanse and honor the land's precious water sources.",h="A Prairie Alpha Tooth is taken from the fierce pack leaders roaming the plains around Thunder Bluff.",sec={{t="A Cleansing Offering",b="The water wells of Mulgore are vital lifelines for the tauren, and maintaining their spiritual purity demands rites of respect and sacrifice. To cleanse the Wildmane Well, Mull Thunderhorn of Bloodhoof Village requires teeth gathered from the prairie wolf alphas that stalk the shadow of Thunder Bluff. As apex predators of the rolling grassy plains, these alphas represent the raw strength of the wild. Offering their teeth honors the balance between hunter, prey, and the spirits of the earth.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is a Prairie Alpha Tooth used for?",a="Mull Thunderhorn uses eight of these teeth in a ritual to cleanse the Wildmane Well, the final contaminated water well near Bloodhoof Village.",al={"alpha tooth purpose","what are wolf teeth for mulgore","wildmane totem item"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Where do Prairie Wolf Alphas roam?",a="Prairie wolf alphas prowl the open grassy plains of central and northern Mulgore, particularly circling the bluffs around the base of Thunder Bluff.",al={"where are prairie wolf alphas","alpha wolf locations","prairie wolf alpha spots"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why does a water cleansing ritual require wolf teeth?",a="Tauren rites honor the balance of nature. Cleansing the wells requires sacred components taken directly from the land's fierce native beasts, symbolizing both the sacrifice and the vitality of Mulgore's wildlife.",al={"why wolf teeth for water","mull thunderhorn tooth ritual lore"},it="purpose"},{q="What is a Prairie Alpha Tooth?",a="A Prairie Alpha Tooth is a tooth taken from a prairie wolf alpha, one of the fiercest pack leaders prowling the plains of Mulgore. Among the tauren, these fangs represent the strength and spirit of the land's apex predators, carrying spiritual weight when offered in ritual.",al={"prairie alpha tooth","what is prairie alpha tooth","prairie wolf alpha tooth"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Where does a Prairie Alpha Tooth come from?",a="These teeth are harvested from prairie wolf alphas that roam the grasslands of Mulgore, particularly near the base of Thunder Bluff to the north. Being natural fangs of living predators, they are taken directly from the beasts during the hunt rather than crafted by mortal hands.",al={"where do prairie alpha teeth come from","prairie alpha tooth origin","who drops prairie alpha tooth"},it="origin",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Taking the fang of a prairie alpha represents the hunter's sacred duty to harvest what is needed with respect, returning that primal essence to heal the poisoned earth.",["class:Shaman"]="The spirits of the water wells must be appeased through earthly balance, and offering the fangs of an apex predator anchors that natural harmony."},kw={"bloodhoof village","mulgore","mull thunderhorn","prairie alpha tooth","prairie wolf alpha","water well","wildmane totem"},rel={"quest:759","npc:mull-thunderhorn","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:thunderbluff","zone:mulgore"},src={"Prairie Alpha Tooth","Wildmane Totem (Classic)"}}
E["item:prairie-wolf-paw"] = {t="item",n="Prairie Wolf Paw",z="mulgore",s="Prairie Wolf Paws are harvested from the predators roaming the plains of Mulgore. Shamanic practitioners, such as Mull Thunderhorn in Bloodhoof Village, use them as organic components to bind the natural power of the land into cleansing totems.",h="Prairie Wolf Paws are harvested from the predators roaming the plains of Mulgore.",sec={{t="Natural Reagents of the Plains",b="Prairie wolves stalk the rolling grasses across Mulgore, hunting in packs alongside prairie stalkers and their pack alphas. While dangerous to young hunters and local livestock, the creatures are deeply intertwined with the spiritual balance of the plains. Tauren shamans view the wolf's paws not merely as trophies, but as vital conduits of the earth's innate vitality.",sp=0},{t="Cleansing the Wells",b="When goblin ventures tainted Mulgore's sacred water reserves, shaman Mull Thunderhorn required the physical aid of the land itself to counteract the pollution. Bound together with plainstrider talons, the wolf paws provide the raw spiritual resonance necessary to fashion cleansing totems, beginning the vital work of purifying the Winterhoof Water Well.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What creatures drop a Prairie Wolf Paw?",a="They are gathered from prairie wolves, prairie stalkers, and prairie wolf alphas found hunting across the plains of Mulgore, primarily southwest of Bloodhoof Village.",al={"who drops prairie wolf paw","where to get prairie wolf paws","prairie wolf paw source"},gp=true},{q="Who wants Prairie Wolf Paws?",a="Mull Thunderhorn, a tauren shaman stationed in Bloodhoof Village, requests them to help craft a cleansing totem to restore the tainted water wells.",al={"who needs prairie wolf paws","mull thunderhorn wolf paws","prairie wolf paw turn in"},gp=true},{q="Why are wolf paws needed to cleanse water?",a="Tauren shamanism relies on channeling the natural vitality of the land to counteract corruption. The wolf paws, combined with bird talons, allow the shaman to bind the living spirit of Mulgore into an elemental totem capable of purging goblin poisons.",al={"why wolf paws","prairie wolf paw lore","why does mull want paws"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Which water well do these paws help cleanse?",a="The paws are collected specifically to craft the cleansing totem for the Winterhoof Water Well, which was poisoned by goblin interlopers and their agents.",al={"which well","winterhoof well cleansing"},gp=true},{q="What is a Prairie Wolf Paw?",a="A Prairie Wolf Paw is a natural ritual component harvested from the wild canines roaming the plains of Mulgore. Rather than serving as mere trophies, these paws carry the living essence of the land, making them valuable to tauren shamans who craft totems to heal and preserve the earth.",al={"prairie wolf paw","what are prairie wolf paws","wolf paw mulgore"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Where do Prairie Wolf Paws come from?",a="These paws are harvested directly from prairie wolves, prairie stalkers, and wolf alphas native to the grassy plateaus of Mulgore. They are natural parts of the wilderness, taken by hunters to offer the land's strength in times of spiritual need.",al={"where do prairie wolf paws come from","prairie wolf paw source","who drops wolf paws"},it="origin",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For the tauren, harvesting wolf paws is a solemn duty of stewardship, calling upon the land's native beasts to heal the ancestral water wells tainted by goblin greed.",["class:Shaman"]="Crafting cleansing totems out of local animal parts demonstrates the elemental principle of using the living essence of the land to purge unnatural corruption."},kw={"bloodhoof village","mulgore","mull thunderhorn","plainstrider talon","poison water","prairie wolf","prairie wolf paw","winterhoof water well"},rel={"quest:748","npc:mull-thunderhorn","subzone:winterhoof-water-well","item:plainstrider-talon","zone:mulgore","topic:goblin"},src={"Prairie Wolf Paw","Poison Water (Classic)"}}
E["item:stalker-claws"] = {t="item",n="Stalker Claws",z="mulgore",s="Stalker Claws are the talons harvested from the prairie stalkers roaming the rolling plains of Mulgore. Tauren shamans use them as vital components in carving traditional cleansing totems to purify tainted water wells.",h="Stalker Claws are the talons harvested from the prairie stalkers roaming the rolling plains of Mulgore.",sec={{t="Predators of the Plains",b="The grassy steppes of Mulgore are home to deadly natural predators, among them the stealthy prairie stalkers. These beasts survive through raw instinct and lethal precision, an essential balance within the Earth Mother's design. Mull Thunderhorn, seeking to heal the blighted land, requires these claws not out of malice toward the animals, but because the pure, natural ferocity with which they hunt holds the spiritual resonance necessary to counteract artificial corruption.",sp=0},{t="Cleansing the Wells",b="When goblin ventures began drilling and mining across central Kalimdor, chemical poisons seeped deep into the subterranean waters feeding the ancestral tauren wells. The Thunderhorn Water Well in particular fell prey to vile taint. By binding Stalker Claws and Cougar Claws into a Thunderhorn Cleansing Totem, tribal shamans create a spiritual conduit capable of scouring the muck and unnatural sludge out of the earth's natural springs.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where do Stalker Claws come from?",a="They are gathered by hunting prairie stalkers across the grassy hills of Mulgore, primarily east and west of Bloodhoof Village.",al={"where to find stalker claws","who drops stalker claws","stalker claws source"},it="origin",gp=true},{q="Why does Mull Thunderhorn need these claws?",a="Mull Thunderhorn uses the claws, along with flatland cougar claws, to craft a Thunderhorn Cleansing Totem. The innate purity and strength of the plains' native hunters are needed to purify the poisoned water wells.",al={"why gather stalker claws","mull thunderhorn purpose","use of stalker claws"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Why is the Thunderhorn Water Well tainted?",a="The well is suffering from unnatural industrial pollution caused by goblin operations in Mulgore, which disrupt the natural balance and poison the local water table.",al={"who poisoned the well","why is thunderhorn well sick","thunderhorn pollution"},it="threat"},{q="What happens after the claws are gathered?",a="Once collected, Mull carves the claws into a cleansing totem, which must then be carried directly to the Thunderhorn Water Well to purge the lingering corruption.",al={"what comes next","next step after claws","cleansing totem ritual"},it="next_step"},{q="What are Stalker Claws?",a="Stalker Claws are the natural talons harvested from prairie stalkers, fierce feline predators roaming the plains of Mulgore. Though plain hunting trophies to an outsider, tauren shamans view them as embodiments of the creature's natural predatory focus and harmony with the land.",al={"what are stalker claws","stalker claws item","stalker claw"},it="identity",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Gathering these claws serves your tribe's sacred duty to restore the sacred waters poisoned by careless industrial trespassers.",["class:Shaman"]="The claws of wild plains predators harness nature's fierce purity, essential material for binding and empowering cleansing totems."},kw={"cleansing totem","mulgore","mull thunderhorn","prairie stalker","stalker claws","thunderhorn totem","water well"},rel={"quest:756","item:thunderhorn-cleansing-totem","npc:mull-thunderhorn","subzone:thunderhorn-water-well","item:cougar-claws","quest:758","zone:mulgore","topic:goblin"},src={"Stalker Claws","Thunderhorn Totem (Classic)"}}
E["item:thunderhorn-cleansing-totem"] = {t="item",n="Thunderhorn Cleansing Totem",z="mulgore",s="The Thunderhorn Cleansing Totem is a consecrated ritual implement crafted by Mull Thunderhorn in Bloodhoof Village. It is imbued with ancestral magic to purify the corrupted waters of the Thunderhorn Water Well.",h="The Thunderhorn Cleansing Totem is a consecrated ritual implement crafted by Mull Thunderhorn in Bloodhoof...",sec={{t="Purifying the Thunderhorn Well",b="Across the rolling plains of Mulgore, the lifeblood of the earth is being fouled by unseen corruption. The ancestral wells dug by the tauren tribes provide vital nourishment to the herds and hunting grounds, but foul agents have tainted the water. Entrusted by the elder Mull Thunderhorn, adventurers carry this prepared totem north of Bloodhoof Village to drive out the pollution settling over the Thunderhorn clan's sacred spring. Through ritual invocation, the totem calms the disturbed spirits of the water and cleanses the ancestral site.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of the Thunderhorn Cleansing Totem?",a="The totem is prepared specifically to cleanse the corrupted waters of the Thunderhorn Water Well, located north of Bloodhoof Village. Using it drives away the foul influence poisoning the well and pleases the ancestral spirits guarding it.",al={"what does the totem do","why use thunderhorn totem","cleansing totem purpose"},it="purpose"},{q="Who crafted the totem?",a="The totem is crafted and bestowed by Mull Thunderhorn, an elder residing in Bloodhoof Village who watches over the health of the land and communicates with the ancestors.",al={"who made this totem","who gives the thunderhorn totem","mull thunderhorn totem"},it="origin"},{q="Where must the totem be used?",a="The totem must be used directly at the Thunderhorn Water Well, found north of Bloodhoof Village in central Mulgore.",al={"where do i use the totem","totem location","where is thunderhorn well"},it="place"},{q="What happens after the ritual is performed?",a="Cleansing the well brings peace to the ancestral spirits of the Thunderhorn clan. Mull Thunderhorn receives visions in a dream of their gratitude, offering praise and ancestral gifts to the one who purified their grounds.",al={"after cleansing the well","ritual outcome","ancestor reaction"},sp=1},{q="What is the Thunderhorn Cleansing Totem?",a="The Thunderhorn Cleansing Totem is a consecrated ritual implement crafted specifically to purge the taint corrupting the Thunderhorn Water Well in Mulgore. Infused with ancestral and elemental magic, it serves as a sacred conduit that channels spiritual energies directly into the fouled spring to restore the pure, life-giving waters upon which the local tauren depend.",al={"what is thunderhorn cleansing totem","thunderhorn totem what is it","thunderhorn cleansing totem lore"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Why is the Thunderhorn Cleansing Totem needed?",a="The Thunderhorn clan's ancestral water well has fallen victim to unnatural corruption, threatening the land and the beasts that drink from it. Ordinary remedies cannot undo such a blight; Mull Thunderhorn prepared this enchanted totem so that an emissary can conduct the solemn ritual required to banish the taint and appease the ancestral spirits watching over the well.",al={"why need thunderhorn totem","why do i need the cleansing totem","thunderhorn cleansing totem purpose"},it="why_wanted",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For the tauren, purifying the ancestral wells is not merely a chore but a sacred duty to the Earth Mother and clan ancestors who watch over Mulgore.",["class:Shaman"]="Shaman understand that cleansing totems act as conduits to pacify disturbed nature spirits and restore balance where elemental energies have turned bitter."},kw={"bloodhoof","cleansing","mulgore","mull","ritual","thunderhorn","totem","water well"},rel={"quest:758","subzone:thunderhorn-water-well","zone:mulgore"},src={"Thunderhorn Cleansing Totem","Thunderhorn Cleansing (Classic)"}}
E["item:totem-of-hawkwind"] = {t="item",n="Totem of Hawkwind",z="mulgore",s="The Totem of Hawkwind is a carved token carried by young tauren departing Camp Narache. Presented to Baine Bloodhoof in Bloodhoof Village, it serves as proof that the bearer has completed their initial trials and is prepared to undergo the remainder of the Rites of the Earthmother.",h="The Totem of Hawkwind is a carved token carried by young tauren departing Camp Narache.",sec={{t="A Chieftain's Endorsement",b="Carved by the hand of Chief Hawkwind atop the Red Cloud Mesa, this wooden totem acts as an introduction and proof of passage for youth coming of age. In tauren tradition, formal writ is rarely used; instead, meaningful symbols and familiar tribal craft carry a leader's intent. When an aspiring youth completes their preliminary trials at Camp Narache, Hawkwind crafts this icon to send them forward along the road into the wider expanse of Mulgore. The distinctive carvings identify the bearer to Baine Bloodhoof, signaling that they possess the spirit and strength required to face the next trials and seek the eventual blessing of Thunder Bluff.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Totem of Hawkwind used for?",a="It serves as a token of passage. Young tauren carry it from Camp Narache to Bloodhoof Village to show Baine Bloodhoof that Chief Hawkwind has deemed them ready to continue the Rites of the Earthmother.",al={"what does totem of hawkwind do","totem of hawkwind purpose","why do i have this totem"},it="purpose"},{q="Who made the totem?",a="The totem is carved directly by Chief Hawkwind, the leader of the settlement on Red Cloud Mesa. Its distinct carvings are instantly recognized by allied chieftains across Mulgore.",al={"who carved the totem","totem maker","hawkwind totem origins"},it="origin"},{q="Who receives the Totem of Hawkwind?",a="It is brought to Baine Bloodhoof, son of Cairne Bloodhoof, who oversees Bloodhoof Village to the east of Red Cloud Mesa.",al={"who do i give the totem to","totem recipient","where does the totem go"},it="target"},{q="Why use a totem instead of a letter?",a="Tauren culture relies heavily on oral tradition, physical artistry, and ancestral symbols. A carved totem bears the personal touch and spirit of the chieftain who shaped it, carrying far more weight among tribal leaders than ink on parchment.",al={"why not a letter","tauren token traditions"}},{q="What is the Totem of Hawkwind?",a="The Totem of Hawkwind is a carved token of introduction entrusted to promising young tauren who have completed their initial trials in the Red Cloud Mesa. Bearing the distinct markings of Chief Hawkwind, it serves as proof that you are ready to venture beyond Camp Narache and undertake the further Rites of the Earthmother.",al={"totem of hawkwind identity","what is this totem","hawkwind totem"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Why does Baine Bloodhoof want the Totem of Hawkwind?",a="Baine Bloodhoof looks for the totem as an unmistakable sign of your readiness and character. Carved by Chief Hawkwind, its patterns show Baine that an aspiring youth has earned Hawkwind's personal recommendation to continue their spiritual trials and seek the acceptance of Thunder Bluff's elders.",al={"why does baine want the totem","why give totem to baine","baine bloodhoof totem"},it="why_wanted",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Carrying this totem marks the definitive rite of passage away from childhood on the Red Cloud Mesa and out into the greater tribal lands of Mulgore."},kw={"baine bloodhoof","bloodhoof village","camp narache","chief hawkwind","hawkwind","rites of the earthmother","totem"},rel={"quest:763","npc:chief-hawkwind","npc:baine-bloodhoof","subzone:camp-narache","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff"},src={"Totem of Hawkwind","Rites of the Earthmother (2)"}}
E["item:trophy-swoop-quill"] = {t="item",n="Trophy Swoop Quill",z="mulgore",s="A Trophy Swoop Quill is a prized hunting token plucked from the large birds of prey that circle over the plains of Mulgore. Among the tauren, gathering these feathers demonstrates keen eyesight, patience, and hunting skill.",h="A Trophy Swoop Quill is a prized hunting token plucked from the large birds of prey that circle over the...",sec={{t="Token of the Hunt",b="Swoops are cunning avian predators indigenous to Mulgore, known for riding high thermals and remaining difficult to track across the grasslands. For young tauren hunters and travelers striving to prove themselves in Bloodhoof Village, claiming a trophy quill from these birds is a traditional rite of cleverness and determination. Presenting these quills to elders such as Harken Windtotem honors the hunter's clan and signifies that one possesses the sharp senses needed to thrive upon the plains.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is a Trophy Swoop Quill used for?",a="Trophy Swoop Quills are collected as proof of hunting merit for Harken Windtotem in Bloodhoof Village. Bringing them to him demonstrates determination, sharp observation, and honors your standing among the local tauren.",al={"what are swoop quills for","who wants swoop quills","swoop quill use"},it="why_wanted",gp=true},{q="Where do Trophy Swoop Quills come from?",a="These quills are taken from various swoops—including common swoops, wiry swoops, and taloned swoops—found soaring across the rolling plains throughout Mulgore.",al={"where to get swoop quills","who drops trophy swoop quill","swoop locations"},it="origin",gp=true},{q="Why are swoops considered difficult to hunt?",a="As described by Harken Windtotem, swoops are cunning birds that circle high above the grass and move quickly across the landscape, requiring sharp eyes to spot and fleet footwork to run down.",al={"why hunt swoops","are swoops hard to kill"}},{q="What is a Trophy Swoop Quill?",a="A Trophy Swoop Quill is a prized feather taken from the predatory swoops that soar across Mulgore. Because swoops are keen-eyed and elusive creatures, gathering their feathers requires sharp senses and swift feet. Among the tauren, carrying these quills serves as a badge of cleverness and determination, demonstrating that a hunter possesses the patience and skill needed to stalk and overcome cunning prey.",al={"trophy swoop quill","what is swoop quill","swoop quills lore"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Why does Harken Windtotem want Trophy Swoop Quills?",a="Harken Windtotem in Bloodhoof Village asks for these quills as a trial of skill and character. Hunting swoops is no easy feat, and returning with a collection of their finest feathers proves your merit to the village. Delivering them shows that you possess the patience and determination expected of a true hunter, bringing honor to both yourself and your clan.",al={"why collect swoop quills","why harken wants swoop quills","swoop hunting purpose"},it="why_wanted",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Hunting the swoop and bringing home its quills is a traditional pursuit of patience and cleverness across Mulgore, honoring both the hunter and the clan.",["class:Hunter"]="Tracking airborne prey across open plains tests the fundamentals of tracking, patience, and marksmanship."},kw={"bloodhoof village","harken windtotem","mulgore","swoop hunting","swoop quill","taloned swoop","trophy swoop quill","wiryswoop"},rel={"quest:761","npc:harken-windtotem","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore","topic:hunter"},src={"Trophy Swoop Quill","Swoop Hunting"}}
E["item:venture-co-documents"] = {t="item",n="Venture Co. Documents",z="mulgore",s="These tightly bound papers were recovered from a crate amidst a ruined caravan in eastern Mulgore. They detail the operations of the Venture Company, confirming the predatory cartel's illegal strip-mining ventures within the tauren homeland.",h="These tightly bound papers were recovered from a crate amidst a ruined caravan in eastern Mulgore.",sec={{t="Evidence of Exploitation",b="Discovered packed atop mined ore and minerals in the remnants of a ransacked wagon, these documents expose the scale of the Venture Company's intrusion into Mulgore. Known across Kalimdor for ruthless deforestation and uncontrolled excavation, the goblin-led cartel has quietly set up drilling and mining outposts in the eastern foothills. Morin Cloudstalker and the tauren Outrunners rely on these seized ledgers to gauge the threat to their sacred ancestral plains and rally hunters to drive the trespassers out.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What do the Venture Co. Documents contain?",a="The sheaf of papers outlines the Venture Company's mining and extraction efforts in eastern Mulgore, revealing that the goblin enterprise is actively harvesting and carting away the region's natural mineral wealth.",al={"venture co papers contents","what are venture documents","what is written in documents"},it="identity"},{q="Where were these documents discovered?",a="They are retrieved from a sealed Venture Company crate found amidst the wreckage of a ransacked wagon at the Ravaged Caravan site, east of Bloodhoof Village.",al={"where do i find the documents","venture documents location","ravaged caravan crate"},it="place"},{q="Who needs to see these papers?",a="They must be brought to Morin Cloudstalker, a tauren hunter stationed along the main trail east of Bloodhoof Village who watches over the security of Mulgore.",al={"who do i bring documents to","turn in venture documents","morin cloudstalker documents"},it="target"},{q="Why are the Venture Company mining in Mulgore?",a="The Venture Company operates purely for profit, seeking out rich mineral veins and untamed territories to strip of valuable ores with zero regard for native treaties, spiritual customs, or the environment.",al={"why are goblins in mulgore","venture co mulgore goal","why venture co digging"},it="motive"},{q="Where do the Venture Co. Documents come from, and who wrote them?",a="These papers concern the Venture Company's clandestine mining operations in the eastern foothills of Mulgore. Addressed to Supervisor Fizsprocket on behalf of the company's advisory board and President Razdunk, the documents were packed atop a crate of raw ore and minerals found near a ransacked caravan, ready to be shipped out alongside the stolen wealth of the plains.",al={"venture co documents origin","who made venture co documents","where did venture co papers come from"},it="origin",add=true},{q="Why does Morin Cloudstalker want the Venture Co. Documents?",a="The tauren Outrunners protecting Mulgore require undeniable proof of the Venture Company's encroaching presence. Morin Cloudstalker needs these records to confirm the exact scope of the goblins' mining schemes, giving the defenders of Bloodhoof Village the vital intelligence they need to halt the exploitation and pillaging of their ancestral lands.",al={"why does morin want the venture co documents","why keep venture co documents","who wants venture co papers"},it="why_wanted",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The Earth Mother's sacred earth is being defiled by goblin machinery. Delivering these papers helps the elders confirm the extent of the desecration and launch retaliatory strikes.",["profession:Mining"]="The papers were discovered packed alongside raw mineral samples, proving that the foothills hold rich, untapped veins ripe for excavation."},kw={"morin cloudstalker","mulgore mining","ravaged caravan","venture co documents","venture company"},rel={"quest:749","npc:morin-cloudstalker","quest:764","subzone:ravaged-caravan","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore","topic:horde"},src={"Venture Co. Documents","The Ravaged Caravan (Horde) (2)"}}
E["item:verdant-note"] = {t="item",n="Verdant Note",z="mulgore",s="The Verdant Note is a summons delivered to novice tauren druids in Camp Narache. Sent on behalf of the druid mentor Gart Mistrunner, it instructs young initiates to report for training and begin learning the rites of the Earthmother.",h="The Verdant Note is a summons delivered to novice tauren druids in Camp Narache.",sec={{t="A Call to the Path",b="Young tauren who demonstrate a spiritual affinity for nature receive the Verdant Note shortly after beginning their trials in the Red Cloud Mesa. Carried by a messenger sent by Gart Mistrunner, the brief missive directs the initiate to present themselves to the mentor at Camp Narache. Restless spirits and shifting elements have begun troubling Mulgore, making the instruction of new druids an urgent duty for the elders.",sp=0},{t="Teachings of the Earthmother",b="Upon answering the note, initiates are inducted into the deeper mysteries of nature communion. Gart Mistrunner cautions young druids that although night elves share a common bond with the wild, their ancient pride keeps them distant and unwelcoming. Tauren druidism focuses strictly on reverence for the Earthmother, guiding initiates to listen to the land's restless ancestral voices and serve as her living instruments.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Verdant Note?",a="It is a written summons delivered to fledgling tauren druids at Camp Narache, instructing them to seek out their local trainer, Gart Mistrunner, to formally begin their spiritual education.",al={"verdant note item","what is this note"},it="identity"},{q="Who sends the Verdant Note?",a="The note is sent by a messenger on behalf of Gart Mistrunner, the druid trainer stationed at Camp Narache on the Red Cloud Mesa.",al={"who sent verdant note","who wrote verdant note"},it="origin"},{q="Why is Gart Mistrunner calling for druid initiates?",a="Ancestral spirits across Mulgore and neighboring regions have grown unusually restless. Gart needs newly awakened druids trained quickly so they can commune with the wild spirits and heal the disturbed land.",al={"why does gart want me","gart mistrunner reason"},it="motive"},{q="What does Gart tell druids regarding the night elves?",a="Gart warns that despite sharing a sacred bond with nature, the night elves are proud and unlikely to welcome tauren into their circles. He advises initiates to keep their attention focused on the teachings of the Earthmother instead.",al={"night elves and tauren druids","what about night elves"}},{q="Why does Gart Mistrunner want you to have the Verdant Note?",a="Gart Mistrunner sends you the Verdant Note to summon you for your druidic training at Camp Narache. Restless spirits have stirred throughout Mulgore and lands beyond, and Gart requires new initiates to learn to listen to the Earthmother and the spirits of nature. The note serves as an urgent summons to step forward and embrace your duties as an avatar of the natural world.",al={"why verdant note","why does gart send the note","verdant note purpose"},it="why_wanted",add=true}},ang={["class:Druid"]="This parchment marks your formal acceptance into druidic study among the tauren, starting your lifelong journey in communion with the Earthmother.",["race:Tauren"]="For the Shu'halo, druidism is an ancestral calling directly tied to healing the balance of their sacred hunting grounds in Mulgore."},kw={"camp narache","druid","earthmother","gart mistrunner","mulgore","red cloud mesa","verdant note"},rel={"quest:3094","npc:gart-mistrunner","topic:druid","subzone:camp-narache","zone:mulgore"},src={"Verdant Note","Verdant Note (quest)"}}
E["item:water-of-the-seers"] = {t="item",n="Water of the Seers",z="mulgore",s="Water of the Seers is a sacred draught given to young tauren undertaking their tribal trials in Bloodhoof Village. Drinking the holy water near the tribal fire opens the mind to the spirit world, summoning an ethereal Plains Vision to guide the pilgrim on their spiritual journey.",h="Water of the Seers is a sacred draught given to young tauren undertaking their tribal trials in Bloodhoof...",sec={{t="A Vessel for the Spirit",b="Prepared by tribal mystics, the Water of the Seers is an essential element of the ancient Rite of Vision. To pierce the veil between physical reality and ancestral guidance, the petitioner must drink the holy water before the central tribal fire in Bloodhoof Village. Once consumed, the potent draught grants spiritual sight, causing a ghostly guide known as the Plains Vision to materialize from the embers and lead the way toward Seer Wiserunner.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What happens after drinking the Water of the Seers?",a="Ingesting the holy water beside the village's tribal bonfire opens your spiritual senses. Within moments, an ancestral spirit manifested as a ghostly wolf, the Plains Vision, steps forth from the flames to guide your footsteps across Mulgore.",al={"what does water of the seers do","drinking water of the seers","plains vision water"},it="power"},{q="Where should the water be consumed?",a="The draught must be taken directly before the large tribal fire located in Bloodhoof Village, just north of the local shamanic elders.",al={"where to drink water of the seers","water of the seers location","tribal fire location"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Who does the vision guide you to?",a="The spectral Plains Vision leads the pilgrim northwest into the foothills of Mulgore, arriving at the cave where Seer Wiserunner awaits to interpret your journey.",al={"where does the wolf go","plains vision destination","who is the seer"},it="target"},{q="What is Water of the Seers?",a="Water of the Seers is a sacred, holy draught prepared by tauren mystics for initiates undertaking the traditional rites of passage. Ingesting these blessed waters opens your spiritual senses, allowing you to perceive omens and guide spirits that remain hidden to ordinary eyes.",al={"what is water of the seers","water of the seers lore","what kind of item is water of the seers"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Where does Water of the Seers come from, and who made it?",a="The water is ritually prepared and bestowed by Zarlman Two-Moons in Bloodhoof Village. Tauren seers bless the liquid through sacred ceremonial practices, imbuing it with spiritual potency specifically for young hunters undergoing the ancestral trials.",al={"who made water of the seers","origin of water of the seers","where does water of the seers come from"},it="origin",add=true},{q="Why does anyone want Water of the Seers?",a="You need the Water of the Seers to complete the Rite of Vision, an essential spiritual trial for the tauren. Drinking the draught by the tribal fire summons the Plains Vision—a spirit guide meant to lead you across Mulgore toward your destined path and elder advisors.",al={"why drink water of the seers","purpose of water of the seers","why do i need water of the seers"},it="why_wanted",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The Water of the Seers is a foundational sacred brew in the education of young shu'halo, bridging ancient shamanic customs with tribal passage into adulthood.",["class:Shaman"]="Experiencing the draught's effects offers early insight into commune with the spirit world, demonstrating how ritual substances can thin the veil between physical and ancestral realms."},kw={"bloodhoof village","plains vision","rite of vision","seer wiserunner","tribal fire","water of the seers"},rel={"quest:767","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore"},src={"Water of the Seers","Rite of Vision (3)"}}
E["item:water-pitcher"] = {t="item",n="Water Pitcher",z="mulgore",s="A simple vessel used to carry fresh spring water from the local well in the Red Cloud Mesa. Greatmother Hawkwind directs young tauren to haul it back to Camp Narache to instill the values of humility, service, and respect for tribal elders.",h="A simple vessel used to carry fresh spring water from the local well in the Red Cloud Mesa.",sec={{t="A Lesson in Humility",b="Though modest in design, the water pitcher serves as an essential teaching tool for the youth of Camp Narache. Resting beside the water well on the plains southeast of the camp, it represents the daily labor required to sustain the community. By asking a novice to fill and carry the heavy vessel to Chief Hawkwind, Greatmother Hawkwind tests their character. The chore demonstrates that no duty is beneath a tribal protector, reminding young braves that tending to the everyday needs of their kin is just as vital as wielding a weapon on the hunt.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the water pitcher used for?",a="It is used to transport fresh well water back to Camp Narache. Hauling it across the plains is a traditional chore that tests a young adventurer's willingness to perform everyday labor for the good of the tribe.",al={"pitcher purpose","why carry water pitcher","humble task pitcher"},it="purpose",gp=true},{q="Where do you find the water pitcher?",a="The pitcher rests right by the water well located southeast of Camp Narache on the Red Cloud Mesa, near where Greatmother Hawkwind tends to her tasks.",al={"pitcher location","where is water pitcher","find pitcher mulgore"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Who asks you to fetch the water pitcher?",a="Greatmother Hawkwind asks young travelers to collect it. Her aging legs make the trek difficult, so she offers the task to eager youths to test their vitality and humility.",al={"who gives pitcher quest","greatmother pitcher"},it="motive",gp=true},{q="Who receives the water pitcher once it is filled?",a="It is brought directly to Chief Hawkwind at Camp Narache, who sees the deed as proof that the bearer possesses the heart and dedication needed to serve the tribe.",al={"who gets pitcher","deliver water pitcher","bring pitcher to"},it="target",gp=true},{q="What is the Water Pitcher?",a="The Water Pitcher is a simple clay vessel kept at the freshwater well on the plains southeast of Camp Narache. Filled with fresh well water, it serves as vital refreshment for the young hunters, braves, and elders living in the camp atop Red Cloud Mesa.",al={"what is water pitcher","water pitcher info","water pitcher mulgore"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Why does Chief Hawkwind need the Water Pitcher?",a="Gathering fresh water from the plains well is essential daily work for the survival of Camp Narache. His mother, Greatmother Hawkwind, tasks young adventurers with hauling the pitcher up to the chief to assist the tribe and prove they are willing to perform even the most humble chores for their people.",al={"why want water pitcher","why bring water pitcher","chief hawkwind water pitcher"},it="why_wanted",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For young tauren coming of age on the Red Cloud Mesa, carrying the pitcher is an early lesson that honoring elders and aiding the community are the true foundations of strength."},kw={"a humble task","camp narache","chief hawkwind","greatmother hawkwind","mulgore well","red cloud mesa","water pitcher"},rel={"quest:752","npc:greatmother-hawkwind","npc:chief-hawkwind","subzone:camp-narache","zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff"},src={"Water Pitcher","A Humble Task (2)"}}
E["item:well-stone"] = {t="item",n="Well Stone",z="mulgore",s="Well Stones are small mineral rocks gathered from the banks and surroundings of Mulgore's water wells. Rich in subtle earthen energies, they serve as a vital component in brewing the Water of the Seers during the traditional tauren rites of passage.",h="Well Stones are small mineral rocks gathered from the banks and surroundings of Mulgore's water wells.",sec={{t="Vessel of Earthen Energies",b="Resting naturally in the soft earth bordering the wells of Mulgore, Well Stones absorb the spiritual resonance of the plains and the waters beneath them. Shaman and seers such as Zarlman Two-Moons recognize these stones not as mundane river pebbles, but as receptacles of vital magic. When combined with grounded Ambercorn beneath the plains' mighty trees, their mineral properties temper the brew needed to awaken visionary trance states in young tauren undergoing the sacred Rite of Vision.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What are Well Stones used for?",a="Well Stones are combined with Ambercorn to brew the Water of the Seers. Once consumed before the tribal fire, the potion grants a guiding vision to initiates seeking to prove their spiritual maturity.",al={"well stone purpose","what is well stone for","why gather well stones"},it="purpose"},{q="Where are Well Stones found?",a="They lie on the ground around the communal water wells scattered across Mulgore, where the life-giving moisture of the plains touches the soil.",al={"where to find well stone","well stone locations","well stone mulgore"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Who asks for these stones?",a="Zarlman Two-Moons, a respected mystic residing in Bloodhoof Village, directs initiates to gather them as part of the Rite of Vision.",al={"who needs well stones","zarlman well stones","rite of vision stones"},gp=true},{q="What is a Well Stone?",a="A Well Stone is a naturally occurring stone infused with magical elements, gathered from the earth surrounding the sacred water wells across Mulgore. Tauren mystics prize these stones for ritual alchemy, as their connection to the land's pure waters makes them potent catalytic reagents.",al={"what are well stones","well stone mulgore","well stone lore"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Where do Well Stones come from?",a="Well Stones are not crafted by mortal hands; they are drawn straight from the soil and sediment surrounding Mulgore's water wells. Shaped and touched over generations by the sacred springs that feed the plains, these minerals naturally absorb the ambient spiritual and elemental energy of the land.",al={"where do well stones come from","who made well stones","well stone origin"},it="origin",add=true},{q="Why does Zarlman Two-Moons want Well Stones?",a="Zarlman Two-Moons requires Well Stones to prepare the Water of the Seers for young tauren undertaking the Rite of Vision. When crushed and steeped alongside Ambercorn, the magical elements within the stones craft a potent sacred brew that awakens your spiritual sight, revealing ancestral guides to light your path.",al={"why gather well stones","why does zarlman want well stones","well stone rite of vision"},it="why_wanted",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Gathering Well Stones is an essential step in demonstrating communion with the land, teaching every young tauren how physical elements of the earth contribute to spiritual enlightenment.",["class:Shaman"]="Shaman understand Well Stones as natural anchors of earth and water magic, drawing latent spirits together into a transformative ceremonial mixture."},kw={"ambercorn","bloodhoof village","mulgore","rite of vision","water of the seers","well stone","zarlman two-moons"},rel={"quest:767","item:water-of-the-seers","item:ambercorn","npc:zarlman-two-moons","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore"},src={"Well Stone","Rite of Vision (2)"}}
E["item:wildmane-cleansing-totem"] = {t="item",n="Wildmane Cleansing Totem",z="mulgore",s="The Wildmane Cleansing Totem is a consecrated ritual implement crafted to purify the northern water well of Mulgore. Entrusted to young defenders of the land, it serves as the final instrument in driving out the toxic corruption left by encroaching goblin ventures.",h="The Wildmane Cleansing Totem is a consecrated ritual implement crafted to purify the northern water well of...",sec={{t="Purifying the Sacred Wells",b="When goblin ventures began poisoning the land and fouling Mulgore's scarce water supplies, the tauren turned to sacred ritual to reverse the blight. After cleansing both the Winterhoof and Thunderhorn water wells, Mull Thunderhorn crafts this final totem to purge the remaining corruption at the Wildmane Water Well north of Thunder Bluff. Infused with natural spirits, the totem cleanses the fetid waters so pure life can nourish the plains once more.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of the Wildmane Cleansing Totem?",a="The totem is used in a tauren purification ritual to neutralize the poisons and foul runoff contaminating the Wildmane Water Well north of Thunder Bluff, restoring the purity of the land.",al={"what does the totem do","wildmane totem use","why cleanse the well"},it="purpose"},{q="Who creates the Wildmane Cleansing Totem?",a="Mull Thunderhorn provides the totem after you assist him in gathering the necessary components and cleansing the other tainted wells throughout Mulgore.",al={"who gave me this totem","mull thunderhorn totem","where do i get the cleansing totem"},it="origin"},{q="Why are the water wells in Mulgore poisoned?",a="Encroaching goblins have introduced noxious poisons and destructive industrial runoff into the local water table, threatening the tauren tribes and the natural balance of the plains.",al={"who poisoned the wells","why is the well poisonous","goblin poisons mulgore"},it="motive"},{q="Where does this totem need to be used?",a="It must be taken to the Wildmane Water Well, situated on the rolling plains north of the great bluffs of Thunder Bluff.",al={"wildmane well location","where is wildmane water well","where do i take the totem"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What is the Wildmane Cleansing Totem?",a="It is a sacred ritual implement prepared by the shaman Mull Thunderhorn in Bloodhoof Village. Imbued with the favor of the spirits after your purification of the Winterhoof and Thunderhorn wells, this totem carries the power needed to perform the cleansing ritual at the final corrupted water well north of Thunder Bluff.",al={"what is wildmane cleansing totem","wildmane totem","cleansing totem wildmane"},it="identity",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Restoring the Wildmane well is a solemn obligation to the Earth Mother, purging foreign industrial toxins to preserve your ancestral homeland.",["class:Shaman"]="Channeling spiritual purification through carved totems is fundamental to mending the wounded water spirits corrupted by reckless industry."},kw={"bloodhoof village","goblin poisons","mulgore","mull thunderhorn","tauren","wildmane cleansing totem","wildmane water well"},rel={"quest:760","quest:759","subzone:wildmane-water-well","npc:mull-thunderhorn","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff","topic:goblin"},src={"Wildmane Cleansing Totem","Wildmane Cleansing (Classic)"}}
E["item:winterhoof-cleansing-totem"] = {t="item",n="Winterhoof Cleansing Totem",z="mulgore",s="The Winterhoof Cleansing Totem is a consecrated ritual implement crafted by Mull Thunderhorn in Bloodhoof Village. Carved to undo the toxic blight spread by Venture Company intruders, it channels the Earth Mother's power to purify the polluted Winterhoof Water Well.",h="The Winterhoof Cleansing Totem is a consecrated ritual implement crafted by Mull Thunderhorn in Bloodhoof...",sec={{t="Purifying the Spring",b="When the goblin engineers of the Venture Company encroached upon the plains of Mulgore, their industrial operations quickly tainted the land's precious subterranean water tables. In response, Mull Thunderhorn prepared the Winterhoof Cleansing Totem, imbuing the carved wood with protective magic intended to wash out the foreign poisons. Carried past hostile goblin guards to the edge of the Winterhoof Water Well, the totem disperses the contamination through a focused ritual, restoring pure drinking water to the wandering herds and native tribes of the plains.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Winterhoof Cleansing Totem used for?",a="It is brought to the Winterhoof Water Well southeast of Bloodhoof Village to perform a purifying ritual, scouring away the industrial contamination dumped into the aquifer by Venture Company goblins.",al={"cleansing totem purpose","winterhoof totem use","what does totem do"},it="purpose"},{q="Who crafted this totem?",a="Mull Thunderhorn, a respected tauren resident of Bloodhoof Village, carved and prepared the totem specifically to undo the damage done to the Winterhoof Water Well.",al={"who made winterhoof totem","creator of cleansing totem","mull totem"},it="origin"},{q="Why was the Winterhoof Water Well polluted?",a="The Venture Company established an encampment around the spring, despoiling the natural watering hole with runoff, waste, and chemical sludge from their logging and mining machinery.",al={"who poisoned winterhoof well","well pollution cause","why is well dirty"},it="conflict"},{q="What happens once the cleansing ritual is complete?",a="The murky, tainted waters become clean and wholesome once more, allowing the local plains fauna and migrating tauren herds to safely drink from the well again.",al={"after using totem","well cleansed result","does well get fixed"},it="next_step"},{q="What is the Winterhoof Cleansing Totem?",a="It is a sacred ritual totem carved by Mull Thunderhorn in Mulgore. Infused with natural spirits, the totem is specifically designed to perform a purification rite over the poisoned Winterhoof Water Well, purging the industrial pollutants introduced by the Venture Company.",al={"winterhoof cleansing totem","what is winterhoof totem","about winterhoof cleansing totem"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Why does Mull Thunderhorn need the Winterhoof Cleansing Totem used?",a="The tauren rely on the water wells of Mulgore to sustain themselves and the wild beasts of the plains. Because goblins from the Venture Company fouled the Winterhoof Water Well, Mull Thunderhorn needs an adventurer to carry this totem into hostile territory and cleanse the water before the taint permanently ruins the land.",al={"why use winterhoof cleansing totem","why cleanse winterhoof well","winterhoof totem purpose"},it="why_wanted",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The desecration of fresh water sources strikes at the heart of the tauren bond with the Earth Mother, making the totem's rite an essential defense of the homeland.",["class:Shaman"]="Using a cleansing totem mirrors core shamanic principles, wielding the elemental harmony of earth and water to cast out artificial corruption."},kw={"bloodhoof village","cleansing","mulgore","mull thunderhorn","totem","venture company","water well","winterhoof"},rel={"quest:754","subzone:winterhoof-water-well","npc:mull-thunderhorn","subzone:bloodhoof-village","topic:venture-company","zone:mulgore","topic:goblin"},src={"Winterhoof Cleansing Totem","Winterhoof Cleansing (Classic)"}}
E["npc:baine-bloodhoof"] = {t="npc",n="Baine Bloodhoof",z="mulgore",s="Baine Bloodhoof is the son of High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof and the acting protector of Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore. While his father governs the tribes from the towering heights of Thunder Bluff, Baine directly oversees local defense, investigating encroaching industrial exploitation and defending against feral predators. He serves as an essential bridge between youthful tauren braves and the ancestral wisdom of his clan.",h="Baine Bloodhoof is the son of High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof and the acting protector of Bloodhoof Village...",sec={{t="Son of the High Chieftain",b="Born to Cairne and the late Tamaala, Baine spent his youth traveling alongside his nomadic people before they claimed the rich grasslands of Mulgore. His early life was marked by the ever-present threat of the centaur clans. Shortly after the Third War, a centaur warband under Khiragg captured young Baine, plunging Cairne into a grief-stricken stupor that nearly shattered the newly founded settlements. It was only through the courage of the champion Rexxar that Baine was liberated from captivity. That rescue restored Cairne's spirit, allowing the tauren to stand with Thrall's Horde against the invading Kul Tirans and secure their foothold on the continent.",sp=0},{t="Guardian of Bloodhoof Village",b="Rather than lingering in the relative comfort of the capital mesas, Baine chooses to reside among his kin in Bloodhoof Village. As Cairne advances in age, Baine carries an increasing burden of stewardship, representing the next generation of tauren leadership. He directs braves and visiting allies to combat the unruly Palemane gnolls who pester the borders, and keeps a watchful eye on suspicious outside influences threatening Mulgore's natural balance. Whenever Cairne departs the capital on state affairs, Baine steps forward as the acting leader for the united tribes.",sp=0},{t="Defending the Earth Mother",b="Beyond territorial border clashes, Baine faces modern encroachers unconcerned with spiritual balance. To the east, the goblin Venture Company cuts deep into the sacred landscape, while dwarven prospectors from Bael Modan dig into ancestral soil at the Bael'dun Digsite. Baine advocates for a firm yet measured response: attempting to warn the miners and destroy their destructive equipment rather than rushing into senseless bloodshed. He warns that the dwarves' violent digging is hurting the spirits of the earth, though the miners refuse to listen.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Baine Bloodhoof?",a="Baine is the only son of Cairne Bloodhoof, the High Chieftain of the tauren tribes. He oversees defense and tribal affairs in Bloodhoof Village, stepping in to direct local braves and protect Mulgore while his father rules from Thunder Bluff.",al={"baine lore","who is baine","cairnes son"},it="identity"},{q="Where can Baine be found right now?",a="Baine stands in Bloodhoof Village, situated in central Mulgore just south of Thunder Bluff. He can be found near the town center organizing tasks for newly arrived adventurers.",al={"baine location","where is baine","baine bloodhoof spot"},it="why_here"},{q="How was Baine rescued from the centaur?",a="Following the Third War, a centaur leader named Khiragg captured Baine, plunging Cairne into debilitating despair. The beastmaster Rexxar sought out Bovan Windtotem's outcasts, tracked the centaur to their encampment, slew the captors, and safely returned Baine to his father.",al={"baine kidnapped","rexxar saves baine","baine captured"},it="history"},{q="What problems does Baine manage in Bloodhoof Village?",a="Baine coordinates efforts against the aggressive Palemane gnolls harassing local herds and keeps a close watch on foreign invaders, such as the goblin Venture Company and Alliance dwarven excavators disrupting the earth.",al={"baine quests","what does baine do","bloodhoof village troubles"},it="purpose"},{q="What is Baine's stance on the Bael'dun dwarven digsite?",a="Baine believes the violent blasting techniques of the Bael'dun miners harm the spirits of the earth. He seeks to sabotage their industrial tools and scare them off before their reckless digging brings elemental retribution upon the region.",al={"baine dwarves","bael dun mining","dwarf digsite mulgore"},it="conflict"},{q="Will Baine succeed Cairne as High Chieftain?",a="As Cairne's beloved son and right-hand advisor, Baine already acts as temporary leader whenever Cairne is away from Thunder Bluff. He is widely considered the natural heir to lead the united tauren tribes in the future.",al={"does baine become leader","future high chieftain","cairne heir"}},{q="Whose side is Baine Bloodhoof on?",a="Baine is completely devoted to the tauren people and the Horde. As the son of High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof, his loyalties lie firmly with his tribe in Mulgore and the larger coalition forged with Thrall's orcs and the Darkspear trolls.",al={"baine bloodhoof faction","is baine horde","baine allegiance"},it="side",add=true},{q="What does Baine Bloodhoof want?",a="Baine seeks to safeguard Mulgore and protect the peaceful homeland his father secured for their people. Right now, he aims to repel the aggressive Palemane gnolls, curtail the destructive excavations of the Venture Company, and convince the encroaching dwarven miners at Bael Modan to stop scarring the sacred earth before the land's spirits lash out.",al={"baine bloodhoof goals","baine goal","what does baine want"},it="wants",add=true},{q="Who is Baine Bloodhoof tied to?",a="Baine is the only child of High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof and his late mother Tamaala. Beyond his family, he shares a lasting debt of gratitude with Rexxar, the Champion of the Horde who rescued him from captivity when centaur raiders threatened his life.",al={"baine family","who is baine related to","baine connections"},it="ties",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Baine represents both the honorable lineage of High Chieftain Cairne and the living hope of the young tribes gathered across the plains of Mulgore.",["faction:Horde"]="Baine embodies the calm wisdom and quiet martial strength that anchors the tauren within Thrall's Horde, offering guidance to those passing through central Mulgore.",["class:Shaman"]="Baine respects the spirits above all else, sending travelers to silence dwarven machines and reckless miners before the fury of the earth elementals is unleashed."},kw={"baine","bloodhoof","bloodhoof village","cairne","centaur","khiragg","mulgore","palemane","rexxar","thunder bluff"},rel={"subzone:bloodhoof-village","npc:cairne-bloodhoof","zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff","subzone:bael-dun-digsite","topic:centaur","topic:tauren","topic:horde"},src={"Baine Bloodhoof"}}
E["npc:cairne-bloodhoof"] = {t="npc",n="Cairne Bloodhoof",z="mulgore",s="Cairne Bloodhoof is the venerable High Chieftain of the united tauren tribes and ruler of Thunder Bluff. A towering warrior of immense wisdom, he bound the fate of the tauren to Thrall's Horde after the orcs aided his people in escaping extinction at the hands of the centaur.",h="Cairne Bloodhoof is the venerable High Chieftain of the united tauren tribes and ruler of Thunder Bluff.",sec={{t="A Nation in Mulgore",b="For generations, the tauren lived as wandering nomads across the Barrens, continually hunted and driven toward extinction by savage centaur marauders. When Thrall and his orcs arrived on the shores of Kalimdor, Cairne recognized a rare nobility in them. Thrall offered military protection, escorting Cairne's desperate caravan across the plains to the safety of Mulgore in exchange for guidance to the Oracle at Stonetalon Peak. Settling upon the towering mesas of Thunder Bluff, Cairne finally established a permanent, unified home for all tauren tribes.",sp=0},{t="Pledge to the Horde",b="Indebted to Thrall, Cairne followed the orcs to Stonetalon Peak and fought side by side with the Horde and Jaina Proudmoore's humans against the Burning Legion during the Third War. Later, when Admiral Daelin Proudmoore invaded Durotar, Cairne personally took up his ancestral runespear once more to fight alongside Rexxar after his captured son, Baine, was safely recovered. Today, Cairne stands as one of the Horde's most respected elders and Thrall's closest confidant, offering calm council against reckless warmongering.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Cairne Bloodhoof?",a="Cairne Bloodhoof is the High Chieftain of the united tauren tribes, chieftain of the Bloodhoof tribe, and leader of Thunder Bluff. He is an ancient warrior whose leadership and strength led the tauren out of nomadic exile and into a secure homeland.",al={"who is cairne","tauren leader","high chieftain"},it="identity"},{q="Why are the tauren allied with the orcs?",a="During the Third War, marauding centaur tribes were hunting the tauren to extinction. Thrall and his newly arrived orcs fought off the marauders and escorted Cairne's great caravan to Mulgore. In gratitude, Cairne pledged his people's loyalty and strength to Thrall's Horde.",al={"why tauren join horde","cairne thrall friendship","tauren orc alliance"},it="side"},{q="Where does Cairne reside?",a="He presides atop Thunder Bluff, the capital city built on the high mesas of Mulgore, ruling alongside the leaders of the various reunited tauren tribes.",al={"where is cairne","cairne location","find cairne bloodhoof"},it="place"},{q="Who is Baine Bloodhoof to Cairne?",a="Baine is Cairne's beloved son and heir. When Baine was abducted by centaur raiders, Cairne fell into deep grief, refusing to fight until Rexxar and his companions tracked Baine down and returned him safely home.",al={"cairne son","is baine cairne's son","baine bloodhoof relation"},it="ties"},{q="What does Cairne Bloodhoof want?",a="Cairne desires lasting peace and prosperity for his people. For years, the nomadic tauren faced near extinction at the hands of marauding centaur tribes. Having united the tribes atop the high mesas of Mulgore, his focus is securing their ancestral home, safeguarding their peaceful spiritual traditions, and standing faithfully alongside his Horde allies against any who threaten Kalimdor.",al={"cairne goals","what does cairne want","cairne ambitions"},it="wants",add=true},{q="Why is Cairne Bloodhoof in Thunder Bluff?",a="As High Chieftain of the united tauren tribes, Cairne rules his people from their capital of Thunder Bluff. He founded the settlement atop the towering mesas of Mulgore to protect the tauren from marauding centaur, and he remains here to govern the tribes, counsel shamans, and coordinate defense alongside his Horde allies.",al={"why is cairne in thunder bluff","why is cairne here","cairne bloodhoof location reason"},it="why_here",add=true},{q="Why is Cairne Bloodhoof famous?",a="Cairne is venerated as a peerless warrior, the wielder of the Bloodhoof Runespear, and the visionary leader who united the scattered tauren tribes. Renowned across Kalimdor for his immense strength and seasoned wisdom, he played a crucial role in defeating the Burning Legion atop Mount Hyjal and establishing the modern Horde alongside Thrall.",al={"cairne bloodhoof reputation","is cairne bloodhoof famous","why is cairne respected"},it="fame",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Cairne is the savior of your people, having united the scattered tribes, repelled the centaur, and led the construction of Thunder Bluff.",["faction:Horde"]="Alongside Thrall, Cairne is a founding father of the modern Horde on Kalimdor, serving as a pillar of honor, wisdom, and military power."},kw={"baine","bloodhoof","cairne","centaur","high chieftain","mulgore","thrall","thunder bluff"},rel={"npc:thrall","topic:tauren","zone:thunderbluff","zone:mulgore","npc:baine-bloodhoof","topic:centaur","topic:horde","subzone:stonetalon-peak"},src={"Cairne Bloodhoof"}}
E["npc:chief-hawkwind"] = {t="npc",n="Chief Hawkwind",z="mulgore",s="Chief Hawkwind is the tribal leader of Camp Narache, the proving grounds nestled in the Red Cloud Mesa of Mulgore. Responsible for guiding the youth of the Shu'halo, he mentors new adventurers as they embark on their traditional rites of passage. Under his watchful gaze, young braves learn humility, respect for the land, and the strength needed to protect their people.",h="Chief Hawkwind is the tribal leader of Camp Narache, the proving grounds nestled in the Red Cloud Mesa of...",sec={{t="Chieftain of the Mesa",b="Standing at the heart of Camp Narache, Chief Hawkwind carries the solemn responsibility of preparing the next generation of tauren for the wider world. Red Cloud Mesa serves as a sheltered haven where fledgling hunters, shamans, druids, and warriors undertake the ancient Rites of the Earthmother. Hawkwind oversees these customs, ensuring that every youth learns duty and endurance before venturing beyond the safety of the mesa.",sp=0},{t="Guiding the Rites",b="Hawkwind's lessons begin with humility rather than martial glory. He sends initiates on simple chores, such as fetching clean spring water, to instill reverence for the natural world and their elders. Only after demonstrating a humble heart are braves guided toward tests of martial prowess, culminating in trials that test their resolve against the aggressive Bristleback quilboar encroaching upon the ancestral plains.",sp=0},{t="Defending Camp Narache",b="Beyond his role as a mentor, Chief Hawkwind must reckon with rising dangers right at his camp's doorstep. The Bristleback tribe has grown increasingly bold, pressing hard against the perimeter of Red Cloud Mesa. Hawkwind coordinates the defense of the settlement, tasking newly proven braves with blunting the quilboar assaults to ensure the sacred training grounds remain secure.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Chief Hawkwind?",a="Chief Hawkwind is the chieftain of Camp Narache in the Red Cloud Mesa. He oversees tribal matters in the area and mentors young tauren as they prepare to take their first steps into the wider world of Mulgore and Kalimdor.",al={"hawkwind","chief hawkwind lore","who is the chief at camp narache"},it="identity"},{q="What are the Rites of the Earthmother?",a="The Rites of the Earthmother are a traditional series of sacred trials undertaken by young tauren coming of age. Guided by elders like Chief Hawkwind, these rites test humility, strength, courage, and spiritual connection to the land and the ancestors.",al={"tauren rites","rites of earthmother","what are the rites"}},{q="Why does Chief Hawkwind have you fetch water first?",a="Hawkwind assigns 'A Humble Task' to remind young braves that service and respect for the community are as vital as martial strength. Gathering fresh water for tribal elders teaches patience and humility before initiates are sent into battle.",al={"a humble task","why fetch water","hawkwind water quest"},it="motive"},{q="What threat is Camp Narache facing?",a="Camp Narache is under constant pressure from hostile Bristleback quilboar who inhabit the surrounding hills of Red Cloud Mesa. Chief Hawkwind must organize the defense of the mesa and send initiates to thin the quillboar ranks.",al={"narache threats","who is attacking camp narache","quilboar camp narache"},it="threat"},{q="Where does Chief Hawkwind send adventurers next?",a="Once a young brave completes the preliminary rites on the mesa, Chief Hawkwind directs them down into the sprawling valleys of Mulgore, sending them toward Bloodhoof Village to continue their trials under the guidance of Cairne Bloodhoof's elders.",al={"where do i go after hawkwind","after camp narache","leaving red cloud mesa"},it="next_step"},{q="Is Chief Hawkwind related to Greatmother Hawkwind?",a="Yes, Greatmother Hawkwind is his mother. The Hawkwind family has long held deep roots and leadership roles among the tauren dwelling on Red Cloud Mesa.",al={"greatmother hawkwind relation","hawkwind family","who is greatmother hawkwind"},it="ties"},{q="Whose side is Chief Hawkwind on?",a="Chief Hawkwind stands loyally with his fellow tauren and the Horde. As a tribal leader stationed in the Red Cloud Mesa, he works directly to guide and strengthen the newest generation of tauren defenders, preparing them to serve both their people and the wider Horde.",al={"chief hawkwind faction","hawkwind horde or alliance","who does chief hawkwind serve"},it="side",add=true},{q="What does Chief Hawkwind want?",a="Hawkwind seeks to protect Camp Narache and ensure young tauren are thoroughly tested before venturing out into Kalimdor. He oversees the sacred Rites of the Earthmother, testing both your humility and strength against the encroaching Bristleback quilboar so you may honorably serve your people.",al={"what is chief hawkwind goal","chief hawkwind purpose","what does hawkwind want"},it="wants",add=true},{q="Why is Chief Hawkwind at Camp Narache?",a="Chief Hawkwind resides at Camp Narache to tend to local tribal matters and guide young adventurers through their first trials. From this elevated mesa, he watches over the training grounds and organizes defense efforts against the aggressive Bristleback quilboar who constantly harass the camp.",al={"why is chief hawkwind here","hawkwind camp narache why","chief hawkwind location why"},it="why_here",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="As the first chieftain to guide you on your journey, Chief Hawkwind instills the foundational values of the Shu'halo: reverence for the Earthmother, loyalty to the tribe, and humility in strength.",["faction:Horde"]="Hawkwind's teachings ensure that newly raised tauren braves step forward not just as capable warriors and spellcasters, but as steadfast and honorable allies to the young Horde."},kw={"a humble task","bristleback","camp narache","chief hawkwind","mulgore","red cloud mesa","rite of strength","rites of the earthmother","tauren chieftain"},rel={"subzone:camp-narache","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren","quest:763","quest:752","quest:757","quest:781","npc:greatmother-hawkwind"},src={"Chief Hawkwind"}}
E["npc:doomsayer-wiserunner"] = {t="npc",n="Seer Wiserunner",z="mulgore",s="This respected tauren elder is known as Seer Wiserunner, serving his people faithfully in the rolling hills of Mulgore. From a cave in the hills northwest of Bloodhoof Village, he aids young tauren braves as they undertake the traditional rites of passage that lead toward spiritual adulthood.",h="This respected tauren elder is known as Seer Wiserunner, serving his people faithfully in the rolling hills...",sec={{t="A Guide on the Sacred Path",b="Seer Wiserunner stands among the gentle bluffs of Mulgore as an elder and mystic of the shu'halo. When young hunters and shamans complete their initial trials of strength and vision, they are sent to seek his counsel during their ancestral rites. He interprets visions of the Earth Mother and guides the youth on how to honor their forebears, offering the steady wisdom that binds the scattered nomadic tribes into a unified community.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why is this NPC associated with the name 'Doomsayer Wiserunner'?",a="He is known as Seer Wiserunner, an honored tauren elder in good standing with the tribes of Mulgore. He has nothing to do with doomsaying; his role is guiding young initiates through sacred rites.",al={"doomsayer wiserunner name","why doomsayer","seer vs doomsayer"},it="identity"},{q="What role does he play in Mulgore?",a="Seer Wiserunner serves as a spiritual mentor for young tauren. After braves embark on their early vision quests, he receives them to complete the traditional Rite of Wisdom, testing their readiness to serve both tribe and Earth Mother.",al={"what does wiserunner do","seer wiserunner purpose","wiserunner role"},it="purpose"},{q="Where can Seer Wiserunner be found?",a="He can be found in a cave in the hills northwest of Bloodhoof Village, where the vision from the Rite of Vision leads tauren youth. From there he sends them east of Thunder Bluff to the ancestral spirits of Red Rocks.",al={"where is wiserunner","seer wiserunner location","wiserunner coords"},gp=true},{q="Whose side is Seer Wiserunner on?",a="He is loyal to the tauren tribes and the Horde. Stationed in Mulgore, Seer Wiserunner guides young tauren through their sacred ancestral trials, upholding the venerable spiritual traditions of his people and honoring the wisdom of the Earth Mother.",al={"seer wiserunner faction","wiserunner side","is seer wiserunner horde"},it="side",add=true},{q="What does Seer Wiserunner want?",a="He seeks to shepherd young tauren through their sacred spiritual milestones. By presiding over trials such as the Rite of Wisdom, he ensures that the next generation honors ancestral guidance and proves their worthiness before the spirits.",al={"seer wiserunner goal","what does wiserunner want","seer wiserunner motive"},it="wants",add=true},{q="Why is Seer Wiserunner here?",a="He resides in the rolling hills of Mulgore to serve as a spiritual mentor. As tauren youths undertake their sacred rites of passage, he awaits them to test their insight and bestow ancestral wisdom upon those who complete their pilgrimage.",al={"why is seer wiserunner in mulgore","seer wiserunner location why","why is wiserunner here"},it="why_here",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For young shu'halo venturing beyond Red Cloud Mesa, Seer Wiserunner is an essential mentor on the ancestral journey toward spiritual maturity.",["class:Shaman"]="Shaman will recognize Wiserunner as a keeper of ancient traditions who understands the messages woven through the earth and sky."},kw={"camp narache","doomsayer wiserunner","mulgore","rite of vision","rite of wisdom","seer wiserunner","tauren elder"},rel={"quest:773","quest:767","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren"},src={"Doomsayer Wiserunner"}}
E["npc:elder-bloodhoof"] = {t="npc",n="Elder Bloodhoof",z="mulgore",s="Elder Bloodhoof is an ancestral spirit found resting just north of Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore. Appearing prominently to travelers during the Lunar Festival, he stands as a revered guardian spirit of the Bloodhoof tribe.",h="Elder Bloodhoof is an ancestral spirit found resting just north of Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore.",sec={{t="Spirit of the Ancestors",b="Elder Bloodhoof represents the deep spiritual bond the tauren share with their lineage. Manifesting near Bloodhoof Village, this ghostly elder watches over his people and rewards respectful travelers who seek wisdom during the Lunar Festival. His presence reinforces the enduring guidance of deceased chieftains and tribal forebears, whose spirits remain intimately tied to the plains of Mulgore.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Elder Bloodhoof?",a="Elder Bloodhoof is a ghostly tauren spirit who appears just north of Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore. He represents the honored ancestors of the Bloodhoof tribe, offering blessings and ancient wisdom to travelers who visit him during seasonal traditions like the Lunar Festival.",al={"elder bloodhoof lore","who is the ghost outside bloodhoof village","elder bloodhoof identity"},it="identity"},{q="When can you see Elder Bloodhoof?",a="Elder Bloodhoof primarily manifests during the annual Lunar Festival, where he acts as an elder spirit offering festival blessings.",al={"where is elder bloodhoof","elder bloodhoof missing","how to find elder bloodhoof"},gp=true},{q="What is his connection to Bloodhoof Village?",a="As an honored ancestor bearing the clan's name, Elder Bloodhoof watches over the ancestral settlement of Bloodhoof Village. Tauren tradition places deep significance on ancestral spirits lingering close to where their descendants live and gather.",al={"why is he near bloodhoof village","elder bloodhoof relation to cairne"},it="ties"},{q="Whose side is Elder Bloodhoof on?",a="Elder Bloodhoof is a revered ancestor of the Bloodhoof tauren and allied with the Horde. As one of the ancestral spirits honored during the Lunar Festival, he offers blessings and wisdom to travelers honoring his memory and his people's sacred traditions.",al={"elder bloodhoof faction","is elder bloodhoof horde","who does elder bloodhoof serve"},it="side",add=true},{q="What does Elder Bloodhoof want?",a="Elder Bloodhoof wishes to be remembered and honored by the living. When travelers seek out the ancient spirits of Kalimdor during the Lunar Festival, he grants his blessings, ancestral coins, and words of wisdom in exchange for your respect and recognition of the past.",al={"elder bloodhoof goal","what does bloodhoof the elder want","why talk to elder bloodhoof"},it="wants",add=true},{q="Why is Elder Bloodhoof here in Mulgore?",a="His spirit lingers just north of Bloodhoof Village because these grassy plains are the ancestral home of his tribe. He watches over his descendants from beyond the veil of death, returning each year to the land where the Bloodhoof people live and herd.",al={"why is elder bloodhoof here","why is elder bloodhoof outside bloodhoof village","elder bloodhoof location reason"},it="why_here",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For tauren pilgrims, paying respects to Elder Bloodhoof is a direct communion with tribal lineage, honoring the sacrifice of those who walked Mulgore long before the founding of Thunder Bluff.",["class:Shaman"]="Shaman revere Elder Bloodhoof as a manifestation of ancestral reverence, demonstrating how ancestral spirits guide and protect their descendants in the physical realm."},kw={"ancestor","bloodhoof village","elder bloodhoof","lunar festival","mulgore","red rocks","tauren spirit"},rel={"subzone:bloodhoof-village","quest:8673","zone:mulgore","subzone:red-rocks","topic:tauren"},src={"Elder Bloodhoof"}}
E["npc:gart-mistrunner"] = {t="npc",n="Gart Mistrunner",z="mulgore",s="Gart Mistrunner is the resident druid trainer stationed at Camp Narache on the Red Cloud Mesa. He welcomes novice tauren called by the Earth Mother and guides them through their first rites of nature magic.",h="Gart Mistrunner is the resident druid trainer stationed at Camp Narache on the Red Cloud Mesa.",sec={{t="Mentor of the Red Cloud Mesa",b="Standing watch over Camp Narache, Gart Mistrunner represents the ancient druidic path rekindled among the Shu'halo. New tauren druids are directed straight to Gart upon taking their first steps on the Red Cloud Mesa, often carrying a verdant note commending them into his care. Through his patient guidance, young practitioners learn to commune with the natural world, drawing upon the restorative powers of nature and the lunar radiance of Mu'sha to heal their allies and strike down threats to the land.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Gart Mistrunner?",a="Gart Mistrunner is a tauren druid trainer located at Camp Narache in the Red Cloud Mesa of Mulgore. He instructs novice tauren druids in their initial spells and rites.",al={"who is gart","gart mistrunner lore","gart mistrunner"},it="identity"},{q="What spells does he teach newly awakened druids?",a="Gart introduces beginners to essential nature abilities, teaching early healing spells such as Healing Touch and offensive nature magic like Moonfire.",al={"what does gart teach","gart mistrunner training","druid spells camp narache"},gp=true},{q="What is his connection to the Mistrunner clan?",a="Mistrunner is simply Gart's family name. No lore describes a Mistrunner tribe or says more about his kin; he is known only as the druid trainer of Camp Narache.",al={"mistrunner clan","who are mistrunners"},it="ties"},{q="Whose side is Gart Mistrunner on?",a="Gart Mistrunner is devoted to the tauren tribes and stands with the Horde. Stationed in the heart of Red Cloud Mesa at Camp Narache, he works within his ancestral homeland to safeguard tauren traditions, nurturing young practitioners who use nature's gifts to protect the land and serve their people.",al={"gart mistrunner faction","who does gart serve","gart mistrunner horde"},it="side",add=true},{q="Why is Gart Mistrunner here?",a="Gart resides at Camp Narache to instruct novice tauren who hear the call of the wild. As a druid trainer on the Red Cloud Mesa, he introduces newly awakened initiates to the teachings of nature, guiding their early spiritual and martial growth before they venture out into the wider plains of Mulgore.",al={"why is gart in camp narache","gart mistrunner purpose","what is gart mistrunner doing here"},it="why_here",add=true},{q="What does Gart Mistrunner want?",a="Gart seeks to foster harmony between the tauren and the Earth Mother by training new druids in natural balance. He desires to see young initiates master early rites such as rejuvenating healing and balance incantations, preparing them to heal the land and defend their tribe against encroaching threats.",al={"gart mistrunner goals","what does gart want","gart mistrunner motives"},it="wants",add=true}},ang={["class:Druid"]="For tauren druids, Gart Mistrunner is the very first master to shape their communion with the wilds and the gifts of the Earth Mother.",["race:Tauren"]="Gart represents the resurgence of the Shu'halo's ancient druidic traditions, passing ancestral wisdom to the youth of Camp Narache."},kw={"camp narache","druid trainer","gart mistrunner","mistrunner","mulgore","red cloud mesa","tauren druid"},rel={"subzone:camp-narache","zone:mulgore","topic:druid","topic:tauren","quest:3094"},src={"Gart Mistrunner"}}
E["npc:greatmother-hawkwind"] = {t="npc",n="Greatmother Hawkwind",z="mulgore",s="Greatmother Hawkwind is a revered tauren elder resting by the water well on Red Cloud Mesa. As the mother of Camp Narache's leader, Chief Hawkwind, she embodies the enduring spirit and communal responsibility of the tribe despite her advanced age.",h="Greatmother Hawkwind is a revered tauren elder resting by the water well on Red Cloud Mesa.",sec={{t="Elder of the Mesa",b="Greatmother Hawkwind spends her days beside the fresh water well at Camp Narache, serving as the spiritual matriarch for young tauren beginning their rites of passage. Though years have slowed her stride, she refuses to let age sideline her duty to the village. She believes that every member of the tribe, young or old, has an active role to play in sustaining the community, often asking fledgling youths to lend a hand with simple domestic chores like fetching fresh water.",sp=0},{t="A Mother's Guidance",b="As the mother of Chief Hawkwind, the chieftain of Camp Narache, Greatmother Hawkwind provides steady counsel and quiet dignity amidst the threats facing Red Cloud Mesa. While her son organizes hunters and warriors against encroaching Bristleback quilboar and local predators, she tends to the moral heart of the settlement, reminding the newly awakened that strength begins with humility and service to one's kin.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Greatmother Hawkwind?",a="Greatmother Hawkwind is an elderly tauren matriarch residing in Camp Narache on Red Cloud Mesa. She is the mother of the camp's chieftain, Chief Hawkwind, and acts as an elder guide to young adventurers.",al={"greatmother hawkwind lore","who is greatmother hawkwind","camp narache elder"},it="identity"},{q="Where can she be found?",a="She sits by the water well southeast of Camp Narache, on Red Cloud Mesa in southwestern Mulgore.",al={"greatmother hawkwind location","where is greatmother hawkwind"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What tasks does she ask of young tauren?",a="She charges young tauren with 'A Humble Task,' asking them to gather water from the village well and deliver it to others in the camp. The chore teaches that no task is beneath a true servant of the tribe.",al={"greatmother hawkwind quest","a humble task"},it="wants",gp=true},{q="What is her relation to Chief Hawkwind?",a="She is the mother of Chief Hawkwind, who oversees the defense and training of young braves at Camp Narache.",al={"chief hawkwind mother","is she related to chief hawkwind"},it="ties"},{q="Whose side is Greatmother Hawkwind on?",a="Greatmother Hawkwind is devoted to her people, the tauren of the Horde. As the elder matriarch of Camp Narache, her loyalty lies entirely with her tribe, her family, and the young braves setting out to serve the ancestors and the Earth Mother.",al={"greatmother hawkwind faction","who does greatmother hawkwind serve","greatmother hawkwind allegiance"},it="side",add=true},{q="What does Greatmother Hawkwind want?",a="Despite her advanced age, Greatmother Hawkwind insists on doing her part to care for Camp Narache. She tends to the settlement's needs, seeing to simple domestic duties such as hauling fresh water from the village well, and she asks young tauren to lend a helping hand with these humble everyday chores.",al={"what does greatmother hawkwind need","greatmother hawkwind goal","why talk to greatmother hawkwind"},it="wants",add=true},{q="Why is Greatmother Hawkwind here?",a="Greatmother Hawkwind resides in Camp Narache as the village elder and the mother of Chief Hawkwind. You will find her by the settlement's central water well, diligently working to support her tribe and watching over the young hunters and shamans beginning their journeys on Red Cloud Mesa.",al={"why is greatmother hawkwind in camp narache","greatmother hawkwind location why","why is she at the well"},it="why_here",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For young tauren starting their journey on Red Cloud Mesa, Greatmother Hawkwind embodies the core values of the Earth Mother: respect for elders, community solidarity, and humbleness in service."},kw={"a humble task","camp narache","chief hawkwind","greatmother hawkwind","mulgore","red cloud mesa","tauren elder","unaya"},rel={"npc:chief-hawkwind","subzone:camp-narache","quest:752","topic:tauren","zone:mulgore"},src={"Greatmother Hawkwind"}}
E["npc:grull-hawkwind"] = {t="npc",n="Grull Hawkwind",z="mulgore",s="Grull Hawkwind is a young tauren brave stationed at Camp Narache on the Red Cloud Mesa in Mulgore. As the son of Chief Hawkwind, he helps guide novice tauren taking their very first steps into the world. He directs young hunters and warriors through their earliest trials to test their resolve and honor their ancestors.",h="Grull Hawkwind is a young tauren brave stationed at Camp Narache on the Red Cloud Mesa in Mulgore.",sec={{t="A Chieftain's Son",b="Standing proudly beside the braves and elders of Camp Narache, Grull Hawkwind bears both the proud lineage of Chief Hawkwind and the responsibility of preparing the tribe's youth. He serves as an initial mentor for new arrivals on the Red Cloud Mesa, assessing their readiness to serve the Shu'halo. By assigning trials that test stamina, tracking, and survival against local beasts such as plainstriders and mountain cougars, Grull ensures that young tauren understand that balance and respect for nature are foundational to life in Mulgore.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Grull Hawkwind?",a="Grull Hawkwind is a tauren quest giver residing in Camp Narache on the Red Cloud Mesa. He is the son of Chief Hawkwind, the leader of the settlement, and acts as one of the primary mentors for young tauren beginning their journey.",al={"grull hawkwind role","who is grull","grull hawkwind lore"},it="identity"},{q="What role does Grull Hawkwind play for young adventurers?",a="Grull assesses the basic training of novice tauren, receiving introductory notes from tribal elders and tasking youth with their opening trials of hunting and survival in the surrounding plains.",al={"grull quests","what does grull do","grull hawkwind purpose"},it="purpose"},{q="Is Grull related to the camp's leadership?",a="Yes, Grull is the son of Chief Hawkwind, the chieftain of Camp Narache. He upholds his father's traditions and helps prepare the camp's defenders.",al={"chief hawkwind son","grull hawkwind family","is grull related to the chief"},it="ties"},{q="Whose side is Grull Hawkwind on?",a="Grull Hawkwind stands proudly with the tauren tribes and the Horde. Residing in Camp Narache, he serves his people under the leadership of his father and High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof, dedicated to helping young tauren grow into capable defenders of their ancestral homeland in Mulgore.",al={"grull hawkwind faction","who does grull serve","grull hawkwind allegiance"},it="side",add=true},{q="What does Grull Hawkwind want?",a="Grull wants to see young tauren prove their strength and devotion to the tribe. He tests incoming youths to ensure they possess the fortitude, discipline, and hunting skills necessary to survive the wilderness and protect Camp Narache from nearby threats.",al={"what does grull want","grull hawkwind goals","why does grull give quests"},it="wants",add=true},{q="Why is Grull Hawkwind here in Camp Narache?",a="Camp Narache is the ancestral testing ground for youth coming of age on the Red Cloud Mesa, and it is governed by Grull's father, Chief Hawkwind. Grull remains here to help guide new initiates through their opening rites and support his family's leadership over the camp.",al={"why is grull here","grull camp narache role","why is grull in the red cloud mesa"},it="why_here",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For young tauren stepping out onto Red Cloud Mesa, Grull Hawkwind is often the very first brave to test their mettle and introduce them to the traditions of the Shu'halo.",["class:Hunter"]="Grull provides novice hunters with fundamental early tasks, testing their patience and skill against the fauna of Mulgore."},kw={"camp narache","chief hawkwind","grull hawkwind","mulgore","red cloud mesa","rite of strength","tauren"},rel={"npc:chief-hawkwind","subzone:camp-narache","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren","quest:757","quest:747","quest:780"},src={"Grull Hawkwind"}}
E["npc:harb-clawhoof"] = {t="npc",n="Harb Clawhoof",z="mulgore",s="Harb Clawhoof is a tauren mount breeder stationed just outside Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore. He tends to the tribe's sturdy kodos, offering them to worthy tauren riders and trusted champions of Thunder Bluff.",h="Harb Clawhoof is a tauren mount breeder stationed just outside Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore.",sec={{t="Breeder of the Great Kodo",b="Situated near the pens of Bloodhoof Village, Harb Clawhoof spends his days tending the massive kodo beasts that serve as the backbone of tauren travel and labor. Built to carry immense weight across vast distances, these sturdy animals are essential to life on the plains. Harb ensures each beast is well-cared for, disciplined, and matched only with riders who command the respect of the tauren people.",sp=0},{t="Steeds of the Plains",b="While tauren naturally earn the trust required to ride Harb's kodos, outsiders face stricter scrutiny. Due to their immense size and temperament, kodos are not easily guided by those unfamiliar with their nature. Non-tauren allies who wish to ride away atop one of Harb's prized mounts must first prove their loyalty and earn exalted status with Thunder Bluff.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where can I find Harb Clawhoof?",a="Harb Clawhoof is stationed alongside the kodo corral on the outskirts of Bloodhoof Village in central Mulgore, just south of Thunder Bluff.",al={"harb clawhoof location","where is harb clawhoof","kodo vendor location"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What does Harb Clawhoof sell?",a="Harb sells various types of kodo mounts, including standard brown and gray kodos, as well as heavily armored great kodos for seasoned riders.",al={"what does harb sell","harb mounts","kodo vendor wares"},it="purpose",gp=true},{q="Can non-tauren buy kodos from Harb?",a="Yes, but only if they have earned exalted standing with Thunder Bluff. Tauren can purchase them directly, but other Horde races must prove themselves to the tribe before Harb will entrust them with a kodo.",al={"can orcs buy kodos","can trolls ride kodos","kodo reputation requirement"},gp=true},{q="Why are kodos so important to the tauren?",a="Kodos are powerful, resilient creatures perfectly suited for the nomadic lifestyle and open plains of central Kalimdor. They serve as reliable beasts of burden, war mounts, and trade transport across the rugged landscape.",al={"tauren kodo lore","why kodos"}},{q="Who is Harb Clawhoof?",a="Harb Clawhoof is a tauren kodo breeder and mount merchant stationed in Bloodhoof Village. Respected among his people for raising and tending to the mighty beasts of Mulgore, he ensures that capable champions have steadfast mounts suited for the rigors of travel and battle across Kalimdor.",al={"who is harb","harb clawhoof lore","who is harb clawhoof"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Whose side is Harb Clawhoof on?",a="Harb is fiercely loyal to Thunder Bluff and the Horde. While he gladly provides mounts to his fellow tauren, outsiders from other Horde races must prove their dedication and earn great standing with Thunder Bluff before he will trust them with one of his prized kodos.",al={"harb clawhoof faction","is harb clawhoof horde","harb clawhoof allegiance"},it="side",add=true},{q="What does Harb Clawhoof want?",a="Harb wants to raise strong, reliable kodos and see them entrusted only to riders who truly respect them. To the tauren, the kodo is not merely a beast of burden but the backbone of their civilization, and Harb works to preserve that sacred partnership.",al={"what does harb want","harb clawhoof goal","harb clawhoof purpose"},it="wants",add=true},{q="Why is Harb Clawhoof in Bloodhoof Village?",a="Bloodhoof Village is a central gathering place for young tauren and travelers journeying outward from Camp Narache into the wider plains of Mulgore. Harb maintains his stables here where there is ample open pasture to graze and rear strong kodo herds for the defense and prosperity of the tribes.",al={"why is harb in bloodhoof","why is harb here","harb bloodhoof village"},it="why_here",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Harb Clawhoof provides the quintessential mount of your heritage, providing both practical transit across Mulgore and a symbol of your nomadic traditions.",["faction:Horde"]="For orcs, trolls, and Forsaken, earning a kodo from Harb is the ultimate mark of honor and friendship recognized by the elders of Thunder Bluff."},kw={"bloodhoof village","harb clawhoof","kodo trainer","kodo vendor","mount vendor","mulgore kodo","thunder bluff mounts"},rel={"subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff","topic:tauren","topic:horde"},src={"Harb Clawhoof"}}
E["npc:harken-windtotem"] = {t="npc",n="Harken Windtotem",z="mulgore",s="Harken Windtotem is a tauren huntsman residing in Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore. Dedicated to the traditions of his people, he tasks young hunters and travelers with proving their tracking and foraging skills across the golden plains.",h="Harken Windtotem is a tauren huntsman residing in Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore.",sec={{t="Huntsman of Bloodhoof",b="Situated near the heart of Bloodhoof Village, Harken Windtotem serves as an instructor and guide for those learning the hunting ways of the tauren. Life on the plains of Mulgore demands respect for the natural world alongside the practical skill to sustain one's tribe. Harken evaluates young adventurers by directing them to hunt the native swoops that circle the mesas, collecting their quills to gauge a hunter's patience, aim, and ability to harvest without waste.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Harken Windtotem?",a="Harken is a tauren hunter living in Bloodhoof Village in central Mulgore. He guides travelers and fledgling adventurers in learning the fundamental tracking and hunting traditions of the tauren.",al={"harken windtotem lore","who is harken"},it="identity"},{q="Where can Harken Windtotem be found?",a="Harken is stationed in Bloodhoof Village, a settlement situated south of Thunder Bluff in central Mulgore.",al={"harken location","where is harken windtotem"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What tasks does Harken give to adventurers?",a="He offers the task Swoop Hunting, asking hunters to venture into the grasslands of Mulgore to bring down wild swoops and gather their sturdy flight quills.",al={"harken windtotem quests","swoop hunting quest"},it="purpose"},{q="Is Harken related to Bovan Windtotem?",a="They share the Windtotem clan name, suggesting a tribal kinship or family connection, though Harken remains focused on local village hunts in Mulgore.",al={"is harken related to bovan","bovan windtotem relation"},it="ties"},{q="Whose side is Harken Windtotem on?",a="Harken Windtotem stands loyally with the tauren tribes and the Horde. Based in Bloodhoof Village, his focus lies entirely on supporting the local settlement and guiding young hunters as they work to sustain their people in Mulgore.",al={"harken windtotem faction","who does harken serve","is harken horde"},it="side",add=true},{q="What does Harken Windtotem want?",a="Harken seeks capable hunters to thin the local swoop population and gather their quills. The plains of Mulgore demand self-reliance, and by putting fledglings to the test against swoops, he helps provide useful materials while honing the skills of young travelers.",al={"what does harken want","harken windtotem goal","why does harken give quests"},it="wants",add=true},{q="Why is Harken Windtotem in Bloodhoof Village?",a="As a member of the Windtotem tribe residing in Bloodhoof Village, Harken oversees hunting efforts across the central plains. He remains in the village to direct passing hunters, ensuring that the wildlife remains balanced and the settlement has the resources it needs.",al={"why is harken here","harken windtotem purpose","why is harken in bloodhoof"},it="why_here",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Harken Windtotem embodies the ancestral tauren philosophy of hunting: proving discipline and self-sufficiency on the plains while honoring the beasts of the Earth Mother.",["class:Hunter"]="For wilderness trackers passing through Bloodhoof Village, Harken's task of bringing down airborne swoops provides an essential early test of marksmanship and quarry collection."},kw={"bloodhoof village","harken windtotem","mulgore","swoop hunting","tauren","windtotem"},rel={"subzone:bloodhoof-village","quest:761","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren"},src={"Harken Windtotem"}}
E["npc:holt-thunderhorn"] = {t="npc",n="Holt Thunderhorn",z="mulgore",s="Holt Thunderhorn is a tauren hunter trainer stationed upon Hunter Rise in the elevated city of Thunder Bluff. He instructs novice and seasoned track-masters in their craft, offering basic martial guidance and pointing maturing marksmen toward specialized trials across Mulgore and beyond.",h="Holt Thunderhorn is a tauren hunter trainer stationed upon Hunter Rise in the elevated city of Thunder Bluff.",sec={{t="Master of the Rise",b="Perched on Hunter Rise above the sweeping plains of Mulgore, Holt Thunderhorn serves the Horde as a seasoned instructor in ranged combat, tracking, and survival. Like many elders of his craft, he guides young tauren as they make the transition from roaming hunters into disciplined defenders of their new capital.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where can Holt Thunderhorn be found?",a="Holt Thunderhorn stands on Hunter Rise, the southeastern elevated plateau within the tauren capital of Thunder Bluff in Mulgore.",al={"holt thunderhorn location","where is holt","hunter trainer thunder bluff"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What does Holt Thunderhorn teach?",a="He is a hunter trainer who instructs players in essential marksmanship, wilderness survival, and beast-taming techniques, as well as directing novices to specialized beast mastery trials.",al={"what does holt teach","holt thunderhorn trainer","what abilities holt"},it="purpose",gp=true},{q="What role do hunters hold in Thunder Bluff?",a="Hunters form the traditional backbone of tauren society, providing sustenance, patrolling territorial borders against centaur raiding parties, and honoring the Earth Mother through balanced, reverent tracking.",al={"hunter rise purpose","tauren hunter lore"}},{q="Who is Holt Thunderhorn?",a="Holt Thunderhorn is a respected tauren hunter trainer stationed on Hunter Rise in Thunder Bluff. Dedicated to passing on the wilderness arts of the shu'halo, he instructs young hunters in marksmanship, survival, and the ancient bond between hunter and beast.",al={"who is holt","holt thunderhorn lore","who is holt thunderhorn"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Whose side is Holt Thunderhorn on?",a="Holt serves the tauren tribes of Thunder Bluff and the greater Horde under the leadership of Cairne Bloodhoof. He aids any hunter loyal to the Horde who seeks to sharpen their instincts and honor the plains.",al={"holt thunderhorn faction","who does holt serve","is holt horde"},it="side",add=true},{q="Why is Holt Thunderhorn here?",a="Holt remains atop Hunter Rise to mentor new generations of trackers, marksmen, and beast masters. With the tauren now settled in Mulgore, skilled hunters are essential to sustain the city and protect the mesas from hostile wildlife and invaders.",al={"why is holt in thunder bluff","what is holt doing here","holt thunderhorn purpose"},it="why_here",add=true}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="Holt Thunderhorn serves as a central mentor in Thunder Bluff, offering weapon techniques, tracking training, and referrals for taming beasts.",["race:Tauren"]="Stationed on Hunter Rise, Holt embodies the traditional tauren hunting culture that fed and protected the nomadic tribes long before they established a permanent home atop the bluffs."},kw={"holt thunderhorn","hunter rise","hunter trainer","mulgore","thunder bluff"},rel={"subzone:hunter-rise","zone:thunderbluff","quest:6081","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren"},src={"Holt Thunderhorn"}}
E["npc:lanka-farshot"] = {t="npc",n="Lanka Farshot",z="mulgore",s="Lanka Farshot is a tauren hunter trainer stationed at Camp Narache within the Red Cloud Mesa. He guides novice tauren hunters as they take their first steps in tracking, marksmanship, and surviving the plains of Mulgore.",h="Lanka Farshot is a tauren hunter trainer stationed at Camp Narache within the Red Cloud Mesa.",sec={{t="Mentor of the Red Cloud Mesa",b="Standing watch over Camp Narache, Lanka Farshot greets new tauren called to the path of the hunter. Fresh recruits arrive carrying an etched note directing them to seek his counsel. Under his steady eye, apprentices learn the fundamentals of ranged combat, tracking prey, and respecting the wild beasts that roam the grassy plains of Mulgore. His teachings ensure that every young hunter serves as a capable scout and provider for the tribe.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Lanka Farshot?",a="Lanka Farshot is the tauren hunter trainer stationed at Camp Narache on the Red Cloud Mesa in Mulgore. He provides initial training and guidance to young hunters beginning their journey.",al={"who is lanka","lanka farshot npc","camp narache hunter"},it="identity"},{q="What quests does Lanka Farshot offer?",a="Lanka receives young tauren hunters sent to him via an etched note upon their arrival in Camp Narache and directs them onto the hunter's path as they learn the basics of their craft.",al={"lanka farshot quests","hunter quests camp narache"},gp=true},{q="Is Lanka Farshot related to Mixie Farshot?",a="Despite sharing the same surname, there is no evidence that Lanka Farshot is related to the goblin Mixie Farshot.",al={"is lanka related to mixie","mixie farshot relation"},it="ties"},{q="Whose side is Lanka Farshot on?",a="Lanka Farshot is loyal to the tauren tribes and the Horde. Stationed within the safe haven of Camp Narache, he serves his people by guiding young tauren hunters as they learn to survive and contribute to the united tribes under High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof.",al={"lanka farshot faction","who does lanka farshot serve","is lanka horde"},it="side",add=true},{q="Why is Lanka Farshot here at Camp Narache?",a="Lanka resides in Camp Narache to instruct novice hunters beginning their trials on the Red Cloud Mesa. The plains of Mulgore demand keen senses and steady aim, and Lanka stands ready to pass down the essential arts of tracking and marksmanship to the next generation.",al={"why is lanka here","lanka farshot location purpose","what brings lanka to camp narache"},it="why_here",add=true},{q="What does Lanka Farshot want?",a="Lanka seeks to ensure that every aspiring tauren hunter masters the ways of the wild. He wants you to respect your quarry, hone your marksmanship, and carry on the proud traditions necessary to feed and defend your people across the grasslands.",al={"lanka farshot goal","what is lanka trying to do","lanka farshot purpose"},it="wants",add=true}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="For tauren hunters beginning their training in Mulgore, Lanka Farshot is the very first mentor they encounter, teaching fundamental marksmanship and wild survival.",["race:Tauren"]="Lanka embodies the traditional tauren hunting philosophy, viewing marksmanship and tracking as vital duties for protecting the tribe and living in balance with the plains."},kw={"camp narache","hunter trainer","lanka farshot","mulgore","red cloud mesa","tauren hunter"},rel={"subzone:camp-narache","zone:mulgore","quest:3092","quest:6065","topic:tauren","topic:horde"},src={"Lanka Farshot"}}
E["npc:lorekeeper-raintotem"] = {t="npc",n="Lorekeeper Raintotem",z="mulgore",s="Lorekeeper Raintotem is a tauren elder stationed in northern Mulgore just south of Red Rocks. He tends to the sacred burial grounds and asks adventurers to drive off the Bristleback quilboar disturbing them.",h="Lorekeeper Raintotem is a tauren elder stationed in northern Mulgore just south of Red Rocks.",sec={{t="Watcher of Red Rocks",b="Positioned near the sacred bluffs of Red Rocks in Mulgore, Lorekeeper Raintotem tends to the spiritual obligations of his people. He asks adventurers to drive Bristleback Interlopers from the burial ground, restoring peace to the ancestors' resting place.",sp=0},{t="Defender of Ancestral Grounds",b="Beyond testing fledgling heroes, Lorekeeper Raintotem watches over the hallowed burial sites at Red Rocks. When Bristleback quilboar interlopers disturb the sanctified resting places of deceased tauren chieftains and shamans, Raintotem enlists travelers to restore dignity and quiet the disturbed dead.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where can I find Lorekeeper Raintotem?",a="Lorekeeper Raintotem is stationed in northern Mulgore, located just south of Red Rocks along the path leading up toward the ancestral cliffs.",al={"raintotem location","where is lorekeeper raintotem","find raintotem mulgore"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why does Raintotem seek help with the burial grounds?",a="He asks adventurers to drive Bristleback Interlopers from Red Rocks, where the quilboar are ravaging the sacred tauren burial ground.",al={"raintotem sacred burial","why help raintotem","red rocks burial grounds"},it="purpose"},{q="Why does Raintotem need help at Red Rocks?",a="Red Rocks serves as a sacred tauren burial ground, but Bristleback quilboar interlopers are ravaging the grave site. Raintotem tasks capable adventurers with putting the dishonored ancestral grounds back in order.",al={"red rocks problem","why sacred burial quest"},it="threat"},{q="Who is Lorekeeper Raintotem?",a="Lorekeeper Raintotem serves his tauren community, preserving ancestral traditions and ensuring the fallen receive sacred burial.",al={"who is raintotem","lorekeeper raintotem info","about raintotem"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Whose side is Lorekeeper Raintotem on?",a="Lorekeeper Raintotem is wholly loyal to the tauren tribes of Mulgore and the Horde under High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof. He serves as an honored cultural elder, assisting any Horde adventurer who shows respect to the sacred rites and ancestors of his people.",al={"raintotem faction","is raintotem horde","raintotem allegiance"},it="side",add=true},{q="Why is Lorekeeper Raintotem here?",a="Raintotem keeps watch south of Red Rocks because it is the sacred ancestral burial grounds of the tauren tribes. He remains stationed nearby to protect the hallowed soil, guide pilgrims seeking ancestral blessing, and confront threats that disturb the spirits resting within the red stone cliffs.",al={"why is raintotem at red rocks","raintotem location reason","why is lorekeeper raintotem in mulgore"},it="why_here",add=true},{q="What does Lorekeeper Raintotem want?",a="He tends to the ancestral burial grounds at Red Rocks and asks adventurers to drive away the Bristleback Interlopers disturbing them.",al={"what does raintotem want","raintotem goals","what is raintotem asking for"},it="wants",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Helping Raintotem defend Red Rocks honors the ancestors and preserves the burial traditions of your people.",["class:Shaman"]="Raintotem's vigilance over restless dead at Red Rocks directly concerns shamanic duty, ensuring ancestral spirits receive proper peace and reverence."},kw={"lorekeeper raintotem","mulgore","raintotem","red rocks","sacred burial"},rel={"subzone:red-rocks","quest:833","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren"},src={"Lorekeeper Raintotem"}}
E["npc:minor-manifestation-of-earth"] = {t="npc",n="Minor Manifestation of Earth",z="mulgore",s="A Minor Manifestation of Earth is a localized elemental spirit bound to sacred stones in Mulgore and Durotar. Invisible to ordinary sight, it reveals itself only to fledgling shamans who drink the sacred Earth Sapta, providing the rough quartz required to carve their very first earth totem.",h="A Minor Manifestation of Earth is a localized elemental spirit bound to sacred stones in Mulgore and Durotar.",sec={{t="Voice of the Stone",b="The spirits of the earth dwell everywhere beneath the dirt and stone, but communicating with them requires deliberate ritual. At sacred landmarks like Kodo Rock in Mulgore and Spirit Rock in Durotar, Minor Manifestations of Earth linger in the unseen realm, surrounded by even smaller elemental presences. Only initiates who have drunk the Earth Sapta—a ritual draught brewed by shamanic mentors—can lift the veil between the physical world and the elements. Once acknowledged, the spirit imparts the earth's blessings along with a piece of rough quartz, granting the novice the spiritual focal point needed to fashion an Earth Totem.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why can't I see the Minor Manifestation of Earth?",a="The manifestation exists on the elemental plane and remains invisible to the naked eye. You must drink an Earth Sapta, provided by your shaman trainer, to enter the altered state of perception required to perceive and speak with elemental spirits.",al={"cannot see manifestation","where is the earth spirit","earth manifestation invisible"},gp=true},{q="Where can these earth spirits be found?",a="They appear at Kodo Rock in the rolling plains of Mulgore and at Spirit Rock in the arid canyons of Durotar. Both sites serve as traditional proving grounds for young tauren, orc, and troll shamans.",al={"minor manifestation location","where to find manifestation of earth"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What is the spirit's purpose during the rite?",a="The manifestation tests and formally acknowledges the initiate's connection to the earth element. Upon speaking with the shaman, it bestows a piece of rough quartz, which is taken back to a shaman trainer to construct the Earth Totem.",al={"what does manifestation do","manifestation quest purpose"},it="purpose"},{q="What are the smaller creatures surrounding it?",a="Surrounding the central spirit are lesser manifestations of earth. They are minor echos of the element drawn to the sacred grounds, existing purely as part of the natural communion, and cannot be directly interacted with.",al={"lesser manifestations","small earth elementals around it"}},{q="Who is the Minor Manifestation of Earth?",a="The Minor Manifestation of Earth is an elemental earth spirit summoned and revered at sacred grounds like Kodo Rock in Mulgore and Spirit Rock in Durotar. Invisible to ordinary sight, it reveals itself only to shamans who have expanded their spiritual awareness through ceremonial draughts such as Earth Sapta.",al={"what is the minor manifestation of earth","who is manifestation of earth","minor manifestation earth lore"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Whose side is the Minor Manifestation of Earth on?",a="It serves neither the Horde nor the Alliance. As an embodiment of the elements, it aligns with the balance of the natural world and answers the petitions of initiates who demonstrate the respect and spiritual clarity required to commune with the earth.",al={"minor manifestation earth faction","is minor manifestation of earth friendly","which side is the earth manifestation"},it="side",add=true},{q="What does the Minor Manifestation of Earth want?",a="It desires communion with those walking the path of the shaman. When approached by an initiate who has consumed the Earth Sapta, it seeks to test their resolve and bestow a piece of Rough Quartz to form the bedrock of their first totem.",al={"what does manifestation of earth want","earth manifestation desire","what does the minor manifestation seek"},it="wants",add=true},{q="Why is the Minor Manifestation of Earth here?",a="It resides at sacred places of elemental power, such as Kodo Rock in Mulgore and Spirit Rock in Durotar, to serve as a bridge between the living land and prospective shamans undergoing the Call of Earth.",al={"why is the minor manifestation of earth at kodo rock","why is manifestation of earth here","why is the earth spirit here"},it="why_here",add=true}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="Every young Horde shaman must commune with a Minor Manifestation of Earth under the influence of the Earth Sapta to earn their very first totem.",["race:Tauren"]="Tauren youth travel east across Mulgore to Kodo Rock to seek communion with this manifestation as part of their ancestral spiritual trials."},kw={"call of earth","earth sapta","earth totem","kodo rock","minor manifestation of earth","rough quartz","spirit rock"},rel={"quest:1520","subzone:kodo-rock","subzone:spirit-rock","zone:mulgore","zone:durotar"},src={"Minor Manifestation of Earth"}}
E["npc:morin-cloudstalker"] = {t="npc",n="Morin Cloudstalker",z="mulgore",s="Morin Cloudstalker is a tauren scout keeping watch along the road leading east from Bloodhoof Village toward the Barrens in Mulgore. Standing vigil over the plains, he monitors local trade routes and investigates the encroaching threats endangering tauren travelers.",h="Morin Cloudstalker is a tauren scout keeping watch along the road leading east from Bloodhoof Village toward...",sec={{t="Watcher on the Southern Trail",b="Positioned beside the dirt path winding east toward the Barrens, Morin Cloudstalker acts as both a scout and a guardian for travelers through Mulgore. The grassy plains here are usually peaceful, but the opening of trade routes and shifting territories have drawn opportunists and bandits dangerously close to the heart of the tauren homeland.",sp=0},{t="Investigating the Caravan",b="Morin's scouts recently attacked and burned a Venture Co. caravan on the northern shore of Stonebull Lake. When goblin salvagers were spotted near the burned wagons, he sent adventurers to examine the surviving supply crates and learn what the Venture Co. was hauling.",sp=0},{t="Defending Against Venture Co.",b="Beyond common brigands, Morin is deeply concerned by the industrial presence of the Venture Company establishing operations within Mulgore. He directs passing Horde adventurers to strike at their logging and mining outposts, targeting key leaders like Supervisor Fizsprocket to curtail the environmental devastation threatening the sacred plains.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Where can I find Morin Cloudstalker?",a="Morin Cloudstalker stands by the dirt trail east of Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore, along the road leading out toward the Barrens.",al={"morin location","where is morin","morin cloudstalker map"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What is Morin's role in Mulgore?",a="He is a watchful tauren scout responsible for monitoring travel through southern Mulgore and organizing defense against bandits and corporate intruders.",al={"who is morin cloudstalker","morin role"},it="purpose"},{q="Why is Morin investigating the ruined caravan?",a="Morin's scouts attacked and burned a Venture Co. caravan near Stonebull Lake. When goblin salvagers were seen around the wreckage, he asked adventurers to examine the surviving supply crates to learn what the Venture Co. was moving.",al={"ravaged caravan quest","caravan attack mulgore"},it="motive"},{q="Why does Morin want the Venture Company stopped?",a="The Venture Company is stripping Mulgore's natural resources with machinery and deforestation. Morin seeks to disrupt their destructive enterprise by eliminating their overseers, including Supervisor Fizsprocket.",al={"venture co mulgore","why kill fizsprocket"},it="threat"},{q="Who does Morin report to?",a="Morin operates on behalf of the elders and chieftains of Bloodhoof Village and the broader tauren leadership across Mulgore.",al={"morin allegiance","who sent morin"},it="ties"},{q="Does Morin offer quests to Alliance players?",a="No. Morin is an NPC loyal strictly to the Horde and will only interact with Horde adventurers.",al={"can alliance talk to morin","morin faction"},gp=true},{q="Who is Morin Cloudstalker?",a="Morin Cloudstalker is a tauren scout and protector watching over Mulgore. Positioned along the road leading east from Bloodhoof Village toward the Barrens, he keeps a sharp eye on threats creeping into ancestral tauren lands, dispatching capable Horde adventurers to safeguard the plains from raiders and exploiters.",al={"morin cloudstalker who is he","who is morin","morin lore"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Whose side is Morin Cloudstalker on?",a="Morin is fiercely loyal to Thunder Bluff and the Horde. He guards the heartland of the tauren people and only offers his guidance and tasks to fellow Horde travelers willing to defend Mulgore's fragile peace.",al={"morin cloudstalker faction","morin alliance or horde","is morin horde"},it="side",add=true},{q="What does Morin Cloudstalker want?",a="Morin seeks to protect the roads and natural resources of Mulgore. He sends adventurers to search the Venture Co. caravan his scouts burned and to stop the destructive operations of the Venture Company, particularly demanding an end to Supervisor Fizsprocket's exploitative mining in the hills.",al={"what does morin want","morin goals","morin tasks"},it="wants",add=true},{q="Why is Morin Cloudstalker stationed here?",a="Morin is stationed along the road east of Bloodhoof Village to watch over travel through Mulgore. This critical junction allows him to monitor traveling caravans and intercept encroaching dangers—such as Venture Company defilers—before they reach Bloodhoof Village.",al={"why is morin here","why is morin on the road","morin location reason"},it="why_here",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For tauren journeying outward from Bloodhoof Village, Morin represents the vigilant defense of your homeland against both opportunistic bandits and corporate defilers.",["faction:Horde"]="Morin offers Horde champions an early opportunity to protect the supplies and sacred lands of their new tauren allies."},kw={"bloodhoof village","great gate","morin cloudstalker","mulgore","ravaged caravan","supervisor fizsprocket","venture co"},rel={"quest:749","quest:764","quest:765","subzone:bloodhoof-village","subzone:ravaged-caravan","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren"},src={"Morin Cloudstalker"}}
E["npc:mull-thunderhorn"] = {t="npc",n="Mull Thunderhorn",z="mulgore",s="Mull Thunderhorn is a tauren elder residing in Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore. Deeply devoted to the Earth Mother and the balance of the land, he watches over the sacred water wells scattered across the plains. As outside exploiters threaten the region's lifegiving waters, Mull guides young tauren through the rituals required to cleanse the ancestral springs.",h="Mull Thunderhorn is a tauren elder residing in Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore.",sec={{t="Guardian of the Sacred Waters",b="Standing watch in Bloodhoof Village, Mull Thunderhorn carries the weight of Mulgore's spiritual well-being. The nomadic tribes have gathered under High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof to establish a permanent home, yet their lands are already under siege by greed and reckless expansion. Mull serves as a spiritual anchor for the young braves stepping out of Camp Narache, calling upon them to safeguard the vital resources of their ancestors.",sp=0},{t="Tainted Wells and the Venture Company",b="The primary crisis facing Mull is the sudden sickness spreading through Mulgore's water sources: the Winterhoof, Thunderhorn, and Wildmane water wells. This foul affliction is traced to the goblins of the Venture Company, an unscrupulous commercial outfit carving operations into the surrounding hills and dumping poisons into the local water table. Mull directs young tauren to prepare specific purification totems and perform cleansing rites at each ancestral well before the corruption spreads irrevocably into the land and its wildlife.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Mull Thunderhorn?",a="Mull Thunderhorn is a tauren quest giver situated in Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore. He acts as a guardian of the sacred water wells in the area, offering rites and rituals to keep the earth and its waters pure from external contamination.",al={"mull thunderhorn lore","who is mull","bloodhoof village mull"},it="identity"},{q="Why is Mull Thunderhorn worried about the water wells?",a="The ancestral water wells—such as the Winterhoof, Thunderhorn, and Wildmane wells—are life-giving sources for the tribes and wildlife of Mulgore. Intrusive operations nearby have tainted these sacred springs with noxious runoff and pollution, threatening the natural balance of the plains.",al={"why are wells poisoned","water wells mulgore","winterhoof well poisoned"},it="why_here"},{q="Who is polluting the water wells in Mulgore?",a="The pollution is caused by the Venture Company, a reckless goblin enterprise establishing industrial mining and logging footholds across the edges of Mulgore. Their operations show no regard for the sacred landscape, spilling industrial toxins into the local water table.",al={"who poisoned the water","venture co in mulgore","who tainted the wells"},it="threat"},{q="Why do Mull's purification quests only involve tauren?",a="The water wells hold deep ancestral and spiritual significance tied to the founding lineages of the tauren tribes—including the Thunderhorn, Winterhoof, and Wildmane clans. Cleansing them is viewed as a sacred rite of passage and cultural duty for maturing tauren.",al={"tauren only quests","why only tauren","mull thunderhorn requirements"},it="why_me"},{q="How are the water wells purified?",a="Mull instructs travelers to assemble specially consecrated totems imbued with local natural reagents, such as claws and hides. Once consecrated, these totems are brought directly to the fouled wells to channel spiritual purification and wash away the unnatural toxins.",al={"how to cleanse wells","cleansing totems","cleaning the water"},it="purpose"},{q="What is the Rite of Vision?",a="The Rite of Vision is a foundational spiritual journey undertaken by young tauren to commune with their ancestors and animal spirits. Initiates are sent to Zarlman Two-Moons in Bloodhoof Village to undertake this visionary trial as one of the Rites of the Earthmother.",al={"rite of vision quest","what is rite of vision"}},{q="Whose side is Mull Thunderhorn on?",a="Mull stands loyally with his people and the Horde, devoted to the tribes dwelling in Mulgore. As a respected member of Bloodhoof Village, he watches over the ancestral plains and answers the spiritual call to protect his kin and their sacred lands from outside corruption.",al={"mull thunderhorn faction","is mull horde","who does mull serve"},it="side",add=true},{q="What does Mull Thunderhorn want?",a="Mull wants to restore and cleanse Mulgore's sacred water wells, including the Winterhoof, Thunderhorn, and Wildmane sources. He seeks trustworthy tauren to craft traditional totems and perform the vital purification rites needed to undo the foul contamination threatening the land's lifeblood.",al={"what does mull want","mull thunderhorn goal","why does mull need help"},it="wants",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For the tauren, Mull Thunderhorn represents tribal memory and ancestral duty, guiding young braves in protecting the sacred plains against greedy foreign invaders.",["class:Shaman"]="Shaman will recognize Mull's reliance on elemental harmony, as his cleansing totems utilize ancestral spirits to mend waters torn by mechanical greed.",["class:Druid"]="Druids share Mull's urgency, witnessing firsthand how the corrupted water wells poison the balance of Mulgore's wildlife.",["faction:Horde"]="Mull's struggle underscores the Horde's friction with unaligned, exploitative organizations like the Venture Company that strip Kalimdor's riches without consent."},kw={"bloodhoof village","mulgore","mull thunderhorn","poison water","thunderhorn totem","venture company","water wells","wildmane cleansing","winterhoof cleansing"},rel={"zone:mulgore","subzone:bloodhoof-village","quest:748","quest:754","quest:756","quest:758","topic:venture-company","topic:tauren"},src={"Mull Thunderhorn"}}
E["npc:ruul-eagletalon"] = {t="npc",n="Ruul Eagletalon",z="mulgore",s="Ruul Eagletalon is a tauren resident of Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore. Stationed near the settlement's perimeter, he keeps a watchful eye on the local wildlife, particularly the aggressive Windfury harpies encroaching upon tauren lands.",h="Ruul Eagletalon is a tauren resident of Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore.",sec={{t="Watcher of Bloodhoof",b="Ruul Eagletalon stands guard in Bloodhoof Village, tending to the security of the settlement as young braves arrive from Camp Narache. While Mulgore is a peaceful homeland compared to the arid wastes of the Barrens, territorial predators and hostile creatures continually press against the pastures. Ruul concerns himself with threats roaming the nearby plains, notably directing capable travelers to confront the savage Windfury harpies who prey on unwary nomads and game.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Ruul Eagletalon?",a="Ruul Eagletalon is a tauren stationed in Bloodhoof Village who helps protect the settlement by directing adventurers against local threats across Mulgore.",al={"who is ruul","ruul eagletalon npc","bloodhoof ruul"},it="identity"},{q="What threat does Ruul Eagletalon ask travelers to deal with?",a="Ruul warns of the hostile Windfury harpies nesting within Mulgore and tasks capable braves with culling their numbers before they bring harm to the village.",al={"ruul quest","dangers of the windfury","harpies mulgore quest"},it="wants"},{q="Where can Ruul Eagletalon be found?",a="He can be found standing along the grounds of Bloodhoof Village, situated by the shores of Stonebull Lake in central Mulgore.",al={"ruul eagletalon location","find ruul","where is ruul"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Whose side is Ruul Eagletalon on?",a="Ruul Eagletalon stands firmly with the Bloodhoof tauren and the Horde. Stationed in Bloodhoof Village, his loyalty is to his people and the protection of their ancestral pastures in Mulgore.",al={"ruul eagletalon faction","is ruul horde","ruul eagletalon allegiance"},it="side",add=true},{q="Why is Ruul Eagletalon in Bloodhoof Village?",a="Ruul stays in Bloodhoof Village to safeguard the settlement and nearby plains from surrounding threats, enlisting capable travelers to cull the aggressive Windfury harpy presence that encroaches upon the tauren lands.",al={"why is ruul here","ruul eagletalon purpose","what is ruul doing in bloodhoof"},it="why_here",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Ruul represents the practical vigilance required of the Shu'halo, ensuring that the newfound sanctuary of Mulgore remains safe from territorial predators and winged raiders."},kw={"bloodhoof","eagletalon","harpies","mulgore","ruul","windfury"},rel={"subzone:bloodhoof-village","quest:743","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren"},src={"Ruul Eagletalon"}}
E["npc:seer-graytongue"] = {t="npc",n="Seer Graytongue",z="mulgore",s="Seer Graytongue is a venerable tauren elder and spiritual guide residing on Red Cloud Mesa in Mulgore. Stationed near Camp Narache, he tests young braves undertaking their earliest tribal trials. Through rituals such as the Rite of Strength, he ensures that the youth honor the Earthmother before stepping into the wider world.",h="Seer Graytongue is a venerable tauren elder and spiritual guide residing on Red Cloud Mesa in Mulgore.",sec={{t="A Guide on the Mesa",b="Seer Graytongue makes his home atop Red Cloud Mesa, serving as an early instructor for aspiring tauren braves and spellweavers. As young adventurers set forth from Camp Narache, Graytongue oversees critical steps of their sacred passage, including the Rites of the Earthmother. By testing their resolve against the harsh plains and dangerous beasts of Mulgore, he instills the virtues of honor, patience, and reverence that every true tauren must carry into adulthood.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Seer Graytongue?",a="Seer Graytongue is an elder tauren seer living in a lodge atop Red Cloud Mesa in Mulgore. He directs young heroes through traditional ceremonial duties to prove their worth to their tribe.",al={"graytongue","seer graytongue role","who is the seer on red cloud mesa"},it="identity"},{q="What trials does Seer Graytongue oversee?",a="He is tasked with guiding youths through components of the Rites of the Earthmother, including the Rite of Strength. These rituals test a brave's fortitude and respect for the natural balance of the plains.",al={"graytongue quests","what does graytongue want","rites of the earthmother graytongue"},it="purpose"},{q="Where can I find Seer Graytongue?",a="He resides in a modest leather lodge resting along the perimeter of Red Cloud Mesa, just outside the central activity of Camp Narache in Mulgore.",al={"seer graytongue location","where is graytongue","graytongue hut"},gp=true},{q="Whose side is Seer Graytongue on?",a="Seer Graytongue is loyal to the tauren tribes and the Horde. As a spiritual elder residing within the heart of Camp Narache, he tends to the sacred customs of his people and works alongside chieftain leadership to nurture the next generation of tauren champions.",al={"seer graytongue faction","is seer graytongue horde","seer graytongue loyalty"},it="side",add=true},{q="What does Seer Graytongue want?",a="Seer Graytongue seeks to guide fledgling tauren along the sacred path of the Earthmother. By directing young braves through spiritual and physical rites, he ensures they prove their resolve, honor the spirits, and become worthy defenders of the tribe.",al={"what does graytongue want","graytongue goal","seer graytongue purpose"},it="wants",add=true},{q="Why is Seer Graytongue here?",a="Seer Graytongue makes his home on Red Cloud Mesa to serve as a vital guide for youth coming of age. Camp Narache is where young tauren take their first steps into the wider world, and Graytongue remains stationed here to oversee their traditional rites of passage.",al={"why is seer graytongue in red cloud mesa","seer graytongue location reason","why is graytongue here"},it="why_here",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For tauren youths, visiting Seer Graytongue marks a sacred rite of passage that connects personal strength with the eternal blessings of the Earthmother.",["class:Shaman"]="Spiritual initiates look to elders like Graytongue to learn how sacred ritual and trial bind the physical might of a warrior to ancestral guidance."},kw={"camp narache","graytongue","mulgore","red cloud mesa","rite of strength","rites of the earthmother","seer graytongue"},rel={"quest:763","quest:757","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren","topic:quilboar"},src={"Seer Graytongue"}}
E["npc:seer-ravenfeather"] = {t="npc",n="Seer Ravenfeather",z="mulgore",s="Seer Ravenfeather is a tauren shaman instructor stationed at Camp Narache on the Red Cloud Mesa. She guides fledging spiritualists through their early trials, helping them attune to the voices of the spirits and the land of Mulgore.",h="Seer Ravenfeather is a tauren shaman instructor stationed at Camp Narache on the Red Cloud Mesa.",sec={{t="A Guide on the Mesa",b="Standing within the quiet shelters of Camp Narache, Seer Ravenfeather serves as a patient teacher for young tauren called to walk the shamanic path. The Red Cloud Mesa is a sacred proving ground, and Ravenfeather ensures that novice practitioners understand their duties before venturing into the wider expanses of Mulgore. She instructs them in the foundational rites needed to commune with elemental forces and honor the Earth Mother.",sp=0},{t="The Call of the Elements",b="Ravenfeather's primary charge is overseeing the initial spiritual steps of nascent shamans, directing them in the traditional rite known as the Call of Earth. Through her guidance, initiates prepare ceremonial saptas and seek communion with the earthen spirits nestled within the mesa's stony outcrops, forging a bond that anchors their elemental magic.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Seer Ravenfeather?",a="Seer Ravenfeather is a spiritual mentor residing in Camp Narache on the Red Cloud Mesa. She trains young tauren shamans taking their first steps in the world.",al={"seer ravenfeather lore","who is ravenfeather","camp narache seer"},it="identity"},{q="What role does she play in shaman training?",a="She guides novice shamans through their earliest rituals, most notably the quest chain to commune with the spirits of the land and answer the Call of Earth.",al={"shaman quests ravenfeather","call of earth trainer","what does ravenfeather teach"},it="purpose"},{q="Where can I find Seer Ravenfeather?",a="She can be found among the tents and lodges of Camp Narache, located on the secluded Red Cloud Mesa in southwestern Mulgore.",al={"ravenfeather location","where is ravenfeather","camp narache location"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Whose side is Seer Ravenfeather on?",a="Seer Ravenfeather is loyal to the tauren tribes and the Horde. Stationed in Camp Narache, she serves her people by guiding young tauren who are called to walk the spiritual paths of shamanism and commune with the spirits of the land.",al={"seer ravenfeather faction","is seer ravenfeather horde","ravenfeather allegiance"},it="side",add=true},{q="What does Seer Ravenfeather want?",a="Her goal is to instruct and prepare novice spiritual seekers in Camp Narache. She guides young initiates through sacred rites—most notably communion with the earth spirits—helping them earn their totems and maintain harmony between the tribe and the land.",al={"seer ravenfeather goals","what does ravenfeather want","seer ravenfeather purpose"},it="wants",add=true},{q="Why is Seer Ravenfeather here in Camp Narache?",a="Camp Narache is the gathering ground where young tauren of the Red Cloud Mesa begin their journeys. Seer Ravenfeather remains here to watch over emerging mystics, ensuring every initiate learns the ancient traditions, rites, and reverent connection to the elements from their very first steps.",al={"why is seer ravenfeather in camp narache","seer ravenfeather red cloud mesa","why is ravenfeather here"},it="why_here",add=true}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="For young tauren shamans, Seer Ravenfeather is the very first guide on the long journey of learning to commune with the elements and bind the powers of earth.",["race:Tauren"]="As a seer of Camp Narache, she represents the deep spiritual tradition that anchors tauren society as they settle into their ancestral home."},kw={"call of earth","camp narache","earth sapta","mulgore","ravenfeather","red cloud mesa","seer ravenfeather","shaman"},rel={"subzone:camp-narache","zone:mulgore","quest:1520","topic:tauren"},src={"Seer Ravenfeather"}}
E["npc:skorn-whitecloud"] = {t="npc",n="Skorn Whitecloud",z="mulgore",s="Skorn Whitecloud is an aging tauren hunter residing at Bloodhoof Village in the rolling plains of Mulgore. He guides young hunters in understanding their relationship with the land's predators and aids travelers who cross paths with Mulgore's lurking dangers.",h="Skorn Whitecloud is an aging tauren hunter residing at Bloodhoof Village in the rolling plains of Mulgore.",sec={{t="A Guide on the Golden Plains",b="Living at Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore, Skorn Whitecloud is an old hunter who now serves as an instructor in the ways of the hunt. His primary role is instilling respect for the wild creatures of the plains. He tests aspiring hunters by sending them after the claws of Mulgore's cunning flatland prowlers, to be shown to Melor Stonehoof in Thunder Bluff, ensuring that those who take up the bow or spear understand the balance between predator and prey.",sp=0},{t="Vigilance Against the Unnatural",b="Beyond ordinary hunting duties, Skorn remains watchful for strange disturbances across the savannah. When unnatural threats surface—such as signs of demonic corruption left upon the wildlife and beasts of the plains—Skorn helps piece together what transpired, ensuring the tribes of Mulgore remain alert to malevolent forces creeping into their ancestral home.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Where can Skorn Whitecloud be found?",a="Skorn Whitecloud is located at Bloodhoof Village, the tauren settlement in the southern grasslands of Mulgore.",al={"skorn whitecloud location","where is skorn","skorn whitecloud map"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What role does Skorn play for young hunters?",a="Skorn teaches the core principles of hunting through the rite known as The Hunter's Way. He tasks aspiring hunters with proving their tracking and combat skills against the plains' cunning flatland prowlers, then sends them to Melor Stonehoof in Thunder Bluff.",al={"what does skorn do","skorn hunter quest","the hunters way"},it="purpose"},{q="What goods does Skorn sell?",a="Skorn is no merchant. His hunting days are over, but he offers one of his own weapons, Skorn's Hammer or Skorn's Rifle, to the hunter who brings him proof that the great wolf Ghost Howl has fallen.",al={"skorn vendor","what does skorn sell","skorn weapons"},it="item",gp=true},{q="Is Skorn Whitecloud related to any other tauren in Mulgore?",a="He shares a surname with Provisioner Whitecloud, a tauren merchant residing at Bloodhoof Village, suggesting ties to the same extended tribal family.",al={"whitecloud family","skorn relatives","provisioner whitecloud"},it="ties"},{q="Who is Skorn Whitecloud?",a="Skorn Whitecloud is an aging tauren hunter residing at Bloodhoof Village in the rolling plains of Mulgore. A respected elder huntsman of his tribe, he mentors rising braves in tracking, hunting, and honoring the traditions of the hunt across tauren lands.",al={"who is skorn","skorn whitecloud lore","who is skorn whitecloud"},it="identity",add=true},{q="Whose side is Skorn Whitecloud on?",a="Skorn is loyal to the Thunder Bluff tauren and stands firmly with the Horde. He welcomes travelers of the Horde in Bloodhoof Village, offering wisdom to those who walk the hunter's path in Mulgore.",al={"skorn whitecloud faction","skorn whitecloud alliance or horde","what side is skorn on"},it="side",add=true},{q="What does Skorn Whitecloud want?",a="Skorn seeks to guide young tauren along the hunter's way, testing their prowess against the predators of Mulgore. He tasks promising hunters with tracking dangerous quarry—including ferocious flatland cougars—to ensure they learn self-reliance and reverence for the beasts they hunt.",al={"what does skorn want","skorn whitecloud quest motive","skorn whitecloud purpose"},it="wants",add=true},{q="Why is Skorn Whitecloud here?",a="Skorn makes his home at Bloodhoof Village in the heart of Mulgore. His hunting days are behind him, but he stays close to the open prairie where game is plentiful and young trackers come to earn their place among their people.",al={"why is skorn at camp sungraze","why is skorn whitecloud here","skorn whitecloud location reason"},it="why_here",add=true}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="For young tauren hunters, Skorn Whitecloud offers early guidance in stalking swift predators like the flatland prowler, reinforcing the spiritual harmony of the hunt.",["race:Tauren"]="Skorn exemplifies the Whitecloud family's dedication to providing shelter, weaponry, and practical wisdom across the scattered settlements of Mulgore."},kw={"bloodhoof village","flatland prowler","mulgore","skorn","tauren","the hunter's way","whitecloud"},rel={"zone:mulgore","topic:tauren","quest:861","quest:770"},src={"Skorn Whitecloud"}}
E["npc:vorn-skyseer"] = {t="npc",n="Vorn Skyseer",z="mulgore",s="Vorn Skyseer is a peaceful tauren resident of Camp Narache atop Red Cloud Mesa in Mulgore. Resting among the camp's tents, he greets young adventurers beginning their journeys and introduces them to local life.",h="Vorn Skyseer is a peaceful tauren resident of Camp Narache atop Red Cloud Mesa in Mulgore.",sec={{t="A Friendly Face at Camp Narache",b="Situated in Camp Narache on Red Cloud Mesa, Vorn Skyseer serves as an informal welcoming presence for young tauren. As they undertake their rites of passage before venturing into the wider valleys of Mulgore, Vorn offers kind words and hospitality, helping new arrivals settle into the camp.",sp=0},{t="A Detached Observer",b="Vorn is no warrior. He leaves the camp's defense to its braves and keeps to a quiet, unhurried life, content to watch over the tranquil plains of his homeland.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Vorn Skyseer?",a="Vorn Skyseer is an elder resident of Camp Narache in Mulgore. He primarily serves to welcome young tauren braves beginning their introductory rites at Red Cloud Mesa.",al={"vorn skyseer lore","who is vorn","vorn mulgore"},it="identity"},{q="Where can I find Vorn Skyseer?",a="He can be found resting outdoors among the tents of Camp Narache, atop Red Cloud Mesa in southwestern Mulgore.",al={"vorn skyseer location","where is vorn","vorn camp narache"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What quests does Vorn offer?",a="He receives the Bloodhoof Village Gift Voucher in the quest Welcome!, greeting its bearer and offering a small companion to join them on their adventures.",al={"vorn skyseer quests","vorn welcome quest"},it="purpose",gp=true},{q="Whose side is Vorn Skyseer on?",a="Vorn Skyseer is a tauren residing at Camp Narache on Red Cloud Mesa, standing firmly with the tribes of Mulgore and the Horde. He is no warrior; his home and loyalties belong to his people.",al={"vorn skyseer faction","who does vorn serve","vorn allegiance"},it="side",add=true},{q="Why is Vorn Skyseer in Camp Narache?",a="Vorn Skyseer lives at Camp Narache to greet the young braves beginning their journeys on Red Cloud Mesa. He offers kind words and hospitality, helping new arrivals settle into the camp.",al={"why is vorn here","vorn skyseer role bloodhoof","what does vorn do"},it="why_here",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For young tauren on Red Cloud Mesa, Vorn Skyseer is often one of the first familiar, reassuring faces to greet them as they prepare to step into the wider life of Mulgore.",["faction:Horde"]="Vorn exemplifies the calm and steady nature of the plains tribes who have gathered under Cairne Bloodhoof's leadership to build a permanent home."},kw={"camp narache","mulgore","red cloud mesa","tauren","vorn skyseer","welcome"},rel={"subzone:camp-narache","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren"},src={"Vorn Skyseer"}}
E["npc:yaw-sharpmane"] = {t="npc",n="Yaw Sharpmane",z="mulgore",s="Yaw Sharpmane is an experienced tauren hunter trainer stationed in Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore. He guides young hunters venturing out from Camp Narache, helping them master the wild beasts of their ancestral plains.",h="Yaw Sharpmane is an experienced tauren hunter trainer stationed in Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore.",sec={{t="A Guide on the Plains",b="Positioned amidst the tents and fires of Bloodhoof Village, Yaw Sharpmane serves the Shu'halo as an instructor in the ways of marksmanship, tracking, and survival. Young hunters who have proven themselves in Red Cloud Mesa are directed to Yaw to hone their craft as they step onto the broader plains of Mulgore.",sp=0},{t="Mastering the Bond",b="Yaw's most essential role is guiding novice hunters through the ancient rite of taming wild beasts. He tasks initiates with bonding with the native fauna of Mulgore, testing their patience and respect for the natural balance before instructing them on how to properly care for and train a permanent companion.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Yaw Sharpmane?",a="Yaw Sharpmane is the resident hunter trainer in Bloodhoof Village. He instructs young tauren hunters in marksmanship, wilderness survival, and the art of taming animal companions.",al={"yaw sharpmane","who is yaw","bloodhoof hunter trainer"},it="identity"},{q="Where can I find Yaw Sharpmane?",a="Yaw is located in Bloodhoof Village, situated in central Mulgore along the shores of Stonebull Lake.",al={"yaw sharpmane location","where is yaw","yaw coords"},gp=true},{q="What does Yaw Sharpmane teach hunters?",a="He offers standard hunter training, selling rank-ups for basic abilities and directing promising hunters through the rites required to tame and command beasts.",al={"what does yaw teach","yaw hunter skills","yaw abilities"},gp=true},{q="What quests does Yaw offer?",a="Yaw provides the tauren version of the beast-taming questline, starting with 'The Hunter's Path' and progressing through 'Taming the Beast' and 'Training the Beast.'",al={"yaw quests","yaw sharpmane quests"},gp=true},{q="Why do hunters need to speak to him around level 10?",a="Upon reaching their tenth season, hunters are sent to Yaw to learn the sacred methods of capturing, feeding, and commanding permanent animal companions from the wilds of Mulgore.",al={"yaw level 10","tame beast bloodhoof"},gp=true},{q="Does Yaw Sharpmane interact with non-hunters?",a="He will speak politely with travelers of other callings, but his professional knowledge and specific quest trials are reserved strictly for hunters.",al={"yaw non hunters","can other classes talk to yaw"},gp=true},{q="Whose side is Yaw Sharpmane on?",a="Yaw Sharpmane stands firmly with the Bloodhoof tauren and the Horde. Stationed in Bloodhoof Village, he helps ensure that the young defenders of Mulgore are well-versed in the ancestral ways of tracking, marksmanship, and taming the wild beasts of their homelands.",al={"yaw sharpmane faction","is yaw sharpmane horde","yaw sharpmane allegiance"},it="side",add=true},{q="What does Yaw Sharpmane want?",a="Yaw seeks to instruct novice hunters in the core traditions of the hunt. He guides young tauren to commune with nature, understand the creatures of Mulgore, and master the ancient rite of taming beasts so that they may survive the wild and serve their tribe.",al={"yaw sharpmane goal","yaw sharpmane motives","what does yaw sharpmane do"},it="wants",add=true},{q="Why is Yaw Sharpmane in Bloodhoof Village?",a="Bloodhoof Village serves as the primary gathering hub for young tauren leaving the training grounds of Camp Narache. Yaw Sharpmane resides here to guide hunters as they take their first major steps across Mulgore, teaching them to bond with animal companions and master their craft.",al={"why is yaw sharpmane here","yaw sharpmane bloodhoof village purpose"},it="why_here",add=true}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="For tauren hunters leaving the safety of Red Cloud Mesa, Yaw Sharpmane is an essential mentor who guides you through bonding with your very first wild companion.",["race:Tauren"]="Yaw represents the revered Shu'halo tradition of living in harmony with nature and honoring the spirits of the wild beasts of Mulgore."},kw={"beasts","bloodhoof","hunter","mulgore","sharpmane","taming","tauren","trainer","yaw"},rel={"subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore","quest:6061","quest:6081","quest:6065","topic:tauren"},src={"Yaw Sharpmane"}}
E["npc:zarlman-two-moons"] = {t="npc",n="Zarlman Two-Moons",z="mulgore",s="Zarlman Two-Moons is a revered tauren mystic residing in Bloodhoof Village in central Mulgore. While Baine Bloodhoof oversees the village's daily earthly affairs, Zarlman guides its spiritual life and communions with the spirits. He plays a vital role in initiating young tauren into adulthood through the sacred Rite of Vision.",h="Zarlman Two-Moons is a revered tauren mystic residing in Bloodhoof Village in central Mulgore.",sec={{t="Keeper of the Unseen",b="Within Bloodhoof Village, leadership is divided between the physical and the mystical. Baine Bloodhoof commands the settlement's defense and temporal needs, but it is Zarlman Two-Moons who maintains the harmony of the spirit world. In a culture deeply rooted in reverence for the Earth Mother and ancestral wisdom, Zarlman serves as a conduit between the everyday struggles of the plains and the timeless voices of the past.",sp=0},{t="The Rite of Vision",b="Zarlman's most crucial duty to his people is administering the Rite of Vision, a traditional trial every maturing tauren must undertake. By guiding initiates to prepare sacred draughts and listen closely to ancestral portents, he helps young hunters, shamans, and warriors discover their destined path and spirit guides. Under his instruction, initiates learn that physical strength on the plains means little without spiritual clarity.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Zarlman Two-Moons?",a="Zarlman Two-Moons is a prominent tauren mystic stationed in Bloodhoof Village. While Baine Bloodhoof handles temporal leadership and the town's practical defense, Zarlman watches over the settlement's spiritual well-being and guides young adventurers through ancient tribal traditions.",al={"who is zarlman","zarlman two moons lore","who is the shaman in bloodhoof"},it="identity"},{q="What is Zarlman's role in Bloodhoof Village?",a="Zarlman oversees spiritual affairs, ancestral communion, and cultural rites for the residents of Bloodhoof Village. He complements Baine Bloodhoof's physical leadership by ensuring the tribe remains in balance with nature spirits and their ancestors.",al={"zarlman job","zarlman purpose","bloodhoof village mystic"},it="purpose"},{q="What is the Rite of Vision?",a="The Rite of Vision is a traditional rite of passage for young tauren passing through Mulgore. Guided by Zarlman, initiates drink a ritual concoction that opens their spiritual sight, allowing them to follow visions and receive guidance from nature spirits.",al={"rite of vision quest","what is the vision rite","tauren vision quest"}},{q="Where can Zarlman Two-Moons be found?",a="Zarlman Two-Moons is located in Bloodhoof Village, situated in the center of Mulgore within the curve of Stonebull Lake.",al={"where is zarlman","zarlman location","how to find zarlman"},it="place",gp=true},{q="How does Zarlman relate to Baine Bloodhoof?",a="Zarlman works alongside Baine Bloodhoof as a spiritual counterpart. While Baine leads the village in physical and martial matters, Zarlman provides mystical wisdom and watches over the tribe's spiritual balance.",al={"baine and zarlman","zarlman relation to baine"},it="ties"},{q="Why is the Rite of Vision important to young tauren?",a="The Rite of Vision is essential for every tauren coming of age. It marks the transition from unproven youth to an acknowledged member of the tribe, revealing their inner spirit guide and cementing their bond with the Earth Mother.",al={"why do rite of vision","rite of vision importance"}},{q="Whose side is Zarlman Two-Moons on?",a="Zarlman Two-Moons is devoted entirely to the Bloodhoof tribe and the Horde. Stationed in Bloodhoof Village, he works closely with Baine Bloodhoof to ensure their people remain spiritually resilient against any threat facing Mulgore.",al={"who does zarlman serve","zarlman two moons faction","is zarlman horde"},it="side",add=true},{q="What does Zarlman Two-Moons want?",a="Zarlman seeks to safeguard the spiritual well-being of Bloodhoof Village. While physical leadership falls to Baine Bloodhoof, Zarlman guides young adventurers through ancient traditions like the Rite of Vision to commune with the spirits and uncover their true path.",al={"what does zarlman want","zarlman goal","what is zarlman doing"},it="wants",add=true},{q="Why is Zarlman Two-Moons here?",a="Zarlman remains in Bloodhoof Village to serve as its primary spiritual guardian. As tribal members grow into adulthood, he oversees the sacred rites necessary to commune with the ancestral spirits and protect their ancestral lands from unseen spiritual perils.",al={"why is zarlman in bloodhoof village","why is zarlman here"},it="why_here",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For tauren adventurers, Zarlman is an essential guide who helps you complete the sacred rites of your youth and awaken your spiritual sight.",["class:Shaman"]="Zarlman exemplifies the classical tauren tradition of shamanism, balancing communion with the spirits alongside practical tribal leadership.",["faction:Horde"]="Stationed in Mulgore's central hub, Zarlman introduces newly arrived Horde allies to the deep spiritual customs that shape tauren culture."},kw={"bloodhoof","mulgore","rite","shaman","spirits","tauren","two-moons","village","vision","zarlman"},rel={"subzone:bloodhoof-village","npc:baine-bloodhoof","quest:767","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren"},src={"Zarlman Two-Moons"}}
E["quest:1520"] = {t="quest",n="Call of Earth",z="mulgore",s="This sacred rite marks a tauren shaman's spiritual attunement to the element of earth. By drinking an Earth Sapta at a holy rock east of Camp Narache, initiates peer beyond physical senses to commune directly with a Minor Manifestation of Earth.",h="This sacred rite marks a tauren shaman's spiritual attunement to the element of earth.",sec={{t="The Purpose of the Sapta",b="A sapta is a carefully concocted ceremonial potion crafted by shamanic elders to bind an initiate's mortal spirit directly to a primal element. Prepared with ancestral magic, only a select few among the tauren preserve the secrets of its brewing. When imbibed at a designated sacred site, the draught expands the imbiber's mind, piercing the veil between the physical realm and the spiritual plane so the elements can be witnessed in their raw form.",sp=0},{t="Communion at Kodo Rock",b="East of Camp Narache stands Kodo Rock, a looming stone pillar that serves as a holy sanctuary. Consuming the sapta here reveals the Minor Manifestation of Earth awaiting the pilgrim. Unlike the volatile, fiery, or raging aspects of nature encountered later along the shaman's path, the spirit of earth asks only that the initiate approach in quiet humility, granting the mountain's enduring strength and the plains' calm patience to all who honor the land.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is an Earth Sapta?",a="An Earth Sapta is an alchemical and spiritual draught brewed by shamanic elders. Its magic opens the drinker's perception, allowing an initiate to see and communicate directly with elemental earth spirits.",al={"what is sapta","earth sapta meaning","what does the drink do"},it="item"},{q="Where is Kodo Rock located?",a="Kodo Rock is an imposing natural stone monolith situated directly east across the Red Cloud Mesa from Camp Narache.",al={"find kodo rock","kodo rock location","where is the shrine"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Who crafted the Earth Sapta for this rite?",a="The potion was prepared and granted by Seer Ravenfeather, an elder shaman residing at Camp Narache who oversees the training of young spiritual guides.",al={"who gives the sapta","seer ravenfeather sapta"},it="origin"},{q="Why must the sapta be consumed at a holy site?",a="A sapta is inherently sacred and dangerous if misused. Tauren tradition demands it only be imbibed within consecrated shrines where elemental spirits naturally dwell and can receive the petitioner safely.",al={"why drink at kodo rock","can i drink sapta anywhere"},it="why_here"},{q="What happens after drinking the potion at Kodo Rock?",a="The potion lifts the mundane veil, revealing a Minor Manifestation of Earth. Speaking with the entity completes the bond between the shaman and the element of earth.",al={"what appears","minor manifestation of earth spawn"},it="next_step"},{q="What lesson does the earth spirit impart to initiates?",a="The spirit teaches that earth demands humble patience rather than combat. While elements like fire or air can be chaotic and violent, earth offers strength, endurance, and quiet foundation to the shaman's soul.",al={"earth spirit lesson","meaning of earth rite"},it="purpose"},{q="Why does Seer Ravenfeather send you to Kodo Rock?",a="Seer Ravenfeather is guiding your path as an initiate shaman. The Earth Sapta he prepared is infused with magic designed to open your perception to the spirits of the world. By directing you to drink the brew at the sacred shrine of Kodo Rock, he aims to permanently bind your spirit to the element of earth, allowing you to commune directly with its manifestations and learn its core wisdom.",al={"seer ravenfeather motive","why drink earth sapta","why ravenfeather call of earth"},it="motive",add=true}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="This rite forms the spiritual core of a tauren shaman's bond with the land, serving as the required bridge to earn communion with the earth and receive an Earth Totem.",["race:Tauren"]="Deep reverence for the Earth Mother makes the sapta ritual one of the most culturally revered coming-of-age tests for tauren spiritualists on the Red Cloud Mesa."},kw={"call of earth","camp narache","earth sapta","kodo rock","manifestation","mulgore","sapta","seer ravenfeather"},rel={"npc:seer-ravenfeather","npc:minor-manifestation-of-earth","subzone:kodo-rock","subzone:camp-narache","zone:mulgore","quest:99141"},m={start="Seer Ravenfeather",["end"]="Minor Manifestation of Earth",level="4",faction="Horde",id=1520},src={"Call of Earth (Mulgore 2)"}}
E["quest:1521"] = {t="quest",n="Call of Earth",z="mulgore",s="This rite concludes a young tauren shaman's initial communion with the spirits of the land in Mulgore. After drinking the Sapta of Earth and speaking directly with a spirit manifestation, the initiate receives a fragment of raw stone to bring home. Returning to Camp Narache marks the fledgling mystic's formal bond with the first of the four elements.",h="This rite concludes a young tauren shaman's initial communion with the spirits of the land in Mulgore.",sec={{t="A Gift from the Mountain",b="Upon communing with the Minor Manifestation of Earth atop the crags near Red Cloud Mesa, the spirit yields a simple Rough Quartz. Though plain in appearance, the stone represents an elemental pact and the endurance of stone itself. The spirit reminds the young shaman that patience and strength are the core virtues demanded by the earth, promising that mutual respect will grant harmony with the land.",sp=0},{t="The First Totem",b="Returning to Camp Narache, Seer Ravenfeather uses the gifted quartz to fashion the shaman's very first Earth Totem. Far more than a decorative symbol of status, the totem serves as a permanent physical and spiritual anchor. Through this carved focus, the initiate channels the grounding, protective magic of the earth itself, taking the first true step along the fourfold elemental path.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of the Rough Quartz?",a="The quartz is a token given directly by the Minor Manifestation of Earth as proof of communion. Seer Ravenfeather uses it as the spiritual heart of your newly carved Earth Totem.",al={"why do i need rough quartz","what is the quartz for","rough quartz use"},it="item"},{q="Why is an Earth Totem so important to a shaman?",a="Totems act as conduits through which a shaman channels elemental power. Without the Earth Totem as a focus, a shaman cannot petition the spirits of the land to manifest their spells and protective wards.",al={"why do i need earth totem","what does earth totem do","earth totem lore"},it="power"},{q="Who is Seer Ravenfeather?",a="Seer Ravenfeather is the spiritual guide and shaman trainer stationed at Camp Narache on Red Cloud Mesa. She oversees the elemental trials of young tauren, guiding them through their introductory visions and crafting their sacred implements.",al={"who is ravenfeather","seer ravenfeather lore","camp narache seer"},it="identity"},{q="What are the four paths Seer Ravenfeather mentions?",a="She refers to the four primal elements that a shaman must master: Earth, Fire, Water, and Air. Earth is the foundation, representing endurance and steadfastness, and serves as the initiate's initial trial.",al={"four paths","four elements shaman","what are the paths"},it="purpose"},{q="Where does this communion take place?",a="The Minor Manifestation of Earth resides in the rugged cliffs bordering Red Cloud Mesa in Mulgore, reachable only after drinking the specialized Earth Sapta.",al={"where do i find the earth spirit","manifestation location","where is the spirit"},it="place"},{q="Do other Horde shamans undertake a similar rite?",a="Yes. Orcs and Darkspear trolls in Durotar undertake their own version of the Call of Earth in the Valley of Trials, seeking communion with local elemental manifestations to carve their first totems.",al={"do orcs do call of earth","troll call of earth","do all shamans get earth totem"},it="people"},{q="Why does the Minor Manifestation of Earth send you back to Seer Ravenfeather?",a="The elemental spirit has tested your resolve and deemed you worthy to walk the path of earth. By bestowing a piece of itself—the rough quartz—it provides tangible proof of your communion and mutual respect. It bids you return to Seer Ravenfeather so the stone can form the heart of your first totem, binding the spirits of earth to your service as you uphold patience, strength, and wisdom.",al={"minor manifestation of earth motive","why send rough quartz","manifestation of earth purpose"},it="motive",add=true}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="This rite marks the acquisition of your very first totem. Carrying it grounds your spiritual identity and unlocks your ability to cast essential earth-aligned wards.",["race:Tauren"]="Tauren culture regards the Earth Mother with profound reverence. Undertaking this trial binds an initiate directly to the living rock and soil of their ancestral homeland.",["faction:Horde"]="Shamans serve as vital spiritual anchors and advisors within the new Horde, bridging the mortal races with the volatile elemental forces of Kalimdor."},kw={"call of earth","camp narache","earth totem","minor manifestation of earth","mulgore","red cloud mesa","rough quartz","seer ravenfeather","tauren shaman"},rel={"npc:seer-ravenfeather","npc:minor-manifestation-of-earth","quest:1520","subzone:camp-narache","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Minor Manifestation of Earth",["end"]="Seer Ravenfeather",level="4",faction="Horde",id=1521},src={"Call of Earth (Mulgore 3)"}}
E["quest:3092"] = {t="quest",n="Etched Note",z="mulgore",s="The Etched Note is a formal summons directed to promising young tauren hunters in Camp Narache. Delivered on behalf of the trainer Lanka Farshot, the message calls the novice forward to begin mastering the practical and spiritual arts of the wild.",h="The Etched Note is a formal summons directed to promising young tauren hunters in Camp Narache.",sec={{t="A Summons to the Hunt",b="While training amidst the bluffs of Red Cloud Mesa, young tauren showing an aptitude for marksmanship and tracking are passed an etched message. Handed down through Grull Hawkwind, the note carries word that Lanka Farshot has taken notice of the youth's bloodline and raw capability, urging them to formalize their study beneath his watchful eye.",sp=0},{t="Tradition of the Plains",b="To the Shu'halo, a hunter is far more than a simple scout or warrior. Hunters sustain the tribes through their quarry, protect nomadic encampments, and live in close balance with the beasts of Kalimdor. By calling the novice into training, Lanka sets the groundwork for mastering weapons, understanding the creatures of Mulgore, and eventually undertaking the ancient art of taming wild companions.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of the Etched Note?",a="The Etched Note serves as a formal introduction connecting a novice tauren hunter with Lanka Farshot, the local class trainer stationed in Camp Narache.",al={"what does etched note do","etched note purpose","why did i get etched note"},it="purpose"},{q="Who delivers the note?",a="Grull Hawkwind passes the note along, explaining that a messenger from the hunter trainer sought out the young hunter moments prior.",al={"who gives etched note","etched note quest giver","rohaku note"}},{q="Where is Lanka Farshot located?",a="Lanka Farshot resides within Camp Narache on Red Cloud Mesa, standing near the hunter training grounds ready to instruct new recruits.",al={"where is lanka farshot","find lanka farshot","lanka location"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why are hunters so revered among the tauren?",a="Hunters play a crucial role in tauren society by providing food, reading the untamed wilderness, and maintaining harmony with the spirits of the plains.",al={"tauren hunter lore","why are tauren hunters important"},it="people"},{q="What skills does this training introduce?",a="Lanka prepares the novice in foundational hunting techniques, including the use of ranged weaponry, understanding wild beasts, and ultimately the rituals required to tame animal companions.",al={"what does lanka teach","hunter basics tauren"},it="for_you",gp=true},{q="What is the next step after reading the note?",a="Reporting to Lanka Farshot completes the summons and makes him your trainer, who will teach you to fire your rifle, tame a pet and more as you grow.",al={"next quest after etched note","etched note follow up"},it="next_step",gp=true},{q="Why does Grull Hawkwind give you the etched note?",a="Grull Hawkwind is merely passing along word on behalf of a messenger sent across Camp Narache. Lanka Farshot, the camp's hunter trainer, sent the messenger specifically to seek you out as you come of age. Grull simply ensures the summons finds your hands without delay so you can begin proper training among your people.",al={"why rohaku gives note","rohaku stonehoof motive","why did rohaku give me this"},it="motive",add=true}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="This summons marks your formal induction into the hunter tradition, preparing you to master marksman arts, wilderness survival, and beast companionship.",["race:Tauren"]="For the Shu'halo, hunting is a sacred duty honoring the Earth Mother through respect for the beasts that sustain the tribe across the plains of Mulgore."},kw={"camp narache","etched note","lanka farshot","mulgore","red cloud mesa","tauren hunter"},rel={"npc:lanka-farshot","subzone:camp-narache","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Grull Hawkwind",["end"]="Lanka Farshot",level="3",faction="Horde",id=3092},src={"Etched Note (quest)"}}
E["quest:3094"] = {t="quest",n="Verdant Note",z="mulgore",s="The Verdant Note is a formal summons delivered to promising young tauren in the Red Cloud Mesa. Entrusted by Grull Hawkwind, the message bids budding protectors of the wilds to seek out the druidic mentor Gart Mistrunner at Camp Narache.",h="The Verdant Note is a formal summons delivered to promising young tauren in the Red Cloud Mesa.",sec={{t="A Summons to the Path",b="Young tauren stepping into their responsibilities on the Red Cloud Mesa are quickly singled out for their natural gifts. Grull Hawkwind passes along this missive on behalf of a messenger sent by Gart Mistrunner. The note acts as an invitation into the ancient fold of tauren druidism, calling the initiate away from basic village errands to heed the whispering balance of the plains.",sp=0},{t="Voices of the Earthmother",b="Upon answering the summons, Gart Mistrunner warns the fledgling druid that ancestral and natural spirits have grown restless across Mulgore. Tauren druids are tasked to act as living avatars of nature, keeping harmony with the will of the Earthmother regardless of distant rivalries. While night elves also practice druidic arts, Gart cautions that their pride blinds them to the tauren's own deep-seated bond with the natural realm.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Verdant Note?",a="It is an introductory summons delivered to young tauren druids in Camp Narache. It instructs the bearer to seek out the local trainer, Gart Mistrunner, to begin structured training in the druidic arts.",al={"verdant note","druid letter","mulgore druid quest note"},it="item"},{q="Who delivers the note to the player?",a="Grull Hawkwind hands the note to the young druid, mentioning that a messenger sent by Gart Mistrunner was recently searching for them.",al={"who gives verdant note","verdant note quest starter"}},{q="Where can Gart Mistrunner be found?",a="Gart Mistrunner resides in Camp Narache, stationed under the shelter of the camp's natural bluffs where he oversees training for novice tauren druids.",al={"gart mistrunner location","where is gart"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why does Gart Mistrunner warn against the night elves?",a="Although both races revere nature and practice druidism through the Cenarion Circle, Gart observes that night elf arrogance often colors their perception of other cultures. He encourages his student to ignore such attitudes and remain focused solely on the Earthmother's wisdom.",al={"tauren and night elf druids","night elf pride druid"}},{q="Why are the spirits in Mulgore restless?",a="Gart Mistrunner senses disturbances stirring ancestral and nature spirits throughout the plains of Mulgore, marking an early sign that balancing the wild will be the new druid's lifelong responsibility.",al={"mulgore spirits restless","why are nature spirits angry"},it="threat"},{q="What ability does Gart teach next after completing this quest?",a="After reporting to Gart Mistrunner, he becomes the initiate's trainer, promising to teach the Earthmother's gifts as they grow in wisdom.",al={"what comes after verdant note","druid quest follow up"},it="next_step",gp=true},{q="Why does Grull Hawkwind give you the Verdant Note?",a="A messenger specifically sought you out on behalf of Gart Mistrunner, the camp's druid mentor. Grull Hawkwind is simply passing along the urgent message so that you do not delay in beginning your formal spiritual training under Gart's guidance.",al={"why rohaku gives note","rohaku stonehoof motive","why does rohaku want this done"},it="motive",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For the tauren, druidism is not merely a school of magic, but a sacred covenant inherited from their mythic devotion to the Earthmother.",["class:Druid"]="This summons represents a druid's earliest induction into channeling the wild energies and restless spirits of Azeroth.",["faction:Horde"]="Tauren druids represent the primary bridge between the modern Horde and the natural mysteries of Kalimdor, providing spiritual balance to the faction's raw martial strength."},kw={"camp narache","druid","earthmother","gart mistrunner","grull hawkwind","mulgore","red cloud mesa","verdant note"},rel={"npc:gart-mistrunner","npc:grull-hawkwind","subzone:camp-narache","zone:mulgore","topic:druid"},m={start="Grull Hawkwind",["end"]="Gart Mistrunner",level="3",faction="Horde",id=3094},src={"Verdant Note (quest)"}}
E["quest:3376"] = {t="quest",n="Break Sharptusk!",z="mulgore",s="Break Sharptusk! is a decisive rite of passage issued by Brave Windfeather on Red Cloud Mesa. To secure the tauren homeland against ongoing raids, young champions are sent east into Brambleblade Ravine to slay Chief Sharptusk Thornmantle. Eliminating the Bristleback chieftain strikes a severe blow against the quilboar encroaching upon Mulgore.",h="Break Sharptusk!",sec={{t="A Home Won in Blood",b="The grassy heights of Red Cloud Mesa have long served as a peaceful sanctuary where young tauren learn the traditions of the hunt and attunement with the Earth Mother. However, this peace is constantly contested. To the east lies Brambleblade Ravine, a thorn-choked gulch claimed by the hostile Bristleback quilboar tribe. The quilboar view the tauren as invaders stealing sacred ground, launching relentless skirmishes against the tribes attempting to establish a stable life in Mulgore.",sp=0},{t="The Threat of Thornmantle",b="The persistent attacks on the mesa are not aimless frenzy; they are coordinated under the brutal leadership of Chief Sharptusk Thornmantle. Residing in a fortified makeshift village deep within the ravine, Sharptusk directs his warriors and geomancers against the Shu'halo. Recognizing that the Bristleback will never cease their raids while their chief still draws breath, Brave Windfeather commands an aspiring hero to breach the ravine, strike down Thornmantle, and return with his severed head as proof.",sp=0},{t="The Lessons of the Hunt",b="For young tauren and their allies, hunting Sharptusk is more than a simple execution—it is an initiation into the harsh realities of survival. As Brave Windfeather teaches, the hunt against beasts teaches patience, but hunting an enemy cunning enough to hunt back tests courage, awareness, and martial discipline. Bringing down Sharptusk delivers justice for fallen kin and marks the hunter's readiness to venture out into the wider plains of Mulgore.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Chief Sharptusk Thornmantle?",a="Chief Sharptusk Thornmantle is the ruthless leader of the Bristleback quilboar inhabiting Brambleblade Ravine. Under his command, the tribe continuously stages raids and skirmishes against the tauren settlement on Red Cloud Mesa.",al={"who is sharptusk","sharptusk thornmantle lore","who is the quilboar chief"},it="target"},{q="Where can Chief Sharptusk Thornmantle be found?",a="Sharptusk resides deep within Brambleblade Ravine, situated on the eastern edge of Red Cloud Mesa. He oversees his tribe from their makeshift village nestled inside the thorn-covered canyon.",al={"where is sharptusk","sharptusk location","how to find sharptusk"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Who gives the quest Break Sharptusk!?",a="The quest is offered by Brave Windfeather, a seasoned tauren warrior who guards Camp Narache on Red Cloud Mesa. He charges promising newcomers with proving their mettle by removing the Bristleback threat.",al={"quest giver break sharptusk","who starts break sharptusk","brave windfeather location"},it="identity",gp=true},{q="Why do the Bristleback quilboar hate the tauren?",a="Quilboar believe that all land adorned with bloodstone and sacred thorn brambles belongs to them by divine right of their demigod creator, Agamaggan. They view the tauren founding settlements in Mulgore as encroaching trespassers on ancestral hunting grounds.",al={"why do quilboar attack","tauren quilboar conflict","why are quilboar hostile"},it="conflict"},{q="What proof does Brave Windfeather demand?",a="Brave Windfeather demands the severed head of Chief Sharptusk Thornmantle. Bringing the head proves beyond doubt that the leader has fallen and serves as a stern warning to any remaining Bristlebacks.",al={"what do i bring windfeather","sharptusk proof","quest objective sharptusk"},it="item"},{q="What is the cultural significance of this hunt for young tauren?",a="In tauren culture, true maturity comes from understanding the balance between hunter and hunted. Windfeather emphasizes that stalking a thinking, cunning adversary like Sharptusk teaches young warriors the gravity of mortal combat and the responsibility of defending the tribe.",al={"why kill sharptusk lore","tauren rite of passage sharptusk","meaning of the hunt"},it="purpose"},{q="Why does Brave Windfeather want Chief Sharptusk killed?",a="The Bristleback quilboar wage a relentless campaign against the tauren of Red Cloud Mesa, threatening the safety of their newly carved home. Chief Sharptusk Thornmantle leads these raids from nearby Brambleblade Ravine. Brave Windfeather seeks to strike a decisive blow against their leadership to halt the bloodshed and deliver a firm warning to any who would contest the tauren's presence on the mesa.",al={"windfeather motive","why kill sharptusk","why break sharptusk"},it="motive",add=true},{q="Why does Brave Windfeather task you instead of dealing with Sharptusk himself?",a="Other seasoned defenders have already attempted to dislodge the quilboar chief and failed, while the remaining braves must stay behind to guard Camp Narache and protect the young from roving threats. Having heard whispers of your growing promise as a rising hunter, Windfeather sees you as an unproven yet formidable talent capable of besting a cunning adversary and learning the true weight of the hunt.",al={"why send me","why not the braves","why not brave windfeather"},it="why_me",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Slaying Sharptusk Thornmantle is one of the final trials a young tauren undertakes before departing Camp Narache, proving they possess the resolve to defend the tribes from hostile neighbors.",["faction:Horde"]="By executing Chief Sharptusk, allies of the Horde demonstrate their commitment to stabilizing Mulgore and securing peaceful lands for their new tauren brothers.",["class:Hunter"]="For hunters, this quest is the ultimate early test: tracking a quarry that possesses its own cunning, weapons, and intent to hunt you in return."},kw={"brambleblade","bristleback","mulgore","narache","quilboar","red cloud mesa","sharptusk","thornmantle","windfeather"},rel={"subzone:brambleblade-ravine","topic:quilboar","topic:tauren","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Brave Windfeather",level="5",faction="Horde",id=3376,ri={"Chief Sharptusk Thornmantle's Head"}},src={"Break Sharptusk!"}}
E["quest:6061"] = {t="quest",n="Taming the Beast",z="mulgore",s="Taming the Beast is the foundational rite of passage for young tauren hunters in Bloodhoof Village. Guided by trainer Yaw Sharpmane, the hunter ventures into Mulgore to tame an adult plainstrider, taking the first step toward forging a spiritual bond with the creatures of the Earthmother.",h="Taming the Beast is the foundational rite of passage for young tauren hunters in Bloodhoof Village.",sec={{t="A Sacred Bond with the Wild",b="For the tauren, hunting has never been purely about slaying beasts for meat and hides. Ancestral traditions hold that overcoming and communion with the wildlife of Mulgore allows a hunter to intake their spirits and draw closer to the Earthmother. Taming a companion beast elevates this connection, turning what could be an adversary into a lifelong partner aligned with the natural balance of the plains.",sp=0},{t="The First Test",b="Yaw Sharpmane begins an initiate's instruction not with ferocious apex predators, but with the swift and watchful plainstriders roaming Mulgore's grasslands. Armed with an enchanted taming rod, the hunter must face an adult plainstrider, calm its wild instincts, and earn its trust. This initial trial proves the hunter's resolve and prepares them for the more demanding beasts that follow.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of this trial?",a="This quest is the first practical test for novice tauren hunters seeking to learn the art of animal companionship. By using a taming rod to pacify an adult plainstrider, the hunter learns the fundamentals of establishing a spiritual and working connection with beasts.",al={"why do i tame a plainstrider","purpose of quest","tauren pet quest purpose"},it="purpose"},{q="Who gives this quest and where are they located?",a="The quest is offered by Yaw Sharpmane, the hunter trainer situated in Bloodhoof Village within the central plains of Mulgore.",al={"who gives quest","quest giver location","yaw sharpmane location"},it="identity",gp=true},{q="Do I keep the adult plainstrider permanently?",a="No. The beasts tamed during Yaw Sharpmane's initial three trials are temporary practice partners. Once you complete the entire training chain and master beast taming, you can venture out and tame any compatible companion permanently.",al={"is this pet permanent","do i keep the plainstrider","keep beast after quest"},gp=true},{q="Why do tauren view taming beasts as a spiritual act?",a="Tauren tradition teaches that interacting with and besting wild creatures allows hunters to intake their spirits and become one with the natural world. Taming a beast reflects mutual respect and unity with the creations of the Earthmother.",al={"tauren pet lore","earthmother taming lore","why tame beasts tauren"},it="history"},{q="Where can adult plainstriders be found?",a="Adult plainstriders roam widely throughout the rolling savannahs and valleys surrounding Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore.",al={"where are plainstriders","plainstrider location","find adult plainstrider"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What comes after this quest?",a="Returning the taming rod to Yaw Sharpmane leads into the next trial, where you are tasked with taming a stalker, followed by an aggressive swoop, before learning the art of permanent beast training.",al={"what is next","quest chain after this","next step taming"},it="next_step",gp=true},{q="Why does Yaw Sharpmane want me to tame a plainstrider?",a="Yaw Sharpmane is guiding you through the sacred traditions of the tauren hunter. To tauren, besting and taming wild beasts is a spiritual discipline that draws the hunter closer to the Earthmother and harmonizes them with the natural world. He starts your training with the swift adult plainstriders of Mulgore so you can master the patience and spiritual focus needed before you bond with a permanent companion.",al={"why tame plainstrider","yaw sharpmane motive","why adult plainstrider"},it="motive",rc="Tauren",add=true},{q="Why does Yaw Sharpmane have me do this trial instead of doing it himself?",a="This is a personal rite of passage that every aspiring hunter must undergo for themselves. Yaw Sharpmane is an experienced mentor entrusted with teaching ancestral customs, not doing the work for you. Taming a wild creature requires forging your own spiritual link with nature and opening yourself to the Earthmother's presence. No elder or guard can build that bond on your behalf.",al={"why send me","why yaw sharpmane needs me","why must i tame the beast"},it="why_me",rc="Tauren",add=true}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="This rite marks the transition from a fledgling tracker into a true beastmaster, teaching you how to commune with animals through the Earthmother's guidance.",["race:Tauren"]="Taming a beast is an ancestral practice central to the nomadic hunting heritage of Mulgore, strengthening the bond between hunter and nature."},kw={"bloodhoof village","earthmother","hunter quest","mulgore","plainstrider","taming","taming rod","yaw sharpmane"},rel={"npc:yaw-sharpmane","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore","quest:6065","quest:6081","topic:tauren"},m={start="Yaw Sharpmane",level="10",faction="Horde",id=6061},src={"Taming the Beast (Tauren 1)"}}
E["quest:6065"] = {t="quest",n="The Hunter's Path",z="mulgore",s="The Hunter's Path is a vital milestone for young tauren hunters in Mulgore, directing them toward advanced training in beast companionship. Dispatched by their local mentors once their basic marksmanship and tracking are proven, novices are sent to Bloodhoof Village to seek the counsel of hunter trainer Yaw Sharpmane. This journey initiates the spiritual and practical trials required to tame and bond with wild beasts.",h="The Hunter's Path is a vital milestone for young tauren hunters in Mulgore, directing them toward advanced...",sec={{t="A Rite of Passage",b="To the nomadic Shu'halo, hunting is sacred rather than mere sport. A hunter must understand the balance of nature, honor the spirits of the plains, and show respect for the animals that sustain the tribes. When a young hunter demonstrates sufficient prowess in surviving the wild and wielding a bow or gun, their elders recognize that they are ready to step beyond solitary tracking. The directive to seek out seasoned mentors marks the threshold of true adulthood in tauren culture.",sp=0},{t="The Road to Bloodhoof Village",b="The path leads novitiates across the sweeping grasslands of Mulgore to Bloodhoof Village, a central gathering settlement nestled beside Stonebull Lake. There awaits Yaw Sharpmane, an experienced huntsman well-versed in the temperament of the local fauna. Speaking with Sharpmane is not a mere formality; it signals a hunter's readiness to undertake the demanding trials of beast taming, learning how to commune with predators rather than merely hunt them.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of The Hunter's Path?",a="The quest serves as the formal invitation for novice tauren hunters to begin their beast-taming trials. It directs the hunter to Bloodhoof Village to speak with Yaw Sharpmane, who oversees the rituals required to bond with an animal companion.",al={"why get this quest","hunters path purpose","hunter pet quest start"},it="purpose"},{q="Who sends you on this quest?",a="The quest is offered by tauren hunter trainers—such as those stationed in Camp Narache—once an aspiring hunter has gained enough experience and mastery over the basics of survival and marksmanship.",al={"who gives the hunters path","hunter trainer quest"}},{q="Who is Yaw Sharpmane?",a="Yaw Sharpmane is a respected tauren hunter trainer residing in Bloodhoof Village. He is entrusted with teaching young hunters the dangerous art of approaching, calming, and taming the wild beasts of Mulgore.",al={"who is sharpmane","yaw sharpmane role"},it="identity"},{q="What happens after completing this quest?",a="Upon reporting to Yaw Sharpmane, he begins the series of 'Taming the Beast' trials. These tasks require the hunter to venture into the wild with a taming rod and pacify several native creatures before earning the right to train a permanent companion.",al={"what comes next","next quest after hunters path"},it="next_step"},{q="Why do tauren hunters bond with beasts instead of just hunting them?",a="In tauren tradition, beasts are kindred spirits of the Earth Mother. A bonded animal is not a servant, but a companion and partner on the hunt, embodying mutual respect and shared survival on the plains.",al={"tauren pet lore","why tame pets tauren"},it="history"},{q="Can hunters of other races take this specific quest?",a="This particular version of the quest is tailored specifically to tauren hunters training in Mulgore. Other Horde races, such as orcs and trolls, receive equivalent training directives suited to their home territories in Durotar.",al={"is this tauren only","can orcs do hunters path tauren"},gp=true},{q="Why does your trainer send you to Yaw Sharpmane?",a="Your trainer recognizes that you have grown experienced enough in the wild to take the next crucial step in a hunter's life: bonding with and training an animal companion. They send you to Yaw Sharpmane in Bloodhoof Village because he possesses the specialized knowledge and ancient traditions required to guide you through the trials of taming beasts.",al={"why see yaw sharpmane","trainer motive hunters path","why send me to bloodhoof village"},it="motive",rc="Tauren",add=true}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="This quest marks the moment an archer or rifleman transitions into a true beastmaster, taking the first step toward acquiring a loyal combat companion.",["race:Tauren"]="For the Shu'halo, learning to tame wild beasts is a spiritual extension of their reverence for nature and the Earth Mother's creation.",["faction:Horde"]="Horde hunters rely heavily on ferocious frontier beasts to act as front-line defenders and trackers in the harsh wilderness of Kalimdor."},kw={"beast","bloodhoof village","camp narache","hunter's path","mulgore","pet quest","taming","yaw sharpmane"},rel={"npc:yaw-sharpmane","quest:6061","quest:6081","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren"},m={["end"]="Yaw Sharpmane",level="10",faction="Horde",id=6065},src={"The Hunter's Path (Tauren)"}}
E["quest:6087"] = {t="quest",n="Taming the Beast",z="mulgore",s="This trial marks the second stage of a tauren hunter's initiation into the art of beast taming. Guided by hunter trainer Yaw Sharpmane in Bloodhoof Village, the novice is charged with mastering the swift and fearless prairie stalker.",h="This trial marks the second stage of a tauren hunter's initiation into the art of beast taming.",sec={{t="The Stalker's Lessons",b="Having successfully mastered the defensive endurance of the adult plainstrider, Yaw Sharpmane challenges the young hunter to look beyond mere survival. The prairie stalker represents an entirely different hunting philosophy: relentless speed, pack coordination, and fearless aggression. To tame one requires approaching a cunning predator without breaking stride, mirroring the wolf's own decisive nature.",sp=0},{t="Muscle to Muscle",b="For the tauren, hunting with a beast is never about servitude or raw subjugation. Yaw Sharpmane stresses that the hunter and predator must fight as one cohesive unit, bound by mutual respect and shared rhythm. By bonding with the prairie stalker under the open skies of Mulgore, the hunter learns to complement a partner's assault and move in tandem during the heat of battle.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What animal does Yaw Sharpmane ask me to tame in this trial?",a="This second lesson requires you to tame a prairie stalker, one of the swift wolves that prowl the rolling plains of Mulgore.",al={"what beast do i tame","which pet is next","tame prairie stalker"},it="target"},{q="Why does a tauren hunter learn to tame wolves?",a="Yaw Sharpmane teaches that the prairie stalker embodies speed and fearlessness. By bonding with such a predator, the hunter internalizes those exact traits, learning how to coordinate attacks seamlessly alongside a swift hunting partner.",al={"why wolves","prairie stalker lore","why tame a wolf"},it="motive"},{q="Where can prairie stalkers usually be found?",a="Prairie stalkers roam the grassy plains of central and northern Mulgore, frequently stalking the prairies surrounding Bloodhoof Village.",al={"prairie stalker location","where are prairie stalkers","find prairie stalker"},gp=true},{q="Do I get to keep the prairie stalker after the quest?",a="No. The taming rod creates only a temporary bond for training purposes. Once you complete the entire series of trials, you will gain the permanent ability to tame whatever wild companion you choose.",al={"can i keep the wolf","keep prairie stalker","is this my permanent pet"},gp=true},{q="Who is Yaw Sharpmane?",a="Yaw Sharpmane is the resident hunter trainer stationed in Bloodhoof Village. He guides young tauren through the spiritual and practical milestones required to master the wilds of Mulgore.",al={"who is yaw","sharpmane lore","yaw bloodhoof"},it="identity"},{q="What comes after taming the prairie stalker?",a="Once Yaw Sharpmane confirms your mastery over the wolf, he will send you to complete the third and final trial of the chain, testing your resolve against the stealthy swoop.",al={"next quest","what is after prairie stalker","third taming quest"},it="next_step"},{q="Why does Yaw Sharpmane send me to tame the beast instead of handling it himself?",a="This is not a task or chore to be delegated; it is a sacred rite of passage in your training as a hunter. Yaw Sharpmane already knows the ways of the wild and commands his own bond with nature. To become a true hunter of the Earthmother, you must walk the path yourself, learning through firsthand trial how to face a swift, fearless predator and bind your spirits together into a unified team.",al={"why me yaw sharpmane","why must i tame the prairie stalker","why doesn't yaw tame it"},it="why_me",add=true}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="This rite of passage bridges foundational survival techniques with offensive coordination, teaching you how to fight side-by-side with a fast-striking companion.",["race:Tauren"]="Unlike cultures that view pets purely as working animals, the Shu'halo view beast taming as forging a sacred kinship under the watchful grace of the Earthmother."},kw={"bloodhoof village","hunter trial","mulgore","prairie stalker","taming rod","taming the beast","tauren hunter","yaw sharpmane"},rel={"npc:yaw-sharpmane","subzone:bloodhoof-village","quest:6061","quest:6081","quest:6065","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren"},m={start="Yaw Sharpmane",level="10",faction="Horde",id=6087},src={"Taming the Beast (Tauren 2)"}}
E["quest:6088"] = {t="quest",n="Taming the Beast",z="mulgore",s="This trial marks the final lesson in a young tauren hunter's training under Yaw Sharpmane in Bloodhoof Village. By using a taming rod to bond with a wild swoop, the hunter proves mastery over creatures of the air and earns the sacred trust of the Earthmother. Completing this task grants the permanent knowledge required to tame and command a lifelong companion.",h="This trial marks the final lesson in a young tauren hunter's training under Yaw Sharpmane in Bloodhoof...",sec={{t="Mastery of the Sky",b="Having already subdued ground-dwelling predators in the rolling plains of Mulgore, Yaw Sharpmane turns your focus toward the open heavens. The swoop is a fierce, sharp-beaked bird of prey known for its sweeping dives and intimidating demeanor. Bringing such a wild, airborne hunter under control tests your reflexes and your calm, demanding that you endure its talons while using the enchanted rod to align your spirit with its instincts.",sp=0},{t="The Earthmother's Gift",b="For the tauren, hunting and animal companionship are fundamentally spiritual pursuits rather than mere subjugation. By mastering creatures of both earth and sky, you demonstrate the balance and respect necessary to walk the plains without disrupting nature. Yaw Sharpmane recognizes this maturity, taking back his temporary taming rod and passing on the lasting techniques of calling, dismissing, and permanently taming a companion of your own choosing.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why do I have to tame a swoop for the final trial?",a="Yaw Sharpmane wants to test your ability against creatures that inhabit the sky. Swoops possess sharp, swift attacks and an imposing presence, proving that a hunter can handle both aerial predators and ground beasts before earning true independence.",al={"why tame swoop","purpose of swoop taming","why a swoop"},it="target"},{q="Do I keep the swoop I tame during this quest?",a="No. The swoop is tamed temporarily using Yaw Sharpmane's taming rod as part of the ritual training. Once you complete the quest, you gain the actual ability to tame any wild beast you wish to keep permanently.",al={"do i keep the swoop","is the swoop my pet","can i keep this bird"},gp=true},{q="Who gives this quest and where are they located?",a="Yaw Sharpmane, the hunter trainer in Bloodhoof Village within central Mulgore, offers this quest as the third and final step of your initial beast taming trials.",al={"quest giver","who starts this","where is yaw sharpmane"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What happens after I return the taming rod?",a="Yaw Sharpmane teaches you the inherent abilities to tame, call, and dismiss beasts on your own. He will also direct you toward Thunder Bluff to learn how to properly train and care for your chosen companion.",al={"what do i learn next","rewards for finishing","what is after this quest"},it="next_step",gp=true},{q="Where can I find wild swoops in Mulgore?",a="Swoops roam the open grasslands and mesa edges throughout central Mulgore, often circling the plains just outside Bloodhoof Village.",al={"swoop locations","where are swoops","find a swoop"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why is pet taming considered a rite of passage for tauren?",a="Tauren culture views beasts not as tools, but as fellow children of the Earthmother. Learning to bond with a companion requires patience, mutual respect, and spiritual balance rather than pure force.",al={"tauren hunter lore","hunter spiritual path","earthmother connection"},it="history"},{q="Why does Yaw Sharpmane want me to tame a swoop?",a="Yaw Sharpmane is guiding you through the final trial of your hunter training. By learning to charm a creature of the skies, you prove your mastery over beasts of all domains. Once you master this last challenge under the grace of the Earthmother, Yaw can finally bestow upon you the permanent power to tame, summon, and dismiss a companion beast of your own choosing.",al={"yaw sharpmane motive","why tame a swoop","why does yaw want this done"},it="motive",add=true},{q="Why does Yaw Sharpmane have me do this trial instead of doing it himself?",a="Yaw Sharpmane has already mastered the hunter's way; his duty now is that of a trainer and elder guide in Bloodhoof Village. Taming is not an errand to be handled by village guards or mentors, but an essential personal rite of passage. You must prove your own spirit and bond with nature before you can be entrusted with a lifelong animal companion.",al={"why me taming the beast","why send me yaw sharpmane","why must i tame the swoop"},it="why_me",add=true}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="This quest completes the foundational training chain that unlocks permanent beast taming, marking the transition from an apprentice to a fully self-reliant scout of the wilds.",["race:Tauren"]="For the Shu'halo, bonding with a wild beast is an act of spiritual communion blessed by the Earthmother, carrying deep cultural significance across the plains of Mulgore."},kw={"bloodhoof village","earthmother","hunter pet","mulgore","swoop","taming rod","taming the beast","yaw sharpmane"},rel={"quest:6081","quest:6065","quest:6061","npc:yaw-sharpmane","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren"},m={start="Yaw Sharpmane",level="10",faction="Horde",id=6088},src={"Taming the Beast (Tauren 3)"}}
E["quest:6089"] = {t="quest",n="Training the Beast",z="mulgore",s="Training the Beast is the culminating rite of passage for young tauren hunters in Mulgore. After demonstrating the spiritual patience and resolve required to soothe the wild beasts of the plains, hunters are sent to Thunder Bluff to learn the lifelong discipline of bonding with, nourishing, and commanding a permanent animal companion.",h="Training the Beast is the culminating rite of passage for young tauren hunters in Mulgore.",sec={{t="The Path to the Great Bluff",b="Having successfully tested their spirit against the dangerous predators of Mulgore—plainstriders, prairie stalkers, and swoops—tauren hunters are judged ready for the true mantle of the hunter. Yaw Sharpmane, the mentor in Bloodhoof Village, entrusts the novice with a journey to Hunter Rise in Thunder Bluff. There stands Holt Thunderhorn, a legendary figure among the shu'halo whose reputation for communing with the wilds is surpassed only by his reverence for the Earthmother.",sp=0},{t="A Sacred Bond",b="To the tauren, a beast is not a simple weapon or thrall; it is a partner granted through harmony with the natural world. Holt Thunderhorn imparts the vital knowledge required to sustain this partnership. A hunter must learn not only how to guide the creature's combat instincts, but how to nourish its body through regular feeding, mend its injuries, and call its spirit back should it fall defending its companion on the plains.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who do I speak to for this quest?",a="You must speak with Holt Thunderhorn, a master hunter located atop Hunter Rise in Thunder Bluff. Yaw Sharpmane directs you to him once your initial trials of taming wild beasts in Mulgore are complete.",al={"quest turn in","who is holt thunderhorn","where is holt"},it="next_step",gp=true},{q="What skills do tauren hunters learn upon completing this quest?",a="Holt Thunderhorn teaches you how to train your pet in new abilities, how to feed your companion to keep it loyal and content, and how to revive your pet should it perish in combat.",al={"what abilities do i get","quest reward skills","what does holt teach"},it="purpose",gp=true},{q="Where does this quest fit in the tauren hunter chain?",a="This is the final quest in the sequence. It follows The Hunter's Path and the three-part trial of Taming the Beast, where the hunter temporarily pacifies an adult plainstrider, a prairie stalker, and a swoop across the plains.",al={"hunter quest chain","pet quest mulgore","training the beast chain"},it="history",gp=true},{q="Why does Holt Thunderhorn hold such high standing among tauren hunters?",a="Holt has spent a lifetime testing himself against Azeroth's most ferocious beasts, commanding multiple powerful companions while maintaining a deep spiritual bond with the Earthmother. His wisdom makes him the principal teacher for young shu'halo hunters in Thunder Bluff.",al={"holt thunderhorn lore","who is holt"},it="identity"},{q="Can hunters tame their permanent pet before turning in this quest?",a="You already have the power to tame wild beasts from the previous trial, but until you speak to Holt Thunderhorn, you lack the knowledge to feed, revive, or teach skills to your chosen companion.",al={"can i tame a pet now","when do i get my pet","do i need training the beast"},gp=true},{q="Why is pet care tied to the Earthmother in tauren culture?",a="Tauren see all living creatures as children of the Earthmother. A hunter does not dominate a beast through sheer force of will, but enters into a spiritual covenant where the hunter provides food and care in exchange for the animal's loyalty and protection.",al={"tauren pet lore","earthmother beasts"},it="ties"},{q="Why does Yaw Sharpmane send you to Holt Thunderhorn?",a="Yaw Sharpmane has taught you how to tame beasts, but commanding and nurturing them requires deeper wisdom. Rather than leave your education half-finished, he directs you to Holt Thunderhorn on Hunter Rise in Thunder Bluff. Holt is among the most revered hunters of the tauren, having bested fierce creatures across Azeroth, and only someone with his profound bond with the Earthmother can properly bestow the sacred responsibilities of raising and training an animal companion.",al={"why does yaw send you away","yaw sharpmane motive","why go to thunder bluff"},it="motive",add=true}},ang={["class:Hunter"]="This rite marks the transition from fighting alone to fighting beside a loyal companion, completing your fundamental training as a warden of the wilderness.",["race:Tauren"]="For the shu'halo, taking an animal companion into battle carries a sacred duty of stewardship and respect for the creations of the Earthmother."},kw={"beast training","holt thunderhorn","hunter rise","mulgore","tauren hunter","thunder bluff","training the beast","yaw sharpmane"},rel={"npc:holt-thunderhorn","npc:yaw-sharpmane","quest:6061","quest:6065","subzone:hunter-rise","zone:thunderbluff","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren"},m={start="Yaw Sharpmane",["end"]="Holt Thunderhorn",level="10",faction="Horde",id=6089},src={"Training the Beast (Tauren)"}}
E["quest:743"] = {t="quest",n="Dangers of the Windfury",z="mulgore",s="Dangers of the Windfury is a rite of martial readiness offered by Ruul Eagletalon in Bloodhoof Village. Young tauren and Horde adventurers are tasked with battling the savage Windfury harpies nesting in the southeastern cliffs of Mulgore. Hunting these feral predators serves as an essential test of skill and survival before journeying beyond the tribal homeland.",h="Dangers of the Windfury is a rite of martial readiness offered by Ruul Eagletalon in Bloodhoof Village.",sec={{t="A Teacher's Assessment",b="In Bloodhoof Village, the veteran warrior Ruul Eagletalon observes all travelers who pass through the heart of Mulgore. For those coming of age or seeking to prove themselves ready for wider adventures, Ruul demands tangible proof of their combat instincts. Survival beyond the sheltered valleys of Mulgore requires poise against aggressive, cunning foes, and Ruul uses the land's fiercest native predators to measure a youth's mettle.",sp=0},{t="The Windfury Threat",b="The Windfury harpies roost along the mountainous southeastern rim of Mulgore, safely off the beaten trails but constantly posing a threat to wandering wildlife and isolated travelers. Vicious and relentlessly hungry for fresh flesh, these avian terrors rely on swift swoops and razor-sharp claws. Defeating them and claiming their talons demonstrates not only the power to defeat an entrenched threat, but the speed required to counter aerial raiders.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Ruul Eagletalon?",a="Ruul Eagletalon is an experienced tauren warrior and teacher residing in Bloodhoof Village. He mentors young adventurers, offering trials to ensure they possess the strength, reflexes, and fortitude needed before setting out into the wider, more perilous corners of Kalimdor.",al={"ruul eagletalon","who gives dangers of the windfury","ruul"},it="identity"},{q="Where are the Windfury harpies found in Mulgore?",a="The Windfury harpies roost along the southeastern mountains of Mulgore. They make their crude nests high along the crags and foothill slopes, secluded away from the main road that cuts through the valley.",al={"windfury harpy location","where are windfury harpies","windfury nest location"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why are the Windfury harpies considered natural enemies of the tauren?",a="Harpies are notoriously aggressive, territorial scavengers that attack anyone trespassing near their roosts. In Mulgore, the Windfury flock is known for its insatiable appetite for fresh meat and relentless aggression toward travelers and grazers alike, making them a perpetual menace to the plains.",al={"why do tauren fight harpies","harpy threat mulgore","windfury lore"},it="hostile"},{q="What proof does Ruul Eagletalon demand from the hunt?",a="Ruul asks adventurers to retrieve eight Windfury Talons directly from the slain harpies. Severing and returning these talons demonstrates mastery over the harpies' primary natural weapons.",al={"dangers of the windfury proof","what to collect for ruul","windfury quest objective"},it="item",gp=true},{q="Why must adventurers undergo this trial before leaving Mulgore?",a="Ruul views the fierce Windfury harpies as an ideal benchmark for martial preparedness. If an adventurer cannot handle the speed, ferocity, and claws of a harpy, they stand little chance against the deadlier dangers brewing beyond Mulgore's defensive bluffs.",al={"purpose of the trial","why do dangers of the windfury","ruul's test"},it="purpose"},{q="Where is Bloodhoof Village located?",a="Bloodhoof Village is situated in central Mulgore near Stonebull Lake, south of the Great Gate and Thunder Bluff. It serves as the primary hub of culture, trade, and training for tauren travelers moving across the plains.",al={"bloodhoof village location","where is bloodhoof","ruul location"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why does Ruul Eagletalon want the Windfury harpies hunted?",a="Ruul Eagletalon is a warrior and mentor charged with preparing young adventurers before they wander beyond the safe boundaries of Mulgore. He sees the ferocious Windfury harpies as a natural trial—a true measure of your martial prowess and readiness to face the harsher threats of the wider world.",al={"why hunt harpies","ruul motive","ruul eagletalon reason"},it="motive",add=true},{q="Who are the Windfury harpies?",a="The Windfury harpies are savage, avian scavengers nesting along the mountain ledges southeast of Bloodhoof Village. Vicious and cunning, they have long preyed upon travelers and wildlife across Mulgore, driven by a relentless hunger for fresh meat and a natural enmity toward the tauren.",al={"windfury harpies","who are windfury","windfury harpies mulgore"},it="target",add=true},{q="Why doesn't Ruul Eagletalon hunt the harpies himself?",a="Ruul is a teacher whose duty in Bloodhoof Village is to foster the next generation of defenders, not fight their battles for them. This task is explicitly a rite of passage: you must prove your own strength against Mulgore's predators before you are considered fit to journey beyond the plains.",al={"why me","why send me","why doesnt ruul do it"},it="why_me",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For young tauren, clearing Windfury nests is both an act of defending the plains and a sacred passage into acknowledged adulthood under the watchful eyes of village elders.",["class:Warrior"]="Ruul Eagletalon's challenge is especially meaningful to aspiring warriors, who must learn to parry swift diving strikes and overpower vicious aerial ambushers with grounded discipline.",["faction:Horde"]="Allies of the tauren who undertake Ruul's trial demonstrate their dedication to safeguarding the Horde's heartland while earning the enduring respect of Bloodhoof Village."},kw={"bloodhoof village","dangers of the windfury","harpies","mulgore","ruul eagletalon","tauren trial","windfury","windfury talons"},rel={"npc:ruul-eagletalon","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Ruul Eagletalon",faction="Horde",id=743},src={"Dangers of the Windfury"}}
E["quest:745"] = {t="quest",n="Sharing the Land",z="mulgore",s="Assigned by Baine Bloodhoof in Bloodhoof Village, this task sends young Horde adventurers to curb the destructive poaching of the local Palemane gnolls. By culling their tanners, skinners, and poachers, you protect Mulgore's wildlife and learn the tauren philosophy of living in balance with the Earth Mother.",h="Assigned by Baine Bloodhoof in Bloodhoof Village, this task sends young Horde adventurers to curb the...",sec={{t="A Land Out of Balance",b="The tauren hold deep reverence for the natural world, taking from it only what is necessary to survive and honoring the Earth Mother in all things. The Palemane gnolls, having settled south of Bloodhoof Village and in a cavern to the west, harbor no such respect. Rather than hunting in moderation, they aggressively butcher wildlife across the plains, leaving stripped carcasses and upset ecosystems in their wake. For a people striving to build a peaceful homeland in Mulgore, this reckless slaughter cannot be tolerated.",sp=0},{t="When Words Fail",b="Baine Bloodhoof initially hoped to resolve the matter peacefully, attempting to open dialogue with the encroaching gnolls. The Palemanes rejected every overture, answering diplomacy with hostility and continued devastation. Seeing that negotiation is futile, Baine turns to force. Young braves are sent directly to their hunting camps to put down the poachers, tanners, and skinners before the grasslands suffer permanent damage.",sp=0},{t="The Circle of Life and Death",b="This mission serves as a rite of passage and a moral lesson for growing champions. Upon returning from the culling, Baine reminds the adventurer that taking life must never be done out of malice, but out of necessity to maintain harmony. Life and death exist as an interconnected circle where one cannot thrive without the other, a foundational truth every guardian of Mulgore must carry forward.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who gives this quest?",a="Baine Bloodhoof, son of High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof, offers this task in Bloodhoof Village in central Mulgore.",al={"quest giver","who starts sharing the land","baine quest"},it="identity"},{q="Why are the tauren targeting the Palemane gnolls?",a="The Palemane gnolls hunt Mulgore's wildlife recklessly, depleting the herds without honoring the natural balance. They also refused all tauren attempts at peaceful communication.",al={"why kill gnolls","palemane problem","what did gnolls do"},it="motive"},{q="Where are these gnolls located?",a="The Palemanes maintain camps to the south of Bloodhoof Village and dwell within the Palemane Rock cave system to the west.",al={"gnoll locations","palemane camp","where to find palemanes"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Did the tauren try to make peace first?",a="Yes. Baine notes that the tauren made several attempts to communicate and share the land, but the gnolls disrespected and rejected every peaceful overture.",al={"did tauren talk to gnolls","palemane diplomacy"},it="history"},{q="What lesson is Baine trying to teach here?",a="Baine wants young defenders to understand that death and life exist as an unbreakable circle. While taking a life is sometimes necessary to defend the balance, it must always be done with deep respect for the Earth Mother.",al={"baine lesson","meaning of sharing the land"},it="purpose"},{q="Who are the Palemane gnolls?",a="They are a primitive, hyena-like tribe native to Mulgore known for their pale fur, territorial aggression, and complete disregard for the harmony of the plains.",al={"who are palemanes","palemane tribe"},it="people"},{q="Who are you sent against in Sharing the Land?",a="Baine sends you against the Palemane gnolls who have encroached upon the plains south of Bloodhoof Village and taken shelter in a western cave. These poachers, skinners, and tanners have been recklessly slaughtering Mulgore's wildlife without reverence for balance or the Earth Mother. Because they violently spurned every peaceful tauren overture, force is the only measure left to halt their poaching.",al={"sharing the land targets","who do i kill sharing the land","palemane gnolls sharing the land"},it="target",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For young tauren, answering Baine's call is an essential step in learning the sacred duty of defending Mulgore and honoring the Earth Mother's balance.",["class:Hunter"]="The Palemane skinners and poachers represent the antithesis of a true hunter, slaughtering wild creatures with cruelty and waste rather than respect.",["class:Druid"]="Druids recognize that unchecked poaching disrupts the spirit of the plains, requiring immediate intervention to heal the land's harmony.",["faction:Horde"]="Assisting Baine Bloodhoof strengthens the bond between new Horde adventurers and the leadership of the fledgling tauren nation."},kw={"baine bloodhoof","bloodhoof village","gnolls","mulgore","palemane","poachers","sharing the land","skinners","tanners"},rel={"npc:baine-bloodhoof","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore","topic:gnoll"},m={start="Baine Bloodhoof",level="6",faction="Horde",id=745},src={"Sharing the Land (Classic)"}}
E["quest:746"] = {t="quest",n="Dwarven Digging",z="mulgore",s="Dwarven Digging is a quest in Mulgore given by Baine Bloodhoof in Bloodhoof Village. Concerned that dwarven excavations will enrage the native beasts and defile the sacred earth, Baine tasks young Horde defenders with sabotaging their efforts by confiscating and shattering their pickaxes.",h="Dwarven Digging is a quest in Mulgore given by Baine Bloodhoof in Bloodhoof Village.",sec={{t="Trespassers in the Hills",b="Northwest of Bloodhoof Village, dwarven prospectors have begun staking out a dig site in the rolling hills of Mulgore. The Explorer's League and its excavation crews seek titan relics and earthen secrets across Kalimdor, but their operations rarely respect indigenous lands or customs. To the tauren, the earth is a living mother to be revered, not stripped and hollowed for stones and metal.",sp=0},{t="Defending the Sacred Earth",b="Baine Bloodhoof recognizes that the dwarven machinery and relentless blasting threaten the fragile harmony of the plains. Beyond the physical destruction, the commotion agitates the local wildlife, stirring the plains kodos into frenzy. By smashing the prospectors' tools at their own forges, the tauren hope to send a blunt message and stall the encroachment before lasting harm is done.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why are dwarves digging in Mulgore?",a="Dwarven prospectors believe hidden secrets and artifacts lie deep within the earth of central Kalimdor. However, their disregard for tribal territory and violent mining methods have brought them into direct conflict with the tauren.",al={"dwarves in mulgore","why are prospectors here","dwarven excavation mulgore"},it="why_here"},{q="Who gives the quest Dwarven Digging?",a="The quest is given by Baine Bloodhoof, the young son of High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof, located at Bloodhoof Village in central Mulgore.",al={"quest giver dwarven digging","who starts dwarven digging"},gp=true},{q="Why does Baine want their tools smashed instead of just stolen?",a="Baine wants to render the equipment utterly useless to immediately halt the excavation. Destroying the tools at the forges sends a clear symbolic and practical message that the land will not yield its secrets through brute force and desecration.",al={"why break the picks","smashing dwarven tools"},it="motive"},{q="How does the digging affect the wildlife of Mulgore?",a="The loud digging and deep scarring of the ground severely agitates the native creatures, particularly the massive kodos roaming the hills, turning peaceful herds irritable and aggressive.",al={"angry kodos","why are beasts mad"},it="danger"},{q="Where can the picks be smashed?",a="The picks can be hammered into broken scrap at any functional forge. Players can use the forge located directly at the dwarven excavation camp itself, or carry them to the blacksmiths in Thunder Bluff.",al={"where is the forge","smash prospector picks forge"},gp=true},{q="Does this quest tie into the larger Explorer's League presence in Kalimdor?",a="Yes. Dwarf expeditions from Ironforge have established outposts across Kalimdor, including Mulgore, the Barrens, and Southern Kalimdor, frequently inciting skirmishes with Horde races over territorial sovereignty and sacred grounds.",al={"explorers league mulgore","ironforge in kalimdor"},it="ties"},{q="What are the Broken Tools for?",a="The Broken Tools serve as tangible proof that you have put a stop to the dwarves' operations. Rather than simply driving off the prospectors or taking their equipment, Baine Bloodhoof wants their picks smashed at a forge and returned to him to ensure their work is halted and a clear message is sent against defiling the sacred earth.",al={"broken tools","why broken tools","purpose of broken tools"},it="item",add=true},{q="Who are the dwarves digging in Mulgore?",a="They are dwarven prospectors who have set up an excavation camp in the northwestern hills of Mulgore. Driven by rumors that ancient secrets lie buried beneath the plains, they seek historical relics and titan lore, heedless of the spiritual defilement their quarries inflict upon the land and its wildlife.",al={"dwarves in mulgore","who are the dwarven diggers","prospectors in mulgore"},it="target",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The Earth Mother's body is sacred, and seeing foreign intruders bore into Mulgore's rolling steppes is an affront that demands an immediate, firm response.",["faction:Horde"]="Alliance-aligned dwarves encroaching on Horde heartlands undermines the fragile peace established after the Third War, showing blatant disregard for tribal borders.",["class:Shaman"]="Shaman understand that disturbing the bedrock through careless excavation upsets the elemental balance and provokes unrest among the land's spirits and creatures.",["profession:Mining"]="While miners revere the wealth hidden in stone, dwarven methods in Mulgore bypass respect and harmony, opting for reckless destruction."},kw={"baine bloodhoof","bloodhoof village","dwarf","forge","kodo","mulgore","picks","prospector"},rel={"subzone:bloodhoof-village","npc:baine-bloodhoof","zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff"},m={start="Baine Bloodhoof",level="8",faction="Horde",id=746,ri={"Broken Tools"}},src={"Dwarven Digging"}}
E["quest:747"] = {t="quest",n="The Hunt Begins",z="mulgore",s="The Hunt Begins is a foundational rite of passage for young adventurers in Camp Narache on Red Cloud Mesa. Tasked by Grull Hawkwind, initiates are sent to hunt local plainstriders to provide meat and feathers for the village. Fulfilling this task marks the first step toward earning the respect of Chief Hawkwind and eventually gaining entry into Thunder Bluff.",h="The Hunt Begins is a foundational rite of passage for young adventurers in Camp Narache on Red Cloud Mesa.",sec={{t="A Tribe's Sustenance",b="On the heights of Red Cloud Mesa, survival and tradition intertwine. The tauren hunt out of necessity to feed and clothe their people, while also viewing the stalk and chase as a vital test of discipline and sport. When the stores of Camp Narache run lean, the community looks to its rising youth to gather fresh plainstrider meat for cooking and sturdy feathers for tribal garments.",sp=0},{t="Earning Recognition",b="No aspiring hero simply walks off Red Cloud Mesa into the wider reaches of Mulgore without proving their worth. Grull Hawkwind serves as a primary judge of character for the newcomers, insisting that one must prove their capability before stepping before his father, Chief Hawkwind. Bringing in a clean hunt demonstrates not only self-reliance, but devotion to supporting the tribe.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who gives this quest?",a="Grull Hawkwind offers this task in Camp Narache. He is the son of the village leader, Chief Hawkwind, and acts as an early mentor to young hunters and adventurers on Red Cloud Mesa.",al={"quest giver","who starts the hunt begins","grull hawkwind"},it="identity"},{q="Why are plainstriders hunted instead of other beasts?",a="Plainstriders are plentiful across the mesa and offer both nutritious meat to replenish dwindling food stores and durable feathers used in traditional tauren clothing and adornments.",al={"why plainstriders","plainstrider meat and feathers","hunting plainstriders"},it="target"},{q="Why do I have to prove myself before seeing Chief Hawkwind?",a="Tauren society places high value on practical contribution and hunting prowess. Grull Hawkwind requires initiates to demonstrate basic survival skills and loyalty to the village before they are granted audience and deeper responsibilities by the chief.",al={"why prove to chief hawkwind","chief hawkwind approval","why hunt first"},it="why_me"},{q="What is the broader goal of these trials on Red Cloud Mesa?",a="The mesa acts as a proving ground. Once a young tauren proves their capability to Chief Hawkwind and the elders of Camp Narache, they are deemed ready to descend into the wider plains of Mulgore and enter the capital city of Thunder Bluff.",al={"mesa trials purpose","why am i on red cloud mesa","leaving red cloud mesa"},it="purpose"},{q="What comes after this hunt?",a="Successfully bringing back the meat and feathers earns Grull's approval, leading directly into 'The Hunt Continues', where initiates track larger, more elusive game across the mesa.",al={"next quest","followup","what is after the hunt begins"},it="next_step"},{q="Do tauren only hunt for survival?",a="While sustenance and resources like leather, bone, and feathers are essential, tauren also practice hunting as sport and spiritual communion, sharpening instincts that keep their tribes alert and strong.",al={"tauren hunting philosophy","hunt for sport","why do tauren hunt"}},{q="Why does Grull Hawkwind want this done?",a="Grull Hawkwind sees promise in you and wants you to prove your worth to the tribe and his father, Chief Hawkwind. Camp Narache's stores of meat have run low, and the camp needs fresh feathers. By sending you to harvest them, he tests your hunting instincts while restocking the village's essential provisions.",al={"why does grull want plainstriders hunted","grull hawkwind motive","why hunt plainstriders for grull"},it="motive",add=true},{q="What is the Plainstrider Meat for?",a="The meat is gathered to replenish Camp Narache's dwindling food stores. Survival on Red Cloud Mesa demands reliable sustenance, and hunting plainstriders provides the fresh meat needed to feed the young hunters and elders dwelling in the camp.",al={"what is plainstrider meat used for","why collect plainstrider meat","plainstrider meat use"},it="item",add=true},{q="What is the Plainstrider Feather for?",a="Plainstrider feathers are used by the tauren of Camp Narache to craft clothing and adornments. The tribe relies on every part of the beasts they hunt, turning sturdy plumage into practical attire and regalia suited for life on the mesa.",al={"what are plainstrider feathers used for","why collect plainstrider feathers","plainstrider feather use"},it="item",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For young shu'halo, taking down your first mesa plainstriders is an essential rite of passage, confirming your bond with the land and your duty to feed Camp Narache.",["class:Hunter"]="Stalking the swift plainstriders of Red Cloud Mesa serves as the earliest practical exercise in marksmanship, tracking, and honoring the beasts that sustain you."},kw={"camp narache","chief hawkwind","feathers","grull hawkwind","hunt begins","mulgore","plainstrider","red cloud mesa"},rel={"npc:grull-hawkwind","npc:chief-hawkwind","subzone:camp-narache","zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff","topic:tauren"},m={start="Grull Hawkwind",level="2",faction="Horde",id=747,ri={"Plainstrider Feather","Plainstrider Meat"}},src={"The Hunt Begins"}}
E["quest:748"] = {t="quest",n="Poison Water",z="mulgore",s="Poison Water begins the tauren effort to purge pollution from Mulgore's sacred wells. Entrusted by Mull Thunderhorn in Bloodhoof Village, young Horde defenders gather natural components from the plains to craft a restorative ritual totem.",h="Poison Water begins the tauren effort to purge pollution from Mulgore's sacred wells.",sec={{t="A Land Desecrated",b="The grassy plains of Mulgore are sacred ground to the tauren, but insidious forces have begun threatening their vital lifeblood. Mull Thunderhorn, an elder stationed in Bloodhoof Village, discovers that goblin interlopers and their lackeys have tainted the precious water wells that sustain the tribes and the local wildlife. The pollution strikes directly at the spiritual heart of the nomadic people who recently settled here.",sp=0},{t="Gathering Nature's Essence",b="To counteract industrial contaminants, Mull relies on traditional tauren shamanism rather than crude remedies. Cleansing the Winterhoof Water Well requires crafting a potent ritual totem imbued with the vitality of Mulgore's indigenous predators. He sends hunters to track prairie wolves for their paws and mature plainstriders for their talons across the southwestern plains, enlisting the strength of the land to heal its own wounds.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who poisoned the water wells in Mulgore?",a="Goblin encroachers and their hirelings are responsible for fouling the sacred watering holes across Mulgore. Their destructive industrial operations and disregard for natural balance threaten both the tauren tribes and local wildlife.",al={"who tainted the wells","who poisoned mulgore","who polluted the water"},it="threat"},{q="Why does Mull Thunderhorn need wolf paws and plainstrider talons?",a="Mull crafts traditional cleansing totems using parts harvested from Mulgore's native beasts. By harnessing the innate spirits and strength of the plains' predators, he creates a focus powerful enough to conduct a water-cleansing ritual.",al={"why wolf paws","why plainstrider talons","totem materials"},it="item"},{q="Which well does this quest prepare to cleanse?",a="This task gathers materials specifically to craft the cleansing totem for the Winterhoof Water Well, the first in a line of polluted wells that require ritual purification.",al={"what well is this for","which water well"},it="target"},{q="Where is Mull Thunderhorn located?",a="Mull Thunderhorn resides in Bloodhoof Village, the primary tauren settlement situated in central Mulgore around the waters of Stonebull Lake.",al={"mull thunderhorn location","where is mull","where to turn in poison water"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Where can the necessary wolves and plainstriders be found?",a="Prairie wolves and adult plainstriders wander the rolling plains southwest of Bloodhoof Village.",al={"where to find prairie wolves","plainstrider location","hunting grounds"},it="target",gp=true},{q="What happens after Mull receives the paws and talons?",a="Mull binds the harvested components into a Winterhoof Cleansing Totem and tasks you with carrying it directly to the Winterhoof Water Well to perform the purification rite.",al={"next quest after poison water","what do i do next"},it="next_step"},{q="Why does Mull Thunderhorn want this done?",a="Goblins and their hirelings have corrupted the sacred water wells across Mulgore, striking at the very lifeblood of the tauren homeland. For Mull Thunderhorn and the people of Bloodhoof Village, leaving the wells tainted invites sickness and defiles the sacred balance of the earth. Purifying them is an urgent spiritual and practical duty.",al={"mull thunderhorn motive","why cleanse the water wells","why poison water quest"},it="motive",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The desecration of fresh water strikes at the core of the Earth Mother's gifts, making the cleansing ritual a fundamental duty for any tauren coming of age in Mulgore.",["class:Shaman"]="Restoring polluted waters through beast-empowered totems illustrates foundational tauren shamanism: working alongside the local wildlife to mend elemental disturbances.",["class:Druid"]="Goblins poisoning fresh springs represents a direct assault on the natural balance, demanding an immediate response to preserve Mulgore's fragile flora and fauna."},kw={"bloodhoof village","cleansing totem","mulgore","mull thunderhorn","plainstrider","poison water","prairie wolf","winterhoof water well"},rel={"npc:mull-thunderhorn","subzone:winterhoof-water-well","zone:mulgore","topic:goblin"},m={start="Mull Thunderhorn",level="5",faction="Horde",id=748,ri={"Plainstrider Talon","Prairie Wolf Paw"}},src={"Poison Water (Classic)"}}
E["quest:749"] = {t="quest",n="The Ravaged Caravan",z="mulgore",s="Morin Cloudstalker directs travelers to examine the wreckage of an ambushed Venture Company caravan near the northern shore of Stonebull Lake. Although the tauren torched the convoy in retribution for lost comrades, goblin salvagers returned to recover intact cargo. Investigating the surviving supply crates reveals the true industrial scale of the Venture Company's operations in Mulgore.",h="Morin Cloudstalker directs travelers to examine the wreckage of an ambushed Venture Company caravan near the...",sec={{t="Retribution by the Lake",b="The charred remnants along the northern bank of Stonebull Lake are the result of a swift strike by the tauren. Grieving the loss of two companions killed in an earlier skirmish, Morin Cloudstalker and his warriors ambushed an armed Venture Company supply convoy. Intending to obliterate the intruders' efforts, the tauren torched the wagons and abandoned the smoking ruin, presuming fire would consume whatever the goblins sought to haul.",sp=0},{t="Unfinished Business",b="The tauren soon noticed Venture Company salvagers scouring the blackened debris, suggesting their fire had failed to destroy the most crucial cargo. Rather than risk further lives blindly, Morin dispatches scouts to inspect the wreckage. Among the soot-stained wreckage rest intact crates marked with the insignia of the Venture Company Mining Division, packed and destined for central processing facilities.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why was the caravan destroyed?",a="Morin Cloudstalker and his tauren warriors ambushed and torched the caravan in retaliation after losing two of their scouts in a previous clash with the Venture Company.",al={"who destroyed the caravan","why is the caravan burned","caravan attack"},it="history"},{q="Where is the Ravaged Caravan located?",a="The burnt wagons sit along the northern shore of Stonebull Lake, east of Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore.",al={"caravan location","where is the caravan","ravaged caravan spot"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why are the goblins returning to the wreckage?",a="The Venture Company sent salvagers because fire failed to destroy all their cargo. They are attempting to retrieve durable supply crates stamped for shipment to their central processing operations.",al={"why are salvagers here","venture co salvagers","what do the goblins want"},it="why_here"},{q="What is inside the supply crates?",a="The crates bear the insignia of the Venture Company Mining Division and contain mineral resources labeled for refinement and processing at their primary industrial plants.",al={"crate contents","what is in the crate","sealed supply crate"},it="item"},{q="Who is Morin Cloudstalker?",a="Morin Cloudstalker is a tauren scout and tracker stationed east of Bloodhoof Village, tasked with monitoring hostile incursions across central Mulgore, particularly from the Venture Company.",al={"who is morin","morin cloudstalker role"},it="identity"},{q="What does this caravan reveal about the Venture Company?",a="It shows that the Venture Company's presence in Mulgore is not a mere exploratory team, but an organized industrial extraction effort complete with armed supply lines and centralized refining networks.",al={"venture co mulgore presence","why are goblins in mulgore"},it="threat"},{q="Why does Morin Cloudstalker want the caravan crates examined?",a="Morin and his braves ambushed the Venture Company caravan by Stonebull Lake to avenge fallen comrades, burning the wagons and assuming the fire would wipe out anything useful. Seeing goblin salvagers picking through the wreckage alerted him that something valuable or dangerous survived the flames. He needs you to inspect the crates to learn what the Venture Company was moving through Mulgore and why they are so eager to retrieve it.",al={"why examine caravan","morin cloudstalker motive","why search supply crates"},it="motive",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The desecration of the plains by Venture Company machines and timber-clearing strikes directly at your ancestral homeland, making the recovery and destruction of their supplies vital to the defense of Mulgore.",["faction:Horde"]="Unchecked corporate expansion into Mulgore threatens the stability of Thunder Bluff, requiring Horde scouts to disrupt Venture Company logistics.",["profession:Mining"]="The sturdy crates and logistics notes demonstrate the Venture Company's aggressive mineral surveying and extraction operations underway across Mulgore."},kw={"morin cloudstalker","mulgore","ravaged caravan","stonebull lake","supply crate","venture co","venture company"},rel={"npc:morin-cloudstalker","subzone:ravaged-caravan","subzone:stonebull-lake","quest:764","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Morin Cloudstalker",["end"]="Sealed Supply Crate",faction="Horde",id=749},src={"The Ravaged Caravan (Horde) (1)"}}
E["quest:751"] = {t="quest",n="The Ravaged Caravan",z="mulgore",s="Investigating a broken transport in Mulgore reveals that goblin industrialists have established an illegal foothold in tauren territory. By recovering documents from a sealed crate, scouts confirm the Venture Company is systematically stripping the land's mineral wealth. This discovery rallies the Bloodhoof Outrunners to mount an active defense of their ancestral plains.",h="Investigating a broken transport in Mulgore reveals that goblin industrialists have established an illegal...",sec={{t="Evidence of Exploitation",b="The wreckage of a ravaged caravan east of Bloodhoof Village tells an unsettling story. Within a sealed supply crate lies a dense cache of mined ore and minerals alongside a bound sheaf of Venture Company documents. This paperwork provides irrefutable proof that the greedy Venture Company has begun an unpermitted mining operation deep in the tranquil valleys of Mulgore.",sp=0},{t="The Outrunners' Vigil",b="Morin Cloudstalker and his fellow Outrunners have maintained a cautious watch over Mulgore's borders, mindful of outside factions looking to exploit their peaceful homeland. Delivering the retrieved documents confirms Morin's worst fears: smoke rising over the eastern foothills signals an escalating commercial invasion. The tauren are left with no choice but to push back against the defilers before their hills are gutted entirely.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is inside the sealed supply crate?",a="Prying open the crate reveals a packed assortment of mined ore, raw minerals, and a sheaf of Venture Company documents outlining their operations.",al={"supply crate loot","what is in the crate","crate contents"},it="item"},{q="Who is Morin Cloudstalker?",a="Morin Cloudstalker is a tauren Outrunner stationed on the road east of Bloodhoof Village toward the Barrens border, tasked with patrolling Mulgore and tracking foreign threats.",al={"who is morin","morin cloudstalker role","outrunner morin"},it="identity"},{q="Why is the Venture Company in Mulgore?",a="The Venture Company seeks to ruthlessly exploit Mulgore's rich natural resources and veins of mineral ore, carving into the hills without regard for the earth or its native people.",al={"why are goblins here","venture co goal mulgore","what do goblins want"},it="why_here"},{q="Where did the caravan come from?",a="The caravan belonged to the Venture Company, transporting mined ore and operational paperwork out from their foothill operations before it was destroyed along the trail.",al={"whose caravan was it","caravan origin","who attacked the caravan"},it="origin"},{q="Where does this investigation lead next?",a="Armed with the documents, Morin Cloudstalker directs warriors to directly confront the Venture Company's leadership and dismantle their mining operation in the eastern foothills.",al={"what comes next","next quest after ravaged caravan","following up on the caravan"},sp=1,it="next_step"},{q="Why does this threaten the tauren?",a="The tauren view the land as sacred and revere the Earth Mother. The Venture Company's reckless strip mining and industrial pollution actively defile Mulgore's pristine landscape.",al={"why do tauren care","venture co threat","threat to mulgore"},it="threat"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For the tauren, seeing crude industrial machinery tear into the ancestral plains of Mulgore is an affront to the Earth Mother and an act of war against their newfound peace.",["faction:Horde"]="The Horde relies on the loyalty and stability of Mulgore; leaving foreign merchant cartels to despoil its hills would threaten the safety of Thunder Bluff itself.",["class:Druid"]="Druids hold the balance of the land sacred, making the destructive, smoke-choked strip mining of the Venture Company a desecration that must be cleansed.",["profession:Mining"]="The high-grade ores packed within the crate prove the eastern hills of Mulgore conceal rich subterranean veins that outsiders are racing to exploit."},kw={"bloodhoof village","morin cloudstalker","mulgore","outrunners","ravaged caravan","supply crate","venture co","venture co documents"},rel={"npc:morin-cloudstalker","quest:764","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Sealed Supply Crate",["end"]="Morin Cloudstalker",faction="Horde",id=751},src={"The Ravaged Caravan (Horde) (2)"}}
E["quest:752"] = {t="quest",n="A Humble Task",z="mulgore",s="A Humble Task is an early rite of duty given by Chief Hawkwind in Camp Narache. As the Chieftain attends to pressing tribal concerns on the Red Cloud Mesa, he asks a young adventurer to venture southeast and check on his elderly mother at the local water well. The request emphasizes communal responsibility, elder reverence, and the daily harmony essential to the tauren way of life.",h="A Humble Task is an early rite of duty given by Chief Hawkwind in Camp Narache.",sec={{t="Communal Harmony and Duty",b="In the shadow of Camp Narache, every member of the tribe is expected to pull their weight and share in the harmony of life. For young tauren coming of age, tribal duty is not measured solely in battle prowess against quillboars or predators; it begins with basic social responsibility. Chief Hawkwind demonstrates this ethos directly: bound to his post by administrative and defensive leadership, he places his trust in novice adventurers to watch over the community's vulnerable elders.",sp=0},{t="The Well Southeast of Narache",b="Mulgore's Red Cloud Mesa relies on vital natural water wells scattered beyond the perimeter of the encampment. Early in the morning, Greatmother Hawkwind left the village unattended to retrieve fresh water from the southeastern spring. In a land stalked by territorial wildlife and encroaching Bristleback quilboars, an elder remaining absent for hours raises quiet concern, prompting Hawkwind to send aid to ensure she is unharmed.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who offers this quest and where do I find them?",a="Chief Hawkwind, the leader of Camp Narache on the Red Cloud Mesa in Mulgore, offers the quest. He stands in the central pavilion of the camp overseeing local affairs.",al={"who gives a humble task","where to pick up quest","chief hawkwind location"},gp=true},{q="Where is Greatmother Hawkwind located?",a="Greatmother Hawkwind is located just southeast of Camp Narache, tending to the water well on the lower elevation of the mesa.",al={"greatmother hawkwind location","where is the mother","water well location"},gp=true},{q="Why doesn't Chief Hawkwind check on his mother himself?",a="Chief Hawkwind must remain at Camp Narache to oversee pressing tribal matters and guide young braves. As a chieftain, he relies on the tribe's collective responsibility and trusts rising adventurers to look after village affairs in his stead.",al={"why cant chief go","why doesn't hawkwind check","chief hawkwind busy"},it="why_me"},{q="Is Greatmother Hawkwind in danger at the well?",a="While the surrounding plains host aggressive mountain cougars and striders, Greatmother Hawkwind is found safe and sound beside the well, pleasantly surprised that someone went out of their way to check on an elder.",al={"is she safe","is greatmother attacked","danger at water well"},it="danger"},{q="What cultural significance does this quest carry for the tauren?",a="Tauren culture places immense value on family, community cohesion, and reverence for elders. The quest teaches young heroes that true strength is rooted in humble service and protecting one's kin rather than chasing personal glory.",al={"tauren culture humble task","why is this a humble task","tauren elders"}},{q="What happens after finding Greatmother Hawkwind?",a="Once you speak with Greatmother Hawkwind, she completes the task and asks for further assistance in carrying the filled water pitcher back to the camp.",al={"next quest","a humble task follow up","what to do after finding mother"},it="next_step",gp=true},{q="Why does Chief Hawkwind want you to check on his mother?",a="His elderly mother left Camp Narache early in the morning to draw water from the well to the southeast, and she has been gone for quite a long time. Tauren life is rooted in communal responsibility and mutual care, so while Hawkwind worries for her well-being, his duties as chieftain keep him anchored at the camp.",al={"why does hawkwind want this done","chief hawkwind motive","why check on greatmother hawkwind"},it="motive",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For young tauren braves, answering the Chieftain's call demonstrates the core tenets of the Earth Mother's teachings: respect for elders and unhesitating commitment to the tribe's shared well-being.",["faction:Horde"]="The quiet, pastoral nature of Narache's tasks highlights how the tauren's tranquil communal philosophy contrasts with the harsher, survivalist grit found across the rest of the fledgling Horde."},kw={"a humble task","camp narache","chief hawkwind","greatmother hawkwind","mulgore","red cloud mesa","water well"},rel={"npc:chief-hawkwind","npc:greatmother-hawkwind","subzone:camp-narache","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Chief Hawkwind",["end"]="Greatmother Hawkwind",level="2",faction="Horde",id=752},src={"A Humble Task"}}
E["quest:753"] = {t="quest",n="A Humble Task",z="mulgore",s="A Humble Task is an introductory rite of service in Red Cloud Mesa, where an elder tests a fledgling adventurer's humility and diligence. By carrying fresh well water across the plains to Camp Narache's leader, young tauren learn that serving their people starts with the simplest duties.",h="A Humble Task is an introductory rite of service in Red Cloud Mesa, where an elder tests a fledgling...",sec={{t="Vitality of the Youth",b="Greatmother Hawkwind has walked many trails in her long life, and while her spirit remains steady, her legs no longer carry her across the plains with the speed of her youth. Stationed near the water well on the southern edges of Red Cloud Mesa, she tends to her responsibilities with patient resolve. When aspiring tribal defenders cross her path, she redirects their youthful energy toward practical community needs, offering them a chance to prove their worth through service rather than violence.",sp=0},{t="Service to the Tribe",b="The task asks the initiate to haul a filled water pitcher back northwest to Camp Narache and present it directly to the settlement's leader, Chief Hawkwind, who happens to be the Greatmother's son. In tauren society, survival across the arid plains of Mulgore demands shared labor and reliable supplies of fresh water. Demonstrating respect for the community's elders by shouldering their burdens is considered the cornerstone of becoming a true protector of the tribe.",sp=0},{t="A Path Toward Thunder Bluff",b="Chief Hawkwind sees more than a mere chore completed when a young wanderer arrives bearing water from his mother. In tauren custom, an individual's readiness to champion their people across wider lands begins with humility and an unprompted willingness to help. By demonstrating this quiet discipline, the initiate catches the chief's eye and earns recommendation for the ancient Rites of the Earthmother, the true spiritual tests that open the gates to Thunder Bluff.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Greatmother Hawkwind?",a="Greatmother Hawkwind is an elder of the Hawkwind family stationed at the well in Red Cloud Mesa. She is the mother of Camp Narache's leader, Chief Hawkwind, and guides young tauren in learning humility and tribal values.",al={"greatmother hawkwind","who gives a humble task","hawkwind elder"},it="identity"},{q="Where is the water well located?",a="The water well sits southeast of Camp Narache on the grassy plains of Red Cloud Mesa. Pitchers of fresh well water rest beside it for travelers and camp caretakers to carry back.",al={"water well location","where is the well","find water pitcher"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Who is Chief Hawkwind?",a="Chief Hawkwind is the chieftain overseeing Camp Narache on Red Cloud Mesa. He directs the training and spiritual rites of young tauren, preparing them to venture out and serve the wider tribe in Mulgore.",al={"chief hawkwind","who is the chief","camp narache leader"},it="leader"},{q="Why is this chore treated as an important test?",a="Tauren culture places great value on humility, community support, and respect for elders. Accomplishing unglamorous, everyday duties demonstrates that an adventurer cares about sustaining their people, not just personal glory in battle.",al={"why carry water","why humble task","is this quest important"},it="purpose"},{q="What comes after completing A Humble Task?",a="Completing this task proves your discipline to Chief Hawkwind, who then directs you to undertake the Rites of the Earthmother—a sequence of traditional challenges required of all tauren coming of age.",al={"what comes next","next quest","after humble task"},it="next_step"},{q="Where does this path eventually lead?",a="Earning the respect of the elders in Camp Narache prepares you to leave the secluded mesa behind, journeying into the wider plains of Mulgore and ultimately toward the high mesas of the capital, Thunder Bluff.",al={"future destination","where are we going","thunder bluff goal"}},{q="Why does Greatmother Hawkwind want this done?",a="Greatmother Hawkwind admits that her aging legs lack their former vigor, making simple daily chores around the Red Cloud Mesa slow and tiring. More importantly, she views this routine errand as a vital moral lesson for you. Delivering essential drinking water to her son, Chief Hawkwind, serves as an early trial to see if you possess the humility, patience, and community spirit expected of a true servant of the tribe.",al={"greatmother hawkwind motive","why bring water chief hawkwind","why humble task"},it="motive",add=true},{q="What is the Water Pitcher for?",a="The Water Pitcher is a simple clay vessel drawn fresh from the well on Red Cloud Mesa to provide clean water for Camp Narache. Transporting it tests your dedication to communal chores, ensuring that even fledgling adventurers learn the value of sustaining their kin before pursuing grander martial glory.",al={"water pitcher purpose","why water pitcher","what is the pitcher"},it="item",add=true},{q="Why does Greatmother Hawkwind ask you instead of doing it herself?",a="Greatmother Hawkwind is elderly and admits her legs no longer have their former strength, meaning carrying heavy pitchers takes her far longer than it used to. When an able-bodied novice like you approaches, she seizes the chance to both ease her own burden and test your willingness to perform unglamorous, everyday labor for the camp.",al={"why not greatmother","why ask me for water","why cant hawkwind get water"},it="why_me",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Fetching water for your elders embodies the foundational tauren virtue of communal care, showing you are ready to walk the path of the Earthmother.",["faction:Horde"]="The Hawkwind family's lessons illustrate how the tauren maintain solidarity and survival in Mulgore, forming a peaceful yet unyielding cornerstone of Thrall's Horde."},kw={"camp narache","chief hawkwind","greatmother hawkwind","humble task","mulgore","red cloud mesa","rites of the earthmother","water pitcher"},rel={"quest:752","npc:greatmother-hawkwind","npc:chief-hawkwind","quest:763","subzone:camp-narache","zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff"},m={start="Greatmother Hawkwind",["end"]="Chief Hawkwind",level="3",faction="Horde",id=753,ri={"Water Pitcher"}},src={"A Humble Task (2)"}}
E["quest:754"] = {t="quest",n="Winterhoof Cleansing",z="mulgore",s="Winterhoof Cleansing is a rite entrusted to Horde adventurers by Mull Thunderhorn in Bloodhoof Village. After goblin operations fouled the local springs, a consecrated totem must be carried into the Venture Company's territory to purify the Winterhoof Water Well. Restoring this spring is a vital first step in healing the poisoned waters of central Mulgore.",h="Winterhoof Cleansing is a rite entrusted to Horde adventurers by Mull Thunderhorn in Bloodhoof Village.",sec={{t="A Land Despoiled",b="The grassy plains of Mulgore rely on a delicate network of ancestral water wells to sustain both wild game and the scattered tauren tribes. Recently, ruthless industrial mercenaries of the Venture Company moved into the rolling hills southeast of Bloodhoof Village. Their operations have contaminated the local aquifers, leaving the Winterhoof Water Well rank and toxic. Wild beasts shun the tainted water, and the nearby camps face an impending crisis if the corruption spreads further through the earth.",sp=0},{t="The Ritual of Purifying Waters",b="To counter the industrial filth, Mull Thunderhorn carves and enchants a ritual cleansing totem infused with the blessings of the Earth Mother. However, delivering this remedy requires braving armed Venture Company goons who have staked their claim over the spring. By placing the totem directly at the wellhead, an adventurer dispels the foul runoff, allowing clean, life-giving water to rise once more from the depths of the earth.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where is the Winterhoof Water Well located?",a="The Winterhoof Water Well is located in the hills to the southeast of Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore. It is heavily patrolled by goblin laborers and mercenaries working for the Venture Company.",al={"winterhoof well location","where is winterhoof well","how to find winterhoof well"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why was the water well poisoned?",a="The Venture Company established mining and excavation operations nearby, allowing toxic industrial runoff and refuse to seep directly into the aquifer, rendering the natural spring undrinkable for local wildlife and the tauren.",al={"who poisoned the water","why is the well dirty","venture company well"},it="threat"},{q="Who is Mull Thunderhorn?",a="Mull Thunderhorn is a spiritual elder stationed in Bloodhoof Village. Deeply tuned to the health of the earth, he crafts ceremonial totems to undo the environmental devastation wrought by outside invaders.",al={"who is mull","mull thunderhorn role"},it="identity"},{q="What happens after the well is cleansed?",a="Once the Winterhoof Water Well runs clear and wildlife begins drinking from it safely again, Mull Thunderhorn directs your attention toward other compromised wells in Mulgore, starting with the Thunderhorn Totem.",al={"what comes next","after winterhoof cleansing","what does mull do next"},sp=1,it="next_step"},{q="Who are the guards at the well?",a="The well is occupied by laborers and hirelings employed by the Venture Company, an unscrupulous goblin-led syndicate that strips lands of natural resources with no regard for the local ecosystem.",al={"who guards winterhoof well","what enemies are at the well"},it="target"},{q="How does the cleansing totem work?",a="Carved with sacred tauren rites and warding rituals, the totem draws upon natural spirits and the blessings of the Earth Mother to purge unnatural chemical pollutants from the spring's source.",al={"cleansing totem lore","how does mull totem work"},it="power"},{q="Why does Mull Thunderhorn want the Winterhoof Water Well cleansed?",a="The Winterhoof Water Well is vital to the balance and survival of Mulgore. Its water has been fouled, threatening both the local wildlife and the tribes who depend on it. Mull Thunderhorn has prepared a consecrated cleansing totem, but its power must be brought directly to the site to heal the waters and restore harmony to the land.",al={"why cleanse the winterhoof well","mull thunderhorn motive","why cleanse winterhoof water"},it="motive",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The wells of Mulgore are sacred life-givers gifted by the Earth Mother; defending them against goblin greed is both a civic duty and a holy obligation for every tauren.",["class:Shaman"]="Channeling spirits through totems to reverse unnatural industrial defilement represents the core purpose of a shaman's bond with the elemental waters.",["class:Druid"]="Seeing the immediate return of thirsty wildlife to the purified spring proves the vital necessity of healing nature wherever civilization threatens to despoil it.",["faction:Horde"]="Securing Mulgore's fresh water supply stabilizes the heartlands of the newly settled tauren, strengthening the southern pillar of the young Horde."},kw={"bloodhoof village","cleansing","mulgore","mull thunderhorn","poison","totem","venture company","water well","winterhoof"},rel={"npc:mull-thunderhorn","subzone:winterhoof-water-well","subzone:bloodhoof-village","topic:venture-company","zone:mulgore","topic:goblin"},m={start="Mull Thunderhorn",level="6",faction="Horde",id=754},src={"Winterhoof Cleansing (Classic)"}}
E["quest:756"] = {t="quest",n="Thunderhorn Totem",z="mulgore",s="Thunderhorn Totem is a task given by Mull Thunderhorn in Bloodhoof Village to craft a sacred cleansing totem. To combat goblin-borne pollution spreading through Mulgore's water wells, young Horde champions must gather natural talons from plains predators to bind the land's primal strength into ritual wood.",h="Thunderhorn Totem is a task given by Mull Thunderhorn in Bloodhoof Village to craft a sacred cleansing totem.",sec={{t="Hunting the Plains Predators",b="As the Venture Company's industrial exploitation poisons Mulgore's precious water sources, Mull Thunderhorn works urgently to craft remedies. Creating the Thunderhorn Cleansing Totem requires the natural essences of the plains' most focused hunters: prairie stalkers and flatland cougars. By taking the claws of these predators roaming east and west of Bloodhoof Village, the ritual channels the raw, uncorrupted predatory vigor of the wildlife directly into the totem.",sp=0},{t="The Nature of the Hunt",b="Tauren tradition sees a profound distinction between hunting for sustenance or spiritual balance and the wholesale plunder practiced by the goblins. Mull emphasizes that the purity with which native predators hunt is vital for purifying corrupted earth. Where goblin machinery defiles the springs with oil and industrial waste, the natural strength of the beasts contending with the land provides the spiritual catalyst needed to restore the Thunderhorn Water Well.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why does Mull Thunderhorn need predator claws?",a="Mull uses the claws of prairie stalkers and flatland cougars to channel the pure, primal predatory spirit of the plains into a cleansing totem. He believes the pure spirit of natural hunters counteracts artificial, industrial corruption.",al={"why claws","purpose of stalker claws","cougar claws purpose"},it="item"},{q="Who is corrupting the water in Mulgore?",a="The Venture Company goblins operating in Mulgore are polluting the land and water. Their reckless resource extraction and mining rigs introduce toxic sludge into the aquifers feeding the tribal wells.",al={"who tainted the well","why is the well poisoned","what poisoned mulgore"},it="threat"},{q="Where are the prairie stalkers and flatland cougars found?",a="Both beasts roam the open plains of central Mulgore, primarily spread out across the rolling savannas directly east and west of Bloodhoof Village.",al={"stalker location","where to find cougars","cougar claw locations"},gp=true},{q="What happens after crafting the Thunderhorn Totem?",a="Once Mull carves and enchants the totem using the claws, he sends you directly to the tainted Thunderhorn Water Well to perform the cleansing ritual and drive out the poison.",al={"what next","after thunderhorn totem","totem follow up"},sp=1,it="next_step"},{q="How does this relate to the previous cleansing at Winterhoof Well?",a="This is part of an ongoing tribal effort to preserve Mulgore's sacred waters. The Winterhoof Water Well was purified first, and now the contamination threatens the Thunderhorn well further north.",al={"winterhoof connection","previous totem quest"},it="history"},{q="Why don't the tauren simply use herbal remedies instead of animal claws?",a="Tauren ritual craft draws on distinct elemental and physical aspects of nature. The sharp purity of apex predators provides an active, contending spiritual force specifically required to cut through aggressive goblin industrial poisons.",al={"why not herbs","shamanic totem materials"}},{q="Why does Mull Thunderhorn want this done?",a="The Thunderhorn Water Well is growing more tainted by the hour, poisoned by reckless goblin operations nearby. Mull Thunderhorn needs to craft a sacred cleansing totem to purify the well before the corruption spreads further through the earth and harms his people.",al={"why mull wants this","mull thunderhorn motive","why craft thunderhorn totem"},it="motive",add=true},{q="What are you sent against for the claws?",a="You are sent to hunt the wild predators prowling Mulgore's plains: the prairie stalkers and flatland cougars. These beasts roam naturally to the east and west of Bloodhoof Village, living and hunting as part of the land's natural balance.",al={"what do i hunt","who are the targets","thunderhorn totem prey"},it="target",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Defending Mulgore's aquifers from foreign exploitation is a sacred tribal duty, reflecting the Earth Mother's mandate to live in balance rather than plunder.",["class:Shaman"]="Totemic restoration relies on invoking the living spirits of the land to cleanse water corrupted by unnatural, careless enterprise.",["class:Hunter"]="Gathering the claws requires understanding the native stalking beasts of the plains, honoring the kill to preserve the ecosystem."},kw={"bloodhoof village","cleansing","cougar claws","flatland cougar","mulgore","mull thunderhorn","prairie stalker","stalker claws","thunderhorn totem","water well"},rel={"quest:758","npc:mull-thunderhorn","subzone:thunderhorn-water-well","zone:mulgore","topic:goblin"},m={start="Mull Thunderhorn",level="7",faction="Horde",id=756,ri={"Cougar Claws","Stalker Claws"}},src={"Thunderhorn Totem (Classic)"}}
E["quest:757"] = {t="quest",n="Rite of Strength",z="mulgore",s="The Rite of Strength is the initial trial in the traditional Rites of the Earthmother undertaken by young tauren at Camp Narache. To demonstrate their bravery and defense of the tribe, initiates must cull encroaching Bristleback quilboar in Brambleblade Ravine and present their belts to Chief Hawkwind.",h="The Rite of Strength is the initial trial in the traditional Rites of the Earthmother undertaken by young...",sec={{t="The First Trial",b="For a young tauren seeking recognition from tribal elders and eventual acceptance in Thunder Bluff, completing the Rites of the Earthmother is an essential duty. Bestowed by Seer Graytongue, the Rite of Strength serves as the starting trial. It demands physical courage and decisive action in defense of Red Cloud Mesa, testing whether an initiate can face the tribe's mortal foes without flinching.",sp=0},{t="The Bristleback Threat",b="The chosen crucible for this trial is Brambleblade Ravine, a thorn-choked gash east of Camp Narache occupied by Bristleback quilboar. These aggressive interlopers continuously trespass on ancestral lands, ambushing hunting parties and raiding camp supplies under cover of darkness. Slaying them and claiming their belts serves as both proof of valor and a necessary measure to keep the settlement secure.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of the Rite of Strength?",a="It is the foundational step of the Rites of the Earthmother, designed to test a young tauren's bravery in combat. By eliminating hostile threats to the camp, an initiate earns the right to advance toward true respect in Thunder Bluff.",al={"why do rite of strength","rite of strength lore","purpose of rite of strength"},it="purpose"},{q="Who assigns and judges this trial?",a="Seer Graytongue delivers the charge to the initiate, outlining the trial's spiritual and practical requirements. Once twelve Bristleback belts are gathered, the proof of valor must be presented directly to Chief Hawkwind in Camp Narache.",al={"who gives rite of strength","who turns in rite of strength"},it="identity"},{q="Where is Brambleblade Ravine located?",a="Brambleblade Ravine lies directly to the east of Camp Narache on the Red Cloud Mesa. It is a thorny canyon swarming with hostile Bristleback quilboar.",al={"where is brambleblade ravine","brambleblade location"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why are the Bristlebacks being targeted?",a="The Bristlebacks are encroaching upon the tribe's ancestral hunting grounds. They repeatedly stage ambushes against tauren hunters and steal provisions from Camp Narache in the dark, making them direct enemies of the tribe.",al={"why kill bristlebacks","what did the quilboar do"},it="threat"},{q="Why collect belts instead of other trophies?",a="Belts serve as tangible tokens of defeated warriors, proving that the initiate faced the quilboar in close quarters and brought down adult combatants rather than merely scavenging from a distance.",al={"why bristleback belts","why belts"},it="item"},{q="What comes after completing the Rite of Strength?",a="Passing the Rite of Strength proves one's valor and qualifies the initiate for the subsequent trials of the Earthmother, continuing the formal path of adulthood and spiritual standing within the tribe.",al={"what is after rite of strength","next earthmother rite"},it="next_step"},{q="Why does Seer Graytongue send you to the Bristlebacks?",a="Seer Graytongue guides the youth of Camp Narache through the sacred Rites of the Earthmother. For your first trial, he must test your martial resolve against a real and pressing danger to the tribe. The Bristleback quilboar have been encroaching on tauren lands, ambushing hunters and stealing under cover of darkness. By sending you to confront them, Graytongue ensures you protect your people while proving your strength and earning the tribe's respect.",al={"why does graytongue want this","seer graytongue motive","why rite of strength"},it="motive",add=true},{q="Why are you sent to do this trial alone?",a="The Rite of Strength is a personal rite of passage, not a simple garrison chore. While camp braves guard the perimeter, every young tauren must independently demonstrate valor and martial capability before they are acknowledged as an adult in Thunder Bluff. The trial demands that you face the enemies of your people yourself so the elders know you can defend the tribe when called upon.",al={"why me rite of strength","why not send guards bristlebacks","why do i have to fight bristlebacks"},it="why_me",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Every young tauren begins their journey through these ancient rites, demonstrating readiness to protect their people from the quilboar threat before leaving Red Cloud Mesa.",["faction:Horde"]="Defending the mesa secures one of the Horde's core recruiting grounds, ensuring young warriors and shamans can mature safely to aid the new union."},kw={"brambleblade ravine","bristleback","camp narache","chief hawkwind","earthmother","mulgore","quilboar","rite of strength","seer graytongue"},rel={"quest:763","npc:seer-graytongue","npc:chief-hawkwind","subzone:brambleblade-ravine","subzone:camp-narache","topic:tauren","zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff"},m={start="Seer Graytongue",["end"]="Chief Hawkwind",level="4",faction="Horde",id=757,ri={"Bristleback Belt"}},src={"Rite of Strength (Classic)"}}
E["quest:758"] = {t="quest",n="Thunderhorn Cleansing",z="mulgore",s="Thunderhorn Cleansing is an essential spiritual rite in Mulgore, charging travelers with purifying the corrupted Thunderhorn Water Well. Armed with a ritual totem prepared by Mull Thunderhorn, adventurers restore the contaminated spring vital to the local tribe. Successful purification earns the heartfelt approval of the Thunderhorn ancestors.",h="Thunderhorn Cleansing is an essential spiritual rite in Mulgore, charging travelers with purifying the...",sec={{t="A Tainted Lifeline",b="Water wells across the sweeping plains of Mulgore are vital lifelines for the tauren nomadic tribes who recently settled the grasslands. When foul poisons taint these underground springs, both wildlife and nearby settlements face devastating consequences. The Thunderhorn Water Well, situated north of Bloodhoof Village, fallen victim to contamination, requires deliberate spiritual intervention to heal the troubled land.",sp=0},{t="Ancestral Cleansing",b="To counteract the corruption, Mull Thunderhorn crafts a consecrated Cleansing Totem imbued with spiritual potency. Carrying this relic directly to the poisoned waters allows adventurers to channel ancestral power into the spring, dispersing the toxins and restoring pristine natural balance. Mull receives direct confirmation of the deed's success when the spirits of Thunderhorn ancestors visit him in a dream to express their enduring gratitude.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where is the Thunderhorn Water Well located?",a="The Thunderhorn Water Well lies in the plains north of Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore. It serves as one of several vital watering holes supplying the local tauren clans.",al={"thunderhorn well location","where is well","where do i use totem"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Who initiates this cleansing ritual?",a="Mull Thunderhorn initiates the rite. He can be found in Bloodhoof Village, where he works to fashion specialized totems to heal Mulgore's distressed environment.",al={"who gives quest","mull thunderhorn location","quest giver"},it="identity"},{q="Why must the water wells in Mulgore be cleansed?",a="The wells across Mulgore have fallen prey to unnatural poisoning and corruption, which threatens the delicate balance of the plains and the survival of the tauren tribes who depend on clean water.",al={"why cleanse wells","what happened to the water","why is well poisoned"},it="motive"},{q="How does Mull know the ritual was successful?",a="Upon completing the rite at the well, the spirits of the Thunderhorn clan's ancestors appear directly to Mull in a dream, praising the adventurer's deeds and offering ancestral thanks.",al={"ancestor dream","how mull knows","well cleansed result"}},{q="What follows the cleansing of the Thunderhorn well?",a="Purifying the Thunderhorn well is part of a broader effort to safeguard Mulgore's springs. Next, Mull sends travelers onward to aid another troubled region by constructing the Wildmane Totem.",al={"what next","next quest after thunderhorn","wildmane totem next"},it="next_step"},{q="What is the Thunderhorn Cleansing Totem?",a="The Thunderhorn Cleansing Totem is a ritually carved spiritual focus prepared by Mull Thunderhorn specifically designed to purify the corrupted waters of his clan's ancestral well.",al={"cleansing totem","what is the totem item","mull totem"},it="item"},{q="Why does Mull Thunderhorn need you to cleanse the well?",a="Mull Thunderhorn has crafted the ritual focus, but cleansing sacred ground is a rite of service and honor for rising champions among the tauren and their allies. By carrying the Thunderhorn Cleansing Totem to the tainted well yourself, you prove your dedication to the Earthmother and the ancestral spirits of his clan, earning the favor and guidance of the ancestors who watch over the waters.",al={"why me mull thunderhorn","why does mull send you","why perform cleansing ritual"},it="why_me",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Restoring the sacred springs honors the Earth Mother and protects your kin as they carve out a permanent, peaceful home in Mulgore.",["class:Shaman"]="Channeling ancestral energy through totems to heal corrupted natural springs is the very foundation of the shamanic calling.",["class:Druid"]="Cleansing poisoned waters halts the spread of corruption across Mulgore's ecosystem, preserving the natural harmony of the plains.",["faction:Horde"]="Assisting the tauren with their sacred springs strengthens the bond between the young Horde and its proud Shu'halo allies."},kw={"ancestral spirits","bloodhoof village","cleansing totem","earth mother","mulgore","mull thunderhorn","thunderhorn cleansing","thunderhorn water well"},rel={"npc:mull-thunderhorn","subzone:thunderhorn-water-well","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Mull Thunderhorn",level="8",faction="Horde",id=758},src={"Thunderhorn Cleansing (Classic)"}}
E["quest:759"] = {t="quest",n="Wildmane Totem",z="mulgore",s="Wildmane Totem represents the third and final stage in Mull Thunderhorn's efforts to craft cleansing totems for Mulgore's poisoned wells. To restore the Wildmane Well, the ritual demands offerings taken from the territory's fiercest natural predators: prairie wolf alphas.",h="Wildmane Totem represents the third and final stage in Mull Thunderhorn's efforts to craft cleansing totems...",sec={{t="The Final Well",b="After securing the materials required to consecrate totems for the Winterhoof and Thunderhorn wells, only one afflicted source remains in central Mulgore: the Wildmane Well. The poisoned water across the plains threatens both migrating wildlife and settlements, demanding powerful primal ingredients to restore the earth's natural balance.",sp=0},{t="The Predator's Strength",b="To counter the potent corruption lingering in the northern waters, Mull Thunderhorn requires a sacrifice of ferocity. Prairie wolf alphas prowl the grasslands, particularly around the towering bluffs beneath Thunder Bluff. Harvesting their teeth channels the relentless drive and resilience of Mulgore's apex hunters directly into the crafting of the cleansing totem.",sp=0},{t="Sacrifice and Balance",b="In tauren spiritual tradition, drawing upon the land's creatures carries emotional and elemental weight. Mull views the gathering of alpha teeth not as conquest, but as a somber necessity where the sacrifice of Mulgore's predators helps heal the Earth Mother's stricken waters.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Wildmane Totem needed for?",a="The totem is crafted by Mull Thunderhorn to cleanse the Wildmane Well, the final contaminated water source endangering the tauren of Mulgore.",al={"why make wildmane totem","wildmane totem purpose"},it="purpose"},{q="Why are prairie wolf alpha teeth specifically required?",a="Purifying the deep contamination at the Wildmane Well requires channeling the fierce, unyielding strength of an apex predator. The teeth of the pack leaders provide the necessary primal power.",al={"why wolf teeth","prairie alpha teeth purpose"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Where are prairie wolf alphas found in Mulgore?",a="Prairie wolf alphas wander the northern plains of Mulgore, commonly prowling the grasslands surrounding the base of Thunder Bluff.",al={"prairie wolf alpha location","where to find prairie alphas"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Who crafts the totem?",a="Mull Thunderhorn, a spiritual elder stationed in Bloodhoof Village, crafts each cleansing totem after an initiate gathers the necessary offerings.",al={"who makes the totem","totem quest giver"},it="identity"},{q="What follows the crafting of the Wildmane Totem?",a="Once Mull Thunderhorn assembles the totem from the alpha teeth, you are sent north with it to perform the actual purification at the Wildmane Well.",al={"what comes next","wildmane totem next quest"},it="next_step"},{q="How does this ritual reflect tauren beliefs?",a="The tauren view taking an animal's life as a solemn exchange with nature. Mull acknowledges the sacrifice of the wolves with both sadness for their death and pride in their role in healing the earth.",al={"tauren philosophy totem","mull thunderhorn beliefs"}},{q="Why does Mull Thunderhorn want the Wildmane Totem made?",a="Only one corrupted water well remains to be purified in Mulgore—the one belonging to the Wildmane clan. Mull Thunderhorn has dedicated himself to cleansing the sacred wells of the earthmother, and crafting this final cleansing totem using the predatory spirit of the land is essential to completing the sacred rite and restoring pure water to the tribe.",al={"why mull wants wildmane totem","mull thunderhorn motive","why make wildmane totem"},it="motive",add=true},{q="What is the Prairie Alpha Tooth used for?",a="The teeth of the prairie wolf alpha represent the sacrifice and ferocious vitality of Mulgore's apex predators. Mull Thunderhorn binds eight of these fangs into the Wildmane Totem so that its spirit can appease the natural order and empower the ritual needed to purge the taint from the Wildmane water well.",al={"prairie alpha tooth use","what are prairie alpha teeth for","teeth for wildmane totem"},it="item",add=true},{q="Why are you hunting prairie wolf alphas?",a="You are sent to hunt prairie wolf alphas, the dominant pack leaders that roam the plains around the base of Thunder Bluff. Their strength and ferocity embody the natural balance of the plains, providing the potent essence Mull Thunderhorn requires for the final water-cleansing ritual.",al={"why kill prairie wolf alphas","prairie wolf alpha target","who are you sent after"},it="target",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The cleansing of ancestral wells underscores the tauren obligation to mend environmental imbalance and honor the sacrifices demanded by the Earth Mother.",["class:Shaman"]="Shaman appreciate the delicate rites involved in harmonizing predator totems with elemental water to purge toxic blights.",["class:Hunter"]="Tracking down the cunning alpha wolves around the high bluffs tests a hunter's respect for the wilderness and its sharpest predators."},kw={"bloodhoof village","mulgore","mull thunderhorn","prairie wolf alpha","thunder bluff","totem","water well","wildmane"},rel={"npc:mull-thunderhorn","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff"},m={start="Mull Thunderhorn",level="10",faction="Horde",id=759,ri={"Prairie Alpha Tooth"}},src={"Wildmane Totem (Classic)"}}
E["quest:760"] = {t="quest",n="Wildmane Cleansing",z="mulgore",s="Wildmane Cleansing marks the culmination of the tauren ritual efforts to purify the vital watering holes of Mulgore. Entrusted by Mull Thunderhorn in Bloodhoof Village, heroes take the completed Wildmane Totem north to cleanse the final poisoned well. Driving back goblin pollutants restores balance to the plains and secures the pastoral livelihood of the tauren tribes.",h="Wildmane Cleansing marks the culmination of the tauren ritual efforts to purify the vital watering holes of...",sec={{t="Purifying the Earth Mother's Gift",b="Water is sacred lifeblood across the sun-drenched plains of Mulgore, sustaining the herds, the soil, and the nomadic tribes who have recently gathered around Thunder Bluff. When the Venture Company and encroaching goblins began contaminating these aquifers with toxic industrial runoff, the tauren elders recognized an existential threat. Following the purification of the Winterhoof and Thunderhorn wells, the Wildmane Water Well stands as the third and final corrupted site requiring spiritual intervention.",sp=0},{t="The Wildmane Totem",b="Crafted from specific elements gathered across the region, the Wildmane Cleansing Totem channels the restorative power of ancestral spirits and elemental earth. Planting the totem beside the foul waters north of Thunder Bluff disperses the foul chemicals, drawing out the toxins and reviving the natural flow. By completing the ritual, the tauren safeguard their people from disease and send an unmistakable message that the despoiling of their sacred ground will not be tolerated.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why was the Wildmane Water Well poisoned?",a="Goblin operations, driven by industrial greed and logging enterprises, introduced foul poisons and chemical waste into Mulgore's subterranean waters, threatening local wildlife and tribal settlements alike.",al={"who poisoned the well","why is the well dirty","goblin poison"},it="conflict"},{q="Where is the Wildmane Water Well located?",a="The Wildmane Water Well is situated on the plains directly north of the towering mesas of Thunder Bluff.",al={"wildmane well location","where is wildmane well","finding wildmane well"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Who initiates this cleansing ritual?",a="Mull Thunderhorn, an elder stationed in Bloodhoof Village, guides you through the creation of the totems and sends you to purify the wells.",al={"who gives wildmane cleansing","quest giver","mull thunderhorn location"},it="identity"},{q="What other wells are part of this purification effort?",a="The Wildmane Well is the third in a trio of vital water sources, preceded by the Winterhoof Water Well and the Thunderhorn Water Well, both to the south.",al={"other wells","mulgore wells","winterhoof thunderhorn wells"},it="history"},{q="How does the cleansing totem work?",a="The totem is infused with ancestral magic and purified natural elements, channeling restorative spiritual energy into the water table to neutralize goblin pollutants.",al={"cleansing totem mechanic","how does totem clean water"},it="power"},{q="What does completing this ritual mean for Bloodhoof Village?",a="Cleansing the three wells guarantees safe drinking water for all tribes in central Mulgore and honors the tauren sacred duty to heal the Earth Mother from industrial exploitation.",al={"outcome of quest","why cleanse the wells","tauren lore importance"},it="purpose"},{q="Why does Mull Thunderhorn want the Wildmane Well cleansed?",a="Mull Thunderhorn watches over the spiritual balance and vital resources of Mulgore. The Venture Company's foul operations poisoned the land's precious water sources, threatening the survival of the tribes and their herds. The Wildmane Well is the last of the three ancient wells still tainted, and purifying it is essential to heal the Earth Mother and secure safe drinking water for Bloodhoof Village and Thunder Bluff.",al={"mull thunderhorn motive","why cleanse wildmane well","why does mull care about the wells"},it="motive",add=true},{q="What is the Wildmane Cleansing Totem?",a="The Wildmane Cleansing Totem is a ritually prepared implement crafted by Mull Thunderhorn to channel ancestral and earthen spirits. When activated at the tainted Wildmane Water Well north of Thunder Bluff, the totem expels the toxic sludge left behind by goblin polluters, purifying the aquifer and returning the spring to its natural, pristine state.",al={"wildmane cleansing totem use","what is the wildmane totem","cleansing totem purpose"},it="item",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Restoring Mulgore's three ancestral wells is a foundational rite of passage that proves a young brave's devotion to preserving the Earth Mother's gifts.",["class:Shaman"]="Employing ritual totems to undo mechanical pollution exemplifies the shamanic duty to maintain harmony between natural elements and living communities.",["class:Druid"]="Reversing goblin environmental destruction directly reflects the Cenarion mandate to heal blighted ecosystems and protect verdant pastures."},kw={"bloodhoof village","cleansing","earth mother","mulgore","mull thunderhorn","totem","venture company","water well","wildmane"},rel={"npc:mull-thunderhorn","quest:759","subzone:wildmane-water-well","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff","topic:goblin"},m={start="Mull Thunderhorn",level="10",faction="Horde",id=760,ri={"Wildmane Cleansing Totem"}},src={"Wildmane Cleansing (Classic)"}}
E["quest:761"] = {t="quest",n="Swoop Hunting",z="mulgore",s="Swoop Hunting is a rite of tracking and marksmanship offered by Harken Windtotem in Bloodhoof Village. To prove their cleverness and patience, hunters and travelers are challenged to stalk the elusive birds across the rolling plains of Mulgore. Returning with their pristine trophy quills demonstrates both dedication to the hunt and respect for the natural trials of the Earth Mother.",h="Swoop Hunting is a rite of tracking and marksmanship offered by Harken Windtotem in Bloodhoof Village.",sec={{t="A Test of Keen Eyes",b="The grassy steppes of Mulgore are teeming with beasts, but few test a pursuer's instincts like the native swoop. These large predatory birds blend seamlessly into the skyline and plains, wheeling overhead or skimming low across the tall grass. Unlike lumbering plainstriders or grazing kodo, swoops demand acute focus and swift feet to run down, making their pursuit a traditional proving exercise for younger hunters seeking to earn their standing among the tribes.",sp=0},{t="Badges of the Hunt",b="For Harken Windtotem, gathering trophy quills is less about culling wildlife and more about cultivating discipline. In tauren culture, carrying tokens from a hard-won stalk signifies cleverness and perseverance. Presenting these unblemished quills to elders in Bloodhoof Village marks an adventurer as capable of sustaining and defending the community, demonstrating that their hooves are swift and their senses sharp enough to master the wider world.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of hunting swoops for Harken Windtotem?",a="Harken Windtotem tasks hunters with tracking swoops across Mulgore to test their cleverness, speed, and dedication. Bringing back intact trophy quills serves as recognized proof of a hunter's skill and earns honor among the tauren of Bloodhoof Village.",al={"why hunt swoops","harken windtotem quest purpose","swoop hunting purpose"},it="motive"},{q="Where are swoops typically encountered?",a="Swoops can be found scattered throughout the open plains of Mulgore, soaring above the prairies or scanning the grasses for prey outside settlements like Bloodhoof Village.",al={"where are swoops","swoop locations mulgore","find swoops"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why are swoops considered tricky to hunt?",a="Unlike herd animals that move together across the flats, swoops are swift, alert raptors that remain widespread and observant. Stalking them requires sharp vision to spot their flight paths and quick strides to bring them down before they escape.",al={"why are swoops hard to catch","swoop behavior"},it="threat"},{q="What do the trophy quills signify to the tauren?",a="Within tauren traditions, trophy swoop quills represent patience, sharp vision, and successful pursuit. Displaying them signifies that a hunter possesses the instincts necessary to provide for and protect the tribe.",al={"trophy swoop quill meaning","significance of quills"},it="item"},{q="Who is Harken Windtotem?",a="Harken Windtotem is a respected tauren resident of Bloodhoof Village who observes newcomers and aspiring hunters, evaluating their resourcefulness through traditional trials across the plains.",al={"who gives swoop hunting","harken windtotem lore"},it="identity"},{q="Does this hunt impact the balance of nature in Mulgore?",a="The tauren approach hunting as a sacred dialogue with the Earth Mother rather than wanton slaughter. Gathering quills is a measured test of discipline that honors the cleverness of the prey while sustaining tribal balance.",al={"is swoop hunting overhunting","swoop balance mulgore"}},{q="Why does Harken Windtotem send an outsider instead of hunting swoops himself?",a="Harken Windtotem does not send you because he is unable to hunt, but to test your worth. For tauren, tracking the cunning swoop across the plains requires keen eyesight, patience, and swift hooves. By tasking you with bringing back their quills, Harken gives you an opportunity to prove your skill and determination to Bloodhoof Village, demonstrating that you are a hunter of genuine merit.",al={"why send me swoop hunting","why harken need me","why can't harken hunt swoops"},it="why_me",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For tauren youths and wanderers, hunting the swift swoops of Mulgore is a venerable rite of passage that sharpens tracking instincts and upholds ancestral hunting customs.",["class:Hunter"]="The hunt offers an early masterclass in leading aerial quarry, assessing behavioral patterns, and executing precise strikes against vigilant predators.",["class:Druid"]="Tracking swoops across the windswept mesas brings druids into closer communion with the predatory birds that maintain the balance of Mulgore's skies."},kw={"bloodhoof village","harken windtotem","mulgore","swoop","swoop hunting","tauren trial","trophy swoop quill"},rel={"npc:harken-windtotem","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Harken Windtotem",faction="Horde",id=761,ri={"Trophy Swoop Quill"}},src={"Swoop Hunting"}}
E["quest:763"] = {t="quest",n="Rites of the Earthmother",z="mulgore",s="Rites of the Earthmother marks the transition of a young tauren out of the secluded Red Cloud Mesa and into the wider plains of Mulgore. Having proven your mettle in Camp Narache, Chief Hawkwind sends you north to Bloodhoof Village with a carved totem to continue the ancient trials under Baine Bloodhoof.",h="Rites of the Earthmother marks the transition of a young tauren out of the secluded Red Cloud Mesa and into...",sec={{t="Leaving the Mesa",b="For generations, young tauren have tested their strength within the safe cradle of Camp Narache on Red Cloud Mesa. Completing the initial trials proves one has the discipline and resolve to survive, but the sacred Rites of the Earthmother require far more than surviving local wildlife. Chief Hawkwind recognizes when an initiate has outgrown the mesa's sheltered hunting grounds and must step into the broader community.",sp=0},{t="A Token for Baine",b="To bridge the journey between Narache and the central settlements, Chief Hawkwind provides a hand-carved totem as a mark of introduction. Bearing this emblem down the winding path to Bloodhoof Village, you present yourself to Baine Bloodhoof, the young chieftain of the village and son of High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof. The totem serves as testament to your readiness for the rites of spirit and wisdom that lie ahead.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of this quest?",a="It serves as the rite of passage that guides you out of the starting area of Camp Narache and directs you toward Bloodhoof Village, connecting you to the next chapter of the tauren ceremonial trials.",al={"why do rites of the earthmother","what does this quest do","purpose of quest"},it="purpose"},{q="Who is Baine Bloodhoof?",a="Baine Bloodhoof is the son of High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof. He leads Bloodhoof Village, serving as a respected local chieftain who oversees the ongoing spiritual and martial testing of young tauren.",al={"who is baine","baine lore","son of cairne"},it="identity"},{q="What is the Totem of Hawkwind?",a="It is a wooden totem hand-carved by Chief Hawkwind of Camp Narache. It acts as an official credential and seal of approval, proving to Baine Bloodhoof that you have earned Hawkwind's blessing to undertake further rites.",al={"totem of hawkwind","hawkwinds totem","carved totem"},it="item"},{q="What rites come after this?",a="Upon arriving in Bloodhoof Village and meeting with Baine, you are tasked with the Rite of Vision, which tests your spiritual attunement through sacred ancestral visions.",al={"next rites","what comes after","next quest"},it="next_step"},{q="Where is Bloodhoof Village located?",a="Bloodhoof Village lies north of Camp Narache, situated along the shores of Stonebull Lake in central Mulgore. Travelers need only follow the main road down from the mesa and head north.",al={"where is bloodhoof village","how to find bloodhoof village","path to bloodhoof"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why must the Earthmother rites be completed?",a="The Rites of the Earthmother are deeply sacred traditions among the tauren. Passing all stages of the rites demonstrates physical fortitude, spiritual connection, and wisdom, earning the initiate true recognition from the elders of Thunder Bluff.",al={"why earthmother rites","tauren trials purpose","rites of earthmother lore"},it="origin"},{q="Why does Chief Hawkwind send you to Bloodhoof Village?",a="Chief Hawkwind has guided you through the introductory trials of the Red Cloud Mesa, but Camp Narache can teach you no more. To complete the sacred Rites of the Earthmother and gain the acceptance of Thunder Bluff's elders, you must broaden your experience in the wider plains of Mulgore. By sending you to Baine Bloodhoof with his carved totem, Hawkwind passes responsibility for your spiritual and physical growth to a leader who can test your readiness for true adulthood.",al={"why send to bloodhoof","chief hawkwind motive","why rites of the earthmother"},it="motive",add=true,nx={8}},{q="Why must you deliver the totem yourself?",a="The journey is not an errand of courier duty, but an integral part of your rite of passage. Stepping beyond the sheltered confines of the Red Cloud Mesa and following the open road into Mulgore proves your independence and endurance. Delivering the Totem of Hawkwind with your own hands allows Baine Bloodhoof to look upon you, recognize Hawkwind's personal commendation carved into the wood, and judge your character firsthand.",al={"why do I take the totem","why can't hawkwind send a messenger","why me rites of the earthmother"},it="why_me",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Every young tauren must walk the path of the Earthmother to prove their worth to the elders and find their place among the united tribes.",["faction:Horde"]="The traditional rites reinforce tribal unity across Mulgore, ensuring that new defenders of the Horde possess both warrior prowess and deep spiritual grounding."},kw={"baine bloodhoof","bloodhoof village","cairne bloodhoof","camp narache","chief hawkwind","mulgore","rites of the earthmother","totem of hawkwind"},rel={"subzone:camp-narache","subzone:bloodhoof-village","npc:chief-hawkwind","npc:baine-bloodhoof","quest:767","zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff"},m={start="Chief Hawkwind",["end"]="Baine Bloodhoof",level="5",faction="Horde",id=763},src={"Rites of the Earthmother (2)"}}
E["quest:764"] = {t="quest",n="The Venture Co.",z="mulgore",s="Investigating a ravaged Venture Company caravan in Mulgore uncovers an aggressive buildup of Venture Company goblins digging into the eastern hills. Having had their formal requests for mining permits flatly rejected, the company chose to quarry the Earth Mother's soil anyway, prompting Morin Cloudstalker to commission a lethal response.",h="Investigating a ravaged Venture Company caravan in Mulgore uncovers an aggressive buildup of Venture Company...",sec={{t="Industrial Intrusion",b="The tauren of Mulgore revere the land as an extension of the Earth Mother, cherishing its pristine plains and balanced wildlife. When the Venture Company approached the tribes seeking rights to blast and excavate the rolling hills for raw ore, their petitions were categorically denied. Undeterred by tribal decrees or territorial sovereignty, the relentless cartel sneaked heavy machinery, laborers, and overseers directly into Mulgore's eastern ridges to mine illicitly.",sp=0},{t="An Unmistakable Message",b="Tauren scouts like Morin Cloudstalker recognize that corporate greed cannot be reasoned with through diplomatic appeals once picks hit the stone. With documents recovered from a ravaged Venture Co. caravan confirming the company's mining operation, Morin dispatches Horde champions to cut down both the taskmasters cracking the whips and the laborers driving the drills. The bloody reprisal is intended to ensure the Venture Company calculates the cost of trespassing as far too steep.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why is the Venture Company in Mulgore?",a="The Venture Company established an illegal outpost and mine along the eastern hills of Mulgore to extract minerals and natural resources. After the tauren formally denied them official mining rights, the cartel ignored tribal authority and set up operations regardless.",al={"why are goblins in mulgore","venture co mulgore motive","what does venture co want"},it="why_here"},{q="Who gave the order to attack the miners?",a="Morin Cloudstalker, a tauren keeping watch near Bloodhoof Village, issues the order. He views the cartel's refusal to accept tribal refusal as a direct violation that demands physical force rather than further talk.",al={"who gives this quest","morin cloudstalker role","quest giver"},it="conflict"},{q="Where is the Venture Company mine located?",a="The main Venture Company Mine sits in the foothills east of the Ravaged Caravan and Bloodhoof Village. A smaller gathering of company personnel can also be encountered at an outpost tucked into the bluffs northeast of Thunder Bluff's northern elevators.",al={"venture co mine location","where are the supervisors","where to find venture co"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Did the tauren ever try to negotiate with the goblins?",a="Yes. The Venture Company petitioned the tauren for formal mining rights in Mulgore. The tribal leaders refused to let them strip-mine their ancestral lands, but the goblins simply chose to dig without permission anyway.",al={"did tauren permit mining","mining rights mulgore","did goblins ask permission"},it="history"},{q="Are these goblins part of the Steamwheedle Cartel?",a="No. The Venture Company is an independent, notoriously predatory industrial cartel distinct from neutral trading groups like the Steamwheedle Cartel. They maintain hostile relations with local populations wherever they exploit resources.",al={"are these ratchet goblins","is venture co friendly","venture company faction"},it="identity"},{q="How does this connect to the ravaged caravans?",a="The ravaged caravan was a Venture Company convoy destroyed by the tauren. The mining-division crates and documents recovered from its wreckage revealed the company's operation in Mulgore, prompting this strike on their mine.",al={"caravan connection","did venture co attack caravans","the ravaged caravan connection"},it="ties"},{q="Why does Morin Cloudstalker want the Venture Co. attacked?",a="The Venture Company was officially denied mining rights in Mulgore, yet they ignored the refusal and began building up equipment and workers regardless. Morin Cloudstalker cannot permit them to despoil the land and plunder its natural resources unchecked. Since diplomacy and flat rejections failed to sway their greed, he believes the tauren must deliver an unmistakable, forceful message that trespass and theft will not be tolerated.",al={"morin cloudstalker motive","why attack venture co","why does morin want venture co killed"},it="motive",add=true},{q="Who are you being sent to fight at the mine?",a="You are sent against the Venture Co. Supervisors and Workers stationed at their mining operation in eastern Mulgore. These goblins and hired laborers are carving deep into the sacred hills to strip the earth of minerals without tribal permission, led by profit-driven foremen who enforce the company's aggressive, illicit expansion.",al={"venture co quest targets","who are the venture co workers","who do i fight in the mine"},it="target",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For the tauren, the Earth Mother is sacred. The Venture Company's strip-mining is not just trespassing, but a violent desecration of their ancestral home that warrants an immediate, forceful eviction.",["faction:Horde"]="Defending Mulgore against commercial exploitation cements your dedication to protecting Cairne Bloodhoof's people as they work to secure their new homeland.",["profession:Mining"]="The Venture Company operates purely through brute-force extraction, cutting corners and destabilizing the land with no regard for the natural balance or safe mining craft."},kw={"goblin","mining rights","morin cloudstalker","mulgore","ravaged caravan","venture co","venture co mine","venture company"},rel={"subzone:venture-co-mine","quest:765","quest:749","npc:morin-cloudstalker","subzone:ravaged-caravan","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren","topic:goblin"},m={start="Morin Cloudstalker",level="10",faction="Horde",id=764},src={"The Venture Co. (Classic)"}}
E["quest:765"] = {t="quest",n="Supervisor Fizsprocket",z="mulgore",s="Supervisor Fizsprocket is a high-ranking Venture Company overseer stationed within the mining tunnels of Mulgore. Charged by Morin Cloudstalker with putting an end to the goblin enterprise's calculated pillaging, Horde adventurers enter the mine to eliminate him and recover his detailed clipboard.",h="Supervisor Fizsprocket is a high-ranking Venture Company overseer stationed within the mining tunnels of...",sec={{t="A Plot Against the Plains",b="Documents recovered from a destroyed Venture Company caravan revealed that the Venture Company was not merely digging for ore—they were deliberately formulating schemes to drive the tauren from Mulgore entirely. By uprooting the locals, the rapacious corporation sought unchecked freedom to strip the rolling hills and deep subterranean veins of every natural resource.",sp=0},{t="Strike at the Foreman",b="Morin Cloudstalker, enraged by the corporate atrocities plotted against his people, directs champions into the Venture Company mine located just east of the ravaged trade wagons. Tucked deep within the right-hand tunnels surrounded by laborers and sub-bosses, Supervisor Fizsprocket manages operations. Striking down Fizsprocket and claiming his clipboard shatters local coordination and yields critical intelligence regarding the company's future designs.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Who is Supervisor Fizsprocket?",a="Supervisor Fizsprocket is a Venture Company boss running operations inside the mining tunnels in eastern Mulgore. He oversees the workforce tearing into the earth and executes corporate strategies aimed at expanding their operations across the region.",al={"fizsprocket","who is fizsprocket","fizsprocket lore"},it="target"},{q="Why does Morin Cloudstalker want Fizsprocket dead?",a="Morin discovered documents on a raided caravan proving the Venture Company plotted to drive the tauren off their ancestral homeland for profit. Fizsprocket is the direct supervisor carrying out these calculated exploitation schemes.",al={"why kill fizsprocket","morin cloudstalker motivation"},it="motive"},{q="Where is Supervisor Fizsprocket located?",a="He is located inside the Venture Company mine directly east of the Ravaged Caravan in Mulgore. Inside the mine, follow the far right tunnel to a chamber where he stands surrounded by workers and supervisors.",al={"where is fizsprocket","fizsprocket mine location","fizsprocket map"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What proof does Morin require of Fizsprocket's defeat?",a="Morin Cloudstalker requires Fizsprocket's personal clipboard, which contains ledgers and records that might reveal further Venture Company ambitions in Mulgore.",al={"proof of death","fizsprocket clipboard"},it="item",gp=true},{q="Why is the Venture Company in Mulgore?",a="The Venture Company is an unscrupulous commercial cartel bent on stripping virgin territory of timber, minerals, and valuable subterranean wealth, completely disregarding indigenous inhabitants or environmental balance.",al={"venture co mulgore","why are goblins in mulgore"},it="why_here"},{q="Does killing Fizsprocket drive the Venture Company away completely?",a="No. While Fizsprocket's demise disrupts their local leadership and provides Morin Cloudstalker with vital notes on their plans, the Venture Company remains a persistent threat embedded in Mulgore's hills.",al={"is venture co gone","after fizsprocket dies"},it="conflict"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The Venture Company's open scheme to displace your tribe and plunder the sacred earth makes slaying Fizsprocket an act of vital territorial defense.",["faction:Horde"]="Assisting the tauren against encroaching corporate pillagers solidifies the Horde's protection over newly claimed heartlands in Kalimdor."},kw={"clipboard","fizsprocket","mine","morin cloudstalker","mulgore","supervisor","venture co"},rel={"npc:morin-cloudstalker","quest:764","quest:749","subzone:ravaged-caravan","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Morin Cloudstalker",level="12",faction="Horde",id=765},src={"Supervisor Fizsprocket (Classic)"}}
E["quest:767"] = {t="quest",n="Rite of Vision",z="mulgore",s="The Rite of Vision is a pivotal coming-of-age trial within the Rites of the Earthmother, testing a young adventurer's spiritual maturity. Initiated by Baine Bloodhoof, the quest directs aspirants to seek counsel from the village seer, Zarlman Two-Moons. Undertaking this journey is essential for anyone seeking the blessings and respect of the elders on the rises of Thunder Bluff.",h="The Rite of Vision is a pivotal coming-of-age trial within the Rites of the Earthmother, testing a young...",sec={{t="A Sacred Stepping Stone",b="For generations, the nomadic tauren have turned to the Earthmother to guide their people across the plains of Kalimdor. Now gathered within the safe borders of Mulgore, their spiritual traditions remain the cornerstone of their society. The Rite of Vision is one of the ancient trials known as the Rites of the Earthmother. While earlier trials test an initiate's physical strength and connection to the hunt, the Rite of Vision demands an inward journey, directing young tauren and honored allies to commune directly with the spirits.",sp=0},{t="The Seer's Guidance",b="Before any spiritual vision can be sought, an initiate must be prepared by someone steeped in ancient wisdom. In Bloodhoof Village, that responsibility falls to the revered shaman Zarlman Two-Moons. Entrusted by Baine Bloodhoof to prepare travelers, Zarlman possesses the keen sight to recognize those walking the path of the ancestors, offering the preliminary rites and sacred preparations necessary to pierce the veil of the physical world.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Rite of Vision?",a="The Rite of Vision is a sacred ritual within the Rites of the Earthmother. It is designed to broaden an adventurer's spiritual sight, allowing them to commune with ancestral spirits and demonstrate their spiritual readiness to the elders of Thunder Bluff.",al={"rite of vision quest","what is rite of vision","rite of vision lore"},it="purpose"},{q="Who starts this quest?",a="The quest is given by Baine Bloodhoof, the son of High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof. Baine watches over the settlement of Bloodhoof Village and directs worthy travelers toward the village's spiritual elders.",al={"who gives rite of vision","rite of vision quest giver"},gp=true},{q="Who is Zarlman Two-Moons?",a="Zarlman Two-Moons is a seasoned tauren shaman and the spiritual leader of Bloodhoof Village. He prepares travelers and initiates who seek to undergo sacred trials such as the Rite of Vision.",al={"who is zarlman","zarlman two moons lore"},it="identity"},{q="Why are the Rites of the Earthmother important to the tauren?",a="The Rites of the Earthmother represent a traditional rite of passage for tauren coming of age. Successfully completing them demonstrates physical capability, spiritual attunement, and an enduring respect for the land, earning the honor of the high elders.",al={"earthmother rites meaning","why do rites of earthmother"},it="history"},{q="Can non-tauren undertake the Rite of Vision?",a="Yes. Horde allies who travel through Mulgore and earn the trust of the Bloodhoof tribe are permitted to walk the path of the Earthmother, showing respect to tauren tradition in exchange for the elders' favor.",al={"can orcs do rite of vision","non tauren earthmother"}},{q="Where does this trial take place?",a="The trial begins in Bloodhoof Village, a prominent tauren settlement situated near the banks of Stonebull Lake in central Mulgore, south of Thunder Bluff.",al={"where is rite of vision","rite of vision location"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why does Baine Bloodhoof want you to undertake the Rite of Vision?",a="Baine Bloodhoof is preparing you to walk the path of your ancestors and prove your worth to the tauren people. As you come of age in Mulgore, you must undertake the sacred Rites of the Earthmother to commune with the spirits, learn reverence for the land, and ultimately earn the respect of the elders in Thunder Bluff. Baine directs you to village mystic Zarlman Two-Moons so you may properly begin the spiritual trial.",al={"why does baine send you on rite of vision","baine bloodhoof motive rite of vision","why do the rite of vision"},it="motive",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The Rite of Vision is an essential cultural milestone for every young tauren, marking your transition into a trusted defender and spirit-walker of the tribes.",["class:Shaman"]="Seeking Zarlman Two-Moons and walking the path of visions resonates deeply with the shamanic duty to balance worldly affairs with the will of the ancestors.",["class:Druid"]="Communing with the Earthmother's visions directly reinforces the druidic calling to listen closely to the wisdom and whispers of the living world.",["faction:Horde"]="Undertaking these traditional sacred rites proves your loyalty and cultural reverence to the tauren elders who help guide the Horde."},kw={"baine bloodhoof","bloodhoof village","mulgore","rite of vision","rites of the earthmother","tauren trial","thunder bluff","zarlman two-moons"},rel={"quest:763","npc:baine-bloodhoof","npc:zarlman-two-moons","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:thunderbluff","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Baine Bloodhoof",["end"]="Zarlman Two-Moons",level="6",faction="Horde",id=767},src={"Rite of Vision"}}
E["quest:770"] = {t="quest",n="The Demon Scarred Cloak",z="mulgore",s="The Demon Scarred Cloak is a ragged pelt recovered from the legendary white wolf Ghost Howl, who roams the grassy plains of Mulgore. Marked by an enduring wound inflicted during past clashes with the Burning Legion, the pelt serves as proof of the beast's release from torment. Bringing it to the elders of Bloodhoof Village brings closure to the story of a tragic local legend.",h="The Demon Scarred Cloak is a ragged pelt recovered from the legendary white wolf Ghost Howl, who roams the...",sec={{t="A Relic of Fel Wounds",b="Ghost Howl is an elusive white wolf known across Mulgore for both his ferocity and his sorrowful howl. When slain, the beast leaves behind a pelt that is visibly scarred by foul demonic corruption. These lingering marks date back to the wolf's desperate battles against the invading demons of the Burning Legion, a confrontation that left Ghost Howl wounded in both flesh and spirit long after the Third War came to a close.",sp=0},{t="Peace for a Troubled Spirit",b="The tauren of Mulgore maintain a deep respect for the wild beasts of their homeland, revering them as children of the Earth Mother. Presenting the scarred cloak to Skorn Whitecloud in Bloodhoof Village confirms the demise of the tormented predator. Skorn recognizes the immense skill required to track and defeat Ghost Howl, expressing the somber hope that in death, the wolf's tortured spirit has finally attained peace.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the Demon Scarred Cloak?",a="It is the ragged hide harvested from Ghost Howl, a legendary white wolf in Mulgore. The pelt is visibly scarred by fel energies from an old battle against the Burning Legion.",al={"what is demon scarred cloak","demon scarred cloak item","ghost howl pelt"},it="item"},{q="Where did the demon scars on the cloak come from?",a="Ghost Howl earned his wounds during clashes against the Burning Legion when the demonic invaders pushed across Kalimdor during the Third War.",al={"why is ghost howl scarred","how did ghost howl get scarred","ghost howl legion"},it="origin"},{q="Who do I bring the cloak to?",a="Bring the cloak to Skorn Whitecloud, an elder tauren hunter who resides in Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore.",al={"where do i turn in demon scarred cloak","who wants demon scarred cloak","skorn whitecloud turn in"},it="next_step",gp=true},{q="Why does Skorn Whitecloud care about Ghost Howl?",a="Skorn Whitecloud is a retired hunter who recognizes Ghost Howl as a formidable beast that suffered deeply from demonic corruption. He respects the skill required to fell such a creature and hopes its spirit can finally rest.",al={"skorn whitecloud lore","why does skorn want ghost howl"},it="ties"},{q="Where is Ghost Howl found?",a="Ghost Howl is a rare white wolf that roams the rolling plains of Mulgore, typically sighted in the northern or central open fields between Red Cloud Mesa and Thunder Bluff.",al={"where to find ghost howl","ghost howl location","ghost howl spawn"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why do the tauren hope the wolf's spirit finds peace?",a="Tauren culture emphasizes harmony with nature and reverence for wild spirits. Because demonic corruption brought unending torment to Ghost Howl's life, his defeat is viewed as a merciful release back to the Earth Mother.",al={"tauren belief ghost howl","ghost howl spirit"},it="history"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For the Shu'halo, hunting Ghost Howl is not an act of cruelty or conquest, but a somber ritual to grant an agonized creature peace from the lingering taint of the Burning Legion.",["class:Hunter"]="Tracking down a lone, scarred alpha like Ghost Howl is an essential rite of passage, earning high praise and a master hunter's gift from the elder Skorn Whitecloud.",["class:Druid"]="Druids view the demon-scarred pelt as a grim reminder that the land and its wild inhabitants still bear unhealed wounds from the demonic invasion of Kalimdor."},kw={"burning legion","camp sungraze","demon scarred cloak","ghost howl","mulgore","skorn whitecloud","white wolf"},rel={"npc:skorn-whitecloud","zone:mulgore","topic:burning-legion"},m={start="Demon Scarred Cloak",["end"]="Skorn Whitecloud",faction="Horde",id=770,ri={"Demon Scarred Cloak"}},src={"The Demon Scarred Cloak"}}
E["quest:771"] = {t="quest",n="Rite of Vision",z="mulgore",s="The Rite of Vision is a foundational spiritual trial undergone by young tauren stepping beyond the Red Cloud Mesa into greater Mulgore. To pierce the veil and receive guidance from ancestral spirits, initiates must gather natural reagents to brew the Water of the Seers under the watchful eye of Zarlman Two-Moons in Bloodhoof Village.",h="The Rite of Vision is a foundational spiritual trial undergone by young tauren stepping beyond the Red Cloud...",sec={{t="The Reagents of the Land",b="Before any vision can be granted, the initiate must demonstrate an intimate understanding of the natural gifts found across Mulgore. Zarlman Two-Moons instructs the seeker to collect well stones found near the sacred communal water wells and ambercorn fallen beneath the roots of Mulgore's ancient trees. These natural elements are not merely physical objects; to the tauren, they hold the latent magic and lifeblood of the Earth Mother, ready to be channeled into ritual use.",sp=0},{t="Brewing the Water of the Seers",b="Once gathered, Zarlman combines the stones and ambercorn into the potent Water of the Seers. When consumed beside the village's tribal flame, this ceremonial draft clears the mind and summons an ethereal guide. Following this manifestation across the plains forms the true heart of the rite, testing the initiate's ability to walk between the physical world and the spiritual whispers of the land.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of the Rite of Vision?",a="The Rite of Vision is a spiritual test for tauren coming of age and seeking their path. By preparing and drinking the Water of the Seers, the initiate learns to commune with spirits and follow ancestral omens across the plains of Mulgore.",al={"what is rite of vision","why do rite of vision","purpose of rite of vision"},it="purpose"},{q="Who is Zarlman Two-Moons?",a="Zarlman Two-Moons is a tauren shaman and seer stationed in Bloodhoof Village. He oversees spiritual trials for young adventurers, preparing ritual draughts such as the Water of the Seers and interpreting the signs of the Earth Mother.",al={"who is zarlman","zarlman two moons role","questgiver zarlman"},it="identity"},{q="Where are well stones and ambercorn found?",a="Well stones are gathered from the ground near the freshwater wells scattered across Mulgore. Ambercorn can be found scattered at the bases of the large, sprawling trees dotting the surrounding grassy landscape.",al={"where to find ambercorn","well stones location","reagents for rite of vision"},gp=true},{q="What happens after drinking the Water of the Seers?",a="Drinking the draft near the tribal fire opens the imbiber's spiritual sight, revealing a ghostly animal guide. The seeker must follow this spirit across the plains to uncover the next stage of their sacred journey.",al={"what happens next rite of vision","water of the seers effect","vision quest follow-up"},sp=1,it="next_step"},{q="Why do the tauren revere water wells so deeply?",a="Water is the lifeblood of Mulgore and a precious gift of the Earth Mother. The wells serve as sacred communal centers that nourish both the land and its herds, making the stones gathered beside them spiritually charged.",al={"tauren water wells","why are wells sacred","mulgore water"},it="history"},{q="Does this quest lead to further rites?",a="Yes. This task is part of a multi-stage ceremony. Once Zarlman prepares the Water of the Seers, initiates must drink it, track the vision that appears, and present themselves to elder leaders across Mulgore.",al={"next quest in rite of vision","is rite of vision a chain","tauren rites chain"},sp=1,it="next_step",gp=true},{q="Why does Zarlman Two-Moons want this done?",a="As a tribal shaman, Zarlman Two-Moons is responsible for guiding promising young adventurers through the sacred traditions of the tauren. He cannot simply grant you spiritual wisdom; you must earn the favor of the Earth Mother and your ancestors by participating directly in the ritual, starting with gathering the sacred elements of the land yourself.",al={"zarlman two moons motive","why does zarlman send you","zarlman rite of vision reason"},it="motive",add=true},{q="What are the Well Stones for?",a="Well stones are gathered from the sacred water wells throughout Mulgore. Imbued with the natural magic of the Earth Mother's purest springs, they serve as one of the key reagents Zarlman Two-Moons needs to brew the mystical Water of the Seers.",al={"well stones purpose","why need well stone","well stone use"},it="item",add=true},{q="What is the Ambercorn for?",a="Fallen from the mighty trees across Mulgore, Ambercorn carries the natural essence of the land's flora. Combined with well stones and steeped by Zarlman Two-Moons, it provides the magical properties required to concoct the Water of the Seers, granting you ancestral visions.",al={"ambercorn purpose","what is ambercorn used for","why get ambercorn"},it="item",add=true},{q="Why must you gather the reagents instead of Zarlman Two-Moons?",a="The Rite of Vision is an individual trial of spiritual growth, not a routine chore. To commune with the spirits, you must demonstrate reverence and harmony with the land by seeking out its natural blessings with your own hands. Having someone else collect the reagents would defeat the trial's sacred purpose.",al={"why do i have to get ambercorn","why doesn't zarlman gather well stones","why me rite of vision"},it="why_me",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For tauren, the Rite of Vision is an essential cultural milestone, binding an emerging warrior, hunter, or mystic to the spiritual heritage of the Earth Mother and the ancestors.",["class:Shaman"]="Shaman will recognize the preparation of the Water of the Seers as a traditional grounding ritual, attuning the spirit to read unseen elements before conversing with spirits directly.",["class:Druid"]="Druids hold deep reverence for the ambercorn and well stones, seeing them as direct manifestations of the flora and life-giving waterways that sustain Mulgore's balance."},kw={"ambercorn","bloodhoof village","earth mother","mulgore","rite of vision","water of the seers","well stones","zarlman two-moons"},rel={"quest:767","npc:zarlman-two-moons","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore","quest:92472"},m={start="Zarlman Two-Moons",level="7",faction="Horde",id=771,ri={"Ambercorn","Well Stone"}},src={"Rite of Vision (2)"}}
E["quest:772"] = {t="quest",n="Rite of Vision",z="mulgore",s="The Rite of Vision is the spiritual climax of an ancient trial undertaken by young tauren in Bloodhoof Village. By drinking the sacred Water of the Seers beside the tribal bonfire, initiates summon a spectral guide to lead them toward their destiny.",h="The Rite of Vision is the spiritual climax of an ancient trial undertaken by young tauren in Bloodhoof...",sec={{t="The Water of the Seers",b="After gathering the necessary ritual herbs and passing the initial trials of faith, an initiate is granted the Water of the Seers by the village shaman, Zarlman Two-Moons. This potent draught must be ingested before the roaring tribal fire at the heart of Bloodhoof Village. When consumed with a focused and respectful mind, the sacred waters expand the drinker's perception beyond physical sight, bridging the veil between the material plane and the spiritual realm.",sp=0},{t="Following the Ghost Wolf",b="Upon drinking the brew, the flames yield an ethereal apparition: an ancestral ghost wolf. The spirit does not speak or fight, but silently lopes northwest across the rolling plains of Mulgore. A true initiate must heed its quiet path, tracking its journey away from the village and deep into the rocky hills where the hermit Seer Wiserunner awaits in secluded meditation.",sp=0},{t="A Walk to Wisdom",b="Reaching Seer Wiserunner at the mouth of his mountain cavern signifies the successful completion of the vision. This journey across the grassy hills tests an initiate's patience, trust in ancestral guidance, and attunement to the Earth Mother. Passing this rite grants the young adventurer the right to undergo the ultimate test of adulthood: the Rite of Wisdom.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What happens when I drink the Water of the Seers?",a="When ingested near the tribal fire in Bloodhoof Village, the water opens your spiritual senses. Shortly after drinking, a ghostly spirit wolf appears in front of the flames to guide your path.",al={"how does water of seers work","drinking water of the seers","what does the water do"},it="item"},{q="Where does the spirit guide lead me?",a="The spectral wolf trots northwest out of Bloodhoof Village across the hills of Mulgore, leading straight to a rocky cave where Seer Wiserunner dwells.",al={"where does ghost wolf go","who does the wolf lead to","wolf destination"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Who is Zarlman Two-Moons?",a="Zarlman Two-Moons is a prominent tauren shaman in Bloodhoof Village tasked with preparing initiates for spiritual adulthood through ancestral rites and sacred concoctions.",al={"who gives the rite of vision","zarlman two moons role"},it="identity"},{q="Who is Seer Wiserunner?",a="Seer Wiserunner is an elder tauren mystic who lives in contemplation within the cliffs northwest of Bloodhoof Village. Initiates who follow their spirit vision find him to finish the trial.",al={"who is wiserunner","seer in cave mulgore"},it="target"},{q="Why must the water be drunk near the tribal fire?",a="The tribal bonfire acts as a sacred anchor for Bloodhoof Village's ancestral spirits. The combination of its consecrated flames and the holy water allows the vision to bridge into the waking world.",al={"why tribal fire","bonfire rite of vision"},it="origin"},{q="What rite comes after the Rite of Vision?",a="Upon completing the Rite of Vision with Seer Wiserunner, initiates are directed to undertake the Rite of Wisdom, the final spiritual trial of their early journey in Mulgore.",al={"next quest after rite of vision","what comes next"},it="next_step"},{q="Why does Zarlman Two-Moons want me to undergo the Rite of Vision?",a="As a shaman of Bloodhoof Village, Zarlman Two-Moons guides young tauren along the sacred paths of their ancestors. Having gathered the components for the Water of the Seers, he now presents the brew so you can commune directly with the spirits, witness your vision, and take the next step toward discovering your destiny within the tribe.",al={"why rite of vision","zarlman two-moons motive","purpose of rite of vision"},it="motive",add=true},{q="Why must I undertake this rite myself?",a="The rites of the Earthmother are deeply personal trials of maturity and spiritual awakening. No guard or elder can walk the path of the spirits for you; only your own spirit can receive the guidance meant to reveal your place and purpose among the tauren people.",al={"why must I do the rite","why me rite of vision","why can't someone else do the rite"},it="why_me",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For young tauren, the Rite of Vision is a deeply sacred passage into adulthood, demonstrating communion with the ancestors and obedience to spiritual guidance.",["class:Shaman"]="Following an ancestral spirit wolf is an early lesson in walking between worlds, grounding a shaman's bond with the spirits that guide the Horde.",["class:Druid"]="The rite reinforces reverence for the natural world and spiritual humility before the guidance of animal forms."},kw={"bloodhoof village","ghost wolf","mulgore","rite of vision","seer wiserunner","tribal fire","water of the seers","zarlman two-moons"},rel={"quest:767","npc:zarlman-two-moons","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Zarlman Two-Moons",["end"]="Seer Wiserunner",level="7",faction="Horde",id=772},src={"Rite of Vision (3)"}}
E["quest:773"] = {t="quest",n="Rite of Wisdom",z="mulgore",s="The Rite of Wisdom is the concluding trial in the traditional Rites of the Earthmother undertaken by young adventurers in Mulgore. After purifying their sight through the Rite of Vision, petitioners travel to the sacred burial grounds of Red Rocks to commune with an Ancestral Spirit and receive the blessing of the dead.",h="The Rite of Wisdom is the concluding trial in the traditional Rites of the Earthmother undertaken by young...",sec={{t="The Trial of Red Rocks",b="Having cleared their perception through the Rite of Vision, young protectors are directed east of Thunder Bluff toward Red Rocks. This ancient burial ground holds the physical remains and lingering spirits of those who fought and died to carve out a peaceful existence in Mulgore. Passing the trial requires approaching the resting grounds with proper reverence and communion, facing whatever territorial threats roam the hills to seek the counsel of the honored dead.",sp=0},{t="Passing the Mantle",b="At the summit of Red Rocks, the communion with the Ancestral Spirit is not a test of martial strength, but of spiritual readiness and continuity. The spirits resting there represent the generation that fought alongside Cairne Bloodhoof to establish Thunder Bluff and safeguard the tribes. By receiving their blessing, the supplicant officially inherits the solemn duty of protecting the city, earning their place as a recognized adult and defender worthy of entering Thunder Bluff with pride.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of the Rite of Wisdom?",a="The Rite of Wisdom completes the Rites of the Earthmother, bridging the visionary communion of youth with the solemn responsibilities of adulthood. By gaining the blessing of the ancestral spirits, petitioners prove they respect the sacrifices of those who came before and are prepared to defend Thunder Bluff.",al={"why do rite of wisdom","what does rite of wisdom do","rite of wisdom purpose"},it="purpose"},{q="Who sends you on the Rite of Wisdom?",a="Seer Wiserunner, in his cave northwest of Bloodhoof Village, sends initiates on this trial once they have successfully completed the Rite of Vision and drunk from the Water of the Seers.",al={"who gives rite of wisdom","where do i start rite of wisdom"},it="origin"},{q="Where do you find the Ancestral Spirit?",a="The Ancestral Spirit is located at Red Rocks, an ancient tauren burial ground nestled in the rocky bluffs directly east of Thunder Bluff.",al={"ancestral spirit location","where is the spirit","where to find ancestral spirit"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why is the Rite of Vision required first?",a="Only those who have drunk from the Water of the Seers and had their spiritual sight cleansed are able to perceive and receive the blessing of the ancient spirits at Red Rocks.",al={"why do vision first","rite of vision prerequisite","water of seers requirement"},it="history"},{q="What happens after completing the Rite of Wisdom?",a="Having earned the approval of the ancestors, initiates are instructed to make their official journey into the high rises of Thunder Bluff.",al={"what next after rite of wisdom","next quest after rite of wisdom"},it="next_step"},{q="What creatures threaten the grounds of Red Rocks?",a="The sacred graveyard at Red Rocks has been encroached upon by aggressive Palemane gnolls, who lurk along the rocky trails leading up to the ancient burial site.",al={"enemies at red rocks","what guards red rocks","gnolls at red rocks"},it="danger"},{q="Why does Seer Wiserunner send you on the Rite of Wisdom?",a="Seer Wiserunner guides promising young tauren through the traditional Rites of the Earthmother. Having seen you endure the vision granted by the Water of the Seers, he directs you to Red Rocks so you can receive the blessing of the ancestors. For Wiserunner and the elders, this rite ensures that only those spiritually prepared and acknowledged by the ancestors are welcomed into Thunder Bluff as protectors of the tribe.",al={"seer wiserunner motive","why rite of wisdom","why send me rite of wisdom"},it="motive",add=true},{q="Why does Seer Wiserunner need you to go instead of going himself?",a="The Rite of Wisdom is a personal trial of passage, not an errand that someone else can run on your behalf. To gain acceptance among the elders and earn the right to enter Thunder Bluff, you must personally seek out the Ancestral Spirit at Red Rocks and demonstrate the spiritual clarity gained from the Water of the Seers. The duty of protecting the city can only be bestowed directly upon you.",al={"why me rite of wisdom","why doesn't seer wiserunner go","why do i have to seek ancestral spirit"},it="why_me",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For the shu'halo, receiving the mantle from the ancestors at Red Rocks is the culminating moment of passage into full adulthood and acceptance among the tribal elders.",["class:Shaman"]="Communing directly with an Ancestral Spirit reinforces the core shamanic duty of honoring the dead and keeping faith with the spirits that watch over Mulgore.",["class:Druid"]="The rite demonstrates the balance between the living world and the eternal cycle overseen by the Earthmother, grounding young druids in their duty to the land."},kw={"ancestral spirit","mulgore","red rocks","rite of wisdom","rites of the earthmother","seer wiserunner","thunder bluff","water of the seers"},rel={"quest:767","quest:763","subzone:red-rocks","zone:thunderbluff","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren","topic:gnoll"},m={start="Seer Wiserunner",["end"]="Ancestral Spirit",level="10",faction="Horde",id=773},src={"Rite of Wisdom (Classic)"}}
E["quest:775"] = {t="quest",n="Journey into Thunder Bluff",z="mulgore",s="Journey into Thunder Bluff is the culminating pilgrimage marking the end of a young hero's trials across Mulgore. Having completed the sacred Rites of the Earthmother under the guidance of the spirits, the adventurer is granted an audience with the High Chieftain of the tauren, Cairne Bloodhoof.",h="Journey into Thunder Bluff is the culminating pilgrimage marking the end of a young hero's trials across...",sec={{t="A Rite of Passage Concluded",b="Before any traveler or fledgling warrior can truly claim to have proven themselves in Mulgore, they must undergo the ancient Rites of the Earthmother. These trials test strength, vision, and wisdom across the rolling plains and ancestral sites of the land. Once the Ancestral Spirit acknowledges the final rite, the initiate is deemed worthy of entering the city of Thunder Bluff not as a mere visitor, but as an honored defender of the tauren people.",sp=0},{t="Ascending to the High Rise",b="The quest directs the traveler from the sacred ground where the spirits wander straight to the towering heights of Thunder Bluff. Perched atop the mesas, the city is a beacon of peace carved from red stone and supported by sweeping rope bridges and massive wooden elevators. There, on the High Rise, High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof awaits to personally greet those who have walked the path of the ancestors.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who sends you on this journey?",a="The quest is bestowed by an Ancestral Spirit after you finish the Rite of Wisdom, the final step in the traditional Rites of the Earthmother.",al={"who gives quest","ancestral spirit quest","quest giver"},it="identity"},{q="Where can I find Cairne Bloodhoof?",a="Cairne Bloodhoof resides on the High Rise, the central elevated mesa in Thunder Bluff overlooking the surrounding red plateaus and plains.",al={"cairne location","where is cairne","thunder bluff leader"},it="leader",gp=true},{q="What is the significance of completing this quest?",a="It serves as the formal graduation from the starting adventures of Mulgore. Presenting yourself to Cairne marks you as fully recognized by the tauren leadership and ready to take on duties throughout the wider Horde.",al={"why visit cairne","rites meaning","significance of thunder bluff journey"},it="purpose"},{q="Do only tauren complete this journey?",a="While it is a deeply traditional tauren rite of passage, any member of the Horde who proves their dedication through the Rites of the Earthmother is granted the same honor and sent to meet the High Chieftain.",al={"can orcs do this","who can do journey to thunder bluff","races"},it="people"},{q="What happens after speaking to Cairne?",a="Cairne welcomes you to his home, acknowledges your deeds, and points you forward into the broader conflicts facing the Horde across Kalimdor.",al={"next quest","what after cairne","after journey into thunder bluff"},it="next_step"},{q="How do you reach the High Rise in Thunder Bluff?",a="Take the great mechanical elevators from the plains below up to the mesas, then cross the rope and timber bridges that lead toward the central High Rise platform where the Chieftain's lodge stands.",al={"getting to high rise","how to get to cairne","thunder bluff elevators"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why does the Ancestral Spirit send you to Thunder Bluff?",a="By surviving the trials across Mulgore and communing with the spirits, you have proven your wisdom, strength, and devotion through the Rites of the Earthmother. The Ancestral Spirit directs you to Thunder Bluff so that your deeds may be recognized by the tauren people and their highest leader, Cairne Bloodhoof. Standing before the High Chieftain marks your formal acceptance into their society as an honored champion.",al={"why ancestral spirit sends you","ancestral spirit motive","why go to cairne"},it="motive",add=true},{q="Why must you deliver the news to Cairne Bloodhoof yourself?",a="The Rites of the Earthmother are a sacred personal trial. The Ancestral Spirit is an otherworldly guide bound to Mulgore's holy grounds, unable and unwilling to carry earthly reports. Because it is your spirit and resolve that were tested, the honor of presenting yourself to High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof belongs solely to you.",al={"why me cairne","why must i visit cairne","why do i have to see cairne bloodhoof"},it="why_me",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For a young tauren, standing before Cairne Bloodhoof after completing the Earthmother's rites is the definitive threshold of adulthood and tribal honor.",["faction:Horde"]="The willingness of the tauren to welcome any allied race who completes their sacred traditions speaks to the mutual respect holding the young Horde together."},kw={"ancestral spirit","cairne bloodhoof","high rise","journey into thunder bluff","mulgore","rites of the earthmother","thunder bluff"},rel={"quest:763","npc:cairne-bloodhoof","zone:thunderbluff","subzone:high-rise","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Ancestral Spirit",["end"]="Cairne Bloodhoof",level="10",faction="Horde",id=775},src={"Journey into Thunder Bluff (Classic)"}}
E["quest:780"] = {t="quest",n="The Battleboars",z="mulgore",s="The Battleboars is a rite-of-passage hunting quest given by Grull Hawkwind in Camp Narache. Young tauren are tasked with culling the vicious war-trained boars of Brambleblade Ravine to defend tribal lands and feed the settlement's youth.",h="The Battleboars is a rite-of-passage hunting quest given by Grull Hawkwind in Camp Narache.",sec={{t="A Threat on the Border",b="To the east of Red Cloud Mesa lies Brambleblade Ravine, a thorn-choked scar held by the Bristleback quilboar. As tensions between the Shu'halo and the quilboar escalate into open war, the Bristlebacks have trained vicious battleboars to probe tauren boundaries and terrorize their traditional hunting grounds. Left unchecked, these aggressive beasts threaten not only game trails but the safety of Camp Narache itself.",sp=0},{t="Feeding the Tribe",b="For the tauren, hunting serves both defensive necessity and communal sustenance. Grull Hawkwind instructs initiates to bring back the snouts and flanks of the slain battleboars. Even beasts trained by quilboar hands are not wasted; their meat and gristle are harvested to prepare hearty stew for the tribe's youth, turning a quilboar weapon into nourishment for the next generation of tauren defenders.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why are the battleboars attacking the mesa?",a="The battleboars have been deliberately bred and trained by the Bristleback quilboar inhabiting Brambleblade Ravine. The quilboar use them as shock animals to harass the tauren and encroach upon Red Cloud Mesa's tribal hunting grounds.",al={"battleboar aggression","why battleboars hostile","battleboars attacking"},it="threat"},{q="Who sends players on this hunt?",a="Grull Hawkwind, a prominent hunter and elder stationed at Camp Narache, directs young tauren through the concluding trials of their early training.",al={"quest giver battleboars","who gives the battleboars quest","grull hawkwind role"},it="people",gp=true},{q="Where are the battleboars found?",a="They wander near and within Brambleblade Ravine, a rugged canyon located just east of Camp Narache on the eastern edge of Red Cloud Mesa.",al={"battleboar location","where are battleboars","brambleblade ravine boars"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What happens to the meat collected from the boars?",a="The battleboar flanks and snouts are brought back to Camp Narache to be simmered into stew for the settlement's children and elders, honoring the tauren tradition of utilizing what they hunt.",al={"battleboar flanks use","what is boar meat for","boar stew"},it="item"},{q="How does this quest fit into the local quilboar conflict?",a="It serves as an early introduction to the active hostility between the tauren and the Bristleback tribe. Culling their war animals sends a direct message to the quilboar before open clashes erupt against the Bristlebacks themselves.",al={"tauren quilboar war","bristleback conflict","quilboar lore"},it="conflict"},{q="What quest precedes The Battleboars?",a="It is preceded by The Hunt Continues, an earlier task from Grull Hawkwind focused on learning the hunting grounds of Red Cloud Mesa.",al={"battleboars prerequisite","previous quest battleboars","hunt continues chain"},gp=true},{q="Why does Grull Hawkwind want the battleboars killed?",a="The battleboars have pushed out from Brambleblade Ravine and encroached straight into the Red Cloud tribe's ancestral hunting grounds. Bred and trained to be vicious by the Bristleback quilboar, their mounting aggression directly threatens Camp Narache. Grull Hawkwind intends to send the quilboar a firm message by thinning their war-beasts while turning the hunt into food for the tribe's young.",al={"why grull hawkwind want battleboars dead","grull hawkwind battleboars motive","why kill battleboars mulgore"},it="motive",add=true},{q="What are battleboars?",a="Battleboars are ferocious beasts raised and trained by the Bristleback quilboar of Brambleblade Ravine. Unlike the wild mountain cougars and plainstriders native to the Red Cloud Mesa, these boars are purposefully conditioned for war and malice, serving as living weapons in the Bristlebacks' ongoing struggle against the tauren.",al={"who are the battleboars","what are the battleboars","battleboars lore"},it="target",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="This task embodies the tauren balance of defending the Earth Mother's territories while ensuring nothing taken in battle is wasted, turning enemies' war beasts into sustenance for the camp's calves.",["class:Hunter"]="Facing beasts trained for malice by the Bristlebacks teaches young hunters the sharp contrast between natural wildlife and animals twisted into weapons of war.",["profession:Cooking"]="The request for flanks and snouts highlights the fundamental tauren culinary ethos: coarse, tough meats can always be rendered down into hearty, sustaining stews."},kw={"battleboar","brambleblade ravine","bristleback","camp narache","grull hawkwind","mulgore","quilboar","red cloud mesa"},rel={"npc:grull-hawkwind","subzone:brambleblade-ravine","subzone:camp-narache","topic:quilboar","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Grull Hawkwind",level="4",faction="Horde",id=780,ri={"Battleboar Flank","Battleboar Snout"}},src={"The Battleboars"}}
E["quest:781"] = {t="quest",n="Attack on Camp Narache",z="mulgore",s="Attack on Camp Narache is a vital discovery made within the quilboar den of Brambleblade Ravine. A dirt-stained map reveals that the aggressive Bristleback tribe is actively plotting an organized siege against Camp Narache. Delivering these plans to Chief Hawkwind alerts Camp Narache in time to summon aid from Bloodhoof Village.",h="Attack on Camp Narache is a vital discovery made within the quilboar den of Brambleblade Ravine.",sec={{t="A Plot in the Brambles",b="While scouting deep inside the caverns of Brambleblade Ravine, young tauren adventurers uncover a crude, dirt-stained map laying on the rock floor. Upon closer inspection, the parchment reveals deliberate tactical markings made by the Bristleback quilboar. Rather than engaging in mere opportunistic skirmishes, the quilboar are coordinating a full-scale, prolonged siege against Camp Narache.",sp=0},{t="Warning the Chieftain",b="Carrying the attack plans back to Camp Narache provides Chief Hawkwind with crucial intelligence. Recognizing the dire threat posed to the relatively undefended camp and its young initiates, Hawkwind prepares to send word to Bloodhoof Village for reinforcements. The intercepted map transforms what could have been a catastrophic ambush into a prepared defense.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where are the Bristleback attack plans found?",a="The plans originate from a dirt-stained map located inside a cave in Brambleblade Ravine, just up a path to the right of Chief Sharptusk's hut.",al={"map location","where is dirt-stained map","find the plans"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Who receives the Bristleback attack plans?",a="The plans must be delivered directly to Chief Hawkwind at the center of Camp Narache.",al={"who to turn in to","who gets the map","quest ender"},it="next_step",gp=true},{q="Why are the Bristleback quilboar targeting Camp Narache?",a="The Bristleback tribe seeks to drive the tauren from the Red Cloud Mesa and reclaim the hunting grounds and sacred plains of Mulgore for their own kind.",al={"why attack camp narache","quilboar motives mulgore","bristleback siege"},it="conflict"},{q="How does Chief Hawkwind plan to counter the siege?",a="With early warning provided by the intercepted map, Chief Hawkwind intends to call upon their brethren in Bloodhoof Village to reinforce the mesa and repel the assault.",al={"hawkwind plan","reinforcements camp narache"},it="next_step"},{q="Does this quest require speaking to anyone before picking up the map?",a="No. The quest begins directly from the Dirt-stained Map lying on the cave floor in Brambleblade Ravine, turning into the battle plans once retrieved.",al={"quest starter","dirt stained map starter","how to start attack on camp narache"},gp=true},{q="What happens if Camp Narache falls?",a="Camp Narache is the primary haven for young tauren braves and rites of passage; losing it would compromise the security of Red Cloud Mesa and sever Mulgore's spiritual training grounds.",al={"stakes of camp narache","why defend narache"},it="danger"}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For young tauren braves undertaking their earliest rites on the mesa, intercepting this map proves your loyalty to your tribe and protects your kin from a sudden massacre.",["faction:Horde"]="Preserving the tauren's foothold in Mulgore ensures the Horde retains its vital southern anchor in central Kalimdor."},kw={"attack plans","bloodhoof village","brambleblade ravine","bristleback","camp narache","chief hawkwind","dirt-stained map","quilboar","red cloud mesa"},rel={"subzone:camp-narache","subzone:brambleblade-ravine","npc:chief-hawkwind","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Dirt-stained Map",["end"]="Chief Hawkwind",level="4",faction="Horde",id=781},src={"Attack on Camp Narache"}}
E["quest:833"] = {t="quest",n="A Sacred Burial",z="mulgore",s="A Sacred Burial is a quest entrusted by Lorekeeper Raintotem, tasking Horde adventurers with protecting Red Rocks in eastern Mulgore. The sacred ancestral burial grounds have been overrun by marauding Bristleback quilboar who are defiling the graves of esteemed tauren warriors. Driving off these interlopers restores honor and peace to those who gave their lives founding and defending Thunder Bluff.",h="A Sacred Burial is a quest entrusted by Lorekeeper Raintotem, tasking Horde adventurers with protecting Red...",sec={{t="Hallowed Ground of the Shu'halo",b="Red Rocks serves as one of the most revered sanctuaries in all of Mulgore. Tucked into the crags beneath the eastern mountains, it is the final resting place reserved exclusively for the most valiant tauren. The heroes interred here are legendary warriors who shed blood to protect their tribes, uphold their chieftains, and carve out a permanent homeland atop the mesas of Thunder Bluff. To the Shu'halo, disturbing their rest is an affront not just to tradition, but to the Earth Mother herself.",sp=0},{t="The Bristleback Desecration",b="The nomadic quilboar of the Bristleback tribe have persistently contested tauren control over central Kalimdor, but their recent push into Red Rocks represents a grave escalation. Bands of Bristleback Interlopers have crept onto the holy grounds, ravaging the earth and disturbing the resting sites. With the guardian Lorekeeper Raintotem too aged to single-handedly drive the invaders away, young champions of the Horde must take up weapons to purge the site of the intruders and defend the honored dead.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Red Rocks?",a="Red Rocks is a sacred ancestral cemetery located along the eastern mountains of Mulgore. It is reserved specifically for the most valiant tauren warriors, particularly those who died defending their tribes and founding Thunder Bluff.",al={"where is red rocks","red rocks mulgore","tauren burial ground"},it="place"},{q="Who is Lorekeeper Raintotem?",a="Lorekeeper Raintotem is an elder tauren tasked with tending and safeguarding the sacred grounds of Red Rocks. Recognizing that he is past his prime and cannot physically repel the invading Bristlebacks alone, he calls upon capable adventurers to defend the dead.",al={"raintotem","lorekeeper raintotem","who gives a sacred burial"},it="identity"},{q="Why are the Bristleback quilboar at Red Rocks?",a="The Bristleback tribe continuously presses against tauren territory across Mulgore and the Barrens. Desperate for territory and resources, bands of interlopers have pushed into the crags of Red Rocks, desecrating the gravesites in the process.",al={"why are quilboar at red rocks","bristleback interlopers","quilboar invasion"},it="target"},{q="Why is this desecration so offensive to the tauren?",a="Tauren culture places immense reverence upon honoring their ancestors and living in balance with the spirits of the fallen. Allowing savage quilboar to ravage the earth and disturb the bones of heroes who died for Thunder Bluff is a deeply spiritual insult.",al={"tauren funerary customs","why tauren care about burial"},it="motive"},{q="Where does this quest lead next?",a="A Sacred Burial is a standalone defensive task offered by Lorekeeper Raintotem. Once the Bristleback Interlopers are dealt with, the immediate desecration is halted, though other tasks at Red Rocks delve into the spiritual unrest within the bluffs.",al={"sacred burial follow up","what comes after sacred burial"},it="next_step"},{q="Who is buried at Red Rocks?",a="The site is reserved for champion tauren who fought for the survival of their tribes and chieftains, especially those whose sacrifices made the establishment and defense of Thunder Bluff possible.",al={"who is buried here","red rocks graves"},it="history"},{q="Why does Lorekeeper Raintotem ask you instead of driving them off himself?",a="Lorekeeper Raintotem is an elder who is simply too old and past his prime to take up arms against a violent warband. Tasked with tending and honoring the spirits of Red Rocks rather than fighting on the front lines, he relies on capable travelers like you to handle the physical danger of driving off the Bristleback invaders.",al={"why can't raintotem do it","why raintotem needs help","why doesn't raintotem fight"},it="why_me",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Defending Red Rocks is a matter of profound ancestral honor, ensuring that the warriors who made your people's unified homeland possible rest undisturbed.",["class:Shaman"]="The desecration of sacred grounds agitates the spirits of the honored ancestors, making your intervention necessary to restore balance to the earth.",["faction:Horde"]="Assisting the tauren elders in securing their sacred monuments solidifies the Horde's pledge to defend Mulgore alongside their allies."},kw={"a sacred burial","ancestors","bristleback interloper","lorekeeper raintotem","mulgore","quilboar","red rocks","thunder bluff"},rel={"subzone:red-rocks","npc:lorekeeper-raintotem","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren","zone:thunderbluff"},m={start="Lorekeeper Raintotem",faction="Horde",id=833},src={"A Sacred Burial"}}
E["quest:861"] = {t="quest",n="The Hunter's Way",z="mulgore",s="The Hunter's Way is a rite of passage offered to aspiring trackers in Mulgore by the elder Skorn Whitecloud. To prove their emerging skill and respect for the wild, wanderers must fell the cunning flatland prowlers and present their claws to Melor Stonehoof atop Thunder Bluff's Hunter Rise.",h="The Hunter's Way is a rite of passage offered to aspiring trackers in Mulgore by the elder Skorn Whitecloud.",sec={{t="A Tradition of the Plains",b="For the tauren, hunting is neither mere sport nor simple subsistence; it is considered one of their people's highest honors. It demands patience, awareness of the Earth Mother's balance, and reverence for the beasts that roam the rolling plains. Elder Skorn Whitecloud, resting in Mulgore after decades of tracking across Kalimdor, recognizes this restlessness in young adventurers. Rather than allowing them to wander aimlessly, he channels their ambition into a traditional trial of maturity and discipline.",sp=0},{t="The Stalkers of the Grasslands",b="To demonstrate that their tracking instincts are genuine, candidates are tasked with taking down Mulgore's flatland prowlers. These great cats are swift, stealthy, and cunning ambush predators that blend easily into the high grass of the plains. Gathering four sets of claws proves that a fledgling wanderer can spot a predator before becoming prey, turning the hunter's own ferocity back upon them.",sp=0},{t="Reaching Hunter Rise",b="Upon securing the trophies, the initiate ascends the soaring wooden towers and bridges leading up to Thunder Bluff. There, upon the Hunter Rise, Melor Stonehoof receives the claws. As a master tracker who values Skorn Whitecloud's ancient wisdom, Stonehoof accepts the offering as proof that the initiate possesses the right spirit to be guided further along the predatory paths of Kalimdor.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who offers the task to walk the Hunter's Way?",a="The quest is offered by Skorn Whitecloud, a venerable tauren plains-wanderer found resting in Mulgore who mentors young adventurers looking to master the wilderness.",al={"quest giver","who gives the quest","skorn whitecloud"},it="identity"},{q="Where do I take the flatland prowler claws?",a="Take the claws to Melor Stonehoof, who resides on the Hunter Rise in the tauren capital of Thunder Bluff.",al={"where is melor stonehoof","turn in location","hunter rise"},it="next_step",gp=true},{q="Why are flatland prowler claws chosen as the tribute?",a="Flatland prowlers are stealthy and dangerous apex hunters of the Mulgore grasslands. Successfully tracking and defeating them proves that an initiate possesses the keen eyes, patience, and combat discipline necessary for advanced hunting.",al={"why flatland prowlers","prowler claws lore","what are prowlers"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Is this quest restricted only to the Hunter class?",a="No. Despite the name, Skorn Whitecloud offers this test of resolve to any young Horde adventurer who displays the hunger to wander and learn the traditions of the plains.",al={"hunters only?","can other classes do this","is it for hunters"},gp=true},{q="Where does the hunter's journey lead after speaking to Melor?",a="Once Melor Stonehoof recognizes your burgeoning talent, he begins directing your steps outward into the harsher territory of the Barrens, eventually sending you toward experienced trackers like Sergra Darkthorn.",al={"what comes next","next quest","where to go after melor"},sp=1,it="next_step"},{q="What significance does hunting hold in tauren culture?",a="In tauren belief, hunting is among the highest honors. It requires living in direct harmony with the Earth Mother's creation, taking only what is needed, and showing profound respect to the quarry that sustains their tribes.",al={"tauren hunting lore","hunting honor tauren","why is hunting important to tauren"},it="fame"},{q="Why does Skorn Whitecloud want this done?",a="Skorn Whitecloud is an elder hunter whose own roaming days are behind him, but his spirit still burns fiercely. Seeing your restlessness and desire to explore, he wants to guide your ambition toward the honorable traditions of the hunt. By testing your capability and directing you to Melor Stonehoof on Hunter's Rise, Skorn ensures that the tauren's sacred hunting heritage is passed on to a promising new generation.",al={"why does skorn give this quest","skorn whitecloud motive","why send me to melor"},it="motive",add=true},{q="What is the Flatland Prowler Claw for?",a="The claws serve as undeniable proof of your tracking skill, cunning, and martial resolve. Flatland prowlers are fierce and elusive predators of the Mulgore plains. Presenting four of their claws to Melor Stonehoof demonstrates that you possess both the patience to hunt dangerous game and the strength to overcome it, showing the masters on Hunter's Rise that you are worthy of further training.",al={"flatland prowler claws purpose","why collect claws","prowler claw tribute"},it="item",add=true},{q="What are flatland prowlers and where are they found?",a="Flatland prowlers are predatory big cats native to the open grasslands of Mulgore. They roam the plains stalking game, relying on their stealth, speed, and razor claws to bring down prey. Because of their natural cunning and strength, they are considered an ideal quarry for testing whether an aspiring hunter has mastered the basic skills needed to track and fell wild beasts.",al={"flatland prowlers","mulgore prowlers","what are the prowlers"},it="target",add=true},{q="Why does Skorn Whitecloud send you instead of hunting the prowlers himself?",a="Skorn has hunted across Mulgore for many years, but his body has grown old even while his spirit remains sharp. More importantly, the task is not a simple gathering chore for camp provisions—it is a rite of passage for you. Skorn recognizes your youthful wanderlust and tests your resolve directly so that you may prove yourself worthy of guidance under Melor Stonehoof.",al={"why doesnt skorn hunt","why me skorn whitecloud","why must i get the claws"},it="why_me",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For young tauren, presenting prowler claws to Hunter Rise is a foundational rite of passage that connects them to generations of ancestors who hunted these same sweeping plains.",["class:Hunter"]="The trial directly tests the core principles of the wild—stalking an ambush predator before it strikes first and reading the subtle signs of the grasslands.",["faction:Horde"]="This rite serves as a bridge for allied Horde wanderers to understand tauren reverence for nature and earn their trust on the heights of Thunder Bluff."},kw={"flatland prowler","flatland prowler claws","hunter rise","melor stonehoof","mulgore","skorn whitecloud","the hunter's way","thunder bluff"},rel={"npc:skorn-whitecloud","npc:melor-stonehoof","zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff","topic:tauren","quest:6065"},m={start="Skorn Whitecloud",["end"]="Melor Stonehoof",level="10",faction="Horde",id=861,ri={"Flatland Prowler Claw"}},src={"The Hunter's Way (Classic)"}}
E["quest:8673"] = {t="quest",n="Bloodhoof the Elder",z="mulgore",s="Bloodhoof the Elder is an encounter with one of the venerated ancestral spirits who manifests during the Lunar Festival. Located just outside Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore, this honored spirit acknowledges the respect paid by wandering travelers and grants an ancestral token in return.",h="Bloodhoof the Elder is an encounter with one of the venerated ancestral spirits who manifests during the...",sec={{t="A Visit from the Ancestors",b="During the celebration of the Lunar Festival, the spirits of great heroes and elders from ages past return to Azeroth. Shrouded in celestial radiance, Elder Bloodhoof appears just north of Bloodhoof Village near the bridge. Approaching these venerated ancestors is an act of honoring past sacrifices, and in exchange, Elder Bloodhoof offers words of encouragement and a sacred Coin of Ancestry.",sp=0},{t="The Bloodhoof Legacy",b="Standing on the ancestral lands of Mulgore, Elder Bloodhoof serves as a bridge between the living tauren tribes and the memory of their forebears. For the tauren, honoring the dead is a pillar of spiritual life, reinforcing the harmony between the Earth Mother, the ancestors, and those currently walking the plains.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where can I find Elder Bloodhoof?",a="Elder Bloodhoof stands illuminated by a beam of celestial light just outside Bloodhoof Village in Mulgore, near the bridge and Ahab Wheathoof.",al={"elder bloodhoof location","where is elder bloodhoof","bloodhoof elder spot"}},{q="What is the purpose of seeking out Elder Bloodhoof?",a="Travelers seek out Elder Bloodhoof during the Lunar Festival to pay their respects to the ancestors. In recognition of this honor, the spirit rewards visitors with a Coin of Ancestry and wisdom about neighboring spirits, such as Elder Wheathoof.",al={"why talk to elder bloodhoof","bloodhoof elder purpose","what does elder bloodhoof give"}},{q="Why is there a beam of light shining down on him?",a="The shaft of light marks the spirit's presence as an ancestral apparition manifested during the Lunar Festival, symbolizing a sacred connection to the skies and the spirits of the past.",al={"why is elder bloodhoof glowing","light on elder bloodhoof"}},{q="Can Alliance characters pay homage to Elder Bloodhoof?",a="The Lunar Festival ancestors honor all mortals who show reverence, regardless of faction. However, Alliance travelers must tread carefully, as Elder Bloodhoof is located right on the doorstep of Horde territory.",al={"can alliance do this quest","is elder bloodhoof horde only"}},{q="Who is Elder Wheathoof that this elder mentions?",a="Elder Wheathoof is another ancestral spirit honoring the festival, located elsewhere in Mulgore. Elder Bloodhoof can provide guidance on where to seek him out.",al={"elder wheathoof","who is wheathoof"}},{q="What do the Coins of Ancestry represent?",a="Coins of Ancestry represent tokens of favor from the honored spirits of Azeroth. They demonstrate that a traveler has shown respect to the past, and festival celebrants trade them for festive attire, schematics, and celebratory wares.",al={"what are coins of ancestry","lunar festival tokens"}}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Greeting Elder Bloodhoof is a sacred duty on home soil, honoring the legacy of the tribe that helped lead the tauren to their peaceful haven in Mulgore.",["class:Druid"]="Druids hold the spirits of the Lunar Festival in high regard, communing with the elders to strengthen their natural bond with the earth and stars.",["class:Shaman"]="For a shaman, conversing with manifest ancestors like Elder Bloodhoof directly reinforces the living communion between the mortal realm and ancestral spirits.",["faction:Alliance"]="Reaching Elder Bloodhoof requires slipping into the heart of Mulgore, making this a bold journey through hostile Horde plains."},kw={"ahab wheathoof","ancestor","bloodhoof village","coin of ancestry","elder bloodhoof","lunar festival","mulgore"},rel={"npc:elder-bloodhoof","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore","topic:light"},m={start="Elder Bloodhoof",id=8673},src={"Bloodhoof the Elder"}}
E["quest:96659"] = {t="quest",n="The Adventurer",z="mulgore",s="As young tauren prepare to depart their ancestral training grounds on Red Cloud Mesa, Chief Hawkwind asks them to perform a final favor. He entrusts them with a bundle of essential supplies meant for Kaga Wildhoof, a seasoned wanderer camped near Bloodhoof Village who once aided the chief in his own past.",h="As young tauren prepare to depart their ancestral training grounds on Red Cloud Mesa, Chief Hawkwind asks...",sec={{t="A Chieftain's Repayment",b="Before travelers descend the mesa path toward the broader plains of Mulgore, Chief Hawkwind pauses to address unfinished personal debts. Long before guiding new generations of hunters and shamans on Camp Narache, Hawkwind crossed paths with Kaga Wildhoof. Described by the chief as an accomplished adventurer, Wildhoof provided vital aid when he needed it most. Hearing that she has pitched camp just outside Bloodhoof Village, Hawkwind uses the departure of newly proven novices to send provisions to his old benefactor.",sp=0},{t="Leaving the Mesa",b="This errand marks a pivotal rite of passage for young Horde champions leaving Red Cloud Mesa. The trail down the plateau leads directly toward Bloodhoof Village, the cultural and logistical heart of central Mulgore. Carrying the supply bundle across the plains bridges the sheltered training life of the mesa with the wider, wilder struggles of the United Tauren Tribes.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who is Kaga Wildhoof?",a="Kaga Wildhoof is a veteran tauren adventurer who has recently made camp just outside Bloodhoof Village. She holds a longstanding history of friendship with Chief Hawkwind, having aided him significantly in years past.",al={"kaga wildhoof","who is wildhoof","where is kaga"},it="target"},{q="Why does Chief Hawkwind send these supplies?",a="Hawkwind views the departure of young champions heading down to Bloodhoof Village as the ideal opportunity to repay an old favor to Wildhoof, ensuring his fellow adventurer has fresh supplies for her travels.",al={"hawkwind debt","why send supplies","purpose of supply bundle"},it="motive"},{q="Where is Kaga Wildhoof's camp located?",a="Her camp is situated on the plains just outside Bloodhoof Village, down the winding southern path from Red Cloud Mesa into central Mulgore.",al={"kaga camp location","find kaga","bloodhoof village camp"},it="place",gp=true},{q="When does this quest become available?",a="Chief Hawkwind offers the task once a traveler has proven their mettle on Red Cloud Mesa and is ready to venture out into the broader territories of Mulgore.",al={"when do i get the adventurer","hawkwind quest timing","mesa exit quest"},gp=true},{q="Does this quest take you away from Camp Narache?",a="Yes. Carrying the supplies serves as a direct prompt to leave Camp Narache behind and travel down the trail toward Bloodhoof Village.",al={"leaving red cloud mesa","journey to bloodhoof"},gp=true},{q="What is contained within the Supply Bundle?",a="The bundle consists of essential provisions gathered by the tribes of Camp Narache to sustain an adventurer surviving outside organized settlements.",al={"what are the supplies","supply bundle contents"},it="item"},{q="Why does Chief Hawkwind ask you to deliver the supplies?",a="You have completed your initial rites in the Red Cloud Mesa and are preparing to leave Camp Narache to venture into the broader plains of Mulgore. With your departure already taking you past Bloodhoof Village, Chief Hawkwind entrusts you with this errand rather than dispatching braves who are needed to guard the mesa and protect the young tauren training there.",al={"why me hawkwind","why send me with supplies","why does hawkwind choose me"},it="why_me",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For young tauren stepping beyond Camp Narache for the first time, delivering Hawkwind's debt of honor to Kaga Wildhoof represents their initial service to their kin in the wider plains of Mulgore.",["faction:Horde"]="Chief Hawkwind ensures that traveling Horde allies leaving the starting mesa form early ties with the experienced wanderers and outposts of Mulgore."},kw={"bloodhoof village","camp narache","chief hawkwind","kaga wildhoof","mulgore","red cloud mesa","supply bundle","the adventurer"},rel={"npc:chief-hawkwind","subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore"},m={start="Chief Hawkwind",["end"]="Kaga Wildhoof",level="6",faction="Horde",id=96659},src={"The Adventurer (Mulgore)"},fv=true}
E["subzone:bael-dun-digsite"] = {t="subzone",n="Bael'dun Digsite",z="mulgore",s="The Bael'dun Digsite is a dwarven excavation carved deep into the mountains along the western edge of Mulgore. Established by Prospector Khazgorm's team out of Bael Modan, the quarry is viewed by the local tauren as a direct desecration of their ancestral homeland and an insult to the Earth Mother.",h="The Bael'dun Digsite is a dwarven excavation carved deep into the mountains along the western edge of Mulgore.",sec={{t="Intrusion in the Hills",b="Perched on the mountain slopes of western Mulgore, the Bael'dun Digsite represents the southernmost reach of the dwarven Explorers' League expedition based out of Bael Modan in the southern Barrens. Unconcerned with territorial borders or the spiritual traditions of the newly settled tribes, dwarven prospectors arrived with picks, shovels, and blasting powder to unearth ancient Titan relics buried beneath the plains.",sp=0},{t="Defending the Earth Mother",b="The Bloodhoof tribe viewed this invasive quarrying as an outright assault on the Earth Mother. Baine Bloodhoof initially urged caution and attempted to warn the interlopers away, but the dwarves flatly refused to abandon their trenches. Concluding that diplomacy had failed, the tauren resolved to break the expedition's tools and forcibly expel the miners before their destructive digging tears further into Mulgore's sacred earth.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why are dwarves digging in Mulgore?",a="The prospectors belong to the dwarven expedition headquartered at Bael Modan in the southern Barrens. Driven by the Explorers' League's quest to uncover ancient artifacts and Titan heritage, they established this outpost wherever their instruments suggested relics might lie, disregarding tauren sovereignty.",al={"why are dwarves here","dwarves in mulgore","baeldun dwarves"},it="motive"},{q="How does the Bloodhoof tribe respond to the digsite?",a="The tauren view the operation as an intolerable wound against the Earth Mother. After the dwarves refused early warnings to leave peacefully, Baine Bloodhoof and the village leaders sent warriors to destroy the miners' tools and drive the trespassers out of Mulgore.",al={"what do tauren think","baine reaction","tauren response to digsite"},it="conflict"},{q="What is the connection between Bael'dun and Bael Modan?",a="Bael'dun is a satellite digsite established directly from the fortress and primary excavation at Bael Modan, located just across the mountain ridge in the southern Barrens.",al={"bael modan link","where do these dwarves come from"},it="ties"},{q="Why didn't the tauren simply negotiate with the expedition?",a="Baine Bloodhoof initially pursued diplomatic solutions and issued formal warnings to the expedition leaders. However, the dwarves treated Mulgore as unclaimed wilderness and completely ignored tauren spiritual concerns, leaving force as the tribe's only recourse.",al={"did tauren try peace","diplomacy with dwarves"}},{q="What is Bael'dun Digsite?",a="Bael'dun Digsite is an active dwarven excavation carved directly into the western mountains of Mulgore. Established by Prospector Khazgorm and members of the Dwarven Archaeological League out of Bael Modan, it represents an unwelcome Alliance intrusion deep inside tauren territory.",al={"what is baeldun digsite","baeldun quarry mulgore","dwarf digsite mulgore"},it="place",add=true},{q="What happened at Bael'dun Digsite?",a="Dwarven excavators arrived from the Barrens to unearth titan relics and ancient secrets buried beneath the soil. Ignoring the furious objections and warnings of Baine Bloodhoof, they began tearing into the earth. The Bloodhoof tribe responded by sabotaging their mining efforts and breaking their pickaxes to force them out of sacred lands.",al={"baeldun history","what happened at the digsite"},it="history",add=true},{q="Who runs Bael'dun Digsite?",a="The site is operated by dwarven miners and excavators dispatched from Bael Modan in the southern Barrens. They labor under the orders of the Explorer's League and dwarven expedition leaders who prioritize uncovering ancient artifacts over maintaining peace with the native tauren.",al={"who controls baeldun","baeldun digsite leader","who runs the digsite"},it="residents",add=true},{q="Is Bael'dun Digsite dangerous?",a="Yes. The dwarven excavators and foremen stationed here are hostile to Horde travelers and defend their claim with picks, rifles, and bludgeons. Furthermore, their relentless dynamiting and gouging of the mountainside threatens to disturb the natural spirits of Mulgore.",al={"how dangerous is baeldun digsite","is baeldun safe"},it="danger",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The intrusive scarring of Mulgore's western hills makes Bael'dun a direct insult to the Earth Mother, demanding decisive action to protect ancestral soil from foreign desecration.",["faction:Alliance"]="Though driven by an earnest thirst for historical discovery, the Bael'dun miners have overextended deep into sovereign Horde territory, sparking violent friction with the locals."},kw={"bael modan","bael'dun","baeldun","baine bloodhoof","digsite","dwarves","earth mother","explorers league","mulgore"},rel={"zone:mulgore","subzone:bael-modan","npc:baine-bloodhoof","topic:earth-mother","topic:tauren"},src={"Bael'dun Digsite"}}
E["subzone:bloodhoof-village"] = {t="subzone",n="Bloodhoof Village",z="mulgore",s="Bloodhoof Village is a central tauren settlement resting along the banks of Stonebull Lake in Mulgore. Named in honor of the Bloodhoof tribe, it serves as the first major gathering hub for young hunters and braves journeying out from Camp Narache toward Thunder Bluff. Led by Baine Bloodhoof, the village coordinates local defense, trade, and traditional rites across the plains.",h="Bloodhoof Village is a central tauren settlement resting along the banks of Stonebull Lake in Mulgore.",sec={{t="Heart of the Plains",b="Situated around the clear waters of Stonebull Lake, Bloodhoof Village is a bustling center of commerce, craft, and ceremony in central Mulgore. Young tauren departing their trials at Camp Narache arrive here to hone their skills before venturing onward to the great mesas of Thunder Bluff. It is also well-known across Kalimdor for its master drum makers and the patient breeders who tame wild kodo beasts for travel.",sp=0},{t="Leadership and Rites",b="Day-to-day governance and the defense of the region fall under Baine Bloodhoof, son of High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof. Under his watchful eye, braves are dispatched to secure the grasslands against aggressive wildlife, quilboar encroachers, and the shadowy presence of the Venture Company. Alongside Baine, spiritual guides such as Zarlman Two-Moons tend to the inner life of the tribe, directing novices through sacred journeys like the Rite of Vision.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Who leads Bloodhoof Village?",a="Baine Bloodhoof, son of High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof, leads the settlement and directs its defenses against threats to the central plains.",al={"village leader","baine bloodhoof location","who is in charge of bloodhoof village"},it="in_charge"},{q="What major rites are conducted here?",a="Spiritual advisors such as Zarlman Two-Moons guide young tauren through the traditional Rite of Vision, an essential spiritual journey on the path to adulthood.",al={"rite of vision","spiritual trials","zarlman two-moons quest"},it="purpose"},{q="What is the settlement known for producing?",a="Bloodhoof Village is famed for its uniquely crafted ceremony drums and is the primary hub where wild plains kodos are tamed and trained as mounts.",al={"tauren mounts","kodo taming","bloodhoof drums"}},{q="Where do travelers go after finishing their tasks here?",a="Once their deeds in central Mulgore are complete, travelers are generally directed northward to Thunder Bluff, the capital city of the tauren.",al={"after bloodhoof","where next after bloodhoof","next hub"},it="next_step"},{q="What is Bloodhoof Village?",a="Bloodhoof Village is a vital tauren settlement nestled in central Mulgore along the shores of Stonebull Lake. It serves as the primary waystation and crossroads for travelers and young braves journeying north from the Red Cloud Mesa toward Thunder Bluff. Here, the tauren gather to trade, seek spiritual guidance, and tame wild kodo beasts for travel.",al={"what is bloodhoof village","bloodhoof village lore","about bloodhoof village"},it="place",add=true},{q="Is Bloodhoof Village dangerous?",a="The village itself is a well-guarded haven, watched over by Baine Bloodhoof and local braves. However, the surrounding plains of Mulgore hold constant dangers, from prowling predators and aggressive gnolls to encroaching Venture Company loggers and shadowy Grimtotem operatives working against the peace of the united tribes.",al={"bloodhoof village danger","is bloodhoof safe","threats to bloodhoof"},it="danger",add=true},{q="Why is it called Bloodhoof Village?",a="The settlement is named in honor of the Bloodhoof tribe, the prominent tauren clan led by High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof. It proudly bears the clan's name and remains under the direct stewardship of Cairne's son, Baine.",al={"bloodhoof village name origin","why bloodhoof village","who is bloodhoof village named after"},it="name",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For young tauren, entering Bloodhoof Village marks the first real step beyond youth into broader tribal responsibility and spiritual maturity.",["faction:Horde"]="This peaceful settlement serves as a critical stopover and logistics link between the southern plains of Mulgore and the Horde capital at Thunder Bluff."},kw={"baine bloodhoof","bloodhoof village","camp narache","kodo","mulgore","stonebull lake","zarlman two-moons"},rel={"npc:baine-bloodhoof","zone:mulgore","subzone:stonebull-lake","npc:zarlman-two-moons","subzone:camp-narache","zone:thunderbluff","quest:767","topic:tauren"},src={"Bloodhoof Village"}}
E["subzone:brambleblade-ravine"] = {t="subzone",n="Brambleblade Ravine",z="mulgore",s="Brambleblade Ravine is a rugged canyon cutting into the northeastern edge of Red Cloud Mesa. It serves as a fortified den for the Bristleback quilboar tribe, who persistently raid the nearby tauren settlement of Camp Narache. Young tauren braves are sent here to prove their strength and protect their homeland from these fierce interlopers.",h="Brambleblade Ravine is a rugged canyon cutting into the northeastern edge of Red Cloud Mesa.",sec={{t="A Thorn in the Mesa's Side",b="Carved into the sun-scorched earth of Red Cloud Mesa, Brambleblade Ravine is choked with dense, thorny briars and quilboar encampments. The Bristleback tribe uses this natural fortress as a staging ground for raids against Camp Narache, threatening the safety of young tauren coming of age. Bristleback shamans and warriors, led by Chief Sharptusk Thornmantle, dwell deep within the ravine among trained battleboars. For the Shu'halo, striking into the heart of the canyon is a crucial rite of passage and a grim necessity to secure the plains.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Brambleblade Ravine?",a="Brambleblade Ravine is a canyon situated on the northeastern edge of Red Cloud Mesa in Mulgore. It is heavily inhabited by Bristleback quilboar and serves as their local stronghold.",al={"brambleblade ravine","what is brambleblade ravine","brambleblade mulgore"},it="place"},{q="Why are the Bristleback quilboar attacking from here?",a="The Bristlebacks are fiercely territorial and view the presence of the tauren as an encroachment upon lands they claim. From Brambleblade Ravine, they launch frequent raids on the nearby settlement of Camp Narache.",al={"why quilboar attacking","bristleback motives","quilboar camp narache"},it="motive"},{q="Who leads the quilboar in Brambleblade Ravine?",a="The quilboar here are led by Chief Sharptusk Thornmantle, a formidable chieftain who resides in a large hut located towards the back of the ravine.",al={"brambleblade leader","quilboar boss mulgore","chief sharptusk thornmantle"},it="leader"},{q="Why do young tauren travel to the ravine?",a="Tauren elders and chieftains at Camp Narache task fledgling braves with entering the ravine to push back the Bristleback threat, cull their numbers, and defeat their leadership to keep the mesa safe.",al={"tauren quests brambleblade","why go to brambleblade","camp narache quests"},it="why_me"},{q="Who lives in Brambleblade Ravine?",a="The ravine serves as a fortified settlement for the Bristleback quilboar. Alongside aggressive battleboars, tribesmen, and shamans, the Bristleback have built their huts throughout the dirt trails, answering to Chief Sharptusk Thornmantle and his kin.",al={"who lives in brambleblade ravine","brambleblade ravine inhabitants","who is in brambleblade ravine"},it="residents",add=true},{q="Is Brambleblade Ravine dangerous?",a="It is very perilous for unwary travelers. The quilboar guard their dens aggressively, swarming intruders with trained battleboars and mystic shamans. Anyone descending into the ravine without caution can quickly find themselves cornered against the high canyon walls.",al={"is brambleblade ravine dangerous","how dangerous is brambleblade ravine","brambleblade ravine danger"},it="danger",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For young tauren coming of age at Camp Narache, striking into Brambleblade Ravine is one of the very first tests of martial and spiritual endurance to defend the tribe.",["faction:Horde"]="Clearing out the aggressive Bristlebacks in Brambleblade Ravine helps solidify the Horde's early foothold across Mulgore."},kw={"brambleblade","bristleback","camp narache","mulgore","quilboar","ravine","red cloud mesa","thornmantle"},rel={"topic:quilboar","topic:tauren","zone:mulgore"},src={"Brambleblade Ravine"}}
E["subzone:camp-narache"] = {t="subzone",n="Camp Narache",z="mulgore",s="Camp Narache is a secluded village nestled upon Red Cloud Mesa in southern Mulgore. It serves as the ancestral home of the Hawkwind tribe and the proving ground where young tauren begin their training. Sheltered high above the plains, the camp maintains ancient traditions while constantly guarding against encroaching quilboar and lingering centaur threats.",h="Camp Narache is a secluded village nestled upon Red Cloud Mesa in southern Mulgore.",sec={{t="Heart of the Hawkwind Tribe",b="Situated at the northern edge of the elevated Red Cloud Mesa, Camp Narache is the primary settlement of the Hawkwind tribe. The village consists of several large hide lodges and pavilions, including a communal tent for wounded braves, an armory, a bakery tent, and a spirit lounge dedicated to meditation and ancestral communion. At the center of the camp, tribal elders and path trainers stand in a circle at the heart of the camp, where the venerable Greatmother Hawkwind still offers guidance, guiding newly initiated youth as they step into their roles as defenders of the Earth Mother.",sp=0},{t="A Proving Ground Under Threat",b="Though naturally fortified by the steep sheer cliffs of the mesa, Camp Narache faces persistent unrest. Bristleback quilboar from Brambleblade Ravine continuously encroach upon Hawkwind lands, while centaur scouts linger in the distance. Young hunters, shamans, druids, and warriors prove their mettle here by culling the aggressive local wildlife, protecting the village's water well, and turning back quilboar raiders before descending the grand path into the wider valleys of Mulgore.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Camp Narache?",a="Camp Narache is the starting village for young tauren, located atop Red Cloud Mesa in southern Mulgore. It is the central encampment of the Hawkwind tribe, serving as a sanctuary where fledgling hunters, warriors, druids, and shamans master their crafts before traveling outward to Bloodhoof Village and Thunder Bluff.",al={"what is camp narache","narache lore","tauren starting town"},it="place"},{q="Who leads the village?",a="Chief Hawkwind guides the tribe's braves and coordinates the defenses of the mesa, alongside the tribe's revered elder, Greatmother Hawkwind. The tribe's trainers and elders gather at the center of the camp to instruct young adventurers.",al={"who leads camp narache","camp narache chief","hawkwind leader"},it="in_charge"},{q="What threats does the camp face?",a="The encampment is continually harassed by Bristleback quilboar operating out of Brambleblade Ravine to the east. In addition to feral battleboars and striders wandering the surrounding bluffs, the lingering danger of predatory centaur bands keeps the Hawkwind braves on constant alert.",al={"camp narache enemies","who attacks camp narache","mesa threats"},it="threat"},{q="Can travelers easily leave the mesa?",a="Red Cloud Mesa is naturally bounded by sheer cliffs. The only way down into the greater plains of Mulgore is along a guarded mountain path leading north, watched over by Hawkwind braves to prevent hostile beasts and quilboar from ambushing travelers.",al={"leaving red cloud mesa","exit camp narache","path off the mesa"}},{q="What happened at Camp Narache?",a="Camp Narache was founded as a sheltered home for the Hawkwind tribe upon the elevated heights of Red Cloud Mesa. Over generations, it evolved from a simple tribal gathering spot into the principal proving ground for young tauren across Mulgore. Protected by steep cliffs and natural ramparts, the camp allowed fledgling hunters, shamans, and warriors to undergo their traditional rites away from the fierce centaur raids that plagued the open plains below.",al={"camp narache history","history of camp narache","what happened here"},it="history",add=true},{q="Who lives in Camp Narache?",a="Camp Narache is the primary village of the Hawkwind tribe, led by Chief Hawkwind and his elder mother, Greatmother Hawkwind. Alongside tribal braves, traders, and craftsmen, mentors from across the tauren traditions gather here. Shaman, druids, warriors, and hunters reside in the camp's hide lodges to train young tauren who have arrived to complete their coming-of-age trials.",al={"who lives at camp narache","camp narache residents","who lives here"},it="residents",add=true},{q="Is Camp Narache dangerous?",a="The settlement itself is peaceful and well-guarded by Hawkwind braves, but the surrounding mesa is increasingly perilous. Aggressive mountain pumas and territorial battleboars prowl the borders, while a growing population of Bristleback quilboar has encroached upon the nearby ravine. While safe within the perimeter of the lodges, wandering far from the camp's hearths brings immediate peril to inexperienced youths.",al={"is camp narache safe","how dangerous is camp narache","danger in camp narache"},it="danger",add=true},{q="Is this where all tauren start out?",a="Yes. Situated high on the protected cliffs of Red Cloud Mesa, Camp Narache serves as the primary training camp for all young tauren. Before you are trusted to venture into the wider valleys of Mulgore or serve the chieftain in Thunder Bluff, you must first prove your strength, wisdom, and respect for nature under the watchful eyes of the elders here.",al={"do all tauren start here","tauren starting area","why do tauren begin here"},it="place",add=true},{q="Who built Camp Narache?",a="The camp was established by the Hawkwind tribe as their ancestral village on Red Cloud Mesa. Built primarily with sturdy leather tents, carved totems, and natural timber, it was designed according to traditional nomadic techniques, though it has stood as a lasting center for tribal gatherings and the education of tauren youth.",al={"who made camp narache","origin of camp narache","who founded camp narache"},it="origin",add=true},{q="Is Chief Hawkwind important to the tauren?",a="Chief Hawkwind is the leader of the Hawkwind tribe and the overseer of Red Cloud Mesa. While he governs the camp and directs the defense against the Bristlebacks, his vital cultural role is guiding newly awakened braves through their early trials before they enter the service of the united tribes.",al={"chief hawkwind role","who is chief hawkwind","importance of chief hawkwind"},it="leader",add=true},{q="Does Chief Hawkwind answer to anyone else?",a="Yes. Like all tribal chieftains in Mulgore, Chief Hawkwind recognizes Cairne Bloodhoof as the High Chieftain of the united tauren tribes. Beyond Cairne, the tauren have pledged their loyalty to Warchief Thrall and the greater Horde, though day-to-day governance of the camp remains in Hawkwind's hands.",al={"who does chief hawkwind answer to","chief hawkwind superior","who rules over hawkwind"},it="in_charge",add=true},{q="What are the Bristlebacks and why are they attacking us?",a="The Bristlebacks are a savage tribe of quilboar that inhabit the crags and thorny thickets of the mesa. Fiercely territorial and desperate for resources, they view the tauren settlements as an intrusion into their feeding grounds. Emboldened by their chieftains, they have begun raiding tauren supply lines and threatening Camp Narache directly.",al={"why are bristlebacks attacking","bristleback quilboar","who are the bristlebacks"},it="threat",add=true},{q="What is Brambleblade Ravine?",a="Brambleblade Ravine is a jagged, bramble-choked canyon located on the northeastern edge of Red Cloud Mesa. It serves as the primary den and stronghold of the Bristleback quilboar in this region, housing their battleboars and their chieftain, Sharptusk Thornmantle.",al={"brambleblade ravine","where is brambleblade ravine","about brambleblade ravine"},it="place",add=true},{q="Who is the Earthmother?",a="The Earthmother is the benevolent creator deity revered by the tauren above all else. Tauren tradition teaches that she gave birth to the world, breathed life into the lands, and created their people to live in harmony with the wilds. Her will guides their spiritual practices, shamanic rituals, and reverence for nature.",al={"who is the earth mother","earthmother lore","tauren earthmother"},it="other",add=true},{q="What is Mulgore?",a="Mulgore is the ancestral homeland of the tauren—a vast, verdant expanse of windswept grassy plains, rolling foothills, and towering plateaus. Ringed on all sides by impassable mountain walls, its fertile valleys are home to roaming kodo beasts, wolves, and the permanent tauren capital of Thunder Bluff.",al={"what is the land of mulgore","about mulgore","mulgore lore"},it="place",add=true},{q="Why did the tauren settle in Mulgore?",a="For generations, brutal centaur marauders forced the tauren into a wandering, nomadic existence across the Barrens. After joining forces with Warchief Thrall and the New Horde to drive the centaur out of the region, High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof led his people to settle permanently in the lush, defensible valleys of their ancestral home.",al={"why tauren live in mulgore","why did tauren come to mulgore","settling mulgore"},it="history",add=true},{q="Is Mulgore safe?",a="Though naturally fortified and peaceful compared to the Barrens, Mulgore is not without danger. Hostile Palemane gnolls, Windfury harpies, aggressive quilboar, and opportunistic goblin miners from the Venture Company continuously disrupt the peace, forcing the tauren to remain vigilant.",al={"is mulgore dangerous","how safe is mulgore","threats in mulgore"},it="danger",add=true},{q="What is Bloodhoof Village?",a="Bloodhoof Village is a central tauren settlement resting upon the shores of Stonebull Lake in central Mulgore. It serves as a major trade and cultural hub between the proving grounds of Red Cloud Mesa and the soaring heights of Thunder Bluff.",al={"about bloodhoof village","where is bloodhoof village","what is bloodhoof"},it="place",add=true},{q="What is Red Cloud Mesa?",a="Red Cloud Mesa is a high, sheltered plateau rising above southern Mulgore. Flanked by sheer cliffs that keep most predators and invaders from the lowlands at bay, the mesa houses Camp Narache and serves as the secluded proving ground for all young tauren.",al={"red cloud mesa","what is this mesa","about red cloud mesa"},it="place",add=true},{q="What is Thunder Bluff?",a="Thunder Bluff is the magnificent capital city of the tauren, perched high atop a series of wind-carved mesas in northern Mulgore. Connected by suspended rope bridges and reached by vast wooden elevators, it was founded by High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof as the united sanctuary for all tauren tribes.",al={"what is thunder bluff","about thunder bluff","tauren capital"},it="place",add=true},{q="When did the tauren join the Horde?",a="The tauren allied with the Horde during the Third War, shortly after Thrall and his orcs landed upon Kalimdor. Recognizing a shared spiritual honor and kinship, Thrall helped Cairne Bloodhoof defend his caravan from centaur raids, cementing an enduring friendship that brought the tauren into the New Horde.",al={"tauren joining the horde","how did tauren join horde","when tauren met thrall"},it="history",add=true},{q="How did I end up in Camp Narache?",a="You came to Camp Narache on the secluded heights of Red Cloud Mesa to train and prove yourself among the Hawkwind tribe. While the lower plains of Mulgore can be reached only through a fortified pass, young tauren gather in this sheltered refuge to master their skills under the watchful eye of tribal elders and class trainers before venturing out into the wider world.",al={"how did i get here","how did i end up here","why am i at camp narache"},it="arrival",add=true},{q="What am I supposed to be doing in Camp Narache?",a="Your duty is to aid Chief Hawkwind and the camp elders while learning the rites of your path. Red Cloud Mesa faces constant harassment from aggressive Bristleback quilboars lurking in Brambleblade Ravine and mountain cougars prowling the plateau. By defending the settlement, assisting wounded braves, and performing sacred rituals for the Earth Mother, you will earn the right to descend to Bloodhoof Village and serve the Horde.",al={"what do i do here","what is my purpose here","why am i here"},it="purpose",add=true},{q="Why does a new Tauren start here, in Camp Narache?",a="Camp Narache is the ancestral proving ground where young tauren take their first steps toward adulthood. Perched safely atop Red Cloud Mesa away from the roaming centaur and outside threats that haunt central Kalimdor, it offers a secure sanctuary. Here, the elders and trainers of the Hawkwind tribe guide new braves in hunting, honoring the spirits, and learning to defend the new homeland Cairne Bloodhoof secured for your people.",al={"why do tauren start here","why start at camp narache","why is this the tauren starting zone"},it="arrival",rc="Tauren",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For the tauren, Camp Narache represents childhood's end—the sacred earth where you take your first rites of the hunt and attune yourself to the ancestral spirits under the watchful gaze of the Hawkwind elders.",["class:Shaman"]="Young shaman take their very first steps along the spiritual path here, undertaking trials to earn their Earth Totem and speak directly with the elementals of the mesa."},kw={"bristleback","greatmother hawkwind","hawkwind","mulgore","narache","red cloud mesa","spirit lounge","tauren"},rel={"zone:mulgore","npc:greatmother-hawkwind","subzone:brambleblade-ravine","topic:tauren","topic:centaur"},src={"Camp Narache"}}
E["subzone:golden-plains"] = {t="subzone",n="Golden Plains",z="mulgore",s="The Golden Plains form a vast, sun-drenched basin stretching southeast of Thunder Bluff in central Mulgore. Cherished by the tauren, these sweeping grasslands serve as prime hunting territory and a vital grazing habitat for wandering herds of kodo.",h="The Golden Plains form a vast, sun-drenched basin stretching southeast of Thunder Bluff in central Mulgore.",sec={{t="Heart of the Hunt",b="Spreading out beneath the towering mesas of Thunder Bluff, the Golden Plains represent the untamed heart of Mulgore. The shifting sea of golden and green grass offers an abundant sanctuary for local wildlife, making it one of the most treasured traditional hunting grounds of the nomadic tauren. Broad kodo herds roam across the rolling basin, while hunters share stories of legendary quarry, most notably the great kodo Arra'chea, who wanders quietly through the tall stalks.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What are the Golden Plains?",a="The Golden Plains are a broad grassland basin located southeast of Thunder Bluff in Mulgore. They are a beloved cultural hunting ground for the tauren, defined by wide vistas and sprawling wildlife.",al={"golden plains mulgore","where are golden plains"},it="place"},{q="What animals roam the Golden Plains?",a="The basin is famous for its abundant herds of wild kodo, alongside other native creatures of Mulgore. The most famous beast said to wander these grasslands is the elusive and revered kodo named Arra'chea.",al={"golden plains beasts","kodos in golden plains"}},{q="Why is this area important to the tauren?",a="The plains serve as a vital source of game and hides, providing sustenance for the newly founded settlements across Mulgore and celebrating the tauren tradition of tracking and honoring their quarry.",al={"tauren golden plains","why tauren hunt here"},it="history"},{q="Is it dangerous to wander the Golden Plains?",a="For an unwary traveler, the plains pose genuine hazards. While not teeming with hostile armies, the basin is wild territory dominated by herds of massive kodo. Disturbance can easily provoke these colossal beasts into a deadly charge, and legendary, aggressive creatures such as Arra'chea roam freely across the undulating grasslands.",al={"golden plains dangerous","danger golden plains","is golden plains safe"},it="danger",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For the tauren, the Golden Plains are an enduring symbol of their homeland's bounty, offering sacred hunting grounds beneath the shadow of Thunder Bluff.",["class:Hunter"]="The vast open expanse and roaming kodo herds make these plains a prime testing ground for trackers seeking legendary beasts like Arra'chea."},kw={"arra'chea","golden plains","hunting grounds","kodo","mulgore","thunder bluff"},rel={"zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff","topic:tauren"},src={"Golden Plains"}}
E["subzone:kodo-rock"] = {t="subzone",n="Kodo Rock",z="mulgore",s="Kodo Rock is a natural stone monument located in the plains of Red Cloud Mesa, between Camp Narache and Brambleblade Ravine. Standing quietly amid the grasses of Mulgore, it serves as an early pilgrimage site for young tauren shamans learning to commune with the spirits of the earth. Because of its modest silhouette against the rolling landscape, travelers must keep a keen eye out to spot it.",h="Kodo Rock is a natural stone monument located in the plains of Red Cloud Mesa, between Camp Narache and...",sec={{t="A Waypoint on the Mesa",b="Situated roughly halfway along the path connecting Camp Narache to the hostile quillboar territory of Brambleblade Ravine, Kodo Rock is marked by a patch of worn earth at its base. While it lacks the sheer grandeur of the towering bluffs surrounding Red Cloud Mesa, its distinct profile resembles the revered kodo beasts that wander the grasslands. For wandering tribesfolk, it acts as a quiet natural beacon in the windswept plains of Mulgore.",sp=0},{t="Spiritual Significance",b="To the Shu'halo, natural formations often carry spiritual weight. Kodo Rock holds special reverence for young tauren shamans beginning their spiritual walk. Novices are directed here on early rites of passage, using the solitary stone monument as a sacred grounds to commune with elemental forces and honor the natural world before venturing into more perilous reaches of the continent.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where is Kodo Rock located?",a="Kodo Rock is situated on Red Cloud Mesa in Mulgore. You can find it roughly halfway along a straight path between Camp Narache and Brambleblade Ravine, marked on the ground by a small clearing of bare dirt.",al={"kodo rock location","where is kodo rock","find kodo rock"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why is Kodo Rock hard to find?",a="The monument blends naturally into the rolling hills and grasses of the mesa. It is only clearly visible from a relatively short distance, requiring travelers to pay close attention to the terrain and watch for the patch of dirt beneath it.",al={"cant find kodo rock","kodo rock hidden"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why is Kodo Rock important to tauren shamans?",a="Tauren shamans visit the rock during their earliest trials out of Camp Narache. It serves as a peaceful, sacred landmark for novices learning to attune their spirits to the land and the elemental forces of Mulgore.",al={"shaman quest kodo rock","why go to kodo rock"},it="purpose"},{q="What dangers are near Kodo Rock?",a="While the immediate patch around Kodo Rock is generally peaceful, heading further northeast leads directly toward Brambleblade Ravine, a canyon densely populated by aggressive Bristleback quilboar.",al={"is kodo rock safe","enemies near kodo rock"},it="danger"},{q="What is Kodo Rock?",a="Kodo Rock is a modest natural monument nestled within the Red Cloud Mesa of Mulgore, standing between Camp Narache and the bristling Brambleblade Ravine. Though unassuming from a distance and easily overlooked in the rolling plains, it holds deep spiritual significance for young tauren shamans beginning their communion with the elemental earth.",al={"what is kodo rock","kodo rock mulgore","kodo rock landmark"},it="place",add=true}},ang={["class:Shaman"]="For novice tauren shamans beginning their spiritual path at Camp Narache, Kodo Rock is a vital landmark used to attune with the elemental world.",["race:Tauren"]="The stone formation serves as a familiar landmark across the plains of Red Cloud Mesa, reflecting the sacred bond between the Shu'halo and the beasts of Mulgore."},kw={"brambleblade ravine","camp narache","kodo rock","monument","mulgore","red cloud mesa","shaman trial","tauren shaman"},rel={"subzone:camp-narache","subzone:brambleblade-ravine","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren"},src={"Kodo Rock"}}
E["subzone:palemane-rock"] = {t="subzone",n="Palemane Rock",z="mulgore",s="Palemane Rock is a cavern and campsite nestled into the western mountains of Mulgore. It serves as a primary stronghold for the aggressive Palemane gnoll tribe, posing a continual nuisance and threat to the nearby tauren of Bloodhoof Village.",h="Palemane Rock is a cavern and campsite nestled into the western mountains of Mulgore.",sec={{t="A Gnoll Stronghold",b="Situated along the western rim of the grassy plains of Mulgore, Palemane Rock consists of a sprawling open-air encampment fronting a deep, shadowed cavern carved into the mountainside. The Palemane gnolls have claimed both the cave interior and the immediate foothills outside as their territory. Hostile and territorial, these gnolls aggressively defend their den, attacking hunters, travelers, and scouts who wander too close to their rocky haven.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is Palemane Rock?",a="Palemane Rock is a natural cave formation and entrenched gnoll settlement set into the western cliffs of Mulgore.",al={"palemane rock info","palemane cave","where is palemane rock"},it="place"},{q="Who lives at Palemane Rock?",a="The cavern and surrounding camp are held by gnolls belonging to the Palemane tribe. They aggressively defend their territory against outsiders, including the native tauren.",al={"who is here","palemane mobs","gnolls at palemane rock"},it="residents"},{q="Why does Palemane Rock matter to the tauren?",a="The proximity of Palemane Rock to central Mulgore and Bloodhoof Village makes it an active nest of hostile raiders that tauren hunters and braves must continually suppress.",al={"why do tauren care","bloodhoof village gnolls","threat to mulgore"}},{q="Is Palemane Rock dangerous?",a="Yes, it is very dangerous. Palemane Rock is heavily infested with aggressive gnolls of the Palemane tribe. They occupy both the exterior camp and the deep cave tunnels winding into the mountainside, and they will fiercely attack anyone who wanders too close to their territory.",al={"is palemane rock dangerous","palemane rock danger","are palemane gnolls hostile"},it="danger",add=true},{q="Why is it called Palemane Rock?",a="The cave and its surrounding crags take their name from the Palemane gnoll tribe that has claimed the area as their home.",al={"why is it called palemane rock","palemane rock name origin","why palemane rock"},it="name",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The Palemane gnolls entrenched in these western caves are a persistent thorn in the side of your people, threatening game and unwary wanderers across Mulgore's pastures.",["faction:Horde"]="Securing Mulgore's borders against local pests like the Palemane gnolls is essential for keeping the tauren homeland safe and stable."},kw={"bloodhoof","cave","gnoll","mulgore","palemane","rock"},rel={"zone:mulgore","topic:gnoll"},src={"Palemane Rock"}}
E["subzone:ravaged-caravan"] = {t="subzone",n="Ravaged Caravan",z="mulgore",s="The Ravaged Caravan is a smoldering wreck located on the northeastern shore of Stonebull Lake in Mulgore. Once a supply convoy bound for Supervisor Fizsprocket's mining operations, it was ambushed and destroyed by local tauren determined to halt goblin exploitation of their homeland.",h="The Ravaged Caravan is a smoldering wreck located on the northeastern shore of Stonebull Lake in Mulgore.",sec={{t="Smoke Over Stonebull Lake",b="Perched on a rise above Stonebull Lake, the Ravaged Caravan marks an early clash between the tauren of Bloodhoof Village and the encroaching Venture Company. Sent to deliver mining supplies, blueprints, and equipment to Supervisor Fizsprocket's dig site, the caravan was cut off and wrecked by tauren defenders before it could reach its destination. Despite the devastation, stubborn Venture Co. survivors still cling to the ruined wagons and smoldering crates, jealously guarding what little cargo remains.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What happened to this caravan?",a="The caravan was intercepted and wrecked by local tauren who spotted the Venture Company moving deep into Mulgore. Seeking to stop the goblins from stripping the land, the tauren destroyed the convoy before its cargo could reach the nearby mine.",al={"why is the caravan burned","what happened here","ravaged caravan backstory"},it="history"},{q="Who owns the wrecked wagons?",a="The caravan belongs to the Venture Company, an unscrupulous goblin mining consortium attempting to establish unpermitted operations in the hills of Mulgore.",al={"whose caravan is this","who owns the caravan","venture company caravan"},it="people"},{q="Where was the caravan heading?",a="It was hauling tools, machinery, and supplies north toward Supervisor Fizsprocket, who leads the Venture Company's strip-mining operation at the Venture Co. Mine.",al={"caravan destination","where was it going","fizsprocket supplies"},it="purpose"},{q="Why are there still Venture Co. workers lingering at the wreck?",a="The surviving Venture Co. hirelings and workers are fiercely protective of company property. Rather than abandoning the loss, they remain entrenched around the charred wagons, attempting to salvage what little remains of their equipment.",al={"why are goblins still here","venture co survivors","who is guarding the wreck"},it="residents"},{q="What is the Ravaged Caravan?",a="The Ravaged Caravan is the smoldering wreckage of a Venture Company supply convoy resting on a mound along the northeastern shore of Stonebull Lake. What once brought fresh tools and provisions now lies in smoking ruins, surrounded by overturned wagons and scattered cargo.",al={"what is ravaged caravan","ravaged caravan mulgore","ravaged caravan"},it="place",add=true},{q="Why is the Ravaged Caravan ruined?",a="The caravan was intercepted and brought down in a violent clash with the local tauren. Watching an unwelcome goblin mining outfit trample through their sacred ancestral lands, the tauren swiftly dismantled the expedition before its mining gear could reinforce operations elsewhere in Mulgore.",al={"why is the caravan destroyed","who attacked the caravan","why is it wrecked"},it="ruin",add=true},{q="Is the Ravaged Caravan dangerous?",a="Yes. While the transport itself was wrecked, surviving Venture Company laborers still linger amidst the smoldering debris. Desperate to protect what little remains of their shipment, they attack anyone who approaches the wreckage.",al={"is ravaged caravan safe","ravaged caravan danger","are the workers hostile"},it="danger",add=true},{q="Why is it called the Ravaged Caravan?",a="The site takes its name from the violent devastation left behind after the tauren attack. All that remains of the expedition is a shattered line of broken wagons, charred supplies, and thick black smoke rising over Stonebull Lake.",al={"why is it called ravaged caravan","ravaged caravan name origin","how did it get its name"},it="name",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The charred wagons represent your people's immediate and decisive refusal to let foreign industrial syndicates despoil the sacred pastures of Mulgore.",["class:Druid"]="The black smoke choking the shore of Stonebull Lake serves as a stark reminder of the reckless damage goblin machinery inflicts on the natural balance."},kw={"mulgore","ravaged caravan","stonebull lake","supervisor fizsprocket","tauren","venture co","venture company","wagon"},rel={"quest:749","quest:764","quest:765","subzone:stonebull-lake","zone:mulgore","topic:venture-company","topic:tauren","topic:horde"},src={"Ravaged Caravan"}}
E["subzone:red-rocks"] = {t="subzone",n="Red Rocks",z="mulgore",s="Red Rocks is a towering sandstone bluff in northeastern Mulgore that serves as a sacred ancestral burial ground for the tauren. Here, fallen champions are commended to the Earth Mother through cleansing flame, and young braves travel to its quiet heights to seek the wisdom of their forebears.",h="Red Rocks is a towering sandstone bluff in northeastern Mulgore that serves as a sacred ancestral burial...",sec={{t="Hallowed Stones and Ancestral Pyres",b="Rising high above the plains of northeastern Mulgore, the crimson formations of Red Rocks mark a sacred threshold between the living world and the next. Tauren tradition reserves this solemn ground for their most valiant heroes—chieftains, defenders, and champions who gave their lives founding and protecting Thunder Bluff. Their remains are surrendered to the sky and soil through ceremonial pyres, returning their spirits to the Earth Mother and Sky Father. It is equally a place of spiritual beginning, where young tauren complete their rites of passage by honoring the ancestral graves and communing with the wind.",sp=0},{t="The Quilboar Desecration",b="The enduring peace of Red Rocks has been shattered by the Bristleback quilboar. Creeping out from their dens, these aggressive scavengers have infested the sandstone ledges and desecrated the ancestral burial pyres with filth. This violation deeply affronts the spiritual traditions of Thunder Bluff. Shaman and lorekeepers now summon aid to cleanse the crags, driving out the Bristlebacks and reclaiming the sanctity of their resting dead.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What is the purpose of Red Rocks?",a="Red Rocks serves as the premier ancestral burial ground and ceremonial rite site for the tauren of Mulgore. Fallen heroes and honored braves are cremated here to return their spirits to the Earth Mother, and young tauren visit its heights to honor the dead during their trials of adulthood.",al={"why is red rocks important","red rocks purpose","what happens at red rocks"},it="purpose"},{q="Why are there quilboar at Red Rocks?",a="The Bristleback quilboar pushed out from their bramble burrows and overrun the plateau, defiling the burial mounds. Their intrusion is seen by the tauren as an intolerable sacrilege against the resting spirits of their ancestors.",al={"quilboar at red rocks","who is invading red rocks","why are bristlebacks here"},it="threat"},{q="Who is buried at Red Rocks?",a="Burial at Red Rocks is an honor reserved for the most valiant tauren, including great warriors who fought alongside Cairne Bloodhoof to secure Mulgore and build Thunder Bluff, as well as heroes who died serving their tribes.",al={"who is cremated here","red rocks dead","whose graves are these"},it="history"},{q="What ceremonies are performed here?",a="Beyond funerary pyres commended to the Earth Mother and Sky Father, the bluff is a focal point for the Rite of Wisdom, where tauren braves leave offerings and listen for the guidance of ancestral spirits on the wind.",al={"rites at red rocks","tauren rituals red rocks"}},{q="What is Red Rocks?",a="Red Rocks is a prominent formation of red sandstone rising in northeastern Mulgore. It serves the tauren as a revered, sacred ground positioned between the earthly realm of Thunder Bluff and the ancestral realm beyond. Here, tauren youth complete sacred rites to enter adulthood, and the tribes honor their greatest fallen champions.",al={"what is red rocks","about red rocks","red rocks overview"},it="place",add=true},{q="Is Red Rocks dangerous?",a="While traditionally a quiet and solemn sanctuary, it is currently perilous due to an aggressive incursion of Bristleback quilboar. These invaders have desecrated the burial sites and actively attack anyone who approaches the sacred grounds.",al={"is red rocks dangerous","red rocks danger","enemies at red rocks"},it="danger",add=true},{q="Why is it called Red Rocks?",a="The site draws its name from its distinctive, naturally occurring cliffs and formations of vivid red sandstone, which distinguish it from the rolling green plains of the surrounding valley.",al={"why is it called red rocks","red rocks name meaning","why red rocks"},it="name",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For your people, Red Rocks is sacred soil. The presence of foul quilboar rooting among your honored ancestors' pyres is an open outrage that demands cleansing.",["class:Shaman"]="The wind carries the sorrow of unsettled spirits disturbed by quilboar defilement, calling on shamanic rites to restore elemental balance and ancestral peace."},kw={"bristleback","burial ground","earth mother","mulgore","quilboar","red rocks","rite of wisdom","thunder bluff"},rel={"zone:mulgore","npc:lorekeeper-raintotem","quest:833","quest:773","zone:thunderbluff","npc:cairne-bloodhoof","topic:tauren","topic:earth-mother"},src={"Red Rocks"}}
E["subzone:rolling-plains"] = {t="subzone",n="Rolling Plains",z="mulgore",s="The Rolling Plains span the southern expanse of Mulgore, stretching between Bloodhoof Village and the border of the Barrens. These windswept grasslands are home to thriving herds of wildlife, but they are also a point of perpetual vigilance due to prowling centaur raiders and aggressive harpies.",h="The Rolling Plains span the southern expanse of Mulgore, stretching between Bloodhoof Village and the border...",sec={{t="Heart of Southern Mulgore",b="Spanning south from Bloodhoof Village toward the natural earthen gates leading into the Barrens, the Rolling Plains embody the vast open ranges of the tauren homeland. The area teems with wild game essential to the tribes, including prairie wolves, kodo herds, flatland cougars, and swift tallstriders. For young hunters proving their worth to the elders, these sweeping savannahs serve as an indispensable proving ground where tracking, patience, and reverence for nature are put to the test.",sp=0},{t="Encroaching Dangers",b="Despite Mulgore's naturally fortified ring of bluffs, the Rolling Plains remain uniquely exposed to outside threats. It is the only frontier where aggressive centaur outriders have managed to push past defensive perimeters to harass passing travelers and hunting parties. Adding to the peril, a nest of predatory Windfury harpies roosts in the eastern crags, swooping down upon stray game and vulnerable nomadic camps.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What animals can be found on the Rolling Plains?",a="The grasslands support a rich variety of native fauna, including roaming herds of wild kodo, nimble tallstriders, prowling prairie wolves, and stalking flatland cougars. These beasts provide vital meat, hides, and sinew for the tribes of Bloodhoof Village.",al={"rolling plains wildlife","mobs rolling plains","animals mulgore plains"}},{q="Why are centaur found wandering this part of Mulgore?",a="The Rolling Plains border the eastern passes into the Barrens, making them the only region in Mulgore where centaur raiders have managed to breach the perimeter. Marauding outriders constantly scout the plains, probing tauren defenses and threatening isolated hunters.",al={"centaur in mulgore","why centaur rolling plains","centaur outriders"},it="threat"},{q="What other hostile creatures threaten the plains?",a="Aside from roaming centaur scouts, a dangerous enclave of Windfury harpies nests along the eastern crags of the region, preying upon unwary travelers and harassing local wildlife.",al={"harpies rolling plains","dangers rolling plains"},it="threat"},{q="Where do the Rolling Plains begin and end?",a="The plains cover much of southern Mulgore, stretching from Bloodhoof Village to the mountain border adjoining the Barrens.",al={"rolling plains location","where are rolling plains","boundaries rolling plains"},it="place"},{q="What are the Rolling Plains?",a="The Rolling Plains are the expansive, open grasslands stretching across southern Mulgore, reaching from Bloodhoof Village all the way to the border of the Barrens. They serve as rich hunting grounds teeming with wildlife such as kodos, tallstriders, wolves, and cougars, forming the pastoral heartland of tauren territory in southern Mulgore.",al={"what is rolling plains","rolling plains lore","about rolling plains"},it="place",add=true},{q="Are the Rolling Plains dangerous?",a="While the plains are open hunting grounds, they are not without peril. In addition to prowling cougars and wolves, the region is notable as the only part of Mulgore penetrated by centaur outriders, who roam the open fields. In the east, a nest of aggressive Windfury harpies poses an additional hazard to travelers and hunters alike.",al={"how dangerous are the rolling plains","rolling plains dangers","is rolling plains safe"},it="danger",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The Rolling Plains provide vital hunting grounds for the newly unified tribes, but the intrusion of ancient centaur enemies so close to Bloodhoof Village makes guarding this southern corridor a matter of tribal survival.",["class:Hunter"]="With ample herds of kodo, tallstriders, wolves, and cougars, the Rolling Plains offer an ideal wilderness for honing tracking skills, gathering provisions, and taming first animal companions."},kw={"bloodhoof village","centaur","harpies","kodo","mulgore","rolling plains","tallstrider","windfury"},rel={"zone:mulgore","subzone:bloodhoof-village","topic:centaur","zone:barrens"},src={"Rolling Plains"}}
E["subzone:stonebull-lake"] = {t="subzone",n="Stonebull Lake",z="mulgore",s="Stonebull Lake is a serene, horseshoe-shaped body of water wrapping around Bloodhoof Village in central Mulgore. Fed by the pristine natural runoff of the plains, it provides vital fresh water, abundant fish, and peaceful shores for the young tauren coming of age under Cairne Bloodhoof's guidance.",h="Stonebull Lake is a serene, horseshoe-shaped body of water wrapping around Bloodhoof Village in central...",sec={{t="Waters of the Plain",b="Curving around Bloodhoof Village on three sides, Stonebull Lake defines the geography of central Mulgore. A wooden bridge crosses the lake's narrowest stretch, carrying the main dirt road between the settlement and the great mesa of Thunder Bluff to the north. Its calm waters are filled with native fish, making its banks a favored spot for resting hunters, weary travelers, and apprentice fishers learning their trade under the sweeping open sky.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where is Stonebull Lake located?",a="Stonebull Lake is located in central Mulgore. It forms a horseshoe around Bloodhoof Village, flanking the settlement on three sides.",al={"stonebull lake location","where is stonebull lake","mulgore lake"},gp=true},{q="What is the bridge across the lake used for?",a="The bridge crosses the narrowest part of Stonebull Lake to carry the main road connecting Bloodhoof Village directly to Thunder Bluff in the north.",al={"stonebull bridge","bridge near bloodhoof","road over stonebull lake"},it="purpose"},{q="Why is Stonebull Lake important to the tauren?",a="The lake is an essential freshwater source and a vital fishing spot for the inhabitants of Bloodhoof Village. It also offers a quiet place of contemplation and gathering for young tauren braves training in central Mulgore.",al={"why does stonebull lake matter","purpose of stonebull lake"},it="history"},{q="What is Stonebull Lake?",a="Stonebull Lake is a wide, horseshoe-shaped body of water cradling Bloodhoof Village in central Mulgore. Its cool, fresh waters are renowned across the plains for their abundant fish, providing both a staple food supply and a tranquil haven where tauren often spend quiet hours casting lines from the shore. A sturdy wooden bridge spans its narrowest point, carrying travelers along the main road between Bloodhoof Village and the bluffs to the north.",al={"stonebull lake mulgore","what is stonebull lake","about stonebull lake"},it="place",add=true},{q="Is Stonebull Lake dangerous?",a="The lake itself is generally peaceful and protected by the proximity of Bloodhoof Village and the tauren braves patrolling nearby. While predators and roaming beasts prowl the broader plains of Mulgore, the waters of Stonebull Lake remain one of the safest fishing and gathering spots in the region, free from any lurking aquatic horrors or hostile settlements along its immediate banks.",al={"is stonebull lake dangerous","threats at stonebull lake","stonebull lake danger"},it="danger",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For the tauren establishing their homes across Mulgore, Stonebull Lake provides gentle sustenance and a reminder of the Earth Mother's bounty near their capital."},kw={"bloodhoof village","fishing","lake","mulgore","stonebull lake","thunder bluff"},rel={"subzone:bloodhoof-village","zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff"},src={"Stonebull Lake"}}
E["subzone:thunder-bluff"] = {t="subzone",n="Thunder Bluff",z="mulgore",s="Thunder Bluff is the high-perched capital city of the tauren, rising hundreds of feet above the rolling plains of Mulgore. Founded by Cairne Bloodhoof following the Third War, it serves as the united sanctuary and cultural heart for a people who roamed as nomads for generations.",h="Thunder Bluff is the high-perched capital city of the tauren, rising hundreds of feet above the rolling...",sec={{t="A Refuge Upon the Mesas",b="For countless generations, the tauren lived as nomadic wanderers across central Kalimdor, constantly beset by brutal centaur raiding parties. That era of flight ended during the Third War, when Cairne Bloodhoof forged a bond of honor with Warchief Thrall and the New Horde. Together, they drove back the centaur menace and claimed the fertile grasslands of Mulgore. Cairne gathered the scattered tribes and established Thunder Bluff atop a series of sweeping, natural stone mesas. Today, the city is connected by dizzying rope bridges and accessed by ingenious wooden elevators, offering both absolute defense and a bustling center of trade and spiritual assembly.",sp=0},{t="Unity and Gathering Mists",b="While Thunder Bluff stands as a beacon of harmony, its towering heights house diverse factions. Most tribes revere the wisdom of Cairne Bloodhoof, yet dissent quietly simmers among traditionalists such as Magatha Grimtotem and her kin on the Elder Rise. Deep beneath the city's surface lie the subterranean Pools of Vision, where spirit walkers and shaman peer into the ancestors' will. Despite underlying tribal rivalries, Thunder Bluff remains the true spiritual pillar of Kalimdor's Horde, welcoming hunters returning from the Barrens, traveling artisans, and druids communing with the Earth Mother.",sp=0}},faq={{q="How do travelers get up to Thunder Bluff?",a="Travelers reach the city via massive wooden rope-and-pulley elevators located on the southwestern and northeastern bluffs, or by taking flight directly to the city's wind rider roost atop the central mesa.",al={"thunder bluff elevator","how to get up to tb","entering thunder bluff"},it="arrival"},{q="Why did the tauren build their city on cliffs?",a="After enduring centuries of relentless centaur raids on open ground, Cairne Bloodhoof chose the towering stone mesas to grant his nomadic people an impregnable sanctuary overlooking the sacred grazing grounds of Mulgore.",al={"why mesas","why build on high bluffs","thunder bluff defense"},it="purpose"},{q="Who rules Thunder Bluff?",a="High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof leads Thunder Bluff. He united the scattered tribes and secured the peace of Mulgore, ruling with the counsel of tribal leaders and elders across the rises.",al={"leader of thunder bluff","who is the chief","cairne thunder bluff"},it="in_charge"},{q="Are all tauren tribes loyal to Cairne?",a="While the vast majority revere Cairne's wisdom, some groups—most notably the Grimtotem tribe led by the elder crone Magatha—harbor doubts about the Horde alliance and seek their own course for the tauren people.",al={"grimtotem thunder bluff","tribes of thunder bluff","tauren political rivals"},it="threat"},{q="What is Thunder Bluff?",a="Thunder Bluff is the capital city of the tauren, located atop a network of soaring, windswept mesas in northern Mulgore. Connected by rope bridges and towering elevators, the city serves as a bustling sanctuary of longhouses, tents, and spirit lodges. It represents the first permanent homeland the tauren have claimed after centuries of nomadic wandering.",al={"what is thunder bluff","tell me about thunder bluff","thunder bluff lore"},it="place",add=true},{q="How was Thunder Bluff founded?",a="For generations, the tauren lived as nomadic tribes constantly hunted by marauding centaur. During the Third War, Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof allied with Thrall and the New Horde. Together, they drove back the centaur and secured the lush plains of Mulgore. Upon these towering bluffs, Cairne established the city as a secure refuge to unite the scattered tauren tribes.",al={"thunder bluff history","who founded thunder bluff","origin of thunder bluff"},it="history",add=true},{q="Is Thunder Bluff dangerous?",a="The city itself is a well-defended sanctuary for the Horde, though its sheer cliffs pose a lethal hazard if you misstep near the edges. Beyond the physical drops, internal political tensions linger quietly beneath the peace, as some rival factions like the Grimtotem tribe remain uneasy participants in Cairne's united nation.",al={"is thunder bluff safe","thunder bluff danger","threats in thunder bluff"},it="danger",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For the tauren, Thunder Bluff represents their first true permanent home after generations of wandering, holding their sacred totems, spirit lodges, and greatest chieftains.",["faction:Horde"]="Thunder Bluff provides the Horde with an essential bastion on southern Kalimdor, resting as a steady anchor of wisdom and resources alongside orcish Orgrimmar."},kw={"cairne bloodhoof","earth mother","grimtotem","mesas","mulgore","pools of vision","tauren","thunder bluff"},rel={"npc:cairne-bloodhoof","zone:mulgore","topic:tauren","subzone:pools-of-vision","npc:magatha-grimtotem","npc:baine-bloodhoof","topic:horde","zone:barrens"},src={"Thunder Bluff"}}
E["subzone:thunderhorn-water-well"] = {t="subzone",n="Thunderhorn Water Well",z="mulgore",s="Thunderhorn Water Well is a vital water source situated in the rolling plains of central Mulgore along the path between Bloodhoof Village and Thunder Bluff. Like all the wells across the valley, it provides essential nourishment to the tribes and the herds roaming the grasslands. Keeping this water clean and defended is crucial to maintaining life in the tauren homeland.",h="Thunderhorn Water Well is a vital water source situated in the rolling plains of central Mulgore along the...",sec={{t="A Life-Giving Spring",b="Situated amid the sprawling grasslands of central Mulgore, the Thunderhorn Water Well taps directly into the pure aquifers feeding the valley. For the nomadic tauren and their newly settled villages, these communal wells represent life, sustenance, and the Earth Mother's bounty. Because water is precious across the sun-drenched plains, controlling the wells means controlling the land itself. Intruders such as the Venture Company have repeatedly eyed these sources, attempting to harness them for industrial exploitation and disrupt the fragile balance the tribes fight to preserve.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where is the Thunderhorn Water Well located?",a="The well is located in central Mulgore, situated along the north-south road running between Bloodhoof Village and the bluffs of Thunder Bluff.",al={"thunderhorn well location","where is thunderhorn well","thunderhorn water well map"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why is this water well important to the tauren?",a="The water wells of Mulgore tap into the pristine subterranean waters of the valley, sustaining the herds, pilgrims, and settlements. To the tauren, water is a sacred gift from the Earth Mother that must be safeguarded from outside corruption and greed.",al={"why is thunderhorn well important","tauren water wells meaning","why protect the wells"},it="purpose"},{q="Who has threatened the well?",a="Goblins of the Venture Company attempted to seize control of the well to monopolize the valley's water supply for their nearby mining and logging ventures, but local tauren fought back to reclaim and purify it.",al={"who attacked the well","venture co well","enemies at thunderhorn well"},sp=1,it="threat"},{q="What is the Thunderhorn Water Well?",a="The Thunderhorn Water Well is a vital tauren water source set among the rolling grasslands of Mulgore. Situated alongside the northern road connecting Bloodhoof Village to the bluffs of Thunder Bluff, the well provides fresh water crucial for the sustenance of local tribes and travelers crossing the plains.",al={"what is thunderhorn water well","thunderhorn well info","about thunderhorn well"},it="place",add=true},{q="What happened at the Thunderhorn Water Well?",a="Seeking to control the plains' scarce water supply, the opportunistic Venture Company seized the well with their armed workers. Recognizing the dire threat to their homeland, the tauren rallied to drive the interlopers out and reclaim the pristine waters for their people.",al={"thunderhorn well history","what happened at thunderhorn well","venture co thunderhorn"},it="history",add=true},{q="Is the Thunderhorn Water Well dangerous?",a="While the well belongs to the tauren, the surrounding grasslands are frequented by predatory wildlife and encroaching Venture Company operatives seeking to exploit the land. Travelers walking the road between Bloodhoof Village and Thunder Bluff should keep their guard up.",al={"is thunderhorn well dangerous","thunderhorn well threats","is thunderhorn safe"},it="danger",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="For the tauren, wells like Thunderhorn are vital lifelines gifted by the Earth Mother, requiring constant guardianship against foreign polluters and greedy trespassers.",["class:Shaman"]="Shaman deeply revere the pure water springs of Mulgore, viewing the protection of water spirits and subterranean currents as a sacred duty."},kw={"bloodhoof","mulgore","thunder bluff","thunderhorn","venture company","water","well"},rel={"zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff","subzone:bloodhoof-village","topic:tauren","topic:venture-company"},src={"Thunderhorn Water Well"}}
E["subzone:venture-co-mine"] = {t="subzone",n="Venture Co. Mine",z="mulgore",s="Tucked into the eastern cliffs of Mulgore, the Venture Co. Mine is an unauthorized industrial excavation run by greedy goblin opportunists. Despite being denied rights by the tauren, the Venture Company dug into the hills regardless, threatening the natural balance of the plains.",h="Tucked into the eastern cliffs of Mulgore, the Venture Co.",sec={{t="Unwanted Industry",b="When the Venture Company sought rights to strip Mulgore of its minerals, the tauren flatly refused. The corporate cartel ignored them, tunneling deep into the eastern mountain wall anyway under the direction of Supervisor Fizsprocket. Their sprawling complex features three entrances—a ground tunnel guarded by hired muscle, a sheer cliffside overlook, and an upper passage leading out to a small lumber camp. The encroaching machines, smoke, and slag heaps represent a blatant defilement of sacred land.",sp=0},{t="Expanding Ambitions",b="Mining was only the opening bid for the cartel. Internal documents show that Mogul Razdunk and the Venture Company board encouraged Fizsprocket to expand further into full-scale timber harvesting, promising advanced goblin shredders to clear the rolling hills. Although tauren Outrunners attacked and burned a corporate supply caravan near Stonebull Lake to stall their expansion, salvagers were immediately dispatched to recover the cargo. To halt the destruction, the tribes have turned toward direct retaliation against the mine's supervisors.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Why are goblins mining in Mulgore?",a="The Venture Company sought permission to extract minerals from Mulgore, but the tauren denied their request. Unwilling to respect native territorial claims or environmental balance, the cartel sneaked workers and machinery into the eastern cliffs to plunder the hills without authorization.",al={"why are there goblins here","venture co mulgore reason","who runs the mine"},it="why_here"},{q="Who is in charge of this mining site?",a="Supervisor Fizsprocket oversees all Venture Company operations in Mulgore from his post inside the main entrance. He takes orders directly from the company's executive board and Mogul Razdunk.",al={"mine boss","who leads the mine","supervisor fizsprocket"},it="in_charge"},{q="What happened to the supply caravan by the lake?",a="Tauren Outrunners ambushed and set fire to a Venture Company supply caravan near the northern shore of Stonebull Lake to stop machinery from reaching the mine. The goblins swiftly dispatched salvage teams to sift through the charred wreckage and recover their goods.",al={"ravaged caravan story","stonebull lake caravan","who burned the caravan"},it="conflict"},{q="What else do the goblins plan to build here?",a="Beyond hollowing out the cliffs for ore, internal communiques reveal plans to construct a lumber mill and deploy shredders to clear-cut Mulgore's trees for additional corporate profit.",al={"venture co plans","goblin shredders mulgore","lumber camp plans"},it="purpose"},{q="What is the Venture Co. Mine?",a="This is an enormous excavation carved deep into the eastern cliffs of Mulgore. Established without permission by the Venture Company, it serves as an illegal industrial foothold within the tauren homeland. The compound features a main entrance overseen by goblin supervisors, high cliffside passages, and an upper opening leading toward a small logging operation.",al={"venture co mine","what is venture co mine","mulgore mine"},it="place",add=true},{q="What happened at the Venture Co. Mine?",a="After the tauren flatly denied the Venture Company mining rights in Mulgore, the cartel ignored them and dug in anyway. Tauren Outrunners struck back, raiding and torching a major supply caravan along Stonebull Lake. Enraged by the despoiling of their sacred ancestral lands, the elders of Bloodhoof Village have organized strikes into the mine to drive the trespassers out.",al={"venture co mine history","what happened here","venture co mine lore"},it="history",add=true},{q="Is the Venture Co. Mine dangerous?",a="Yes. The tunnels swarm with heavily armed goblin enforcers, laborers, and engineers fiercely protective of their illegal claim. Venture Company guards do not hesitate to shoot trespassers on sight, and navigating the winding, multi-level shafts puts you directly in the crosshairs of their ruthless workforce.",al={"is venture co mine dangerous","venture co mine dangers","venture co hazards"},it="danger",add=true},{q="Why is it called the Venture Co. Mine?",a="It takes its name directly from the notorious goblin syndicate operating it: the Venture Company. Run under the distant directive of Mogul Razdunk, this ruthless enterprise cares nothing for local sovereignty or environmental stewardship, branding their illicit excavation with the company moniker.",al={"why venture co mine","venture co mine name meaning","venture company name"},it="name",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The mine is a profound insult to the Earth Mother, bringing soot, noise, and reckless scarring to the ancestral valleys the tribes have sworn to safeguard.",["profession:Engineering"]="Past the mine tunnels and through the higher lumber clearing lurks Twizwick Sprocketgrind, a tinkerer willing to share mechanical trade secrets away from prying corporate eyes."},kw={"fizsprocket","goblins","mulgore","ravaged caravan","razdunk","shredders","stonebull lake","venture co mine"},rel={"topic:venture-company","quest:765","quest:764","subzone:ravaged-caravan","zone:mulgore","subzone:stonebull-lake","topic:tauren","topic:goblin"},src={"Venture Co. Mine"}}
E["subzone:wildmane-water-well"] = {t="subzone",n="Wildmane Water Well",z="mulgore",s="The Wildmane Water Well is the northernmost of the sacred water wells scattered across Mulgore, resting just north of the mesas of Thunder Bluff. Vital to the plains and their herds, it has become a contested flashpoint targeted by goblin opportunists seeking to choke the tauren's lifeblood.",h="The Wildmane Water Well is the northernmost of the sacred water wells scattered across Mulgore, resting just...",sec={{t="A Lifeblood Under Siege",b="For generations, the nomadic tauren have relied on sacred water wells dug across the golden plains of Mulgore to sustain their people and their wandering kodo herds. The Wildmane Well guards the northern expanse just beyond the shadow of Thunder Bluff. Its vital importance makes it an irresistible target for enemies of the tribes. The goblins of the Venture Company have fouled it with their poisons in a bid to control the regional water supply, and only a cleansing ritual with a totem crafted by the tauren can make its waters pure again.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Where is the Wildmane Water Well located?",a="The Wildmane Water Well is situated on the plains of Mulgore, directly north of the towering mesas of Thunder Bluff. It is the northernmost of the sacred wells that sustain the region.",al={"wildmane well location","where is wildmane well","wildmane water well map"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Why is the well important to the tauren?",a="Fresh water is life on the plains. The tauren hold the land's wells sacred as vital lifelines for their settlements, traveling hunters, and the great kodo herds roaming the plains of Mulgore.",al={"why do tauren care about the well","wildmane well sacred"},it="purpose"},{q="Who has tried to sabotage or control the well?",a="The goblins of the Venture Company have poisoned the well in an effort to control Mulgore's water supply, and the tauren must cleanse it with a sacred totem.",al={"who attacked the well","wildmane well enemies","who poisoned wildmane well"},it="threat"},{q="What is the Wildmane Water Well?",a="The Wildmane Water Well is the northernmost of the sacred water wells that sustain the tauren of Mulgore, situated just north of the bluffs beneath Thunder Bluff. It provides clean, vital water for the plains and herds, making it an essential lifeline and sacred site for the tribes who have made their home in these ancestral lands.",al={"what is wildmane water well","wildmane water well info","wildmane well mulgore"},it="place",add=true},{q="What happened at the Wildmane Water Well?",a="The well was seized by the goblins of the Venture Company, who fouled its waters with their poisons in a bid to control Mulgore's water supply. The tauren drove them off and must now cleanse the well with a sacred totem so that clean water can flow again.",al={"wildmane water well history","what happened at wildmane well"},it="history",add=true},{q="Is the Wildmane Water Well dangerous?",a="Yes. While the well itself is a peaceful tauren site, its waters have been fouled by the Venture Company's poisons, and prairie wolves and other predators roam the plains around it. Anyone approaching should stay on guard and avoid drinking the tainted water until the well is cleansed.",al={"is wildmane water well dangerous","wildmane well danger","threats at wildmane well"},it="danger",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The sacred wells of Mulgore are a gift from the Earth Mother. Defending the Wildmane Well from defilement and poison is a sacred duty for every young brave.",["class:Druid"]="The goblins' attempts to corrupt Mulgore's fresh water threaten the delicate balance of the plains, demanding druidic vigilance to cleanse and heal the land."},kw={"mulgore","sacred well","thunder bluff","venture company","water well","wildmane"},rel={"zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff","topic:tauren","topic:venture-company"},src={"Wildmane Water Well"}}
E["subzone:windfury-ridge"] = {t="subzone",n="Windfury Ridge",z="mulgore",s="Windfury Ridge is a rugged highland area flanking the pastures of Mulgore. Notorious for harboring vicious harpies alongside predatory wolves and cougars, it poses a constant hazard to travelers while offering prized feathers to tauren artisans.",h="Windfury Ridge is a rugged highland area flanking the pastures of Mulgore.",sec={{t="A Perilous Perch",b="Windfury Ridge rises sharply above Mulgore's serene plains, serving as the hunting grounds of the Windfury harpies. These vicious winged creatures nest high along the crags, readily swooping down upon any unwary wanderers who skirt the hills. The rocky terrain is further populated by prowling prairie wolves and wild mountain cougars, making the ridge one of the more treacherous borders of the tauren heartland. Despite the danger, the harpies are frequently hunted by brave tauren warriors; their vibrant, iridescent plumage is highly sought after across Thunder Bluff to adorn ceremonial garments for lavish feasts and wedding celebrations.",sp=0}},faq={{q="What makes Windfury Ridge dangerous?",a="The ridge is teeming with predatory cougars and prairie wolves, but its most persistent menace is the flock of aggressive harpies nesting along the heights. They actively dive from the crags to capture or butcher intruders who wander too close to their nesting sites.",al={"why is windfury ridge dangerous","hazards of windfury ridge","windfury ridge monsters"},it="danger"},{q="Why do the tauren hunt the harpies on Windfury Ridge?",a="Beyond securing the borders from their predations, tauren hunt the Windfury harpies for their brilliant feathers. These colorful plumes are traditionally harvested and woven into ceremonial headdresses and decorations used during feasts and weddings.",al={"why kill windfury harpies","harpy feathers mulgore","hunting harpies"},it="why_wanted"},{q="Where is Windfury Ridge located?",a="Windfury Ridge lies along the elevated bluffs in the pastoral region of Mulgore, in the far north of Mulgore, beyond Thunder Bluff.",al={"windfury ridge location","where is windfury ridge","windfury ridge map"},it="place",gp=true},{q="What is Windfury Ridge?",a="Windfury Ridge is a rugged highland nestled within the hills of Mulgore. Known primarily as the roost of fierce harpies, it is a perilous stretch of high ground frequented by deadly predators and bypassed by cautious travelers.",al={"what is windfury ridge","windfury ridge info","about windfury ridge"},it="place",add=true},{q="Who lives in Windfury Ridge?",a="The ridge is claimed and fiercely defended by the Windfury harpies, who build their nests among the crags. Aside from these winged scavengers, prowling cougars and wild wolves stalk the slopes, preying upon anything wandering too close to the hills.",al={"who lives at windfury ridge","windfury ridge inhabitants","windfury ridge creatures"},it="residents",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="The ridge is both a recurring nuisance to young hunters and a source of vital ceremonial ornaments used to honor marriages and seasonal gatherings.",["class:Hunter"]="The steep inclines and cunning predators provide an excellent early testing ground for tracking, beast taming, and range management."},kw={"cougars","feathers","harpies","mulgore","thunder bluff","windfury","windfury ridge"},rel={"zone:mulgore","zone:thunderbluff","topic:tauren"},src={"Windfury Ridge"}}
E["subzone:winterhoof-water-well"] = {t="subzone",n="Winterhoof Water Well",z="mulgore",s="The Winterhoof Water Well is a vital fresh water source situated on the edge of the Rolling Plains in southern Mulgore. Crucial to the local wildlife and nomadic tauren tribes, the well has recently fallen under threat from the exploitative Venture Company, who seek to poison and control the region's water supply.",h="The Winterhoof Water Well is a vital fresh water source situated on the edge of the Rolling Plains in...",sec={{t="A Threatened Lifeline",b="In the arid stretches of central Kalimdor, water is life, and Mulgore's subterranean wells sustain both the herds of the plains and the encampments of the tauren. The Winterhoof Water Well, named for one of the respected tribes of the plains, taps into the deep aquifers beneath the Rolling Plains. Unfortunately, its strategic importance has drawn the avarice of the Venture Company. Rather than sharing the land or negotiating, the ruthless goblin cartel has attempted to poison the water supply to exert leverage over the surrounding territory.",sp=0},{t="Purification and Defense",b="To counter the industrial sabotage of the Venture Company, tauren elders and shamans have mobilized young braves across Mulgore. Mull Thunderhorn has crafted sacred totems designed to counter the industrial toxins and restore the spiritual and physical balance of the well. Cleansing the well is not merely an act of survival for the tauren, but a spiritual duty to ensure that the earth and its waters remain uncorrupted by foreign greed.",sp=1}},faq={{q="Where is the Winterhoof Water Well located?",a="The well is located in southern Mulgore, right on the fringes of the Rolling Plains south of Bloodhoof Village.",al={"winterhoof well location","where is winterhoof well","find winterhoof water well"},it="place",gp=true},{q="Who poisoned the Winterhoof Water Well?",a="The Venture Company poisoned the well. The unscrupulous goblin trading organization attempted to foul the water supply in a bid to control the local resources and weaken the native tauren.",al={"who poisoned winterhoof well","why is the well poisoned","who ruined the water"},it="threat"},{q="How can the poisoned well be cleansed?",a="The well is cleansed through a ritual utilizing an enchanted totem crafted by Mull Thunderhorn, which uses primal magic to purge the Venture Company's foul toxins from the water.",al={"cleanse winterhoof well","fix the well","winterhoof cleansing"},it="purpose",gp=true},{q="Why are water wells so sacred to the tauren?",a="Beyond providing necessary hydration for tribes and roaming herds, water sources in Mulgore are viewed as gifts from the Earth Mother. Corrupting them is considered both a direct physical threat and a severe spiritual affront.",al={"why do tauren care about wells","tauren well lore"},it="ties"},{q="What is the Winterhoof Water Well?",a="The Winterhoof Water Well is one of several vital sources of fresh water carved into the earth of Mulgore, situated along the edge of the southern Rolling Plains. For the nomadic tauren and the local wildlife, these wells provide life across the arid grasslands. However, its tranquil presence has recently drawn the unwanted attention of ruthless exploiters looking to turn the land's natural resources into profit.",al={"winterhoof water well overview","what is winterhoof well","about winterhoof well"},it="place",add=true},{q="What happened at the Winterhoof Water Well?",a="Greedy operatives from the Venture Company seized the well in an underhanded effort to gain control of the local water supply. In the process, they fouled and poisoned its depths, threatening the health of the plains and the herds that depend upon it. Tauren elders and shamans have since called upon brave wanderers to drive out the defilers and use consecrated totems to restore the water's natural purity.",al={"winterhoof well history","what happened at winterhoof well","winterhoof well events"},it="history",add=true},{q="Is the Winterhoof Water Well dangerous?",a="Yes. Beyond the toxic corruption seeping into the groundwater, the well site is overrun by armed hirelings of the Venture Company. These ruthless intruders are fiercely protective of their stake in the land and will violently attack any tauren or Horde traveler who approaches to investigate or cleanse the site.",al={"winterhoof well danger","is winterhoof well safe","winterhoof well threats"},it="danger",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="Defending and purifying the ancestral water wells is one of the first trials young braves face to safeguard the land from outside exploitation.",["class:Shaman"]="Cleansing the well requires elemental harmony, serving as a practical demonstration of using totems to heal wounded water spirits."},kw={"cleansing","mulgore","mull thunderhorn","rolling plains","totem","venture company","water well","winterhoof"},rel={"npc:mull-thunderhorn","quest:748","quest:754","quest:756","subzone:rolling-plains","zone:mulgore","topic:venture-company","topic:tauren"},src={"Winterhoof Water Well"}}
E["zone:mulgore"] = {t="zone",n="Mulgore",z="mulgore",s="Mulgore is the ancestral homeland of the tauren, a lush, sheltered valley of emerald grasslands, towering plateaus, and rolling plains nestled beneath the Stonetalon Mountains. Surrounded by high natural mountain walls, the realm was secured after Cairne Bloodhoof allied with Thrall's Horde to drive out the marauding centaur. Today, it serves as the spiritual and territorial heartland of the united tauren tribes, overseen from the great mesa city of Thunder Bluff.",h="Mulgore is the ancestral homeland of the tauren, a lush, sheltered valley of emerald grasslands, towering...",sec={{t="A Land Reclaimed",b="For generations, the tauren lived as nomads, perpetually hunted and displaced across the central plains by aggressive centaur warbands. Their fortune shifted when High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof encountered Thrall and his displaced orcs. Recognizing shared ideals of honor, shamanism, and strength, the tauren forged an alliance with the nascent Horde. With orcish assistance, Cairne broke the centaur's grip on the region and led his people into Mulgore. Here, they built permanent settlements for the first time in centuries, crowning the highest northern mesas with their capital, Thunder Bluff.",sp=0},{t="The Trials of the Earthmother",b="Reaching maturity in Mulgore requires answering the call of the Great Hunt and honoring the Earthmother. Young tauren begin their training on the southern plateau of Red Cloud Mesa at Camp Narache under Chief Hawkwind. As they descend into the Golden Plains and Bloodhoof Village, they undertake the Rites of the Earthmother, learning to commune with ancestral spirits, forage native herbs, and respect the herds of kodos and tallstriders that sustain their civilization.",sp=0},{t="Encroaching Shadows",b="Despite its tranquil appearance, Mulgore faces growing threats from all quarters. Displaced quilboar have entrenched themselves within Brambleblade Ravine and defiled the sacred burial grounds of Red Rocks. In the mountains, Windfury harpies prey upon wild game, while Palemane gnolls strike from hidden caves. More troubling still are external exploitations: the commercial goblins of the Venture Company have begun defiling the sacred water wells and quarrying the eastern hills, while dwarven explorers from Bael Modan have established the Bael'dun Digsite to unearth ancient titan artifacts against tauren protests.",sp=0}},faq={{q="Why did the tauren settle permanently in Mulgore?",a="The tauren were historically a nomadic people who traveled Kalimdor while pursued by centaur marauders. After joining forces with Thrall's Horde, Cairne Bloodhoof gained the military strength needed to drive off the centaur and reclaim the ancestral valley of Mulgore. They constructed permanent settlements, including Bloodhoof Village and Thunder Bluff, to establish a unified homeland where all tauren tribes could thrive in peace.",al={"why do tauren live here","tauren homeland history","why settle thunder bluff"},it="history"},{q="How do you leave Mulgore on foot?",a="Mulgore is completely ringed by impassable mountain walls except for a single southeastern pass. Following the main dirt highway east past Bloodhoof Village and Morin Cloudstalker's post leads through this mountain corridor directly into the arid plains of the Barrens.",al={"mulgore exit","how to get to barrens from mulgore","mulgore road out"},gp=true},{q="Who are the Venture Company and why are they here?",a="The Venture Company is an unscrupulous goblin-run commercial cartel seeking Kalimdor's natural riches. In Mulgore, they run a mining operation in the eastern mountains and have occupied the vital water wells—such as Thunderhorn, Wildmane, and Winterhoof—desecrating the land and threatening the regional water supply.",al={"venture co in mulgore","why are there goblins here","venture co mine"},it="threat"},{q="Why are dwarves digging in tauren territory?",a="Dwarves from the Bael Modan expedition have established the Bael'dun Digsite in Mulgore's northern foothills. Driven by the Explorers' League to unearth relics of their Titan creators, they excavate tauren land without permission, ignoring tauren protests that such operations scar the Earthmother.",al={"baeldun digsite","why are dwarves in mulgore","bael dun"},it="threat"},{q="What are the Rites of the Earthmother?",a="The Rites of the Earthmother are a sacred series of trials that every young tauren undertakes upon reaching adulthood. These tasks test hunting, tracking, vision communion, and knowledge of the natural balance, proving the young initiate's devotion to the land and the ancestral traditions of their people.",al={"rites of earthmother","tauren maturity quest","rite of the earthmother"},it="purpose"},{q="Who rules Mulgore?",a="High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof governs all tauren lands from the capital city of Thunder Bluff atop the northern mesas. His son, Baine Bloodhoof, governs Bloodhoof Village and manages the daily concerns of the lower plains under Cairne's wisdom.",al={"mulgore leader","who is the chief of mulgore","cairne bloodhoof leader"},it="in_charge"},{q="What is Mulgore?",a="Mulgore is the verdant, sheltered ancestral homeland of the tauren, nestled within a ring of protective mountains in central Kalimdor. Its rolling golden grasslands and towering windswept mesas offer prime hunting grounds and a peaceful haven, accessible on foot only through a single mountain pass to the east leading into the Barrens.",al={"what is this zone","tell me about mulgore","mulgore overview"},it="place",add=true,nx={8}},{q="Is Mulgore dangerous?",a="While Mulgore is far more peaceful than the Barrens, it faces mounting troubles. Aggressive Bristleback quilboar encroach on sacred grounds, Palemane gnolls lurk in southern caves, and Windfury harpies roost along the ridges. Furthermore, the Venture Company exploits pristine water wells and mountainsides, while dwarven excavators desecrate ancestral lands to the west.",al={"is mulgore safe","threats in mulgore","danger here"},it="danger",add=true},{q="Who are the tauren and how did they end up in Mulgore?",a="The tauren, or shu'halo, are an ancient, nomadic people devoted to the Earth Mother. For generations they wandered the Barrens, endlessly hunted and hounded toward extinction by marauding centaur tribes. After forging a bond of honor with Thrall and the newly arrived orcs during the Third War, High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof led his united people back into their ancestral valley of Mulgore to claim a lasting home.",al={"where did tauren come from","tauren history mulgore","how did tauren get here"},it="arrival",add=true},{q="Who is Cairne Bloodhoof?",a="Cairne Bloodhoof is the venerable High Chieftain of the united tauren tribes, leader of the Bloodhoof, and ruler of Thunder Bluff. A peerless warrior and wise elder wielding the Bloodhoof Runespear, Cairne allied his people with Thrall's Horde and guided them through perilous centaur wars to found a lasting sanctuary atop the mesas.",al={"cairne bloodhoof lore","who is the high chieftain","tauren leader"},it="leader",add=true,nx={11}},{q="Is Baine his son?",a="Yes, Baine Bloodhoof is the only son of Cairne and his late lifemate Tamaala. Known for his martial courage and diplomatic restraint, Baine oversees Bloodhoof Village in central Mulgore, serving as chieftain of the settlement while his father prepares him to eventually inherit the mantle of high chieftain.",al={"is baine cairne's son","baine bloodhoof relationship","baine bloodhoof"},it="ties",add=true},{q="What is Bloodhoof Village?",a="Bloodhoof Village is a major settlement situated beside the clear waters of Stonebull Lake in central Mulgore. Named in honor of Cairne's tribe and governed by his son Baine, it serves as the primary trade, crafting, and staging hub where young hunters and braves gather after journeying down from Red Cloud Mesa.",al={"bloodhoof village lore","what is bloodhoof","village in mulgore"},it="place",add=true},{q="What is Thunder Bluff?",a="Thunder Bluff is the magnificent capital of the tauren nation, constructed high atop a series of windswept, defensible mesas towering over northern Mulgore. Founded by Cairne Bloodhoof following the Third War, its raised plateaus and rope bridges shelter tauren of all tribes and provide a safe bastion against centaur invaders.",al={"thunder bluff lore","tauren capital city","city on the mesas"},it="place",add=true},{q="What are the Palemane gnolls doing in Mulgore?",a="The Palemane tribe of gnolls has carved out dens around Palemane Rock and scattered camps along Mulgore's southern mountains. While tauren philosophy values sharing the Earth Mother's bounty, these aggressive gnolls harass travelers, raid nearby hunting grounds, and attack any braves passing near their territory.",al={"palemane gnolls mulgore","why are gnolls here","palemane rock"},it="threat",add=true},{q="How did I end up in Mulgore?",a="If you are a tauren, you were raised here among the nomadic tribes recently united under High Chieftain Cairne Bloodhoof. Following the Third War, your people settled these verdant ancestral plains to escape the centaur and build a permanent homeland, beginning your training on the southern plateau of Red Cloud Mesa. Travelers from other races arrive through the lone, fortified mountain pass leading from the eastern Barrens, or by hitching passage on a wyvern flight directly up to the sprawling mesa city of Thunder Bluff.",al={"how did i get here","why am i in mulgore","how did i get to mulgore"},it="arrival",add=true},{q="What am I supposed to be doing in Mulgore?",a="Young tauren must undertake the Rites of the Earthmother to prove their worth, honor ancestral traditions, and learn the ways of the land. Beyond your rites, Mulgore faces encroaching perils that threaten its peace: aggressive Bristleback quilboar swarm the eastern ravines, Palemane gnolls lurk in the hills, and the goblin-run Venture Company defiles sacred water wells and tears into the mountains for ore. Whether answering the call of Chief Hawkwind or aiding Baine Bloodhoof at Bloodhoof Village, your duty is to protect the tribes and defend the new homeland.",al={"what do i do here","what should i be doing here","what is my goal in mulgore"},it="purpose",add=true}},ang={["race:Tauren"]="This valley is your ancestral home, reclaimed through sacrifice and unity. Preserving its wells and cleansing its sacred grounds are fundamental duties to the Earthmother.",["faction:Horde"]="Mulgore represents the heart of Horde spirituality and agriculture in central Kalimdor, bound to Thrall's people through a blood oath of mutual defense.",["class:Druid"]="The rolling plains and untouched groves serve as one of Kalimdor's purest natural sanctuaries, where tauren druids study balance and the gifts of nature.",["class:Hunter"]="The tradition of the Great Hunt thrives across these plains, where cougars, wolves, and wild kodo test the skill and patience of every tracker."},kw={"bael'dun","baine bloodhoof","bloodhoof village","cairne bloodhoof","camp narache","earthmother","kodo","mulgore","red cloud mesa","red rocks","thunder bluff","venture co"},rel={"zone:thunderbluff","zone:barrens","npc:cairne-bloodhoof","npc:baine-bloodhoof","subzone:bloodhoof-village","subzone:camp-narache","quest:763","subzone:bael-dun-digsite"},m={levels="1-10"},src={"Mulgore (Classic)","Mulgore"}}
